const DATA=[{"t":"scene","act":1,"scene":"I","loc":"Sicilia. An Antechamber in Leontes' Palace.","id":0},{"t":"sd","text":"Enter Camillo and Archidamus.","id":1},{"t":"sp","who":"ARCHIDAMUS","lines":["If you shall chance, Camillo, to visit Bohemia, on the like occasion","whereon my services are now on foot, you shall see, as I have said,","great difference betwixt our Bohemia and your Sicilia."],"id":2,"m":"Camillo, if you ever happen to visit Bohemia on the same kind of errand that brings me here, you will see, as I have said, a great difference between our Bohemia and your Sicilia."},{"t":"sp","who":"CAMILLO","lines":["I think this coming summer the King of Sicilia means to pay Bohemia the","visitation which he justly owes him."],"id":3,"m":"I think the King of Sicilia means to pay Bohemia the visit he rightly owes him this coming summer."},{"t":"sp","who":"ARCHIDAMUS","lines":["Wherein our entertainment shall shame us; we will be justified in our","loves. For indeed,—"],"id":4,"m":"And our hospitality will put us to shame. But our love will make up for it. For really,—"},{"t":"sp","who":"CAMILLO","lines":["Beseech you—"],"id":5,"m":"Please, don't."},{"t":"sp","who":"ARCHIDAMUS","lines":["Verily, I speak it in the freedom of my knowledge. We cannot with such","magnificence—in so rare—I know not what to say. We will give you sleepy","drinks, that your senses, unintelligent of our insufficience, may,","though they cannot praise us, as little accuse us."],"id":6,"m":"Honestly, I speak from what I know. We cannot put on such splendour, in such rare... I don't know what to say. We will give you sleeping drinks, so that your senses, not noticing how poor our entertainment is, may not praise us but will not blame us either."},{"t":"sp","who":"CAMILLO","lines":["You pay a great deal too dear for what's given freely."],"id":7,"m":"You pay far too much for something given freely."},{"t":"sp","who":"ARCHIDAMUS","lines":["Believe me, I speak as my understanding instructs me and as mine","honesty puts it to utterance."],"id":8,"m":"Believe me, I only say what my understanding tells me and what honesty makes me say."},{"t":"sp","who":"CAMILLO","lines":["Sicilia cannot show himself over-kind to Bohemia. They were trained","together in their childhoods, and there rooted betwixt them then such","an affection which cannot choose but branch now. Since their more","mature dignities and royal necessities made separation of their","society, their encounters, though not personal, have been royally","attorneyed with interchange of gifts, letters, loving embassies, that","they have seemed to be together, though absent; shook hands, as over a","vast; and embraced as it were from the ends of opposed winds. The","heavens continue their loves!"],"id":9,"m":"The King of Sicilia cannot be too kind to the King of Bohemia. They were brought up together as children, and a love took root between them then that cannot help but grow branches now. Since they became grown men and kings, and their royal duties separated them, they have met through deputies instead of in person, exchanging gifts, letters and loving embassies, so that they have seemed to be together though apart: they have shaken hands across a great gap and embraced, as it were, from opposite ends of the earth. May heaven keep them loving!"},{"t":"sp","who":"ARCHIDAMUS","lines":["I think there is not in the world either malice or matter to alter it.","You have an unspeakable comfort of your young Prince Mamillius. It is a","gentleman of the greatest promise that ever came into my note."],"id":10,"m":"I don't think there is any malice or any cause in the world that could change it. You have a wonderful comfort in your young Prince Mamillius. He is the most promising young gentleman I have ever come across."},{"t":"sp","who":"CAMILLO","lines":["I very well agree with you in the hopes of him. It is a gallant child;","one that indeed physics the subject, makes old hearts fresh. They that","went on crutches ere he was born desire yet their life to see him a","man."],"id":11,"m":"I quite agree with you about his promise. He is a fine child, one who does his subjects good like medicine and makes old hearts young again. People who were on crutches before he was born want to live long enough to see him grown up."},{"t":"sp","who":"ARCHIDAMUS","lines":["Would they else be content to die?"],"id":12,"m":"Otherwise they would be happy to die?"},{"t":"sp","who":"CAMILLO","lines":["Yes, if there were no other excuse why they should desire to live."],"id":13,"m":"Yes, if they had no other reason for wanting to live."},{"t":"sp","who":"ARCHIDAMUS","lines":["If the king had no son, they would desire to live on crutches till he","had one."],"id":14,"m":"If the king had no son, they would want to go on living on crutches until he had one."},{"t":"sd","text":"Exeunt.","id":15},{"t":"scene","act":1,"scene":"II","loc":"The same. A Room of State in the Palace.","id":16},{"t":"sd","text":"Enter Leontes, Polixenes, Hermione, Mamillius, Camillo and Attendants.","id":17},{"t":"sp","who":"POLIXENES","lines":["Nine changes of the watery star hath been","The shepherd's note since we have left our throne","Without a burden. Time as long again","Would be fill'd up, my brother, with our thanks;","And yet we should, for perpetuity,","Go hence in debt: and therefore, like a cipher,","Yet standing in rich place, I multiply","With one \"we thank you\" many thousands more","That go before it."],"id":18,"m":"The moon has changed nine times, as shepherds count it, since we left our throne empty. We could fill as long again with our thanks, my brother, and still leave for ever in your debt. So, like a nought that stands in a valuable place in a number, I multiply the thousands of thanks that came before with one more 'thank you'."},{"t":"sp","who":"LEONTES","lines":["Stay your thanks a while,","And pay them when you part."],"id":19,"m":"Hold your thanks for a while, and pay them when you leave."},{"t":"sp","who":"POLIXENES","lines":["Sir, that's tomorrow.","I am question'd by my fears, of what may chance","Or breed upon our absence; that may blow","No sneaping winds at home, to make us say","\"This is put forth too truly.\" Besides, I have stay'd","To tire your royalty."],"id":20,"m":"Sir, that will be tomorrow. I am troubled by fears of what may happen or grow at home while I am away: may no biting winds blow there to make us say 'My fears were only too right.' Besides, I have stayed long enough to weary your Majesty."},{"t":"sp","who":"LEONTES","lines":["We are tougher, brother,","Than you can put us to 't."],"id":21,"m":"We are tougher than that, brother. You cannot tire us out."},{"t":"sp","who":"POLIXENES","lines":["No longer stay."],"id":22,"m":"I can stay no longer."},{"t":"sp","who":"LEONTES","lines":["One seve'night longer."],"id":23,"m":"One more week."},{"t":"sp","who":"POLIXENES","lines":["Very sooth, tomorrow."],"id":24,"m":"Truly, tomorrow."},{"t":"sp","who":"LEONTES","lines":["We'll part the time between 's then: and in that","I'll no gainsaying."],"id":25,"m":"Then we'll split the difference, and I will take no refusal."},{"t":"sp","who":"POLIXENES","lines":["Press me not, beseech you, so,","There is no tongue that moves, none, none i' th' world,","So soon as yours, could win me: so it should now,","Were there necessity in your request, although","'Twere needful I denied it. My affairs","Do even drag me homeward: which to hinder","Were, in your love a whip to me; my stay","To you a charge and trouble: to save both,","Farewell, our brother."],"id":26,"m":"Please don't press me so. No tongue in the world could persuade me sooner than yours, and it would win me now if your request were truly needed, even if I had to refuse it. My business drags me home. To hold me back would be, for all your love, a whip to me, and my staying would be an expense and a nuisance to you. To spare us both, farewell, my brother."},{"t":"sp","who":"LEONTES","lines":["Tongue-tied, our queen? Speak you."],"id":27,"m":"Lost for words, my queen? You speak."},{"t":"sp","who":"HERMIONE","lines":["I had thought, sir, to have held my peace until","You had drawn oaths from him not to stay. You, sir,","Charge him too coldly. Tell him you are sure","All in Bohemia's well: this satisfaction","The by-gone day proclaimed. Say this to him,","He's beat from his best ward."],"id":28,"m":"I meant to keep quiet, sir, until you had made him swear he would not stay. You ask him too coldly, sir. Tell him you are sure all is well in Bohemia: yesterday's news confirmed it. Tell him that and he has lost his best defence."},{"t":"sp","who":"LEONTES","lines":["Well said, Hermione."],"id":29,"m":"Well said, Hermione."},{"t":"sp","who":"HERMIONE","lines":["To tell he longs to see his son were strong.","But let him say so then, and let him go;","But let him swear so, and he shall not stay,","We'll thwack him hence with distaffs.","[To Polixenes] Yet of your royal presence I'll adventure","The borrow of a week. When at Bohemia","You take my lord, I'll give him my commission","To let him there a month behind the gest","Prefix'd for's parting:—yet, good deed, Leontes,","I love thee not a jar of th' clock behind","What lady she her lord. You'll stay?"],"id":30,"m":"If he said he longed to see his son, that would be a strong reason. But let him say so, then, and let him go. Let him swear to it and he shall not stay: we'll chase him home with our spindles. (To Polixenes) Still, I'll risk asking to borrow your royal presence for one more week. When you have my husband in Bohemia, I'll give him leave to stay there a month past the date set for his departure. And yet, truly, Leontes, I love you not a tick of the clock less than any wife loves her husband. You'll stay?"},{"t":"sp","who":"POLIXENES","lines":["No, madam."],"id":31,"m":"No, madam."},{"t":"sp","who":"HERMIONE","lines":["Nay, but you will?"],"id":32,"m":"But you will, won't you?"},{"t":"sp","who":"POLIXENES","lines":["I may not, verily."],"id":33,"m":"I may not, truly."},{"t":"sp","who":"HERMIONE","lines":["Verily!","You put me off with limber vows; but I,","Though you would seek t' unsphere the stars with oaths,","Should yet say \"Sir, no going.\" Verily,","You shall not go. A lady's verily is","As potent as a lord's. Will go yet?","Force me to keep you as a prisoner,","Not like a guest: so you shall pay your fees","When you depart, and save your thanks. How say you?","My prisoner or my guest? By your dread \"verily,\"","One of them you shall be."],"id":34,"m":"'Truly'! You fob me off with feeble vows. But even if you tried to shake the stars from their spheres with oaths, I would still say 'Sir, you are not going.' Truly, you shall not go. A lady's 'truly' is as powerful as a lord's. Will you still go? Then you force me to keep you as a prisoner, not a guest. Then you will pay a jailer's fees when you leave, and save your thanks. What do you say? My prisoner or my guest? By your solemn 'truly', you shall be one or the other."},{"t":"sp","who":"POLIXENES","lines":["Your guest, then, madam.","To be your prisoner should import offending;","Which is for me less easy to commit","Than you to punish."],"id":35,"m":"Your guest, then, madam. To be your prisoner would mean I had done wrong, and that would be harder for me to do than for you to punish."},{"t":"sp","who":"HERMIONE","lines":["Not your gaoler then,","But your kind hostess. Come, I'll question you","Of my lord's tricks and yours when you were boys.","You were pretty lordings then."],"id":36,"m":"Not your jailer, then, but your kind hostess. Come, I'll question you about the tricks my husband and you got up to as boys. You must have been pretty little lords then."},{"t":"sp","who":"POLIXENES","lines":["We were, fair queen,","Two lads that thought there was no more behind","But such a day tomorrow as today,","And to be boy eternal."],"id":37,"m":"We were, fair queen, two boys who thought there was nothing ahead but another day tomorrow just like today, and that we would be boys for ever."},{"t":"sp","who":"HERMIONE","lines":["Was not my lord","The verier wag o' th' two?"],"id":38,"m":"Wasn't my husband the naughtier of the two?"},{"t":"sp","who":"POLIXENES","lines":["We were as twinn'd lambs that did frisk i' th' sun","And bleat the one at th' other. What we chang'd","Was innocence for innocence; we knew not","The doctrine of ill-doing, nor dream'd","That any did. Had we pursu'd that life,","And our weak spirits ne'er been higher rear'd","With stronger blood, we should have answer'd heaven","Boldly \"Not guilty,\" the imposition clear'd","Hereditary ours."],"id":39,"m":"We were like twin lambs that frisked in the sun and bleated at each other. What we exchanged was innocence for innocence. We had not learnt how to do wrong, nor dreamt that anyone did. If we had gone on living like that, and our weak spirits had never been stirred by hotter blood, we could have answered heaven boldly 'Not guilty', apart from the original sin we were born with."},{"t":"sp","who":"HERMIONE","lines":["By this we gather","You have tripp'd since."],"id":40,"m":"From that we take it that you have slipped since."},{"t":"sp","who":"POLIXENES","lines":["O my most sacred lady,","Temptations have since then been born to 's! for","In those unfledg'd days was my wife a girl;","Your precious self had then not cross'd the eyes","Of my young play-fellow."],"id":41,"m":"O my most sacred lady, temptations have been born to us since then! For in those unfledged days my wife was a girl, and your precious self had not yet caught the eye of my young playmate."},{"t":"sp","who":"HERMIONE","lines":["Grace to boot!","Of this make no conclusion, lest you say","Your queen and I are devils. Yet go on;","Th' offences we have made you do we'll answer,","If you first sinn'd with us, and that with us","You did continue fault, and that you slipp'd not","With any but with us."],"id":42,"m":"Heaven help us! Don't draw any conclusion from that, or you'll be saying your queen and I are devils. But go on. We will answer for the sins we made you commit, as long as you first sinned with us, kept on sinning with us, and slipped with nobody but us."},{"t":"sp","who":"LEONTES","lines":["Is he won yet?"],"id":43,"m":"Have you won him over yet?"},{"t":"sp","who":"HERMIONE","lines":["He'll stay, my lord."],"id":44,"m":"He'll stay, my lord."},{"t":"sp","who":"LEONTES","lines":["At my request he would not.","Hermione, my dearest, thou never spok'st","To better purpose."],"id":45,"m":"He would not stay when I asked him. Hermione, my dearest, you never spoke to better purpose."},{"t":"sp","who":"HERMIONE","lines":["Never?"],"id":46,"m":"Never?"},{"t":"sp","who":"LEONTES","lines":["Never but once."],"id":47,"m":"Never but once."},{"t":"sp","who":"HERMIONE","lines":["What! have I twice said well? when was't before?","I prithee tell me. Cram 's with praise, and make 's","As fat as tame things: one good deed dying tongueless","Slaughters a thousand waiting upon that.","Our praises are our wages. You may ride 's","With one soft kiss a thousand furlongs ere","With spur we heat an acre. But to th' goal:","My last good deed was to entreat his stay.","What was my first? It has an elder sister,","Or I mistake you: O, would her name were Grace!","But once before I spoke to the purpose—when?","Nay, let me have't; I long."],"id":48,"m":"What! Have I spoken well twice? When was the other time? Please tell me. Stuff us with praise and make us as fat as pets: one good deed that goes unpraised kills a thousand more that would have followed it. Praise is our wages. You can ride us a thousand furlongs with one soft kiss before you can spur us across one acre. But to the point. My last good deed was to beg him to stay. What was my first? It has an older sister, unless I misunderstand you. O, I wish her name were Grace! But once before I spoke to the purpose. When? Come, tell me; I'm longing to know."},{"t":"sp","who":"LEONTES","lines":["Why, that was when","Three crabbed months had sour'd themselves to death,","Ere I could make thee open thy white hand","And clap thyself my love; then didst thou utter","\"I am yours for ever.\""],"id":49,"m":"Why, that was when three sour months had dragged themselves to death before I could make you open your white hand and give it to me as my love. Then you said 'I am yours for ever.'"},{"t":"sp","who":"HERMIONE","lines":["'Tis Grace indeed.","Why, lo you now, I have spoke to th' purpose twice.","The one for ever earn'd a royal husband;","Th' other for some while a friend."],"id":50,"m":"That was Grace indeed. Well then, you see, I have spoken to good purpose twice. The first time earned me a royal husband for ever; the second, a friend for a while."},{"t":"sd","text":"Giving her hand to Polixenes.","id":51},{"t":"sp","who":"LEONTES","lines":["[Aside] Too hot, too hot!","To mingle friendship far is mingling bloods.","I have tremor cordis on me. My heart dances,","But not for joy,—not joy. This entertainment","May a free face put on, derive a liberty","From heartiness, from bounty, fertile bosom,","And well become the agent: 't may, I grant:","But to be paddling palms and pinching fingers,","As now they are, and making practis'd smiles","As in a looking-glass; and then to sigh, as 'twere","The mort o' th' deer. O, that is entertainment","My bosom likes not, nor my brows. Mamillius,","Art thou my boy?"],"id":52,"m":"(Aside) Too hot, too hot! To mix friendship so far is to mix bloods. My heart is trembling. It dances, but not for joy, not joy. This kind of welcome may look innocent, and come from warmth, generosity and a loving heart, and suit the person who gives it: it may, I grant. But to be fondling palms and pinching fingers, as they are now, and giving each other practised smiles as if in a mirror, and then sighing like a horn at the death of a deer: O, that is a welcome my heart does not like, nor my brow. Mamillius, are you my boy?"},{"t":"sp","who":"MAMILLIUS","lines":["Ay, my good lord."],"id":53,"m":"Yes, my good lord."},{"t":"sp","who":"LEONTES","lines":["I' fecks!","Why, that's my bawcock. What! hast smutch'd thy nose?","They say it is a copy out of mine. Come, captain,","We must be neat; not neat, but cleanly, captain:","And yet the steer, the heifer, and the calf","Are all call'd neat.—Still virginalling","Upon his palm?—How now, you wanton calf!","Art thou my calf?"],"id":54,"m":"Bless me! Why, that's my fine fellow. What, have you smudged your nose? They say it's a copy of mine. Come, captain, we must be neat. No, not 'neat': clean, captain. And yet bulls, cows and calves are all called neat, too. (Watching Hermione and Polixenes) Still playing on his palm like a keyboard? (To Mamillius) How now, you playful calf! Are you my calf?"},{"t":"sp","who":"MAMILLIUS","lines":["Yes, if you will, my lord."],"id":55,"m":"Yes, if you like, my lord."},{"t":"sp","who":"LEONTES","lines":["Thou want'st a rough pash and the shoots that I have","To be full like me:—yet they say we are","Almost as like as eggs; women say so,","That will say anything. But were they false","As o'er-dy'd blacks, as wind, as waters, false","As dice are to be wish'd by one that fixes","No bourn 'twixt his and mine, yet were it true","To say this boy were like me. Come, sir page,","Look on me with your welkin eye: sweet villain!","Most dear'st! my collop! Can thy dam?—may't be?","Affection! thy intention stabs the centre:","Thou dost make possible things not so held,","Communicat'st with dreams;—how can this be?—","With what's unreal thou coactive art,","And fellow'st nothing: then 'tis very credent","Thou may'st co-join with something; and thou dost,","And that beyond commission, and I find it,","And that to the infection of my brains","And hardening of my brows."],"id":56,"m":"You need a rough head and horns like mine to be fully like me. Yet they say we are almost as alike as eggs. Women say so, and they will say anything. But even if they were as false as black cloth dyed too often, as wind, as water, as false as loaded dice to a man who doesn't know his from mine, it would still be true to say this boy is like me. Come, young sir, look at me with your sky-blue eyes. Sweet rascal! Dearest! My own flesh! Can your mother... could it be? Passion! Your force pierces to the centre. You make things possible that were thought impossible, and deal with dreams. How can this be? You work together with what is unreal and keep company with nothing. Then it is very believable that you can join with something real. And you do, beyond what is allowed, and I find it, to the poisoning of my mind and the hardening of my brow."},{"t":"sp","who":"POLIXENES","lines":["What means Sicilia?"],"id":57,"m":"What is the matter with the King of Sicilia?"},{"t":"sp","who":"HERMIONE","lines":["He something seems unsettled."],"id":58,"m":"He seems somewhat unsettled."},{"t":"sp","who":"POLIXENES","lines":["How, my lord?","What cheer? How is't with you, best brother?"],"id":59,"m":"What is it, my lord? Are you all right? How are you, my dearest brother?"},{"t":"sp","who":"HERMIONE","lines":["You look","As if you held a brow of much distraction:","Are you mov'd, my lord?"],"id":60,"m":"You look as if your mind is very troubled. Are you upset, my lord?"},{"t":"sp","who":"LEONTES","lines":["No, in good earnest.","How sometimes nature will betray its folly,","Its tenderness, and make itself a pastime","To harder bosoms! Looking on the lines","Of my boy's face, methoughts I did recoil","Twenty-three years, and saw myself unbreech'd,","In my green velvet coat; my dagger muzzled","Lest it should bite its master, and so prove,","As ornaments oft do, too dangerous.","How like, methought, I then was to this kernel,","This squash, this gentleman. Mine honest friend,","Will you take eggs for money?"],"id":61,"m":"No, truly. How nature sometimes gives away its own foolishness and tenderness, and becomes a joke to harder hearts! Looking at my boy's face, I thought I went back twenty-three years and saw myself not yet in breeches, in my green velvet coat, with my dagger blunted in case it bit its master and proved, as ornaments often do, too dangerous. How like this little kernel I seemed to be then, this young pea-pod, this gentleman. (To Mamillius) My honest friend, will you let yourself be fooled?"},{"t":"sp","who":"MAMILLIUS","lines":["No, my lord, I'll fight."],"id":62,"m":"No, my lord, I'll fight."},{"t":"sp","who":"LEONTES","lines":["You will? Why, happy man be 's dole! My brother,","Are you so fond of your young prince as we","Do seem to be of ours?"],"id":63,"m":"You will? Then may good luck be yours! My brother, are you as fond of your young prince as we seem to be of ours?"},{"t":"sp","who":"POLIXENES","lines":["If at home, sir,","He's all my exercise, my mirth, my matter:","Now my sworn friend, and then mine enemy;","My parasite, my soldier, statesman, all.","He makes a July's day short as December;","And with his varying childness cures in me","Thoughts that would thick my blood."],"id":64,"m":"When I'm at home, sir, he is my whole occupation, my joy, my concern: one moment my sworn friend, the next my enemy; my flatterer, my soldier, my statesman, everything. He makes a July day as short as one in December, and with his childish changes he cures me of thoughts that would thicken my blood."},{"t":"sp","who":"LEONTES","lines":["So stands this squire","Offic'd with me. We two will walk, my lord,","And leave you to your graver steps. Hermione,","How thou lov'st us show in our brother's welcome;","Let what is dear in Sicily be cheap:","Next to thyself and my young rover, he's","Apparent to my heart."],"id":65,"m":"This young squire does the same for me. We two will walk off, my lord, and leave you to your more serious business. Hermione, show how much you love us by how you welcome our brother. Let the most precious things in Sicily be cheap to him. Next to you and my young rover here, he is heir to my heart."},{"t":"sp","who":"HERMIONE","lines":["If you would seek us,","We are yours i' the garden. Shall 's attend you there?"],"id":66,"m":"If you want us, we'll be yours in the garden. Shall we wait for you there?"},{"t":"sp","who":"LEONTES","lines":["To your own bents dispose you: you'll be found,","Be you beneath the sky. [Aside] I am angling now,","Though you perceive me not how I give line.","Go to, go to!","How she holds up the neb, the bill to him!","And arms her with the boldness of a wife","To her allowing husband!"],"id":67,"m":"Go where you please. You'll be found, wherever you are under the sky. (Aside) I am fishing now, though you don't see me letting out the line. Go on, go on! How she holds up her lips, her mouth to him! And arms herself with a wife's boldness, as if her husband allowed it!"},{"t":"sd","text":"Exeunt Polixenes, Hermione and Attendants.","id":68},{"t":"sp","who":"LEONTES","cont":1,"lines":["Gone already!","Inch-thick, knee-deep, o'er head and ears a fork'd one!—","Go, play, boy, play. Thy mother plays, and I","Play too; but so disgrac'd a part, whose issue","Will hiss me to my grave: contempt and clamour","Will be my knell. Go, play, boy, play. There have been,","Or I am much deceiv'd, cuckolds ere now;","And many a man there is, even at this present,","Now while I speak this, holds his wife by th' arm,","That little thinks she has been sluic'd in 's absence,","And his pond fish'd by his next neighbour, by","Sir Smile, his neighbour. Nay, there's comfort in 't,","Whiles other men have gates, and those gates open'd,","As mine, against their will. Should all despair","That hath revolted wives, the tenth of mankind","Would hang themselves. Physic for't there's none;","It is a bawdy planet, that will strike","Where 'tis predominant; and 'tis powerful, think it,","From east, west, north, and south. Be it concluded,","No barricado for a belly. Know't;","It will let in and out the enemy","With bag and baggage. Many thousand of us","Have the disease, and feel't not.—How now, boy!"],"id":69,"m":"Gone already! Inch-thick, knee-deep, over head and ears a horned cuckold! Go and play, boy, play. Your mother plays, and I play too, but such a shameful part that its ending will hiss me to my grave. Scorn and jeering will be my funeral bell. Go and play, boy, play. There have been cuckolds before now, unless I'm much mistaken, and many a man, even at this moment, as I speak, holds his wife by the arm with no idea that she has been flooded while he was away, and his pond fished by his next-door neighbour, Sir Smile. Well, there's comfort in that: other men have gates, and those gates opened against their will, like mine. If every man with an unfaithful wife despaired, a tenth of mankind would hang themselves. There's no medicine for it. It is a lecherous planet that strikes wherever it is in the ascendant, and believe me, it is powerful east, west, north and south. In short, there is no barricade for a womb. Make no mistake: it will let the enemy in and out with all his baggage. Thousands of us have the disease and don't feel it. Well now, boy!"},{"t":"sp","who":"MAMILLIUS","lines":["I am like you, they say."],"id":70,"m":"They say I'm like you."},{"t":"sp","who":"LEONTES","lines":["Why, that's some comfort.","What! Camillo there?"],"id":71,"m":"Why, that's some comfort. What, is Camillo there?"},{"t":"sp","who":"CAMILLO","lines":["Ay, my good lord."],"id":72,"m":"Yes, my good lord."},{"t":"sp","who":"LEONTES","lines":["Go play, Mamillius; thou'rt an honest man."],"id":73,"m":"Go and play, Mamillius; you're an honest man."},{"t":"sd","text":"Exit Mamillius.","id":74},{"t":"sp","who":"LEONTES","cont":1,"lines":["Camillo, this great sir will yet stay longer."],"id":75,"m":"Camillo, this great lord is going to stay longer after all."},{"t":"sp","who":"CAMILLO","lines":["You had much ado to make his anchor hold:","When you cast out, it still came home."],"id":76,"m":"You had a hard job getting his anchor to hold. Every time you cast it out, it came back."},{"t":"sp","who":"LEONTES","lines":["Didst note it?"],"id":77,"m":"Did you notice that?"},{"t":"sp","who":"CAMILLO","lines":["He would not stay at your petitions; made","His business more material."],"id":78,"m":"He would not stay when you asked him. He made out his business was more important."},{"t":"sp","who":"LEONTES","lines":["Didst perceive it?","[Aside] They're here with me already; whisp'ring, rounding,","\"Sicilia is a so-forth.\" 'Tis far gone","When I shall gust it last.—How came't, Camillo,","That he did stay?"],"id":79,"m":"Did you see that? (Aside) They are on to me already: whispering, muttering 'The King of Sicilia is a you-know-what.' It's gone a long way if I'm the last to taste it. How did it happen, Camillo, that he stayed?"},{"t":"sp","who":"CAMILLO","lines":["At the good queen's entreaty."],"id":80,"m":"Because the good queen begged him."},{"t":"sp","who":"LEONTES","lines":["At the queen's be't: \"good\" should be pertinent,","But so it is, it is not. Was this taken","By any understanding pate but thine?","For thy conceit is soaking, will draw in","More than the common blocks. Not noted, is't,","But of the finer natures? by some severals","Of head-piece extraordinary? lower messes","Perchance are to this business purblind? say."],"id":81,"m":"'At the queen's', let it be. 'Good' would be the right word, but as things are, it is not. Has anyone with brains but you understood this? Your mind soaks things up and takes in more than common blockheads. It hasn't been noticed, has it, except by finer minds, by a few exceptional heads? The lower ranks are blind to this business, perhaps? Tell me."},{"t":"sp","who":"CAMILLO","lines":["Business, my lord? I think most understand","Bohemia stays here longer."],"id":82,"m":"Business, my lord? I think most people understand that the King of Bohemia is staying here longer."},{"t":"sp","who":"LEONTES","lines":["Ha?"],"id":83,"m":"What?"},{"t":"sp","who":"CAMILLO","lines":["Stays here longer."],"id":84,"m":"Is staying here longer."},{"t":"sp","who":"LEONTES","lines":["Ay, but why?"],"id":85,"m":"Yes, but why?"},{"t":"sp","who":"CAMILLO","lines":["To satisfy your highness, and the entreaties","Of our most gracious mistress."],"id":86,"m":"To please your Highness, and because our most gracious mistress begged him."},{"t":"sp","who":"LEONTES","lines":["Satisfy?","Th' entreaties of your mistress? Satisfy?","Let that suffice. I have trusted thee, Camillo,","With all the nearest things to my heart, as well","My chamber-counsels, wherein, priest-like, thou","Hast cleans'd my bosom; I from thee departed","Thy penitent reform'd. But we have been","Deceiv'd in thy integrity, deceiv'd","In that which seems so."],"id":87,"m":"Please? Because your mistress begged him? Please? Enough said. I have trusted you, Camillo, with everything closest to my heart, my private thoughts too. Like a priest you cleansed my heart, and I left you a reformed penitent. But we have been deceived in your honesty, deceived in what seemed to be so."},{"t":"sp","who":"CAMILLO","lines":["Be it forbid, my lord!"],"id":88,"m":"Heaven forbid, my lord!"},{"t":"sp","who":"LEONTES","lines":["To bide upon't: thou art not honest; or,","If thou inclin'st that way, thou art a coward,","Which hoxes honesty behind, restraining","From course requir'd; or else thou must be counted","A servant grafted in my serious trust,","And therein negligent; or else a fool","That seest a game play'd home, the rich stake drawn,","And tak'st it all for jest."],"id":89,"m":"To stick to the point: you are not honest. Or, if you lean that way, you are a coward, who hamstrings honesty and holds it back from what it should do. Or else you are a servant I took deep into my trust who has been careless with it. Or else a fool who watches a game played out to the end, the big stakes won, and thinks it all a joke."},{"t":"sp","who":"CAMILLO","lines":["My gracious lord,","I may be negligent, foolish, and fearful;","In every one of these no man is free,","But that his negligence, his folly, fear,","Among the infinite doings of the world,","Sometime puts forth. In your affairs, my lord,","If ever I were wilful-negligent,","It was my folly; if industriously","I play'd the fool, it was my negligence,","Not weighing well the end; if ever fearful","To do a thing, where I the issue doubted,","Whereof the execution did cry out","Against the non-performance, 'twas a fear","Which oft affects the wisest: these, my lord,","Are such allow'd infirmities that honesty","Is never free of. But, beseech your Grace,","Be plainer with me; let me know my trespass","By its own visage: if I then deny it,","'Tis none of mine."],"id":90,"m":"My gracious lord, I may be careless, foolish and fearful. No man is free of any of these: in the endless business of the world his carelessness, his folly or his fear sometimes shows. In your affairs, my lord, if I was ever wilfully careless, it was my folly. If I ever played the fool on purpose, it was carelessness, not thinking about the outcome. If I was ever afraid to do something when I doubted how it would end, and doing it cried out against not doing it, that was a fear that often affects the wisest. These, my lord, are accepted weaknesses that even honesty is never free of. But, I beg your Grace, be plainer with me. Let me see my offence by its own face. If I then deny it, it isn't mine."},{"t":"sp","who":"LEONTES","lines":["Ha' not you seen, Camillo?","(But that's past doubt: you have, or your eye-glass","Is thicker than a cuckold's horn) or heard?","(For, to a vision so apparent, rumour","Cannot be mute) or thought? (for cogitation","Resides not in that man that does not think)","My wife is slippery? If thou wilt confess,","Or else be impudently negative,","To have nor eyes nor ears nor thought, then say","My wife's a hobby-horse, deserves a name","As rank as any flax-wench that puts to","Before her troth-plight: say't and justify't."],"id":91,"m":"Haven't you seen, Camillo (but that's beyond doubt, you have, or your eyeglass is thicker than a cuckold's horn), or heard (for when something is so plain to see, rumour cannot be silent), or thought (for a man who does not think has no mind), that my wife is unfaithful? If you will admit it, or else shamelessly deny that you have eyes, ears or thoughts, then say my wife is a whore, deserving a name as foul as any flax-girl who sleeps with a man before her betrothal. Say it and stand by it."},{"t":"sp","who":"CAMILLO","lines":["I would not be a stander-by to hear","My sovereign mistress clouded so, without","My present vengeance taken: 'shrew my heart,","You never spoke what did become you less","Than this; which to reiterate were sin","As deep as that, though true."],"id":92,"m":"I would not stand by and hear my royal mistress slandered like that without taking revenge at once. Curse me, but you never said anything that suited you less. To repeat it would be a sin as great as that one, even if it were true."},{"t":"sp","who":"LEONTES","lines":["Is whispering nothing?","Is leaning cheek to cheek? is meeting noses?","Kissing with inside lip? Stopping the career","Of laughter with a sigh?—a note infallible","Of breaking honesty?—horsing foot on foot?","Skulking in corners? Wishing clocks more swift?","Hours, minutes? Noon, midnight? and all eyes","Blind with the pin and web but theirs, theirs only,","That would unseen be wicked? Is this nothing?","Why, then the world and all that's in't is nothing,","The covering sky is nothing, Bohemia nothing,","My wife is nothing, nor nothing have these nothings,","If this be nothing."],"id":93,"m":"Is whispering nothing? Is leaning cheek to cheek? Is touching noses? Kissing with open lips? Stopping a laugh with a sigh, a sure sign of failing virtue? Riding foot on foot? Hiding in corners? Wishing the clocks would go faster, hours into minutes, noon into midnight? And wishing everyone else's eyes were blind with cataracts except theirs, so they could be wicked unseen? Is this nothing? Why, then the world and everything in it is nothing, the sky above is nothing, Bohemia is nothing, my wife is nothing, and these nothings have nothing in them, if this is nothing."},{"t":"sp","who":"CAMILLO","lines":["Good my lord, be cur'd","Of this diseas'd opinion, and betimes,","For 'tis most dangerous."],"id":94,"m":"My good lord, cure yourself of this sick opinion, and quickly, for it is very dangerous."},{"t":"sp","who":"LEONTES","lines":["Say it be, 'tis true."],"id":95,"m":"Say it is: it's true."},{"t":"sp","who":"CAMILLO","lines":["No, no, my lord."],"id":96,"m":"No, no, my lord."},{"t":"sp","who":"LEONTES","lines":["It is; you lie, you lie:","I say thou liest, Camillo, and I hate thee,","Pronounce thee a gross lout, a mindless slave,","Or else a hovering temporizer that","Canst with thine eyes at once see good and evil,","Inclining to them both. Were my wife's liver","Infected as her life, she would not live","The running of one glass."],"id":97,"m":"It is. You lie, you lie. I say you lie, Camillo, and I hate you. I call you a great lout, a mindless slave, or else a wavering time-server who can see good and evil at the same time and lean towards both. If my wife's liver were as diseased as her life, she would not live another hour."},{"t":"sp","who":"CAMILLO","lines":["Who does infect her?"],"id":98,"m":"Who has infected her?"},{"t":"sp","who":"LEONTES","lines":["Why, he that wears her like her medal, hanging","About his neck, Bohemia: who, if I","Had servants true about me, that bare eyes","To see alike mine honour as their profits,","Their own particular thrifts, they would do that","Which should undo more doing: ay, and thou,","His cupbearer,—whom I from meaner form","Have bench'd and rear'd to worship, who mayst see","Plainly as heaven sees earth and earth sees heaven,","How I am galled,—mightst bespice a cup,","To give mine enemy a lasting wink;","Which draught to me were cordial."],"id":99,"m":"Why, the man who wears her like a medal hanging round his neck: Bohemia. If I had loyal servants around me, with eyes to look after my honour as well as their own profits and private gains, they would do something to stop any more of this. Yes, and you, his cupbearer, whom I raised from humble rank to high office, you who can see as plainly as heaven sees earth and earth sees heaven how I am wounded, you could spice a cup to give my enemy a sleep that lasts for ever. That drink would be medicine to me."},{"t":"sp","who":"CAMILLO","lines":["Sir, my lord,","I could do this, and that with no rash potion,","But with a ling'ring dram, that should not work","Maliciously like poison. But I cannot","Believe this crack to be in my dread mistress,","So sovereignly being honourable.","I have lov'd thee,—"],"id":100,"m":"Sir, my lord, I could do this, and not with a quick poison but with a slow dose that would not work openly like poison. But I cannot believe there is this flaw in my royal mistress, who is so supremely honourable. I have loved you—"},{"t":"sp","who":"LEONTES","lines":["Make that thy question, and go rot!","Dost think I am so muddy, so unsettled,","To appoint myself in this vexation; sully","The purity and whiteness of my sheets,","(Which to preserve is sleep, which being spotted","Is goads, thorns, nettles, tails of wasps)","Give scandal to the blood o' th' prince, my son,","(Who I do think is mine, and love as mine)","Without ripe moving to't? Would I do this?","Could man so blench?"],"id":101,"m":"Ask that question and go and rot! Do you think I am so muddled, so unbalanced, that I would bring this torment on myself? Stain the purity and whiteness of my sheets (keeping them clean means sleep; stained, they are goads, thorns, nettles and wasps' stings)? Cast doubt on the birth of the prince, my son, whom I think is mine and love as mine, without good reason? Would I do this? Could any man stray so far?"},{"t":"sp","who":"CAMILLO","lines":["I must believe you, sir:","I do; and will fetch off Bohemia for't;","Provided that, when he's remov'd, your highness","Will take again your queen as yours at first,","Even for your son's sake, and thereby for sealing","The injury of tongues in courts and kingdoms","Known and allied to yours."],"id":102,"m":"I must believe you, sir. I do, and I will get rid of the King of Bohemia for it, provided that, once he is out of the way, your Highness will take back your queen as you did at first, if only for your son's sake, and so stop the wagging of tongues in courts and kingdoms that know you and are allied to you."},{"t":"sp","who":"LEONTES","lines":["Thou dost advise me","Even so as I mine own course have set down:","I'll give no blemish to her honour, none."],"id":103,"m":"You advise me exactly as I had planned myself. I will cast no stain on her honour, none."},{"t":"sp","who":"CAMILLO","lines":["My lord,","Go then; and with a countenance as clear","As friendship wears at feasts, keep with Bohemia","And with your queen. I am his cupbearer.","If from me he have wholesome beverage,","Account me not your servant."],"id":104,"m":"My lord, go then, and with a face as clear as friendship wears at feasts, keep company with the King of Bohemia and your queen. I am his cupbearer. If he gets a wholesome drink from me, don't count me as your servant."},{"t":"sp","who":"LEONTES","lines":["This is all:","Do't, and thou hast the one half of my heart;","Do't not, thou splitt'st thine own."],"id":105,"m":"That's all. Do it, and you have half my heart. Don't, and you split your own."},{"t":"sp","who":"CAMILLO","lines":["I'll do't, my lord."],"id":106,"m":"I'll do it, my lord."},{"t":"sp","who":"LEONTES","lines":["I will seem friendly, as thou hast advis'd me."],"id":107,"m":"I will seem friendly, as you have advised me."},{"t":"sd","text":"Exit.","id":108},{"t":"sp","who":"CAMILLO","lines":["O miserable lady! But, for me,","What case stand I in? I must be the poisoner","Of good Polixenes, and my ground to do't","Is the obedience to a master; one","Who, in rebellion with himself, will have","All that are his so too. To do this deed,","Promotion follows. If I could find example","Of thousands that had struck anointed kings","And flourish'd after, I'd not do't. But since","Nor brass, nor stone, nor parchment, bears not one,","Let villainy itself forswear't. I must","Forsake the court: to do't, or no, is certain","To me a break-neck. Happy star reign now!","Here comes Bohemia."],"id":109,"m":"O miserable lady! But as for me, what position am I in? I must poison good Polixenes, and my reason for doing it is obedience to a master who is at war with himself and wants everyone who serves him to be the same. Promotion would follow the deed. If I could find thousands of examples of men who struck down anointed kings and prospered afterwards, I still would not do it. But since not a single one is recorded in brass, stone or parchment, let villainy itself refuse it. I must leave the court: whether I do it or not, it means breaking my neck. May my lucky star rule now! Here comes the King of Bohemia."},{"t":"sd","text":"Enter Polixenes.","id":110},{"t":"sp","who":"POLIXENES","lines":["This is strange. Methinks","My favour here begins to warp. Not speak?","Good day, Camillo."],"id":111,"m":"This is strange. I think my welcome here is beginning to warp. Not speaking? Good day, Camillo."},{"t":"sp","who":"CAMILLO","lines":["Hail, most royal sir!"],"id":112,"m":"Greetings, most royal sir!"},{"t":"sp","who":"POLIXENES","lines":["What is the news i' th' court?"],"id":113,"m":"What is the news at court?"},{"t":"sp","who":"CAMILLO","lines":["None rare, my lord."],"id":114,"m":"Nothing special, my lord."},{"t":"sp","who":"POLIXENES","lines":["The king hath on him such a countenance","As he had lost some province, and a region","Lov'd as he loves himself. Even now I met him","With customary compliment, when he,","Wafting his eyes to the contrary, and falling","A lip of much contempt, speeds from me, and","So leaves me to consider what is breeding","That changes thus his manners."],"id":115,"m":"The king has a face on him as if he had lost a province, a region he loves as much as himself. Just now I greeted him in the usual way, and he turned his eyes away, let his lip drop in contempt, hurried off, and left me to wonder what has happened to change his manner like this."},{"t":"sp","who":"CAMILLO","lines":["I dare not know, my lord."],"id":116,"m":"I dare not know, my lord."},{"t":"sp","who":"POLIXENES","lines":["How, dare not? Do not? Do you know, and dare not?","Be intelligent to me? 'Tis thereabouts;","For, to yourself, what you do know, you must,","And cannot say you dare not. Good Camillo,","Your chang'd complexions are to me a mirror","Which shows me mine chang'd too; for I must be","A party in this alteration, finding","Myself thus alter'd with't."],"id":117,"m":"What, dare not? Do not? Do you know, and dare not tell me? That's about it, for you must know what you know, and cannot say you dare not know it. Good Camillo, your changed looks are a mirror to me, showing that mine have changed too. I must be part of this change, since I find myself changed along with it."},{"t":"sp","who":"CAMILLO","lines":["There is a sickness","Which puts some of us in distemper, but","I cannot name the disease, and it is caught","Of you that yet are well."],"id":118,"m":"There is a sickness that has upset some of us, but I cannot name the disease, and it was caught from you, though you are still well."},{"t":"sp","who":"POLIXENES","lines":["How caught of me?","Make me not sighted like the basilisk.","I have look'd on thousands who have sped the better","By my regard, but kill'd none so. Camillo,—","As you are certainly a gentleman, thereto","Clerk-like, experienc'd, which no less adorns","Our gentry than our parents' noble names,","In whose success we are gentle,—I beseech you,","If you know aught which does behove my knowledge","Thereof to be inform'd, imprison't not","In ignorant concealment."],"id":119,"m":"Caught from me? Don't make me out to be a basilisk that kills with a look. I have looked at thousands of people who have done better for my favour, but killed none of them. Camillo, as you are certainly a gentleman, and learned and experienced too, which grace our rank as much as our parents' noble names that we inherit, I beg you, if you know anything I ought to be told, don't lock it up in silence."},{"t":"sp","who":"CAMILLO","lines":["I may not answer."],"id":120,"m":"I may not answer."},{"t":"sp","who":"POLIXENES","lines":["A sickness caught of me, and yet I well?","I must be answer'd. Dost thou hear, Camillo,","I conjure thee, by all the parts of man","Which honour does acknowledge, whereof the least","Is not this suit of mine, that thou declare","What incidency thou dost guess of harm","Is creeping toward me; how far off, how near;","Which way to be prevented, if to be;","If not, how best to bear it."],"id":121,"m":"A sickness caught from me, and yet I am well? I must have an answer. Do you hear, Camillo? I call on you, by everything in a man that honour recognises, not the least of which is this request of mine, to tell me what harm you guess is creeping towards me: how far off, how near, how it can be prevented if it can, and if not, how best to bear it."},{"t":"sp","who":"CAMILLO","lines":["Sir, I will tell you;","Since I am charg'd in honour, and by him","That I think honourable. Therefore mark my counsel,","Which must be ev'n as swiftly follow'd as","I mean to utter it, or both yourself and me","Cry lost, and so goodnight!"],"id":122,"m":"Sir, I will tell you, since I am called on in the name of honour, and by a man I think honourable. So listen to my advice, which you must follow as quickly as I speak it, or you and I can both say 'We are lost', and goodnight!"},{"t":"sp","who":"POLIXENES","lines":["On, good Camillo."],"id":123,"m":"Go on, good Camillo."},{"t":"sp","who":"CAMILLO","lines":["I am appointed him to murder you."],"id":124,"m":"I have been appointed to murder you."},{"t":"sp","who":"POLIXENES","lines":["By whom, Camillo?"],"id":125,"m":"By whom, Camillo?"},{"t":"sp","who":"CAMILLO","lines":["By the king."],"id":126,"m":"By the king."},{"t":"sp","who":"POLIXENES","lines":["For what?"],"id":127,"m":"Why?"},{"t":"sp","who":"CAMILLO","lines":["He thinks, nay, with all confidence he swears,","As he had seen't or been an instrument","To vice you to't, that you have touch'd his queen","Forbiddenly."],"id":128,"m":"He thinks, no, he swears with total confidence, as if he had seen it or had helped persuade you to it, that you have touched his queen unlawfully."},{"t":"sp","who":"POLIXENES","lines":["O, then my best blood turn","To an infected jelly, and my name","Be yok'd with his that did betray the Best!","Turn then my freshest reputation to","A savour that may strike the dullest nostril","Where I arrive, and my approach be shunn'd,","Nay, hated too, worse than the great'st infection","That e'er was heard or read!"],"id":129,"m":"O, then let my best blood turn to infected jelly, and my name be coupled with Judas, who betrayed Christ! Let my freshest reputation turn into a stink that would strike the dullest nose wherever I go, and let people shun my approach, no, hate it, worse than the worst plague ever heard or read of!"},{"t":"sp","who":"CAMILLO","lines":["Swear his thought over","By each particular star in heaven and","By all their influences, you may as well","Forbid the sea for to obey the moon","As or by oath remove or counsel shake","The fabric of his folly, whose foundation","Is pil'd upon his faith, and will continue","The standing of his body."],"id":130,"m":"You can swear him out of it by every star in heaven and all their influences, but you might as well forbid the sea to obey the moon as hope to remove, by oath or advice, the building of his folly. Its foundation rests on his belief, and it will stand as long as his body does."},{"t":"sp","who":"POLIXENES","lines":["How should this grow?"],"id":131,"m":"How could this have grown?"},{"t":"sp","who":"CAMILLO","lines":["I know not: but I am sure 'tis safer to","Avoid what's grown than question how 'tis born.","If therefore you dare trust my honesty,","That lies enclosed in this trunk, which you","Shall bear along impawn'd, away tonight.","Your followers I will whisper to the business,","And will by twos and threes, at several posterns,","Clear them o' th' city. For myself, I'll put","My fortunes to your service, which are here","By this discovery lost. Be not uncertain,","For, by the honour of my parents, I","Have utter'd truth: which if you seek to prove,","I dare not stand by; nor shall you be safer","Than one condemned by the king's own mouth,","Thereon his execution sworn."],"id":132,"m":"I don't know. But I am sure it is safer to avoid what has grown than to ask how it was born. So, if you dare trust my honesty, which is locked in this body of mine that you shall take with you as a pledge, leave tonight. I will whisper the business to your followers, and get them out of the city in twos and threes by different back gates. As for me, I'll put my fortunes at your service, since they are lost here now that I have told you. Do not hesitate, for by my parents' honour I have told the truth. If you try to test it, I dare not stay, and you will be no safer than a man condemned by the king's own mouth, with his execution sworn."},{"t":"sp","who":"POLIXENES","lines":["I do believe thee.","I saw his heart in 's face. Give me thy hand,","Be pilot to me, and thy places shall","Still neighbour mine. My ships are ready, and","My people did expect my hence departure","Two days ago. This jealousy","Is for a precious creature: as she's rare,","Must it be great; and, as his person's mighty,","Must it be violent; and as he does conceive","He is dishonour'd by a man which ever","Profess'd to him, why, his revenges must","In that be made more bitter. Fear o'ershades me.","Good expedition be my friend, and comfort","The gracious queen, part of his theme, but nothing","Of his ill-ta'en suspicion! Come, Camillo,","I will respect thee as a father if","Thou bear'st my life off hence. Let us avoid."],"id":133,"m":"I believe you. I saw his heart in his face. Give me your hand. Be my pilot, and your place shall always be next to mine. My ships are ready, and my people expected me to leave two days ago. This jealousy is over a precious creature: as she is rare, it must be great; as he is mighty, it must be violent; and as he believes he is dishonoured by a man who always claimed to be his friend, his revenge must be all the more bitter. Fear overshadows me. May a quick escape be my friend, and comfort the gracious queen, who is the subject of his suspicion but has done nothing to earn it! Come, Camillo, I will honour you like a father if you carry my life away from here. Let us go."},{"t":"sp","who":"CAMILLO","lines":["It is in mine authority to command","The keys of all the posterns: please your highness","To take the urgent hour. Come, sir, away."],"id":134,"m":"I have authority to command the keys of all the back gates. Please, your Highness, seize the moment. Come, sir, away."},{"t":"sd","text":"Exeunt.","id":135},{"t":"scene","act":2,"scene":"I","loc":"Sicilia. A Room in the Palace.","id":136},{"t":"sd","text":"Enter Hermione, Mamillius and Ladies.","id":137},{"t":"sp","who":"HERMIONE","lines":["Take the boy to you: he so troubles me,","'Tis past enduring."],"id":138,"m":"Take the boy away. He bothers me so much it's past bearing."},{"t":"sp","who":"FIRST LADY","lines":["Come, my gracious lord,","Shall I be your playfellow?"],"id":139,"m":"Come, my gracious lord, shall I be your playmate?"},{"t":"sp","who":"MAMILLIUS","lines":["No, I'll none of you."],"id":140,"m":"No, I don't want you."},{"t":"sp","who":"FIRST LADY","lines":["Why, my sweet lord?"],"id":141,"m":"Why not, my sweet lord?"},{"t":"sp","who":"MAMILLIUS","lines":["You'll kiss me hard, and speak to me as if","I were a baby still. I love you better."],"id":142,"m":"You'll kiss me hard and talk to me as if I were still a baby. (To the Second Lady) I like you better."},{"t":"sp","who":"SECOND LADY","lines":["And why so, my lord?"],"id":143,"m":"And why is that, my lord?"},{"t":"sp","who":"MAMILLIUS","lines":["Not for because","Your brows are blacker; yet black brows, they say,","Become some women best, so that there be not","Too much hair there, but in a semicircle","Or a half-moon made with a pen."],"id":144,"m":"Not because your eyebrows are darker. Though they say dark eyebrows suit some women best, as long as there isn't too much hair, just a semicircle or a half-moon drawn with a pen."},{"t":"sp","who":"SECOND LADY","lines":["Who taught this?"],"id":145,"m":"Who taught you this?"},{"t":"sp","who":"MAMILLIUS","lines":["I learn'd it out of women's faces. Pray now,","What colour are your eyebrows?"],"id":146,"m":"I learnt it from women's faces. Now tell me, what colour are your eyebrows?"},{"t":"sp","who":"FIRST LADY","lines":["Blue, my lord."],"id":147,"m":"Blue, my lord."},{"t":"sp","who":"MAMILLIUS","lines":["Nay, that's a mock. I have seen a lady's nose","That has been blue, but not her eyebrows."],"id":148,"m":"No, you're teasing. I have seen a lady's nose that was blue, but not her eyebrows."},{"t":"sp","who":"FIRST LADY","lines":["Hark ye,","The queen your mother rounds apace. We shall","Present our services to a fine new prince","One of these days, and then you'd wanton with us,","If we would have you."],"id":149,"m":"Listen, the queen your mother is getting rounder fast. One of these days we will be serving a fine new prince, and then you'd be glad to play with us, if we'd have you."},{"t":"sp","who":"SECOND LADY","lines":["She is spread of late","Into a goodly bulk: good time encounter her!"],"id":150,"m":"She has grown nicely big lately. May she have a safe delivery!"},{"t":"sp","who":"HERMIONE","lines":["What wisdom stirs amongst you? Come, sir, now","I am for you again. Pray you sit by us,","And tell 's a tale."],"id":151,"m":"What wise talk is going on among you? Come, sir, I'm ready for you again. Please sit by me and tell us a story."},{"t":"sp","who":"MAMILLIUS","lines":["Merry or sad shall't be?"],"id":152,"m":"Shall it be happy or sad?"},{"t":"sp","who":"HERMIONE","lines":["As merry as you will."],"id":153,"m":"As happy as you like."},{"t":"sp","who":"MAMILLIUS","lines":["A sad tale's best for winter. I have one","Of sprites and goblins."],"id":154,"m":"A sad tale's best for winter. I have one about ghosts and goblins."},{"t":"sp","who":"HERMIONE","lines":["Let's have that, good sir.","Come on, sit down. Come on, and do your best","To fright me with your sprites: you're powerful at it."],"id":155,"m":"Let's have that, good sir. Come on, sit down. Come on, and do your best to frighten me with your ghosts. You're good at it."},{"t":"sp","who":"MAMILLIUS","lines":["There was a man,—"],"id":156,"m":"There was a man—"},{"t":"sp","who":"HERMIONE","lines":["Nay, come, sit down, then on."],"id":157,"m":"No, come, sit down first, then go on."},{"t":"sp","who":"MAMILLIUS","lines":["Dwelt by a churchyard. I will tell it softly,","Yond crickets shall not hear it."],"id":158,"m":"Who lived by a churchyard. I'll tell it quietly: those crickets over there shan't hear it."},{"t":"sp","who":"HERMIONE","lines":["Come on then,","And give't me in mine ear."],"id":159,"m":"Come on then, and whisper it in my ear."},{"t":"sd","text":"Enter Leontes, Antigonus, Lords and Guards.","id":160},{"t":"sp","who":"LEONTES","lines":["Was he met there? his train? Camillo with him?"],"id":161,"m":"Was he seen there? His followers? Camillo with him?"},{"t":"sp","who":"FIRST LORD","lines":["Behind the tuft of pines I met them, never","Saw I men scour so on their way: I ey'd them","Even to their ships."],"id":162,"m":"I met them behind the clump of pines. I never saw men hurry so on their way. I watched them all the way to their ships."},{"t":"sp","who":"LEONTES","lines":["How blest am I","In my just censure, in my true opinion!","Alack, for lesser knowledge! How accurs'd","In being so blest! There may be in the cup","A spider steep'd, and one may drink, depart,","And yet partake no venom, for his knowledge","Is not infected; but if one present","Th' abhorr'd ingredient to his eye, make known","How he hath drunk, he cracks his gorge, his sides,","With violent hefts. I have drunk, and seen the spider.","Camillo was his help in this, his pander.","There is a plot against my life, my crown;","All's true that is mistrusted. That false villain","Whom I employ'd, was pre-employ'd by him.","He has discover'd my design, and I","Remain a pinch'd thing; yea, a very trick","For them to play at will. How came the posterns","So easily open?"],"id":163,"m":"How blessed I am in my right judgement, my true opinion! O, I wish I knew less! How cursed I am in being so blessed! There may be a spider soaked in a cup, and a man may drink and go away and take no poison, because his mind is not infected. But if someone shows him the disgusting ingredient and tells him what he has drunk, he retches violently till his throat and sides crack. I have drunk, and seen the spider. Camillo helped him in this, his go-between. There is a plot against my life and my crown. Everything I suspected is true. That false villain I hired had already been hired by him. He has revealed my plan, and I am left a pinched fool, yes, a mere toy for them to play with as they like. How did the back gates come to be so easily opened?"},{"t":"sp","who":"FIRST LORD","lines":["By his great authority,","Which often hath no less prevail'd than so","On your command."],"id":164,"m":"By his great authority, which has often worked just as well when you commanded it."},{"t":"sp","who":"LEONTES","lines":["I know't too well.","Give me the boy. I am glad you did not nurse him.","Though he does bear some signs of me, yet you","Have too much blood in him."],"id":165,"m":"I know it too well. (To Hermione) Give me the boy. I'm glad you did not nurse him. Though he has some marks of me, there is too much of your blood in him."},{"t":"sp","who":"HERMIONE","lines":["What is this? sport?"],"id":166,"m":"What is this? A game?"},{"t":"sp","who":"LEONTES","lines":["Bear the boy hence, he shall not come about her,","Away with him, and let her sport herself","With that she's big with; for 'tis Polixenes","Has made thee swell thus."],"id":167,"m":"Take the boy away. He shall not come near her. Away with him, and let her play with the one she's pregnant with. For it is Polixenes who has made you swell like this."},{"t":"sd","text":"Exit Mamillius with some of the Guards.","id":168},{"t":"sp","who":"HERMIONE","lines":["But I'd say he had not,","And I'll be sworn you would believe my saying,","Howe'er you learn th' nayward."],"id":169,"m":"But I'd only have to say he had not, and I swear you would believe me, however you lean the other way."},{"t":"sp","who":"LEONTES","lines":["You, my lords,","Look on her, mark her well. Be but about","To say, \"she is a goodly lady,\" and","The justice of your hearts will thereto add","\"'Tis pity she's not honest, honourable\":","Praise her but for this her without-door form,","Which on my faith deserves high speech, and straight","The shrug, the hum or ha, these petty brands","That calumny doth use—O, I am out,","That mercy does; for calumny will sear","Virtue itself—these shrugs, these hum's, and ha's,","When you have said \"she's goodly,\" come between,","Ere you can say \"she's honest\": but be it known,","From him that has most cause to grieve it should be,","She's an adultress!"],"id":170,"m":"You, my lords, look at her, look at her well. Just start to say 'She is a fine lady', and the honesty of your hearts will add 'It's a pity she's not chaste and honourable.' Praise her just for her outward looks, which I swear deserve high praise, and at once the shrug, the 'hm' or 'ha', these little marks of slander—O, I am wrong, of mercy, for slander would brand virtue itself—these shrugs, these hums and has, will come in between 'she's fine' and 'she's honest'. But let it be known, from the man who has most cause to grieve that it is so: she's an adulteress!"},{"t":"sp","who":"HERMIONE","lines":["Should a villain say so,","The most replenish'd villain in the world,","He were as much more villain: you, my lord,","Do but mistake."],"id":171,"m":"If a villain said so, the most complete villain in the world, it would make him even more of a villain. You, my lord, are only mistaken."},{"t":"sp","who":"LEONTES","lines":["You have mistook, my lady,","Polixenes for Leontes. O thou thing,","Which I'll not call a creature of thy place,","Lest barbarism, making me the precedent,","Should a like language use to all degrees,","And mannerly distinguishment leave out","Betwixt the prince and beggar. I have said","She's an adultress; I have said with whom:","More, she's a traitor, and Camillo is","A federary with her; and one that knows","What she should shame to know herself","But with her most vile principal, that she's","A bed-swerver, even as bad as those","That vulgars give bold'st titles; ay, and privy","To this their late escape."],"id":172,"m":"You are the one who has mistaken, my lady, Polixenes for Leontes. O you thing, I won't call you by a name that fits your rank, in case rudeness, following my example, uses the same language to all ranks and leaves out the polite difference between prince and beggar. I have said she's an adulteress. I have said with whom. More, she's a traitor, and Camillo is her accomplice, and someone who knows what she should be ashamed to know, except with her vile partner: that she's a bed-hopper, as bad as the women the common people call the boldest names. Yes, and she knew about their recent escape."},{"t":"sp","who":"HERMIONE","lines":["No, by my life,","Privy to none of this. How will this grieve you,","When you shall come to clearer knowledge, that","You thus have publish'd me! Gentle my lord,","You scarce can right me throughly then, to say","You did mistake."],"id":173,"m":"No, by my life, I knew nothing about any of this. How it will grieve you, when you know better, that you have shamed me so publicly! My gentle lord, you will hardly be able to put me right then by saying you were mistaken."},{"t":"sp","who":"LEONTES","lines":["No. If I mistake","In those foundations which I build upon,","The centre is not big enough to bear","A school-boy's top. Away with her to prison!","He who shall speak for her is afar off guilty","But that he speaks."],"id":174,"m":"No. If I am wrong in the foundations I build on, the centre of the earth is not big enough to hold a schoolboy's spinning top. Away with her to prison! Anyone who speaks for her is guilty, at a distance, just by speaking."},{"t":"sp","who":"HERMIONE","lines":["There's some ill planet reigns:","I must be patient till the heavens look","With an aspect more favourable. Good my lords,","I am not prone to weeping, as our sex","Commonly are; the want of which vain dew","Perchance shall dry your pities. But I have","That honourable grief lodg'd here which burns","Worse than tears drown: beseech you all, my lords,","With thoughts so qualified as your charities","Shall best instruct you, measure me; and so","The king's will be perform'd."],"id":175,"m":"Some evil planet is ruling. I must be patient till the heavens look on me more favourably. Good my lords, I am not inclined to weep, as women commonly are, and the lack of that useless dew may perhaps dry up your pity. But I have an honourable grief lodged here that burns worse than tears can drown. I beg you all, my lords, judge me with thoughts tempered by your charity. And so let the king's will be done."},{"t":"sp","who":"LEONTES","lines":["Shall I be heard?"],"id":176,"m":"Am I to be obeyed?"},{"t":"sp","who":"HERMIONE","lines":["Who is't that goes with me? Beseech your highness","My women may be with me, for you see","My plight requires it. Do not weep, good fools;","There is no cause: when you shall know your mistress","Has deserv'd prison, then abound in tears","As I come out: this action I now go on","Is for my better grace. Adieu, my lord:","I never wish'd to see you sorry; now","I trust I shall. My women, come; you have leave."],"id":177,"m":"Who is coming with me? I beg your Highness, let my women be with me, for you see my condition needs it. Don't cry, you good fools. There's no reason. When you find out your mistress deserved prison, then weep floods as I come out. What I go through now is for my greater honour. Goodbye, my lord. I never wished to see you sorry, but now I believe I shall. Come, my women; you have permission."},{"t":"sp","who":"LEONTES","lines":["Go, do our bidding. Hence!"],"id":178,"m":"Go, do as we command. Away!"},{"t":"sd","text":"Exeunt Queen and Ladies with Guards.","id":179},{"t":"sp","who":"FIRST LORD","lines":["Beseech your highness, call the queen again."],"id":180,"m":"I beg your Highness, call the queen back."},{"t":"sp","who":"ANTIGONUS","lines":["Be certain what you do, sir, lest your justice","Prove violence, in the which three great ones suffer,","Yourself, your queen, your son."],"id":181,"m":"Be sure of what you are doing, sir, in case your justice turns out to be violence, and three great people suffer for it: yourself, your queen and your son."},{"t":"sp","who":"FIRST LORD","lines":["For her, my lord,","I dare my life lay down, and will do't, sir,","Please you to accept it, that the queen is spotless","I' th' eyes of heaven and to you—I mean","In this which you accuse her."],"id":182,"m":"For her sake, my lord, I dare lay down my life, and will, sir, if you will accept it, that the queen is spotless in the eyes of heaven and towards you: I mean in what you accuse her of."},{"t":"sp","who":"ANTIGONUS","lines":["If it prove","She's otherwise, I'll keep my stables where","I lodge my wife; I'll go in couples with her;","Than when I feel and see her no further trust her.","For every inch of woman in the world,","Ay, every dram of woman's flesh, is false,","If she be."],"id":183,"m":"If she proves otherwise, I'll keep my wife locked up like a horse in the stable. I'll go about tied to her like a pair of hounds, and trust her no further than I can see and touch her. For every inch of woman in the world, yes, every ounce of woman's flesh, is false, if she is."},{"t":"sp","who":"LEONTES","lines":["Hold your peaces."],"id":184,"m":"Be quiet, both of you."},{"t":"sp","who":"FIRST LORD","lines":["Good my lord,—"],"id":185,"m":"My good lord—"},{"t":"sp","who":"ANTIGONUS","lines":["It is for you we speak, not for ourselves:","You are abus'd, and by some putter-on","That will be damn'd for't: would I knew the villain,","I would land-damn him. Be she honour-flaw'd,","I have three daughters; the eldest is eleven;","The second and the third, nine and some five;","If this prove true, they'll pay for't. By mine honour,","I'll geld 'em all; fourteen they shall not see,","To bring false generations: they are co-heirs,","And I had rather glib myself than they","Should not produce fair issue."],"id":186,"m":"We speak for your sake, not our own. You are deceived, and by some troublemaker who will be damned for it. If I knew the villain, I would damn him here on earth. If her honour is flawed: I have three daughters, the eldest eleven, the second and third nine and about five. If this proves true, they'll pay for it. On my honour, I'll sterilise them all. They shall not reach fourteen to bear false children. They are my heirs, and I would rather geld myself than have them produce children who are not legitimate."},{"t":"sp","who":"LEONTES","lines":["Cease; no more.","You smell this business with a sense as cold","As is a dead man's nose: but I do see't and feel't,","As you feel doing thus; and see withal","The instruments that feel."],"id":187,"m":"Stop; no more. You sniff at this business with a sense as cold as a dead man's nose. But I see it and feel it, as you feel me doing this (he grips him), and I see the instruments that feel."},{"t":"sp","who":"ANTIGONUS","lines":["If it be so,","We need no grave to bury honesty.","There's not a grain of it the face to sweeten","Of the whole dungy earth."],"id":188,"m":"If that is so, we need no grave to bury honesty in. There's not a grain of it left to sweeten the face of the whole dungheap of the earth."},{"t":"sp","who":"LEONTES","lines":["What! Lack I credit?"],"id":189,"m":"What! Am I not believed?"},{"t":"sp","who":"FIRST LORD","lines":["I had rather you did lack than I, my lord,","Upon this ground: and more it would content me","To have her honour true than your suspicion,","Be blam'd for't how you might."],"id":190,"m":"I would rather you were not believed than I, my lord, on this matter. And it would please me more to have her honour proved true than your suspicion, however much you blamed me for it."},{"t":"sp","who":"LEONTES","lines":["Why, what need we","Commune with you of this, but rather follow","Our forceful instigation? Our prerogative","Calls not your counsels, but our natural goodness","Imparts this; which, if you, or stupified","Or seeming so in skill, cannot or will not","Relish a truth, like us, inform yourselves","We need no more of your advice: the matter,","The loss, the gain, the ord'ring on't, is all","Properly ours."],"id":191,"m":"Why, what need is there for us to discuss this with you at all, instead of following our own strong impulse? Our royal right does not need your advice. Only our natural goodness tells you this. And if you, either stupefied or skilfully pretending to be, cannot or will not taste a truth as we do, understand that we need no more of your advice. The matter, the loss, the gain and how to handle it all belong to us."},{"t":"sp","who":"ANTIGONUS","lines":["And I wish, my liege,","You had only in your silent judgement tried it,","Without more overture."],"id":192,"m":"And I wish, my lord, you had only tested it in your own silent judgement, without making it public."},{"t":"sp","who":"LEONTES","lines":["How could that be?","Either thou art most ignorant by age,","Or thou wert born a fool. Camillo's flight,","Added to their familiarity,","(Which was as gross as ever touch'd conjecture,","That lack'd sight only, nought for approbation","But only seeing, all other circumstances","Made up to th' deed) doth push on this proceeding.","Yet, for a greater confirmation","(For in an act of this importance, 'twere","Most piteous to be wild), I have dispatch'd in post","To sacred Delphos, to Apollo's temple,","Cleomenes and Dion, whom you know","Of stuff'd sufficiency: now from the oracle","They will bring all, whose spiritual counsel had,","Shall stop or spur me. Have I done well?"],"id":193,"m":"How could that be? Either you are very ignorant from old age, or you were born a fool. Camillo's flight, added to their familiarity, which was as plain as anything ever guessed at, lacking only being seen, with nothing missing for proof but the sight, and every other circumstance pointing to the deed, drives me to act. Still, for greater confirmation (for in an act this important it would be pitiful to be rash), I have sent Cleomenes and Dion in haste to holy Delphi, to Apollo's temple. You know they are thoroughly capable. They will bring everything from the oracle, and its holy advice will stop or spur me. Have I done well?"},{"t":"sp","who":"FIRST LORD","lines":["Well done, my lord."],"id":194,"m":"Well done, my lord."},{"t":"sp","who":"LEONTES","lines":["Though I am satisfied, and need no more","Than what I know, yet shall the oracle","Give rest to the minds of others, such as he","Whose ignorant credulity will not","Come up to th' truth. So have we thought it good","From our free person she should be confin'd,","Lest that the treachery of the two fled hence","Be left her to perform. Come, follow us;","We are to speak in public; for this business","Will raise us all."],"id":195,"m":"Though I am satisfied and need nothing more than what I know, the oracle will set other minds at rest, like his (pointing to Antigonus), whose foolish trustfulness will not rise to the truth. So we have thought it good to keep her shut away from our person, in case she is left to carry out the treachery of the two who have fled. Come, follow us. We are going to speak in public, for this business will stir us all."},{"t":"sp","who":"ANTIGONUS","lines":["[Aside] To laughter, as I take it,","If the good truth were known."],"id":196,"m":"(Aside) To laughter, I think, if the real truth were known."},{"t":"sd","text":"Exeunt.","id":197},{"t":"scene","act":2,"scene":"II","loc":"The same. The outer Room of a Prison.","id":198},{"t":"sd","text":"Enter Paulina, a Gentleman and Attendants.","id":199},{"t":"sp","who":"PAULINA","lines":["The keeper of the prison, call to him;","Let him have knowledge who I am."],"id":200,"m":"Call the keeper of the prison. Let him know who I am."},{"t":"sd","text":"Exit the Gentleman.","id":201},{"t":"sp","who":"PAULINA","cont":1,"lines":["Good lady!","No court in Europe is too good for thee;","What dost thou then in prison?"],"id":202,"m":"Good lady! No court in Europe is too good for you. Then what are you doing in prison?"},{"t":"sd","text":"Enter Gentleman with the Gaoler.","id":203},{"t":"sp","who":"PAULINA","cont":1,"lines":["Now, good sir,","You know me, do you not?"],"id":204,"m":"Now, good sir, you know me, don't you?"},{"t":"sp","who":"GAOLER","lines":["For a worthy lady","And one who much I honour."],"id":205,"m":"As a worthy lady, and one I greatly respect."},{"t":"sp","who":"PAULINA","lines":["Pray you then,","Conduct me to the queen."],"id":206,"m":"Then please take me to the queen."},{"t":"sp","who":"GAOLER","lines":["I may not, madam.","To the contrary I have express commandment."],"id":207,"m":"I may not, madam. I have strict orders against it."},{"t":"sp","who":"PAULINA","lines":["Here's ado, to lock up honesty and honour from","Th' access of gentle visitors! Is't lawful, pray you,","To see her women? any of them? Emilia?"],"id":208,"m":"What a fuss, locking up honesty and honour from gentle visitors! Is it allowed, please, to see her women? Any of them? Emilia?"},{"t":"sp","who":"GAOLER","lines":["So please you, madam,","To put apart these your attendants, I","Shall bring Emilia forth."],"id":209,"m":"If you please, madam, send these attendants of yours away, and I will bring Emilia out."},{"t":"sp","who":"PAULINA","lines":["I pray now, call her.","Withdraw yourselves."],"id":210,"m":"Call her now, please. (To her attendants) Leave us."},{"t":"sd","text":"Exeunt Gentleman and Attendants.","id":211},{"t":"sp","who":"GAOLER","lines":["And, madam,","I must be present at your conference."],"id":212,"m":"And, madam, I must be present while you talk."},{"t":"sp","who":"PAULINA","lines":["Well, be't so, prithee."],"id":213,"m":"Well, so be it, please."},{"t":"sd","text":"Exit Gaoler.","id":214},{"t":"sp","who":"PAULINA","cont":1,"lines":["Here's such ado to make no stain a stain","As passes colouring."],"id":215,"m":"Such a fuss to make a stain out of no stain, it's beyond all excuse."},{"t":"sd","text":"Re-enter Gaoler with Emilia.","id":216},{"t":"sp","who":"PAULINA","cont":1,"lines":["Dear gentlewoman,","How fares our gracious lady?"],"id":217,"m":"Dear lady, how is our gracious queen?"},{"t":"sp","who":"EMILIA","lines":["As well as one so great and so forlorn","May hold together: on her frights and griefs,","(Which never tender lady hath borne greater)","She is, something before her time, deliver'd."],"id":218,"m":"As well as someone so great and so abandoned can hold together. Because of her frights and griefs, which no gentle lady ever bore greater, she has given birth, somewhat early."},{"t":"sp","who":"PAULINA","lines":["A boy?"],"id":219,"m":"A boy?"},{"t":"sp","who":"EMILIA","lines":["A daughter; and a goodly babe,","Lusty, and like to live: the queen receives","Much comfort in 't; says \"My poor prisoner,","I am as innocent as you.\""],"id":220,"m":"A daughter, and a fine baby, strong and likely to live. The queen takes great comfort in her, and says 'My poor prisoner, I am as innocent as you.'"},{"t":"sp","who":"PAULINA","lines":["I dare be sworn.","These dangerous unsafe lunes i' th' king, beshrew them!","He must be told on't, and he shall: the office","Becomes a woman best. I'll take't upon me.","If I prove honey-mouth'd, let my tongue blister,","And never to my red-look'd anger be","The trumpet any more. Pray you, Emilia,","Commend my best obedience to the queen.","If she dares trust me with her little babe,","I'll show't the king, and undertake to be","Her advocate to th' loud'st. We do not know","How he may soften at the sight o' th' child:","The silence often of pure innocence","Persuades, when speaking fails."],"id":221,"m":"I'd swear it. Curse these dangerous, mad fits of the king's! He must be told about it, and he shall. The job suits a woman best. I'll take it on. If I turn out sweet-tongued, let my tongue blister and never again trumpet my red-faced anger. Please, Emilia, give the queen my most obedient respects. If she dares trust me with her little baby, I'll show it to the king and promise to be her advocate as loudly as anyone. We don't know how he may soften at the sight of the child. The silence of pure innocence often persuades when speaking fails."},{"t":"sp","who":"EMILIA","lines":["Most worthy madam,","Your honour and your goodness is so evident,","That your free undertaking cannot miss","A thriving issue: there is no lady living","So meet for this great errand. Please your ladyship","To visit the next room, I'll presently","Acquaint the queen of your most noble offer,","Who but today hammer'd of this design,","But durst not tempt a minister of honour,","Lest she should be denied."],"id":222,"m":"Most worthy madam, your honour and goodness are so obvious that your generous venture cannot fail to succeed. There is no lady alive so fit for this great errand. If your ladyship will step into the next room, I'll tell the queen straight away of your most noble offer. Only today she was turning over this very plan, but dared not ask anyone of rank, in case she was refused."},{"t":"sp","who":"PAULINA","lines":["Tell her, Emilia,","I'll use that tongue I have: if wit flow from 't","As boldness from my bosom, let't not be doubted","I shall do good."],"id":223,"m":"Tell her, Emilia, I'll use the tongue I have. If wit flows from it as boldness does from my heart, there's no doubt I shall do some good."},{"t":"sp","who":"EMILIA","lines":["Now be you blest for it!","I'll to the queen: please you come something nearer."],"id":224,"m":"Bless you for it! I'll go to the queen. Please come a little nearer."},{"t":"sp","who":"GAOLER","lines":["Madam, if 't please the queen to send the babe,","I know not what I shall incur to pass it,","Having no warrant."],"id":225,"m":"Madam, if the queen agrees to send the baby, I don't know what punishment I'll get for letting it out, since I have no warrant."},{"t":"sp","who":"PAULINA","lines":["You need not fear it, sir:","This child was prisoner to the womb, and is,","By law and process of great nature thence","Freed and enfranchis'd: not a party to","The anger of the king, nor guilty of,","If any be, the trespass of the queen."],"id":226,"m":"You need not fear that, sir. This child was a prisoner in the womb, and by the law and process of great nature it has been freed and made a citizen. It is no party to the king's anger, nor guilty of the queen's offence, if there is one."},{"t":"sp","who":"GAOLER","lines":["I do believe it."],"id":227,"m":"I believe it."},{"t":"sp","who":"PAULINA","lines":["Do not you fear: upon mine honour, I","Will stand betwixt you and danger."],"id":228,"m":"Don't be afraid. On my honour, I will stand between you and danger."},{"t":"sd","text":"Exeunt.","id":229},{"t":"scene","act":2,"scene":"III","loc":"The same. A Room in the Palace.","id":230},{"t":"sd","text":"Enter Leontes, Antigonus, Lords and other Attendants.","id":231},{"t":"sp","who":"LEONTES","lines":["Nor night nor day no rest: it is but weakness","To bear the matter thus, mere weakness. If","The cause were not in being,—part o' th' cause,","She th' adultress; for the harlot king","Is quite beyond mine arm, out of the blank","And level of my brain, plot-proof. But she","I can hook to me. Say that she were gone,","Given to the fire, a moiety of my rest","Might come to me again. Who's there?"],"id":232,"m":"No rest night or day. It's only weakness to take the matter like this, pure weakness. If the cause were gone, or part of the cause, she, the adulteress (for the whoring king is quite beyond my reach, out of range of my mind's aim, safe from my plots)... But her I can hook. Suppose she were gone, given to the fire, then half my rest might come back to me. Who's there?"},{"t":"sp","who":"FIRST ATTENDANT","lines":["My lord."],"id":233,"m":"My lord."},{"t":"sp","who":"LEONTES","lines":["How does the boy?"],"id":234,"m":"How is the boy?"},{"t":"sp","who":"FIRST ATTENDANT","lines":["He took good rest tonight;","'Tis hop'd his sickness is discharg'd."],"id":235,"m":"He slept well last night. It's hoped his sickness is over."},{"t":"sp","who":"LEONTES","lines":["To see his nobleness,","Conceiving the dishonour of his mother.","He straight declin'd, droop'd, took it deeply,","Fasten'd and fix'd the shame on't in himself,","Threw off his spirit, his appetite, his sleep,","And downright languish'd. Leave me solely: go,","See how he fares."],"id":236,"m":"Think how noble he is! Understanding his mother's dishonour, he at once declined, drooped, took it to heart, fixed the shame of it on himself, lost his spirits, his appetite, his sleep, and simply wasted away. Leave me alone. Go and see how he is."},{"t":"sd","text":"Exit First Attendant.","id":237},{"t":"sp","who":"LEONTES","cont":1,"lines":["Fie, fie! no thought of him.","The very thought of my revenges that way","Recoil upon me: in himself too mighty,","And in his parties, his alliance. Let him be,","Until a time may serve. For present vengeance,","Take it on her. Camillo and Polixenes","Laugh at me; make their pastime at my sorrow:","They should not laugh if I could reach them, nor","Shall she, within my power."],"id":238,"m":"No, no, don't think of him. The very thought of revenge in that direction recoils on me. He is too mighty in himself, and in his friends and allies. Let him be until the time is right. For vengeance now, take it on her. Camillo and Polixenes laugh at me and make a game of my sorrow. They would not laugh if I could reach them, and nor shall she, who is in my power."},{"t":"sd","text":"Enter Paulina carrying a baby, with Antigonus, lords and servants.","id":239},{"t":"sp","who":"FIRST LORD","lines":["You must not enter."],"id":240,"m":"You must not go in."},{"t":"sp","who":"PAULINA","lines":["Nay, rather, good my lords, be second to me:","Fear you his tyrannous passion more, alas,","Than the queen's life? a gracious innocent soul,","More free than he is jealous."],"id":241,"m":"No, good my lords, support me instead. Alas, do you fear his tyrannous rage more than the queen's life? A gracious, innocent soul, more pure than he is jealous."},{"t":"sp","who":"ANTIGONUS","lines":["That's enough."],"id":242,"m":"That's enough."},{"t":"sp","who":"SERVANT","lines":["Madam, he hath not slept tonight; commanded","None should come at him."],"id":243,"m":"Madam, he has not slept tonight, and has ordered that no one should come to him."},{"t":"sp","who":"PAULINA","lines":["Not so hot, good sir;","I come to bring him sleep. 'Tis such as you,","That creep like shadows by him, and do sigh","At each his needless heavings,—such as you","Nourish the cause of his awaking. I","Do come with words as med'cinal as true,","Honest as either, to purge him of that humour","That presses him from sleep."],"id":244,"m":"Not so hasty, good sir. I come to bring him sleep. It's people like you, creeping round him like shadows and sighing at every one of his needless groans, people like you who feed the cause of his sleeplessness. I come with words as healing as they are true, and as honest as both, to purge him of the mood that keeps him from sleep."},{"t":"sp","who":"LEONTES","lines":["What noise there, ho?"],"id":245,"m":"What's that noise, there?"},{"t":"sp","who":"PAULINA","lines":["No noise, my lord; but needful conference","About some gossips for your highness."],"id":246,"m":"No noise, my lord, only some necessary talk about godparents for your Highness's child."},{"t":"sp","who":"LEONTES","lines":["How!","Away with that audacious lady! Antigonus,","I charg'd thee that she should not come about me.","I knew she would."],"id":247,"m":"What! Away with that impudent woman! Antigonus, I ordered you not to let her come near me. I knew she would."},{"t":"sp","who":"ANTIGONUS","lines":["I told her so, my lord,","On your displeasure's peril and on mine,","She should not visit you."],"id":248,"m":"I told her so, my lord, on pain of your displeasure and of mine: she was not to visit you."},{"t":"sp","who":"LEONTES","lines":["What, canst not rule her?"],"id":249,"m":"What, can't you control her?"},{"t":"sp","who":"PAULINA","lines":["From all dishonesty he can. In this,","Unless he take the course that you have done,","Commit me for committing honour—trust it,","He shall not rule me."],"id":250,"m":"He can keep me from anything dishonest. In this, unless he does what you have done and imprisons me for doing honour, believe me, he shall not control me."},{"t":"sp","who":"ANTIGONUS","lines":["La you now, you hear.","When she will take the rein I let her run;","But she'll not stumble."],"id":251,"m":"There, you hear. When she takes the reins, I let her run. But she won't stumble."},{"t":"sp","who":"PAULINA","lines":["Good my liege, I come,—","And, I beseech you hear me, who professes","Myself your loyal servant, your physician,","Your most obedient counsellor, yet that dares","Less appear so, in comforting your evils,","Than such as most seem yours—I say I come","From your good queen."],"id":252,"m":"My good lord, I come (and I beg you to hear me, who claims to be your loyal servant, your doctor, your most obedient adviser, yet who dares to seem less so by not soothing your faults than those who seem most devoted to you), I say I come from your good queen."},{"t":"sp","who":"LEONTES","lines":["Good queen!"],"id":253,"m":"Good queen!"},{"t":"sp","who":"PAULINA","lines":["Good queen, my lord, good queen: I say, good queen,","And would by combat make her good, so were I","A man, the worst about you."],"id":254,"m":"Good queen, my lord, good queen. I say good queen, and if I were a man, even the weakest one around you, I would prove her good in a duel."},{"t":"sp","who":"LEONTES","lines":["Force her hence."],"id":255,"m":"Throw her out."},{"t":"sp","who":"PAULINA","lines":["Let him that makes but trifles of his eyes","First hand me: on mine own accord I'll off;","But first I'll do my errand. The good queen,","(For she is good) hath brought you forth a daughter;","Here 'tis; commends it to your blessing."],"id":256,"m":"Let anyone who cares nothing for his eyes lay hands on me first. I'll go of my own accord, but first I'll do my errand. The good queen, for she is good, has given birth to a daughter for you. Here she is. She commends it to your blessing."},{"t":"sd","text":"Laying down the child.","id":257},{"t":"sp","who":"LEONTES","lines":["Out!","A mankind witch! Hence with her, out o' door:","A most intelligencing bawd!"],"id":258,"m":"Out! A mannish witch! Away with her, out of the door! A spying, scheming bawd!"},{"t":"sp","who":"PAULINA","lines":["Not so.","I am as ignorant in that as you","In so entitling me; and no less honest","Than you are mad; which is enough, I'll warrant,","As this world goes, to pass for honest."],"id":259,"m":"Not so. I know as little about that as you do in calling me that, and I am no less honest than you are mad, which I promise is enough, the way this world goes, to pass for honest."},{"t":"sp","who":"LEONTES","lines":["Traitors!","Will you not push her out? [To Antigonus] Give her the bastard,","Thou dotard! Thou art woman-tir'd, unroosted","By thy Dame Partlet here. Take up the bastard,","Take't up, I say; give't to thy crone."],"id":260,"m":"Traitors! Won't you push her out? (To Antigonus) Give her the bastard, you old fool! You're henpecked, knocked off your perch by your Dame Partlet here. Pick up the bastard, pick it up, I say. Give it to your old hag."},{"t":"sp","who":"PAULINA","lines":["For ever","Unvenerable be thy hands, if thou","Tak'st up the princess by that forced baseness","Which he has put upon 't!"],"id":261,"m":"May your hands be dishonoured for ever if you pick up the princess under that false, low name he has forced on her!"},{"t":"sp","who":"LEONTES","lines":["He dreads his wife."],"id":262,"m":"He's afraid of his wife."},{"t":"sp","who":"PAULINA","lines":["So I would you did; then 'twere past all doubt","You'd call your children yours."],"id":263,"m":"I wish you were afraid of yours. Then there would be no doubt you'd call your children yours."},{"t":"sp","who":"LEONTES","lines":["A nest of traitors!"],"id":264,"m":"A nest of traitors!"},{"t":"sp","who":"ANTIGONUS","lines":["I am none, by this good light."],"id":265,"m":"I'm not one, by this good daylight."},{"t":"sp","who":"PAULINA","lines":["Nor I; nor any","But one that's here, and that's himself. For he","The sacred honour of himself, his queen's,","His hopeful son's, his babe's, betrays to slander,","Whose sting is sharper than the sword's; and will not,","(For, as the case now stands, it is a curse","He cannot be compell'd to't) once remove","The root of his opinion, which is rotten","As ever oak or stone was sound."],"id":266,"m":"Nor am I, nor is anyone here but one, and that's himself. For he betrays to slander, whose sting is sharper than a sword's, his own sacred honour, his queen's, his promising son's and his baby's, and will not (and as things stand it's a curse that he can't be forced to) once pull up the root of his belief, which is as rotten as any oak or stone was ever sound."},{"t":"sp","who":"LEONTES","lines":["A callat","Of boundless tongue, who late hath beat her husband,","And now baits me! This brat is none of mine;","It is the issue of Polixenes.","Hence with it, and together with the dam","Commit them to the fire."],"id":267,"m":"A scold with an endless tongue, who has just beaten her husband and now baits me! This brat is not mine. It is the child of Polixenes. Away with it, and throw it and its mother into the fire together."},{"t":"sp","who":"PAULINA","lines":["It is yours;","And, might we lay th' old proverb to your charge,","So like you 'tis the worse. Behold, my lords,","Although the print be little, the whole matter","And copy of the father: eye, nose, lip,","The trick of 's frown, his forehead; nay, the valley,","The pretty dimples of his chin and cheek; his smiles;","The very mould and frame of hand, nail, finger:","And thou, good goddess Nature, which hast made it","So like to him that got it, if thou hast","The ordering of the mind too, 'mongst all colours","No yellow in 't, lest she suspect, as he does,","Her children not her husband's!"],"id":268,"m":"It is yours, and, as the old proverb says, it's so like you it's the worse for it. Look, my lords: although the print is small, it is a complete copy of the father. Eye, nose, lip, the way he frowns, his forehead; even the hollow, the pretty dimples of his chin and cheek; his smiles; the very shape of hand, nail and finger. And you, good goddess Nature, who have made her so like the man who fathered her, if you have charge of the mind too, put no yellow of jealousy in it among all its colours, in case she suspects, as he does, that her children are not her husband's!"},{"t":"sp","who":"LEONTES","lines":["A gross hag!","And, losel, thou art worthy to be hang'd","That wilt not stay her tongue."],"id":269,"m":"A foul hag! (To Antigonus) And, you good-for-nothing, you deserve to be hanged for not stopping her tongue."},{"t":"sp","who":"ANTIGONUS","lines":["Hang all the husbands","That cannot do that feat, you'll leave yourself","Hardly one subject."],"id":270,"m":"If you hang all the husbands who can't do that, you'll hardly have one subject left."},{"t":"sp","who":"LEONTES","lines":["Once more, take her hence."],"id":271,"m":"Once more, take her away."},{"t":"sp","who":"PAULINA","lines":["A most unworthy and unnatural lord","Can do no more."],"id":272,"m":"A most unworthy and unnatural lord could do no worse."},{"t":"sp","who":"LEONTES","lines":["I'll have thee burnt."],"id":273,"m":"I'll have you burnt."},{"t":"sp","who":"PAULINA","lines":["I care not.","It is an heretic that makes the fire,","Not she which burns in 't. I'll not call you tyrant;","But this most cruel usage of your queen,","Not able to produce more accusation","Than your own weak-hing'd fancy, something savours","Of tyranny, and will ignoble make you,","Yea, scandalous to the world."],"id":274,"m":"I don't care. It is the one who lights the fire who is the heretic, not the woman who burns in it. I won't call you a tyrant. But this cruel treatment of your queen, without being able to produce any accusation but your own unhinged imagination, smacks somewhat of tyranny, and will disgrace you, yes, make you a scandal to the world."},{"t":"sp","who":"LEONTES","lines":["On your allegiance,","Out of the chamber with her! Were I a tyrant,","Where were her life? She durst not call me so,","If she did know me one. Away with her!"],"id":275,"m":"On your allegiance, get her out of the room! If I were a tyrant, where would her life be? She wouldn't dare call me one if she really thought I was. Away with her!"},{"t":"sp","who":"PAULINA","lines":["I pray you, do not push me; I'll be gone.","Look to your babe, my lord; 'tis yours: Jove send her","A better guiding spirit! What needs these hands?","You that are thus so tender o'er his follies,","Will never do him good, not one of you.","So, so. Farewell; we are gone."],"id":276,"m":"Please don't push me. I'll go. Look after your baby, my lord. It's yours. May Jove give her a better guiding spirit! What need for these hands? You who are so tender towards his follies will never do him any good, not one of you. There, there. Farewell; I'm going."},{"t":"sd","text":"Exit.","id":277},{"t":"sp","who":"LEONTES","lines":["Thou, traitor, hast set on thy wife to this.","My child? Away with't. Even thou, that hast","A heart so tender o'er it, take it hence,","And see it instantly consum'd with fire;","Even thou, and none but thou. Take it up straight:","Within this hour bring me word 'tis done,","And by good testimony, or I'll seize thy life,","With that thou else call'st thine. If thou refuse","And wilt encounter with my wrath, say so;","The bastard brains with these my proper hands","Shall I dash out. Go, take it to the fire;","For thou set'st on thy wife."],"id":278,"m":"You traitor, you put your wife up to this. My child? Away with it! You, who have such a tender heart for it, take it away and see it burnt at once. You and nobody else. Pick it up now. Within the hour bring me word it's done, with good witnesses, or I'll take your life and everything else you call yours. If you refuse and want to face my anger, say so, and I'll dash out the bastard's brains with my own hands. Go, take it to the fire, for you set your wife on."},{"t":"sp","who":"ANTIGONUS","lines":["I did not, sir:","These lords, my noble fellows, if they please,","Can clear me in 't."],"id":279,"m":"I did not, sir. These lords, my noble colleagues, can clear me of it, if they will."},{"t":"sp","who":"LORDS","lines":["We can: my royal liege,","He is not guilty of her coming hither."],"id":280,"m":"We can. My royal lord, he is not to blame for her coming here."},{"t":"sp","who":"LEONTES","lines":["You're liars all."],"id":281,"m":"You're all liars."},{"t":"sp","who":"FIRST LORD","lines":["Beseech your highness, give us better credit:","We have always truly serv'd you; and beseech","So to esteem of us. And on our knees we beg,","As recompense of our dear services","Past and to come, that you do change this purpose,","Which being so horrible, so bloody, must","Lead on to some foul issue. We all kneel."],"id":282,"m":"I beg your Highness, trust us more. We have always served you truly, and we beg you to think so of us. On our knees we beg you, as a reward for our loyal service past and to come, to change this plan, which is so horrible and bloody that it must lead to something foul. We all kneel."},{"t":"sp","who":"LEONTES","lines":["I am a feather for each wind that blows.","Shall I live on to see this bastard kneel","And call me father? better burn it now","Than curse it then. But be it; let it live.","It shall not neither. [To Antigonus] You, sir, come you hither,","You that have been so tenderly officious","With Lady Margery, your midwife, there,","To save this bastard's life—for 'tis a bastard,","So sure as this beard's grey. What will you adventure","To save this brat's life?"],"id":283,"m":"I am a feather blown by every wind. Shall I live to see this bastard kneel and call me father? Better burn it now than curse it then. But so be it: let it live. No, it shall not either. (To Antigonus) You, sir, come here, you who have been so tenderly busy with Lady Margery, your midwife there, to save this bastard's life (for it is a bastard, as sure as this beard is grey). What will you risk to save this brat's life?"},{"t":"sp","who":"ANTIGONUS","lines":["Anything, my lord,","That my ability may undergo,","And nobleness impose: at least thus much:","I'll pawn the little blood which I have left","To save the innocent. Anything possible."],"id":284,"m":"Anything, my lord, that my strength can bear and honour requires. At least this much: I'll pledge what little blood I have left to save the innocent. Anything possible."},{"t":"sp","who":"LEONTES","lines":["It shall be possible. Swear by this sword","Thou wilt perform my bidding."],"id":285,"m":"It shall be possible. Swear on this sword that you will do what I command."},{"t":"sp","who":"ANTIGONUS","lines":["I will, my lord."],"id":286,"m":"I will, my lord."},{"t":"sp","who":"LEONTES","lines":["Mark, and perform it, seest thou? for the fail","Of any point in't shall not only be","Death to thyself, but to thy lewd-tongu'd wife,","Whom for this time we pardon. We enjoin thee,","As thou art liegeman to us, that thou carry","This female bastard hence, and that thou bear it","To some remote and desert place, quite out","Of our dominions; and that there thou leave it,","Without more mercy, to it own protection","And favour of the climate. As by strange fortune","It came to us, I do in justice charge thee,","On thy soul's peril and thy body's torture,","That thou commend it strangely to some place","Where chance may nurse or end it. Take it up."],"id":287,"m":"Listen, and do it, do you see? If you fail in any detail, it will mean death not only for you but for your foul-tongued wife, whom we pardon for now. We command you, as our sworn subject, to carry this female bastard away from here, take it to some remote and deserted place quite outside our kingdom, and leave it there, without any more mercy, to its own protection and the kindness of the weather. As it came to us by strange fortune, I charge you in justice, at the risk of your soul and body's torture, to leave it as a stranger in some place where chance may nurse it or end it. Pick it up."},{"t":"sp","who":"ANTIGONUS","lines":["I swear to do this, though a present death","Had been more merciful. Come on, poor babe:","Some powerful spirit instruct the kites and ravens","To be thy nurses! Wolves and bears, they say,","Casting their savageness aside, have done","Like offices of pity. Sir, be prosperous","In more than this deed does require! And blessing","Against this cruelty, fight on thy side,","Poor thing, condemn'd to loss!"],"id":288,"m":"I swear to do it, though instant death would have been more merciful. Come on, poor baby. May some powerful spirit teach the kites and ravens to be your nurses! Wolves and bears, they say, have put their savageness aside and done such kindly duties. (To Leontes) Sir, may you prosper more than this deed deserves! (To the baby) And may blessing fight on your side against this cruelty, poor thing, condemned to be lost!"},{"t":"sd","text":"Exit with the child.","id":289},{"t":"sp","who":"LEONTES","lines":["No, I'll not rear","Another's issue."],"id":290,"m":"No, I won't bring up another man's child."},{"t":"sd","text":"Enter a Servant.","id":291},{"t":"sp","who":"SERVANT","lines":["Please your highness, posts","From those you sent to th' oracle are come","An hour since: Cleomenes and Dion,","Being well arriv'd from Delphos, are both landed,","Hasting to th' court."],"id":292,"m":"If it please your Highness, messengers came an hour ago from the men you sent to the oracle. Cleomenes and Dion have arrived safely from Delphi, landed, and are hurrying to court."},{"t":"sp","who":"FIRST LORD","lines":["So please you, sir, their speed","Hath been beyond account."],"id":293,"m":"If you please, sir, their speed has been beyond belief."},{"t":"sp","who":"LEONTES","lines":["Twenty-three days","They have been absent: 'tis good speed; foretells","The great Apollo suddenly will have","The truth of this appear. Prepare you, lords;","Summon a session, that we may arraign","Our most disloyal lady; for, as she hath","Been publicly accus'd, so shall she have","A just and open trial. While she lives,","My heart will be a burden to me. Leave me,","And think upon my bidding."],"id":294,"m":"They have been away twenty-three days. That's good speed. It means great Apollo wants the truth of this to be known quickly. Get ready, lords. Call a court session so that we can put our most disloyal lady on trial. For as she has been publicly accused, so she shall have a just and open trial. While she lives, my heart will be a burden to me. Leave me, and think about what I have ordered."},{"t":"sd","text":"Exeunt.","id":295},{"t":"scene","act":3,"scene":"I","loc":"Sicilia. A Street in some Town.","id":296},{"t":"sd","text":"Enter Cleomenes and Dion.","id":297},{"t":"sp","who":"CLEOMENES","lines":["The climate's delicate; the air most sweet,","Fertile the isle, the temple much surpassing","The common praise it bears."],"id":298,"m":"The climate is mild, the air very sweet, the island fertile, and the temple far beyond the praise it usually gets."},{"t":"sp","who":"DION","lines":["I shall report,","For most it caught me, the celestial habits","(Methinks I so should term them) and the reverence","Of the grave wearers. O, the sacrifice!","How ceremonious, solemn, and unearthly,","It was i' th' offering!"],"id":299,"m":"I shall report, for it struck me most, the heavenly robes (I think that's the right word for them) and the reverence of the solemn priests who wore them. O, the sacrifice! How ceremonious, solemn and unearthly the offering was!"},{"t":"sp","who":"CLEOMENES","lines":["But of all, the burst","And the ear-deaf'ning voice o' th' oracle,","Kin to Jove's thunder, so surprised my sense","That I was nothing."],"id":300,"m":"But above everything, the blast and ear-deafening voice of the oracle, like Jove's thunder, so overwhelmed my senses that I was nothing."},{"t":"sp","who":"DION","lines":["If the event o' th' journey","Prove as successful to the queen,—O, be't so!—","As it hath been to us rare, pleasant, speedy,","The time is worth the use on't."],"id":301,"m":"If the outcome of the journey proves as good for the queen (O, let it!) as it has been rare, pleasant and quick for us, the time was well spent."},{"t":"sp","who":"CLEOMENES","lines":["Great Apollo","Turn all to th' best! These proclamations,","So forcing faults upon Hermione,","I little like."],"id":302,"m":"Great Apollo turn everything to the best! I don't like these proclamations that force faults on Hermione."},{"t":"sp","who":"DION","lines":["The violent carriage of it","Will clear or end the business: when the oracle,","(Thus by Apollo's great divine seal'd up)","Shall the contents discover, something rare","Even then will rush to knowledge. Go. Fresh horses!","And gracious be the issue!"],"id":303,"m":"The violent haste of it will clear up the business or end it. When the oracle, sealed up like this by Apollo's great priest, reveals its contents, something extraordinary will come to light. Go. Fresh horses! And may it end well!"},{"t":"sd","text":"Exeunt.","id":304},{"t":"scene","act":3,"scene":"II","loc":"The same. A Court of Justice.","id":305},{"t":"sd","text":"Enter Leontes, Lords and Officers appear, properly seated.","id":306},{"t":"sp","who":"LEONTES","lines":["This sessions (to our great grief we pronounce)","Even pushes 'gainst our heart: the party tried","The daughter of a king, our wife, and one","Of us too much belov'd. Let us be clear'd","Of being tyrannous, since we so openly","Proceed in justice, which shall have due course,","Even to the guilt or the purgation.","Produce the prisoner."],"id":307,"m":"This trial, we say with great grief, pushes against our very heart. The accused is the daughter of a king, our wife, and someone we loved too much. Let us be cleared of tyranny, since we proceed so openly in justice, which shall take its proper course to a verdict of guilty or a clearing of her name. Bring in the prisoner."},{"t":"sp","who":"OFFICER","lines":["It is his highness' pleasure that the queen","Appear in person here in court. Silence!"],"id":308,"m":"It is his Highness's wish that the queen appear in person here in court. Silence!"},{"t":"sd","text":"Hermione is brought in guarded; Paulina and Ladies attending.","id":309},{"t":"sp","who":"LEONTES","lines":["Read the indictment."],"id":310,"m":"Read the charge."},{"t":"sp","who":"OFFICER","lines":["[Reads] \"Hermione, queen to the worthy Leontes, king of Sicilia,","thou art here accused and arraigned of high treason, in committing","adultery with Polixenes, king of Bohemia; and conspiring with Camillo","to take away the life of our sovereign lord the king, thy royal","husband: the pretence whereof being by circumstances partly laid open,","thou, Hermione, contrary to the faith and allegiance of a true subject,","didst counsel and aid them, for their better safety, to fly away by","night.\""],"id":311,"m":"(Reads) 'Hermione, queen to the worthy Leontes, King of Sicilia, you are accused and put on trial here for high treason, for committing adultery with Polixenes, King of Bohemia, and conspiring with Camillo to take the life of our sovereign lord the king, your royal husband. When this plan was partly uncovered by circumstances, you, Hermione, against the faith and loyalty of a true subject, advised and helped them to escape by night for their greater safety.'"},{"t":"sp","who":"HERMIONE","lines":["Since what I am to say must be but that","Which contradicts my accusation, and","The testimony on my part no other","But what comes from myself, it shall scarce boot me","To say \"Not guilty\". Mine integrity,","Being counted falsehood, shall, as I express it,","Be so receiv'd. But thus, if powers divine","Behold our human actions, as they do,","I doubt not, then, but innocence shall make","False accusation blush, and tyranny","Tremble at patience. You, my lord, best know,","Who least will seem to do so, my past life","Hath been as continent, as chaste, as true,","As I am now unhappy; which is more","Than history can pattern, though devis'd","And play'd to take spectators. For behold me,","A fellow of the royal bed, which owe","A moiety of the throne, a great king's daughter,","The mother to a hopeful prince, here standing","To prate and talk for life and honour 'fore","Who please to come and hear. For life, I prize it","As I weigh grief, which I would spare. For honour,","'Tis a derivative from me to mine,","And only that I stand for. I appeal","To your own conscience, sir, before Polixenes","Came to your court, how I was in your grace,","How merited to be so; since he came,","With what encounter so uncurrent I","Have strain'd t' appear thus: if one jot beyond","The bound of honour, or in act or will","That way inclining, harden'd be the hearts","Of all that hear me, and my near'st of kin","Cry fie upon my grave!"],"id":312,"m":"Since all I can say must simply contradict the charge, and the only evidence on my side comes from me, it will hardly help me to say 'Not guilty'. My honesty, being counted as falsehood, will be taken as falsehood when I speak it. But if the gods watch our human actions, as they do, I have no doubt that innocence will make false accusation blush and tyranny tremble at patience. You, my lord, know best, though you will least seem to, that my past life has been as self-controlled, as chaste and as true as I am now unhappy, which is more than any story can match, even one invented and acted to move an audience. For look at me: partner in the royal bed, owner of half the throne, a great king's daughter, mother of a promising prince, standing here to chatter and plead for my life and honour in front of anyone who cares to come and listen. As for life, I value it as I value grief, which I would gladly be spared. As for honour, it passes from me to my children, and that is all I stand up for. I appeal to your own conscience, sir: before Polixenes came to your court, how I stood in your favour and how I deserved to; and since he came, what improper behaviour I have stooped to that I should appear like this. If I went one jot beyond the bounds of honour, in act or in will, let the hearts of everyone who hears me be hardened, and my nearest relatives cry 'Shame!' on my grave."},{"t":"sp","who":"LEONTES","lines":["I ne'er heard yet","That any of these bolder vices wanted","Less impudence to gainsay what they did","Than to perform it first."],"id":313,"m":"I never yet heard that any of these bold vices lacked the nerve to deny what they did any less than they had the nerve to do it."},{"t":"sp","who":"HERMIONE","lines":["That's true enough;","Though 'tis a saying, sir, not due to me."],"id":314,"m":"That's true enough, though it's a saying, sir, that does not apply to me."},{"t":"sp","who":"LEONTES","lines":["You will not own it."],"id":315,"m":"You won't admit it."},{"t":"sp","who":"HERMIONE","lines":["More than mistress of","Which comes to me in name of fault, I must not","At all acknowledge. For Polixenes,","With whom I am accus'd, I do confess","I lov'd him as in honour he requir'd,","With such a kind of love as might become","A lady like me; with a love even such,","So and no other, as yourself commanded:","Which not to have done, I think had been in me","Both disobedience and ingratitude","To you and toward your friend, whose love had spoke,","Ever since it could speak, from an infant, freely,","That it was yours. Now, for conspiracy,","I know not how it tastes, though it be dish'd","For me to try how: all I know of it","Is that Camillo was an honest man;","And why he left your court, the gods themselves,","Wotting no more than I, are ignorant."],"id":316,"m":"I must not admit to any more faults than are truly mine. As for Polixenes, whom I am accused with, I confess I loved him as his honour required, with the kind of love that suits a lady like me, exactly the love, no more and no other, that you yourself commanded. Not to have given it would, I think, have been disobedience and ingratitude to you and to your friend, whose love has openly declared itself yours ever since he could speak, from a baby. Now, as for conspiracy, I don't know what it tastes like, though it is served up for me to try. All I know of it is that Camillo was an honest man. And why he left your court, the gods themselves don't know, if they know no more than I do."},{"t":"sp","who":"LEONTES","lines":["You knew of his departure, as you know","What you have underta'en to do in 's absence."],"id":317,"m":"You knew he was leaving, just as you know what you have agreed to do while he's away."},{"t":"sp","who":"HERMIONE","lines":["Sir,","You speak a language that I understand not:","My life stands in the level of your dreams,","Which I'll lay down."],"id":318,"m":"Sir, you speak a language I don't understand. My life is a target for your dreams, and I'll give it up to them."},{"t":"sp","who":"LEONTES","lines":["Your actions are my dreams.","You had a bastard by Polixenes,","And I but dream'd it. As you were past all shame","(Those of your fact are so) so past all truth,","Which to deny concerns more than avails; for as","Thy brat hath been cast out, like to itself,","No father owning it (which is, indeed,","More criminal in thee than it), so thou","Shalt feel our justice; in whose easiest passage","Look for no less than death."],"id":319,"m":"Your actions are my dreams. You had a bastard by Polixenes, and I only dreamt it. As you were past all shame (those who do what you did always are), so you are past all truth. Denying it costs you more than it gains. For just as your brat has been thrown out, as it deserves, with no father to own it (which is more your crime than its), so you shall feel our justice, and in its mildest form, expect nothing less than death."},{"t":"sp","who":"HERMIONE","lines":["Sir, spare your threats:","The bug which you would fright me with, I seek.","To me can life be no commodity.","The crown and comfort of my life, your favour,","I do give lost, for I do feel it gone,","But know not how it went. My second joy,","And first-fruits of my body, from his presence","I am barr'd, like one infectious. My third comfort,","Starr'd most unluckily, is from my breast,","(The innocent milk in its most innocent mouth)","Hal'd out to murder; myself on every post","Proclaim'd a strumpet; with immodest hatred","The child-bed privilege denied, which 'longs","To women of all fashion; lastly, hurried","Here to this place, i' th' open air, before","I have got strength of limit. Now, my liege,","Tell me what blessings I have here alive,","That I should fear to die. Therefore proceed.","But yet hear this: mistake me not: no life,","I prize it not a straw, but for mine honour,","Which I would free, if I shall be condemn'd","Upon surmises, all proofs sleeping else","But what your jealousies awake, I tell you","'Tis rigour, and not law. Your honours all,","I do refer me to the oracle:","Apollo be my judge!"],"id":320,"m":"Sir, spare your threats. The bogeyman you would frighten me with is what I seek. Life can be no benefit to me. The crown and comfort of my life, your love, I count as lost, for I feel it gone, but don't know how it went. My second joy, the first child of my body, I am barred from, as if I were infectious. My third comfort, born under the unluckiest star, is dragged from my breast, with the innocent milk in its innocent mouth, to be murdered. I myself am proclaimed a whore on every post. With shameless hatred I am denied the rest after childbirth that belongs to women of every rank. And last, I am rushed here to this place, into the open air, before I have got my strength back. Now, my lord, tell me what blessings I have left alive that I should fear to die. So go on. But hear this, don't misunderstand me. It is not my life I care about (I don't value it a straw) but my honour, which I want to clear. If I am condemned on suspicion, with every other proof asleep except what your jealousy wakes up, I tell you it is cruelty, not law. Your honours all, I appeal to the oracle. Let Apollo be my judge!"},{"t":"sp","who":"FIRST LORD","lines":["This your request","Is altogether just: therefore bring forth,","And in Apollo's name, his oracle:"],"id":321,"m":"This request of yours is entirely just. So, in Apollo's name, bring out his oracle."},{"t":"sd","text":"Exeunt certain Officers.","id":322},{"t":"sp","who":"HERMIONE","lines":["The Emperor of Russia was my father.","O that he were alive, and here beholding","His daughter's trial! that he did but see","The flatness of my misery; yet with eyes","Of pity, not revenge!"],"id":323,"m":"The Emperor of Russia was my father. O, if only he were alive and here, watching his daughter's trial! If only he could see the depth of my misery, but with eyes of pity, not revenge!"},{"t":"sd","text":"Enter Officers with Cleomenes and Dion.","id":324},{"t":"sp","who":"OFFICER","lines":["You here shall swear upon this sword of justice,","That you, Cleomenes and Dion, have","Been both at Delphos, and from thence have brought","This seal'd-up oracle, by the hand deliver'd","Of great Apollo's priest; and that since then","You have not dared to break the holy seal,","Nor read the secrets in't."],"id":325,"m":"You shall swear here on this sword of justice that you, Cleomenes and Dion, have both been to Delphi and brought back this sealed oracle, handed to you by great Apollo's priest, and that since then you have not dared to break the holy seal or read the secrets inside."},{"t":"sp","who":"CLEOMENES AND DION","lines":["All this we swear."],"id":326,"m":"We swear all this."},{"t":"sp","who":"LEONTES","lines":["Break up the seals and read."],"id":327,"m":"Break the seals and read it."},{"t":"sp","who":"OFFICER","lines":["[Reads] \"Hermione is chaste; Polixenes blameless; Camillo a true","subject; Leontes a jealous tyrant; his innocent babe truly begotten;","and the king shall live without an heir, if that which is lost be not","found.\""],"id":328,"m":"(Reads) 'Hermione is chaste; Polixenes blameless; Camillo a loyal subject; Leontes a jealous tyrant; his innocent baby truly his own; and the king shall live without an heir, if that which is lost is not found.'"},{"t":"sp","who":"LORDS","lines":["Now blessed be the great Apollo!"],"id":329,"m":"Now blessed be the great Apollo!"},{"t":"sp","who":"HERMIONE","lines":["Praised!"],"id":330,"m":"Praise him!"},{"t":"sp","who":"LEONTES","lines":["Hast thou read truth?"],"id":331,"m":"Have you read the truth?"},{"t":"sp","who":"OFFICER","lines":["Ay, my lord, even so","As it is here set down."],"id":332,"m":"Yes, my lord, exactly as it is written here."},{"t":"sp","who":"LEONTES","lines":["There is no truth at all i' th' oracle:","The sessions shall proceed: this is mere falsehood."],"id":333,"m":"There is no truth at all in the oracle. The trial shall go on. This is pure falsehood."},{"t":"sd","text":"Enter a Servant hastily.","id":334},{"t":"sp","who":"SERVANT","lines":["My lord the king, the king!"],"id":335,"m":"My lord the king, the king!"},{"t":"sp","who":"LEONTES","lines":["What is the business?"],"id":336,"m":"What is it?"},{"t":"sp","who":"SERVANT","lines":["O sir, I shall be hated to report it.","The prince your son, with mere conceit and fear","Of the queen's speed, is gone."],"id":337,"m":"O sir, I shall be hated for reporting it. The prince your son, from sheer imagination and fear about what would happen to the queen, is gone."},{"t":"sp","who":"LEONTES","lines":["How! gone?"],"id":338,"m":"What! Gone?"},{"t":"sp","who":"SERVANT","lines":["Is dead."],"id":339,"m":"Is dead."},{"t":"sp","who":"LEONTES","lines":["Apollo's angry, and the heavens themselves","Do strike at my injustice."],"id":340,"m":"Apollo is angry, and the heavens themselves are striking at my injustice."},{"t":"sd","text":"Hermione faints.","id":341},{"t":"sp","who":"LEONTES","cont":1,"lines":["How now there?"],"id":342,"m":"What's this?"},{"t":"sp","who":"PAULINA","lines":["This news is mortal to the queen. Look down","And see what death is doing."],"id":343,"m":"This news is deadly to the queen. Look down and see what death is doing."},{"t":"sp","who":"LEONTES","lines":["Take her hence:","Her heart is but o'ercharg'd; she will recover.","I have too much believ'd mine own suspicion.","Beseech you tenderly apply to her","Some remedies for life."],"id":344,"m":"Take her away. Her heart is only overburdened. She will recover. I have believed my own suspicion too much. I beg you, gently give her something to revive her."},{"t":"sd","text":"Exeunt Paulina and Ladies with Hermione.","id":345},{"t":"sp","who":"LEONTES","cont":1,"lines":["Apollo, pardon","My great profaneness 'gainst thine oracle!","I'll reconcile me to Polixenes,","New woo my queen, recall the good Camillo,","Whom I proclaim a man of truth, of mercy;","For, being transported by my jealousies","To bloody thoughts and to revenge, I chose","Camillo for the minister to poison","My friend Polixenes: which had been done,","But that the good mind of Camillo tardied","My swift command, though I with death and with","Reward did threaten and encourage him,","Not doing it and being done. He, most humane","And fill'd with honour, to my kingly guest","Unclasp'd my practice, quit his fortunes here,","Which you knew great, and to the certain hazard","Of all incertainties himself commended,","No richer than his honour. How he glisters","Thorough my rust! And how his piety","Does my deeds make the blacker!"],"id":346,"m":"Apollo, pardon my great sacrilege against your oracle! I'll make my peace with Polixenes, woo my queen again, and call back good Camillo, whom I declare a man of truth and mercy. For, carried away by my jealousy to bloody thoughts and revenge, I chose Camillo to poison my friend Polixenes. And it would have been done, if Camillo's good mind had not delayed my hasty order, though I threatened him with death if he did not do it and promised him reward if he did. He, most humane and full of honour, revealed my plot to my royal guest, left his fortunes here, which you knew were great, and gave himself up to the certain risks of every uncertainty, with nothing but his honour to his name. How he shines through my rust! And how his goodness makes my deeds look blacker!"},{"t":"sd","text":"Enter Paulina.","id":347},{"t":"sp","who":"PAULINA","lines":["Woe the while!","O, cut my lace, lest my heart, cracking it,","Break too!"],"id":348,"m":"Alas the day! O, cut my laces, or my heart, cracking them, will break too!"},{"t":"sp","who":"FIRST LORD","lines":["What fit is this, good lady?"],"id":349,"m":"What fit is this, good lady?"},{"t":"sp","who":"PAULINA","lines":["What studied torments, tyrant, hast for me?","What wheels? racks? fires? what flaying? boiling","In leads or oils? What old or newer torture","Must I receive, whose every word deserves","To taste of thy most worst? Thy tyranny,","Together working with thy jealousies,","Fancies too weak for boys, too green and idle","For girls of nine. O, think what they have done,","And then run mad indeed, stark mad! for all","Thy by-gone fooleries were but spices of it.","That thou betray'dst Polixenes, 'twas nothing;","That did but show thee, of a fool, inconstant","And damnable ingrateful; nor was't much","Thou wouldst have poison'd good Camillo's honour,","To have him kill a king; poor trespasses,","More monstrous standing by: whereof I reckon","The casting forth to crows thy baby daughter,","To be or none or little, though a devil","Would have shed water out of fire ere done't,","Nor is't directly laid to thee the death","Of the young prince, whose honourable thoughts,","Thoughts high for one so tender, cleft the heart","That could conceive a gross and foolish sire","Blemish'd his gracious dam: this is not, no,","Laid to thy answer: but the last—O lords,","When I have said, cry Woe!—the queen, the queen,","The sweet'st, dear'st creature's dead, and vengeance for't","Not dropp'd down yet."],"id":350,"m":"What carefully devised tortures have you for me, tyrant? What wheels? Racks? Fires? What flaying, boiling in lead or oil? What old or new torture must I suffer, when every word I say deserves the very worst you can do? Your tyranny, working together with your jealousy, fancies too weak for boys, too green and silly for girls of nine, O, think what they have done, and then go mad indeed, stark mad! For all your past follies were only a taste of it. That you betrayed Polixenes was nothing: that only showed you up as a fickle and damnably ungrateful fool. Nor was it much that you would have poisoned good Camillo's honour by making him kill a king: small offences, next to something more monstrous. And among those I count throwing out your baby daughter to the crows as nothing or little, though a devil would have wept tears out of fire before doing it. Nor is the death of the young prince directly blamed on you, whose honourable thoughts, high thoughts for one so young, broke his heart when he understood that a coarse and foolish father had stained his gracious mother. No, that is not charged to you. But the last thing (O lords, when I have said it, cry 'Woe!'): the queen, the queen, the sweetest, dearest creature, is dead, and vengeance for it has not yet fallen."},{"t":"sp","who":"FIRST LORD","lines":["The higher powers forbid!"],"id":351,"m":"The gods forbid!"},{"t":"sp","who":"PAULINA","lines":["I say she's dead: I'll swear't. If word nor oath","Prevail not, go and see: if you can bring","Tincture, or lustre, in her lip, her eye,","Heat outwardly or breath within, I'll serve you","As I would do the gods. But, O thou tyrant!","Do not repent these things, for they are heavier","Than all thy woes can stir. Therefore betake thee","To nothing but despair. A thousand knees","Ten thousand years together, naked, fasting,","Upon a barren mountain, and still winter","In storm perpetual, could not move the gods","To look that way thou wert."],"id":352,"m":"I say she's dead. I'll swear to it. If neither word nor oath convinces you, go and see. If you can bring colour or shine to her lip or her eye, warmth to her body or breath inside it, I'll serve you as I would the gods. But, O you tyrant, don't repent these things, for they are too heavy for all your sorrows to shift. So give yourself up to nothing but despair. A thousand people kneeling for ten thousand years together, naked and fasting, on a barren mountain in constant winter and endless storm, could not move the gods to look in your direction."},{"t":"sp","who":"LEONTES","lines":["Go on, go on:","Thou canst not speak too much; I have deserv'd","All tongues to talk their bitterest."],"id":353,"m":"Go on, go on. You can't say too much. I have deserved every tongue to speak its bitterest."},{"t":"sp","who":"FIRST LORD","lines":["Say no more:","Howe'er the business goes, you have made fault","I' th' boldness of your speech."],"id":354,"m":"Say no more. However the matter stands, you were wrong to speak so boldly."},{"t":"sp","who":"PAULINA","lines":["I am sorry for 't:","All faults I make, when I shall come to know them,","I do repent. Alas, I have show'd too much","The rashness of a woman: he is touch'd","To th' noble heart. What's gone and what's past help,","Should be past grief. Do not receive affliction","At my petition; I beseech you, rather","Let me be punish'd, that have minded you","Of what you should forget. Now, good my liege,","Sir, royal sir, forgive a foolish woman:","The love I bore your queen—lo, fool again!","I'll speak of her no more, nor of your children.","I'll not remember you of my own lord,","Who is lost too. Take your patience to you,","And I'll say nothing."],"id":355,"m":"I am sorry for it. Whenever I find out my faults, I repent of them. Alas, I have shown too much of a woman's rashness. He is hurt to his noble heart. What's gone and can't be helped should be past grieving. Don't take my words as a torment. I beg you, rather let me be punished, for reminding you of what you should forget. Now, my good lord, sir, royal sir, forgive a foolish woman. The love I bore your queen... There, a fool again! I'll speak no more of her, nor of your children. I won't remind you of my own husband, who is lost too. Arm yourself with patience, and I'll say nothing."},{"t":"sp","who":"LEONTES","lines":["Thou didst speak but well","When most the truth, which I receive much better","Than to be pitied of thee. Prithee, bring me","To the dead bodies of my queen and son:","One grave shall be for both. Upon them shall","The causes of their death appear, unto","Our shame perpetual. Once a day I'll visit","The chapel where they lie, and tears shed there","Shall be my recreation. So long as nature","Will bear up with this exercise, so long","I daily vow to use it. Come, and lead me","To these sorrows."],"id":356,"m":"You only spoke well when you spoke the truth, and I take that much better than your pity. Please take me to the bodies of my queen and son. One grave shall hold them both. On it shall be written the causes of their death, to our everlasting shame. Once a day I'll visit the chapel where they lie, and weeping there shall be my only pastime. As long as nature can bear this exercise, I vow to do it every day. Come, and lead me to these sorrows."},{"t":"sd","text":"Exeunt.","id":357},{"t":"scene","act":3,"scene":"III","loc":"Bohemia. A desert Country near the Sea.","id":358},{"t":"sd","text":"Enter Antigonus with the Child and a Mariner.","id":359},{"t":"sp","who":"ANTIGONUS","lines":["Thou art perfect, then, our ship hath touch'd upon","The deserts of Bohemia?"],"id":360,"m":"You're sure, then, that our ship has landed on the deserted coast of Bohemia?"},{"t":"sp","who":"MARINER","lines":["Ay, my lord, and fear","We have landed in ill time: the skies look grimly,","And threaten present blusters. In my conscience,","The heavens with that we have in hand are angry,","And frown upon 's."],"id":361,"m":"Yes, my lord, and I fear we've landed at a bad time. The skies look grim and threaten storms any moment. In my opinion, heaven is angry with what we are doing and frowns on us."},{"t":"sp","who":"ANTIGONUS","lines":["Their sacred wills be done! Go, get aboard;","Look to thy bark: I'll not be long before","I call upon thee."],"id":362,"m":"Their holy will be done! Go, get on board. Look after your ship. I won't be long before I call for you."},{"t":"sp","who":"MARINER","lines":["Make your best haste, and go not","Too far i' th' land: 'tis like to be loud weather;","Besides, this place is famous for the creatures","Of prey that keep upon 't."],"id":363,"m":"Be as quick as you can, and don't go too far inland. It's likely to be stormy. Besides, this place is known for the wild beasts of prey that live here."},{"t":"sp","who":"ANTIGONUS","lines":["Go thou away:","I'll follow instantly."],"id":364,"m":"Go on. I'll follow straight away."},{"t":"sp","who":"MARINER","lines":["I am glad at heart","To be so rid o' th' business."],"id":365,"m":"I'm glad at heart to be rid of this business."},{"t":"sd","text":"Exit.","id":366},{"t":"sp","who":"ANTIGONUS","lines":["Come, poor babe.","I have heard, but not believ'd, the spirits of the dead","May walk again: if such thing be, thy mother","Appear'd to me last night; for ne'er was dream","So like a waking. To me comes a creature,","Sometimes her head on one side, some another.","I never saw a vessel of like sorrow,","So fill'd and so becoming: in pure white robes,","Like very sanctity, she did approach","My cabin where I lay: thrice bow'd before me,","And, gasping to begin some speech, her eyes","Became two spouts. The fury spent, anon","Did this break from her: \"Good Antigonus,","Since fate, against thy better disposition,","Hath made thy person for the thrower-out","Of my poor babe, according to thine oath,","Places remote enough are in Bohemia,","There weep, and leave it crying. And, for the babe","Is counted lost for ever, Perdita","I prithee call't. For this ungentle business,","Put on thee by my lord, thou ne'er shalt see","Thy wife Paulina more.\" And so, with shrieks,","She melted into air. Affrighted much,","I did in time collect myself and thought","This was so, and no slumber. Dreams are toys,","Yet for this once, yea, superstitiously,","I will be squar'd by this. I do believe","Hermione hath suffer'd death, and that","Apollo would, this being indeed the issue","Of King Polixenes, it should here be laid,","Either for life or death, upon the earth","Of its right father. Blossom, speed thee well!","There lie; and there thy character: there these;"],"id":367,"m":"Come, poor baby. I have heard, but not believed, that the spirits of the dead can walk again. If it's true, your mother appeared to me last night, for no dream was ever so like being awake. A figure came to me, with her head leaning first one way, then the other. I never saw a vessel of sorrow so full and so beautiful. In pure white robes, like holiness itself, she came to the cabin where I lay, bowed three times before me, and, gasping to begin a speech, her eyes became two fountains. When the fit was spent, this broke from her: 'Good Antigonus, since fate, against your better nature, has made you the one to throw out my poor baby, as you swore to, there are remote enough places in Bohemia. Weep there, and leave it crying. And because the baby is counted lost for ever, please call her Perdita. For this unkind business, put on you by my husband, you will never see your wife Paulina again.' And so, with shrieks, she melted into air. Badly frightened, I gradually pulled myself together and thought this was real and no dream. Dreams are trifles, but this once, yes, superstitiously, I will be ruled by this one. I believe Hermione has died, and that Apollo wants this child, being truly King Polixenes's, to be laid here, to live or die, on its true father's soil. Little blossom, good luck to you! Lie there, and there's your written account; there, these things,"},{"t":"sd","text":"Laying down the child and a bundle.","id":368},{"t":"sp","who":"ANTIGONUS","cont":1,"lines":["Which may if fortune please, both breed thee, pretty,","And still rest thine. The storm begins: poor wretch,","That for thy mother's fault art thus expos'd","To loss and what may follow! Weep I cannot,","But my heart bleeds, and most accurs'd am I","To be by oath enjoin'd to this. Farewell!","The day frowns more and more. Thou'rt like to have","A lullaby too rough. I never saw","The heavens so dim by day. A savage clamour!","Well may I get aboard! This is the chase:","I am gone for ever."],"id":369,"m":"which may, if fortune wills, pay for your upbringing, pretty one, and still leave something over. The storm is beginning. Poor wretch, exposed like this to loss and whatever follows, for your mother's fault! I cannot weep, but my heart bleeds, and I am most cursed to be bound by oath to do this. Farewell! The day frowns more and more. You're likely to have too rough a lullaby. I never saw the sky so dark by day. A savage roar! I'd better get on board! This is the hunt: I'm gone for ever."},{"t":"sd","text":"Exit, pursued by a bear.","id":370},{"t":"sd","text":"Enter an old Shepherd.","id":371},{"t":"sp","who":"SHEPHERD","lines":["I would there were no age between ten and three-and-twenty, or that","youth would sleep out the rest; for there is nothing in the between but","getting wenches with child, wronging the ancientry, stealing,","fighting—Hark you now! Would any but these boiled brains of nineteen","and two-and-twenty hunt this weather? They have scared away two of my","best sheep, which I fear the wolf will sooner find than the master: if","anywhere I have them, 'tis by the sea-side, browsing of ivy. Good luck,","an 't be thy will, what have we here?"],"id":372,"m":"I wish there were no age between ten and twenty-three, or that young men would sleep through it, for there's nothing in between but getting girls pregnant, insulting their elders, stealing, fighting... Listen to that! Would anyone but these hot-headed nineteen- and twenty-two-year-olds go hunting in this weather? They have scared off two of my best sheep, which I'm afraid the wolf will find before their owner does. If they're anywhere, it's by the seaside, nibbling ivy. Good luck, if it's your will! What have we here?"},{"t":"sd","text":"Taking up the child.","id":373},{"t":"sp","who":"SHEPHERD","cont":1,"lines":["Mercy on 's, a bairn! A very pretty bairn! A boy or a child, I wonder?","A pretty one; a very pretty one. Sure, some scape. Though I am not","bookish, yet I can read waiting-gentlewoman in the scape. This has","been some stair-work, some trunk-work, some behind-door-work. They","were warmer that got this than the poor thing is here. I'll take it up","for pity: yet I'll tarry till my son come; he halloed but even now.","Whoa-ho-hoa!"],"id":374,"m":"Mercy on us, a baby! A very pretty baby! A boy or a girl, I wonder? A pretty one, a very pretty one. Surely some mistake from a fling. Though I'm no scholar, I can read a lady's maid in this escapade. This has been some business on the back stairs, in a trunk, behind a door. Whoever made this was warmer than the poor thing is here. I'll pick it up out of pity. But I'll wait till my son comes. He shouted a moment ago. Whoa-ho-hoa!"},{"t":"sd","text":"Enter Clown.","id":375},{"t":"sp","who":"CLOWN","lines":["Hilloa, loa!"],"id":376,"m":"Hallo, hallo!"},{"t":"sp","who":"SHEPHERD","lines":["What, art so near? If thou'lt see a thing to talk on when thou art dead","and rotten, come hither. What ail'st thou, man?"],"id":377,"m":"What, are you so near? If you want to see something you'll still be talking about when you're dead and rotten, come here. What's the matter with you, man?"},{"t":"sp","who":"CLOWN","lines":["I have seen two such sights, by sea and by land! But I am not to say it","is a sea, for it is now the sky: betwixt the firmament and it, you","cannot thrust a bodkin's point."],"id":378,"m":"I have seen two such sights, at sea and on land! But I can't call it the sea, because it's the sky now. You couldn't stick a pin between the heavens and the waves."},{"t":"sp","who":"SHEPHERD","lines":["Why, boy, how is it?"],"id":379,"m":"Why, boy, what's happened?"},{"t":"sp","who":"CLOWN","lines":["I would you did but see how it chafes, how it rages, how it takes up","the shore! But that's not to the point. O, the most piteous cry of the","poor souls! sometimes to see 'em, and not to see 'em. Now the ship","boring the moon with her mainmast, and anon swallowed with yest and","froth, as you'd thrust a cork into a hogshead. And then for the land","service, to see how the bear tore out his shoulder-bone, how he cried","to me for help, and said his name was Antigonus, a nobleman. But to","make an end of the ship, to see how the sea flap-dragon'd it: but","first, how the poor souls roared, and the sea mocked them, and how the","poor gentleman roared, and the bear mocked him, both roaring louder","than the sea or weather."],"id":380,"m":"I wish you could see how it rages and storms, how it swallows the shore! But that's not the point. O, the pitiful cries of the poor souls! Sometimes you could see them, sometimes not. One moment the ship was poking the moon with her mainmast, the next swallowed in foam and froth, like a cork thrown into a barrel. And then on land, to see how the bear tore out his shoulder-bone, how he cried to me for help and said his name was Antigonus, a nobleman. But to finish with the ship, to see how the sea gulped it down: but first, how the poor souls roared, and the sea mocked them, and how the poor gentleman roared, and the bear mocked him, both roaring louder than the sea or the storm."},{"t":"sp","who":"SHEPHERD","lines":["Name of mercy, when was this, boy?"],"id":381,"m":"For mercy's sake, when was this, boy?"},{"t":"sp","who":"CLOWN","lines":["Now, now. I have not winked since I saw these sights: the men are not","yet cold under water, nor the bear half dined on the gentleman. He's at","it now."],"id":382,"m":"Now, just now. I haven't blinked since I saw these sights. The men aren't cold under the water yet, and the bear isn't half finished dining on the gentleman. He's at it now."},{"t":"sp","who":"SHEPHERD","lines":["Would I had been by to have helped the old man!"],"id":383,"m":"I wish I had been there to help the old man!"},{"t":"sp","who":"CLOWN","lines":["I would you had been by the ship side, to have helped her: there your","charity would have lacked footing."],"id":384,"m":"I wish you had been by the ship to help her. Your charity would have had nothing to stand on there."},{"t":"sp","who":"SHEPHERD","lines":["Heavy matters, heavy matters! But look thee here, boy. Now bless","thyself: thou met'st with things dying, I with things new-born. Here's","a sight for thee. Look thee, a bearing-cloth for a squire's child! Look","thee here; take up, take up, boy; open't. So, let's see. It was told me","I should be rich by the fairies. This is some changeling: open't.","What's within, boy?"],"id":385,"m":"Sad things, sad things! But look here, boy. Now bless yourself: you met with things dying, I with things newborn. Here's a sight for you. Look, a christening cloth fit for a gentleman's child! Look here. Pick it up, pick it up, boy. Open it. So, let's see. I was told by fortune-tellers that the fairies would make me rich. This is some fairy changeling. Open it. What's inside, boy?"},{"t":"sp","who":"CLOWN","lines":["You're a made old man. If the sins of your youth are forgiven you,","you're well to live. Gold! all gold!"],"id":386,"m":"You're a made man, old man. If the sins of your youth are forgiven, you'll live well. Gold! All gold!"},{"t":"sp","who":"SHEPHERD","lines":["This is fairy gold, boy, and 'twill prove so. Up with it, keep it","close: home, home, the next way. We are lucky, boy, and to be so still","requires nothing but secrecy. Let my sheep go: come, good boy, the next","way home."],"id":387,"m":"This is fairy gold, boy, and it will prove so. Pick it up, keep it hidden. Home, home, the quickest way. We are lucky, boy, and to stay that way we need only keep it secret. Let my sheep go. Come, good boy, the quickest way home."},{"t":"sp","who":"CLOWN","lines":["Go you the next way with your findings. I'll go see if the bear be gone","from the gentleman, and how much he hath eaten. They are never curst","but when they are hungry: if there be any of him left, I'll bury it."],"id":388,"m":"You go the quickest way with your find. I'll go and see if the bear has left the gentleman, and how much he's eaten. They're only fierce when they're hungry. If there's anything left of him, I'll bury it."},{"t":"sp","who":"SHEPHERD","lines":["That's a good deed. If thou mayest discern by that which is left of him","what he is, fetch me to th' sight of him."],"id":389,"m":"That's a good deed. If you can tell from what's left of him who he is, fetch me to see him."},{"t":"sp","who":"CLOWN","lines":["Marry, will I; and you shall help to put him i' th' ground."],"id":390,"m":"I certainly will, and you shall help put him in the ground."},{"t":"sp","who":"SHEPHERD","lines":["'Tis a lucky day, boy, and we'll do good deeds on 't."],"id":391,"m":"It's a lucky day, boy, and we'll do good deeds on it."},{"t":"sd","text":"Exeunt.","id":392},{"t":"scene","act":4,"scene":"I","loc":"Time, as Chorus.","id":393},{"t":"sd","text":"Enter Time, the Chorus.","id":394},{"t":"sp","who":"TIME","lines":["I that please some, try all: both joy and terror","Of good and bad, that makes and unfolds error,","Now take upon me, in the name of Time,","To use my wings. Impute it not a crime","To me or my swift passage, that I slide","O'er sixteen years, and leave the growth untried","Of that wide gap, since it is in my power","To o'erthrow law, and in one self-born hour","To plant and o'erwhelm custom. Let me pass","The same I am, ere ancient'st order was","Or what is now received. I witness to","The times that brought them in; so shall I do","To th' freshest things now reigning, and make stale","The glistering of this present, as my tale","Now seems to it. Your patience this allowing,","I turn my glass, and give my scene such growing","As you had slept between. Leontes leaving","Th' effects of his fond jealousies, so grieving","That he shuts up himself, imagine me,","Gentle spectators, that I now may be","In fair Bohemia, and remember well,","I mentioned a son o' th' king's, which Florizel","I now name to you; and with speed so pace","To speak of Perdita, now grown in grace","Equal with wondering. What of her ensues","I list not prophesy; but let Time's news","Be known when 'tis brought forth. A shepherd's daughter,","And what to her adheres, which follows after,","Is th' argument of Time. Of this allow,","If ever you have spent time worse ere now;","If never, yet that Time himself doth say","He wishes earnestly you never may."],"id":395,"m":"I, who please some and test everyone, who am joy and terror to good and bad alike, who make mistakes and reveal them, now take it on myself, as Time, to use my wings. Don't count it a crime of mine, or of my swift passing, that I slide over sixteen years and leave what happened in that wide gap untold, since it is in my power to overturn law, and in a single hour of my own making to plant and overthrow custom. Let me pass as the same I was before the oldest order existed, or what is accepted now. I saw the times that brought them in, and I shall do the same to the newest things in fashion now, and make the glitter of the present as stale as my story seems now. If you will allow it, I turn my hourglass and move my play on as if you had slept in between. Leaving Leontes, who is grieving so much over the results of his foolish jealousy that he shuts himself away, imagine me, gentle audience, now in lovely Bohemia. And remember that I mentioned a son of the king's, whom I now name to you as Florizel. And now let me hurry on to speak of Perdita, grown in grace to be the wonder of everyone. What happens to her I don't care to prophesy, but let Time's news be known when it is born. A shepherd's daughter, and what comes with her, is the subject of Time's story. Allow this, if you have ever spent time worse before now. If never, then Time himself says he earnestly wishes you never may."},{"t":"sd","text":"Exit.","id":396},{"t":"scene","act":4,"scene":"II","loc":"Bohemia. A Room in the palace of Polixenes.","id":397},{"t":"sd","text":"Enter Polixenes and Camillo.","id":398},{"t":"sp","who":"POLIXENES","lines":["I pray thee, good Camillo, be no more importunate: 'tis a sickness","denying thee anything; a death to grant this."],"id":399,"m":"Please, good Camillo, don't press me any more. It makes me sick to refuse you anything, but it would kill me to grant this."},{"t":"sp","who":"CAMILLO","lines":["It is fifteen years since I saw my country. Though I have for the most","part been aired abroad, I desire to lay my bones there. Besides, the","penitent king, my master, hath sent for me; to whose feeling sorrows I","might be some allay, or I o'erween to think so,—which is another spur","to my departure."],"id":400,"m":"It is fifteen years since I saw my country. Though I have lived abroad most of my life, I want to lay my bones there. Besides, the repentant king, my master, has sent for me. I might be some comfort in his deep sorrow, or I flatter myself that I might, which is another spur to my leaving."},{"t":"sp","who":"POLIXENES","lines":["As thou lov'st me, Camillo, wipe not out the rest of thy services by","leaving me now: the need I have of thee, thine own goodness hath made;","better not to have had thee than thus to want thee. Thou, having made","me businesses which none without thee can sufficiently manage, must","either stay to execute them thyself, or take away with thee the very","services thou hast done, which if I have not enough considered (as too","much I cannot) to be more thankful to thee shall be my study; and my","profit therein the heaping friendships. Of that fatal country Sicilia,","prithee speak no more; whose very naming punishes me with the","remembrance of that penitent, as thou call'st him, and reconciled king,","my brother; whose loss of his most precious queen and children are even","now to be afresh lamented. Say to me, when sawest thou the Prince","Florizel, my son? Kings are no less unhappy, their issue not being","gracious, than they are in losing them when they have approved their","virtues."],"id":401,"m":"If you love me, Camillo, don't wipe out all your other services by leaving me now. Your own goodness has made me need you. It would be better never to have had you than to lose you like this. You have started business for me that nobody but you can manage properly, so you must either stay and carry it out yourself, or take away with you the very services you have done. If I have not thanked you enough (and I cannot thank you too much), I'll make it my study to be more thankful, and my profit will be more of your friendship. Please say no more about that fatal country Sicilia. The very name punishes me by reminding me of that repentant king, as you call him, my brother, now reconciled, whose loss of his most precious queen and children is still to be mourned afresh. Tell me, when did you last see Prince Florizel, my son? Kings are as unhappy when their children turn out badly as when they lose them after they have proved their worth."},{"t":"sp","who":"CAMILLO","lines":["Sir, it is three days since I saw the prince. What his happier affairs","may be, are to me unknown, but I have missingly noted he is of late","much retired from court, and is less frequent to his princely exercises","than formerly he hath appeared."],"id":402,"m":"Sir, it is three days since I saw the prince. What pleasanter affairs he has, I don't know, but I have noticed his absence lately: he spends much less time at court and at his princely exercises than he used to."},{"t":"sp","who":"POLIXENES","lines":["I have considered so much, Camillo, and with some care; so far that I","have eyes under my service which look upon his removedness; from whom I","have this intelligence, that he is seldom from the house of a most","homely shepherd, a man, they say, that from very nothing, and beyond","the imagination of his neighbours, is grown into an unspeakable estate."],"id":403,"m":"I have noticed that too, Camillo, and with some concern, so much that I have spies in my service watching his absences. From them I've learnt that he is hardly ever away from the house of a very humble shepherd, a man who, they say, has grown from nothing to an incredible fortune, beyond anything his neighbours can imagine."},{"t":"sp","who":"CAMILLO","lines":["I have heard, sir, of such a man, who hath a daughter of most rare","note: the report of her is extended more than can be thought to begin","from such a cottage."],"id":404,"m":"I have heard of such a man, sir, who has a daughter of quite extraordinary reputation. Talk of her spreads further than you would think could start from such a cottage."},{"t":"sp","who":"POLIXENES","lines":["That's likewise part of my intelligence: but, I fear, the angle that","plucks our son thither. Thou shalt accompany us to the place, where we","will, not appearing what we are, have some question with the shepherd;","from whose simplicity I think it not uneasy to get the cause of my","son's resort thither. Prithee, be my present partner in this business,","and lay aside the thoughts of Sicilia."],"id":405,"m":"That is part of my report too. But I fear it's the hook that pulls our son there. You shall come with us to the place, where, without showing who we are, we will question the shepherd. He's simple, so I think it won't be hard to get from him why my son goes there. Please be my partner in this business now, and put aside your thoughts of Sicilia."},{"t":"sp","who":"CAMILLO","lines":["I willingly obey your command."],"id":406,"m":"I gladly obey your command."},{"t":"sp","who":"POLIXENES","lines":["My best Camillo! We must disguise ourselves."],"id":407,"m":"My best Camillo! We must disguise ourselves."},{"t":"sd","text":"Exeunt.","id":408},{"t":"scene","act":4,"scene":"III","loc":"The same. A Road near the Shepherd's cottage.","id":409},{"t":"sd","text":"Enter Autolycus, singing.","id":410},{"t":"sp","who":"AUTOLYCUS","lines":["~When daffodils begin to peer,","~With, hey! the doxy over the dale,","~Why, then comes in the sweet o' the year,","~For the red blood reigns in the winter's pale.","~The white sheet bleaching on the hedge,","~With, hey! the sweet birds, O, how they sing!","~Doth set my pugging tooth on edge;","~For a quart of ale is a dish for a king.","~The lark, that tirra-lirra chants,","~With, hey! with, hey! the thrush and the jay,","~Are summer songs for me and my aunts,","~While we lie tumbling in the hay.","I have served Prince Florizel, and in my time wore three-pile, but now","I am out of service.","~But shall I go mourn for that, my dear?","~The pale moon shines by night:","~And when I wander here and there,","~I then do most go right.","~If tinkers may have leave to live,","~And bear the sow-skin budget,","~Then my account I well may give","~And in the stocks avouch it.","My traffic is sheets; when the kite builds, look to lesser linen. My","father named me Autolycus; who being, I as am, littered under Mercury,","was likewise a snapper-up of unconsidered trifles. With die and drab I","purchased this caparison, and my revenue is the silly cheat. Gallows","and knock are too powerful on the highway. Beating and hanging are","terrors to me. For the life to come, I sleep out the thought of it. A","prize! a prize!"],"id":411,"m":"(Singing) When daffodils begin to peep, with hey, the girl over the valley, why then comes in the sweet of the year, for the red blood rules where winter's pale used to. The white sheet bleaching on the hedge, with hey, the sweet birds, O, how they sing! sets my thieving tooth on edge, for a quart of ale is a dish for a king. The lark that sings tirra-lirra, with hey, with hey, the thrush and the jay, are summer songs for me and my girlfriends while we lie tumbling in the hay. I have served Prince Florizel, and in my time wore the finest velvet, but now I'm out of work. (Singing) But shall I go and mourn for that, my dear? The pale moon shines by night, and when I wander here and there I'm most on the right road. If tinkers are allowed to live and carry their pigskin bags, then I can well give an account of myself, and swear to it in the stocks. My trade is sheets: when the kite is building its nest, watch your smaller linen. My father named me Autolycus. Being born, as I was, under Mercury, the god of thieves, I was likewise a snapper-up of trifles nobody watches. Dice and whores bought me these rags, and my income is cheating fools. The gallows and a beating are too great a risk on the highway. Beating and hanging terrify me. As for the life to come, I sleep away the thought of it. A prize! A prize!"},{"t":"sd","text":"Enter Clown.","id":412},{"t":"sp","who":"CLOWN","lines":["Let me see: every 'leven wether tods; every tod yields pound and odd","shilling; fifteen hundred shorn, what comes the wool to?"],"id":413,"m":"Let me see: every eleven sheep give a tod of wool, every tod yields a pound and some shillings; fifteen hundred sheared, what does the wool come to?"},{"t":"sp","who":"AUTOLYCUS","lines":["[Aside] If the springe hold, the cock's mine."],"id":414,"m":"(Aside) If the snare holds, the bird is mine."},{"t":"sp","who":"CLOWN","lines":["I cannot do't without counters. Let me see; what am I to buy for our","sheep-shearing feast? \"Three pound of sugar, five pound of currants,","rice\"—what will this sister of mine do with rice? But my father hath","made her mistress of the feast, and she lays it on. She hath made me","four-and-twenty nosegays for the shearers, three-man song-men all, and","very good ones; but they are most of them means and basses, but one","puritan amongst them, and he sings psalms to hornpipes. I must have","saffron to colour the warden pies; \"mace; dates\", none, that's out of","my note; \"nutmegs, seven; a race or two of ginger\", but that I may beg;","\"four pound of prunes, and as many of raisins o' th' sun.\""],"id":415,"m":"I can't do it without counters. Let me see, what am I to buy for our sheep-shearing feast? 'Three pounds of sugar, five pounds of currants, rice.' What will this sister of mine do with rice? But my father has made her mistress of the feast, and she's going all out. She has made twenty-four posies for the shearers, all singers of three-part songs, and very good ones, but they're mostly tenors and basses, except one Puritan among them, and he sings psalms to dance tunes. I must have saffron to colour the pear pies. 'Mace; dates': no, that's not on my list. 'Nutmegs, seven; a root or two of ginger,' but I can beg that; 'four pounds of prunes, and as many raisins dried in the sun.'"},{"t":"sp","who":"AUTOLYCUS","lines":["[Grovelling on the ground] O that ever I was born!"],"id":416,"m":"(Grovelling on the ground) O, that I was ever born!"},{"t":"sp","who":"CLOWN","lines":["I' th' name of me!"],"id":417,"m":"Goodness me!"},{"t":"sp","who":"AUTOLYCUS","lines":["O, help me, help me! Pluck but off these rags; and then, death, death!"],"id":418,"m":"O, help me, help me! Just pull off these rags, and then death, death!"},{"t":"sp","who":"CLOWN","lines":["Alack, poor soul! thou hast need of more rags to lay on thee, rather","than have these off."],"id":419,"m":"Alas, poor soul! You need more rags put on you, not these taken off."},{"t":"sp","who":"AUTOLYCUS","lines":["O sir, the loathsomeness of them offends me more than the stripes I","have received, which are mighty ones and millions."],"id":420,"m":"O sir, their filthiness offends me more than the blows I've had, which were mighty ones, and millions of them."},{"t":"sp","who":"CLOWN","lines":["Alas, poor man! a million of beating may come to a great matter."],"id":421,"m":"Alas, poor man! A million blows can add up to a lot."},{"t":"sp","who":"AUTOLYCUS","lines":["I am robbed, sir, and beaten; my money and apparel ta'en from me, and","these detestable things put upon me."],"id":422,"m":"I've been robbed, sir, and beaten. My money and clothes were taken from me, and these disgusting things put on me."},{"t":"sp","who":"CLOWN","lines":["What, by a horseman or a footman?"],"id":423,"m":"What, by a man on horseback or on foot?"},{"t":"sp","who":"AUTOLYCUS","lines":["A footman, sweet sir, a footman."],"id":424,"m":"On foot, sweet sir, on foot."},{"t":"sp","who":"CLOWN","lines":["Indeed, he should be a footman by the garments he has left with thee:","if this be a horseman's coat, it hath seen very hot service. Lend me","thy hand, I'll help thee: come, lend me thy hand."],"id":425,"m":"Yes, he must have been a footpad, by the clothes he's left you. If this is a horseman's coat, it's seen some very hot service. Give me your hand. I'll help you. Come, give me your hand."},{"t":"sd","text":"Helping him up.","id":426},{"t":"sp","who":"AUTOLYCUS","lines":["O, good sir, tenderly, O!"],"id":427,"m":"O, good sir, gently, O!"},{"t":"sp","who":"CLOWN","lines":["Alas, poor soul!"],"id":428,"m":"Alas, poor soul!"},{"t":"sp","who":"AUTOLYCUS","lines":["O, good sir, softly, good sir. I fear, sir, my shoulder blade is out."],"id":429,"m":"O, good sir, softly, good sir. I'm afraid, sir, my shoulder blade is out."},{"t":"sp","who":"CLOWN","lines":["How now! canst stand?"],"id":430,"m":"What's this? Can you stand?"},{"t":"sp","who":"AUTOLYCUS","lines":["Softly, dear sir! [Picks his pocket] good sir, softly. You ha' done","me a charitable office."],"id":431,"m":"Softly, dear sir! (He picks the Clown's pocket) Good sir, softly. You have done me a kind service."},{"t":"sp","who":"CLOWN","lines":["Dost lack any money? I have a little money for thee."],"id":432,"m":"Do you need any money? I have a little money for you."},{"t":"sp","who":"AUTOLYCUS","lines":["No, good sweet sir; no, I beseech you, sir: I have a kinsman not past","three-quarters of a mile hence, unto whom I was going. I shall there","have money or anything I want. Offer me no money, I pray you; that","kills my heart."],"id":433,"m":"No, good sweet sir, no, I beg you, sir. I have a relative not more than three-quarters of a mile from here, where I was going. I shall have money there, or anything I need. Don't offer me money, please; that breaks my heart."},{"t":"sp","who":"CLOWN","lines":["What manner of fellow was he that robbed you?"],"id":434,"m":"What sort of fellow was it that robbed you?"},{"t":"sp","who":"AUTOLYCUS","lines":["A fellow, sir, that I have known to go about with troll-my-dames. I","knew him once a servant of the prince; I cannot tell, good sir, for","which of his virtues it was, but he was certainly whipped out of the","court."],"id":435,"m":"A fellow, sir, that I have known to go round with a gambling game. I knew him once as a servant of the prince. I can't say, good sir, which of his virtues it was for, but he was certainly whipped out of court."},{"t":"sp","who":"CLOWN","lines":["His vices, you would say; there's no virtue whipped out of the court.","They cherish it to make it stay there; and yet it will no more but","abide."],"id":436,"m":"His vices, you mean. No virtue gets whipped out of court. They cherish virtue to make it stay there, and yet it only ever visits."},{"t":"sp","who":"AUTOLYCUS","lines":["Vices, I would say, sir. I know this man well. He hath been since an","ape-bearer, then a process-server, a bailiff. Then he compassed a","motion of the Prodigal Son, and married a tinker's wife within a mile","where my land and living lies; and, having flown over many knavish","professions, he settled only in rogue. Some call him Autolycus."],"id":437,"m":"Vices, I mean, sir. I know this man well. Since then he has been a man with a performing monkey, then a server of summonses, a bailiff. Then he got hold of a puppet show of the Prodigal Son, and married a tinker's wife within a mile of where my land and living are. After flitting through many crooked trades, he settled down as a rogue. Some call him Autolycus."},{"t":"sp","who":"CLOWN","lines":["Out upon him! prig, for my life, prig: he haunts wakes, fairs, and","bear-baitings."],"id":438,"m":"Curse him! A thief, I swear, a thief. He hangs around church festivals, fairs and bear-baitings."},{"t":"sp","who":"AUTOLYCUS","lines":["Very true, sir; he, sir, he; that's the rogue that put me into this","apparel."],"id":439,"m":"Very true, sir. He, sir, he. That's the rogue that put me in these clothes."},{"t":"sp","who":"CLOWN","lines":["Not a more cowardly rogue in all Bohemia. If you had but looked big and","spit at him, he'd have run."],"id":440,"m":"There isn't a more cowardly rogue in all Bohemia. If you had only looked big and spat at him, he'd have run."},{"t":"sp","who":"AUTOLYCUS","lines":["I must confess to you, sir, I am no fighter. I am false of heart that","way; and that he knew, I warrant him."],"id":441,"m":"I must confess to you, sir, I'm no fighter. My heart's weak that way, and he knew it, I'm sure."},{"t":"sp","who":"CLOWN","lines":["How do you now?"],"id":442,"m":"How are you now?"},{"t":"sp","who":"AUTOLYCUS","lines":["Sweet sir, much better than I was. I can stand and walk: I will even","take my leave of you and pace softly towards my kinsman's."],"id":443,"m":"Sweet sir, much better than I was. I can stand and walk. I'll even say goodbye to you and walk slowly to my relative's."},{"t":"sp","who":"CLOWN","lines":["Shall I bring thee on the way?"],"id":444,"m":"Shall I take you on your way?"},{"t":"sp","who":"AUTOLYCUS","lines":["No, good-faced sir; no, sweet sir."],"id":445,"m":"No, kind-faced sir; no, sweet sir."},{"t":"sp","who":"CLOWN","lines":["Then fare thee well. I must go buy spices for our sheep-shearing."],"id":446,"m":"Then goodbye. I must go and buy spices for our sheep-shearing."},{"t":"sp","who":"AUTOLYCUS","lines":["Prosper you, sweet sir!"],"id":447,"m":"Good luck to you, sweet sir!"},{"t":"sd","text":"Exit Clown.","id":448},{"t":"sp","who":"AUTOLYCUS","cont":1,"lines":["Your purse is not hot enough to purchase your spice. I'll be with you","at your sheep-shearing too. If I make not this cheat bring out","another, and the shearers prove sheep, let me be unrolled, and my name","put in the book of virtue!"],"id":449,"m":"Your purse isn't warm enough now to buy your spice. I'll be with you at your sheep-shearing too. If I don't make this trick lead to another, and the shearers turn out to be sheep, let my name be struck off the roll of rogues and written in the book of virtue!"},{"t":"sd","text":"Sings.","id":450},{"t":"sp","who":"AUTOLYCUS","cont":1,"lines":["~Jog on, jog on, the footpath way,","~And merrily hent the stile-a:","~A merry heart goes all the day,","~Your sad tires in a mile-a."],"id":451,"m":"(Singing) Jog on, jog on, along the footpath, and merrily jump over the stile. A merry heart goes all day long; a sad one tires in a mile."},{"t":"sd","text":"Exit.","id":452},{"t":"scene","act":4,"scene":"IV","loc":"The same. A Shepherd's Cottage.","id":453},{"t":"sd","text":"Enter Florizel and Perdita.","id":454},{"t":"sp","who":"FLORIZEL","lines":["These your unusual weeds to each part of you","Do give a life, no shepherdess, but Flora","Peering in April's front. This your sheep-shearing","Is as a meeting of the petty gods,","And you the queen on 't."],"id":455,"m":"These unusual clothes of yours give life to every part of you. You are no shepherdess, but Flora, goddess of flowers, peeping out at the start of April. This sheep-shearing of yours is like a meeting of the minor gods, and you are its queen."},{"t":"sp","who":"PERDITA","lines":["Sir, my gracious lord,","To chide at your extremes it not becomes me;","O, pardon that I name them! Your high self,","The gracious mark o' th' land, you have obscur'd","With a swain's wearing, and me, poor lowly maid,","Most goddess-like prank'd up. But that our feasts","In every mess have folly, and the feeders","Digest it with a custom, I should blush","To see you so attir'd; swoon, I think,","To show myself a glass."],"id":456,"m":"Sir, my gracious lord, it isn't my place to scold you for your excesses. O, forgive me for mentioning them! You have hidden your high self, the gracious model of the land, in a peasant's clothes, and dressed me, a poor humble girl, like a goddess. If it weren't that our feasts have foolery in every course, and the guests are used to swallowing it, I would blush to see you dressed like that, and faint, I think, to see myself in a mirror."},{"t":"sp","who":"FLORIZEL","lines":["I bless the time","When my good falcon made her flight across","Thy father's ground."],"id":457,"m":"I bless the time when my good falcon flew across your father's land."},{"t":"sp","who":"PERDITA","lines":["Now Jove afford you cause!","To me the difference forges dread. Your greatness","Hath not been us'd to fear. Even now I tremble","To think your father, by some accident,","Should pass this way, as you did. O, the Fates!","How would he look to see his work, so noble,","Vilely bound up? What would he say? Or how","Should I, in these my borrow'd flaunts, behold","The sternness of his presence?"],"id":458,"m":"May Jove give you cause to! The difference in our rank makes me afraid. Your greatness has never been used to fear. Even now I tremble to think your father might come this way by chance, as you did. O, the Fates! How would he look to see his own noble work, his son, so vilely bound up? What would he say? Or how could I, in this borrowed finery, face the sternness of his presence?"},{"t":"sp","who":"FLORIZEL","lines":["Apprehend","Nothing but jollity. The gods themselves,","Humbling their deities to love, have taken","The shapes of beasts upon them. Jupiter","Became a bull and bellow'd; the green Neptune","A ram and bleated; and the fire-rob'd god,","Golden Apollo, a poor humble swain,","As I seem now. Their transformations","Were never for a piece of beauty rarer,","Nor in a way so chaste, since my desires","Run not before mine honour, nor my lusts","Burn hotter than my faith."],"id":459,"m":"Expect nothing but fun. The gods themselves, lowering their godhead for love, have taken the shapes of beasts. Jupiter became a bull and bellowed; green Neptune a ram, and bleated; and the god in robes of fire, golden Apollo, a poor humble shepherd, as I seem now. Their transformations were never for a rarer beauty, nor in such a chaste way, since my desires don't run ahead of my honour, nor my lust burn hotter than my faith."},{"t":"sp","who":"PERDITA","lines":["O, but, sir,","Your resolution cannot hold when 'tis","Oppos'd, as it must be, by the power of the king:","One of these two must be necessities,","Which then will speak, that you must change this purpose,","Or I my life."],"id":460,"m":"O, but, sir, your resolve cannot hold when it is opposed, as it must be, by the king's power. One of two things will then have to happen: either you must change your plan, or I must lose my life."},{"t":"sp","who":"FLORIZEL","lines":["Thou dearest Perdita,","With these forc'd thoughts, I prithee, darken not","The mirth o' th' feast. Or I'll be thine, my fair,","Or not my father's. For I cannot be","Mine own, nor anything to any, if","I be not thine. To this I am most constant,","Though destiny say no. Be merry, gentle.","Strangle such thoughts as these with anything","That you behold the while. Your guests are coming:","Lift up your countenance, as it were the day","Of celebration of that nuptial which","We two have sworn shall come."],"id":461,"m":"Dearest Perdita, please don't darken the fun of the feast with these far-fetched thoughts. Either I'll be yours, my fair one, or not my father's. For I cannot be my own, or anything to anyone, if I am not yours. I am firmly fixed on this, even if destiny says no. Be merry, gentle one. Strangle thoughts like these with anything you see around you. Your guests are coming. Lift up your face, as if it were the day of the wedding that we two have sworn shall come."},{"t":"sp","who":"PERDITA","lines":["O lady Fortune,","Stand you auspicious!"],"id":462,"m":"O Lady Fortune, be kind to us!"},{"t":"sp","who":"FLORIZEL","lines":["See, your guests approach:","Address yourself to entertain them sprightly,","And let's be red with mirth."],"id":463,"m":"Look, your guests are coming. Get ready to entertain them in high spirits, and let's be red-faced with fun."},{"t":"sd","text":"Enter Shepherd with Polixenes and Camillo, disguised; Clown, Mopsa, Dorcas with others.","id":464},{"t":"sp","who":"SHEPHERD","lines":["Fie, daughter! When my old wife liv'd, upon","This day she was both pantler, butler, cook,","Both dame and servant; welcom'd all; serv'd all;","Would sing her song and dance her turn; now here","At upper end o' th' table, now i' th' middle;","On his shoulder, and his; her face o' fire","With labour, and the thing she took to quench it","She would to each one sip. You are retired,","As if you were a feasted one, and not","The hostess of the meeting: pray you, bid","These unknown friends to 's welcome, for it is","A way to make us better friends, more known.","Come, quench your blushes, and present yourself","That which you are, mistress o' th' feast. Come on,","And bid us welcome to your sheep-shearing,","As your good flock shall prosper."],"id":465,"m":"For shame, daughter! When my old wife was alive, on this day she was pantry-maid, butler and cook, mistress and servant. She welcomed everyone, served everyone, sang her song and danced her turn, now at the top of the table, now in the middle, leaning on his shoulder and his, her face on fire with work, and she would sip to each one with the drink she took to cool it. You keep in the background, as if you were a guest and not the hostess of the party. Please welcome these strangers to us, for that's the way to make us better friends and better acquainted. Come, put out your blushes and show yourself for what you are, mistress of the feast. Come on, and welcome us to your sheep-shearing, as you want your good flock to thrive."},{"t":"sp","who":"PERDITA","lines":["[To Polixenes] Sir, welcome.","It is my father's will I should take on me","The hostess-ship o' the day.","[To Camillo] You're welcome, sir.","Give me those flowers there, Dorcas. Reverend sirs,","For you there's rosemary and rue; these keep","Seeming and savour all the winter long.","Grace and remembrance be to you both!","And welcome to our shearing!"],"id":466,"m":"(To Polixenes) Sir, welcome. It is my father's wish that I take on the duties of hostess today. (To Camillo) You're welcome, sir. Give me those flowers there, Dorcas. Reverend sirs, here's rosemary and rue for you. These keep their look and their scent all winter long. Grace and remembrance be with you both! And welcome to our shearing!"},{"t":"sp","who":"POLIXENES","lines":["Shepherdess—","A fair one are you—well you fit our ages","With flowers of winter."],"id":467,"m":"Shepherdess (and a lovely one you are), you suit our ages well with winter flowers."},{"t":"sp","who":"PERDITA","lines":["Sir, the year growing ancient,","Not yet on summer's death nor on the birth","Of trembling winter, the fairest flowers o' th' season","Are our carnations and streak'd gillyvors,","Which some call nature's bastards: of that kind","Our rustic garden's barren; and I care not","To get slips of them."],"id":468,"m":"Sir, as the year grows old, not yet at summer's death nor at the birth of shivering winter, the loveliest flowers of the season are our carnations and striped gillyflowers, which some call nature's bastards. Our country garden has none of that kind, and I don't care to get cuttings of them."},{"t":"sp","who":"POLIXENES","lines":["Wherefore, gentle maiden,","Do you neglect them?"],"id":469,"m":"Why, gentle maiden, do you neglect them?"},{"t":"sp","who":"PERDITA","lines":["For I have heard it said","There is an art which, in their piedness, shares","With great creating nature."],"id":470,"m":"Because I have heard it said that in their streaks there is an art of breeding that shares the work with great creating nature."},{"t":"sp","who":"POLIXENES","lines":["Say there be;","Yet nature is made better by no mean","But nature makes that mean. So, over that art","Which you say adds to nature, is an art","That nature makes. You see, sweet maid, we marry","A gentler scion to the wildest stock,","And make conceive a bark of baser kind","By bud of nobler race. This is an art","Which does mend nature, change it rather, but","The art itself is nature."],"id":471,"m":"Suppose there is. Yet nature is improved by no means but nature makes that means. So, above the art that you say adds to nature, there is an art that nature makes. You see, sweet girl, we marry a gentler shoot to the wildest stock, and make a tree of humbler kind conceive by a bud of nobler breed. This is an art that improves nature, or rather changes it, but the art itself is nature."},{"t":"sp","who":"PERDITA","lines":["So it is."],"id":472,"m":"So it is."},{"t":"sp","who":"POLIXENES","lines":["Then make your garden rich in gillyvors,","And do not call them bastards."],"id":473,"m":"Then fill your garden with gillyflowers, and don't call them bastards."},{"t":"sp","who":"PERDITA","lines":["I'll not put","The dibble in earth to set one slip of them;","No more than, were I painted, I would wish","This youth should say 'twere well, and only therefore","Desire to breed by me. Here's flowers for you:","Hot lavender, mints, savory, marjoram,","The marigold, that goes to bed with th' sun","And with him rises weeping. These are flowers","Of middle summer, and I think they are given","To men of middle age. You're very welcome."],"id":474,"m":"I won't push a spade into the earth to plant a single cutting of them, any more than, if I wore make-up, I would want this young man to say it looked well and desire to have children with me only for that. Here are flowers for you: hot lavender, mint, savory, marjoram, the marigold that goes to bed with the sun and rises with him, weeping dew. These are midsummer flowers, and I think they are given to men of middle age. You're very welcome."},{"t":"sp","who":"CAMILLO","lines":["I should leave grazing, were I of your flock,","And only live by gazing."],"id":475,"m":"If I were one of your flock, I would stop grazing and live only by gazing."},{"t":"sp","who":"PERDITA","lines":["Out, alas!","You'd be so lean that blasts of January","Would blow you through and through. [To Florizel] Now, my fair'st friend,","I would I had some flowers o' th' spring, that might","Become your time of day; and yours, and yours,","That wear upon your virgin branches yet","Your maidenheads growing. O Proserpina,","From the flowers now that, frighted, thou let'st fall","From Dis's waggon! daffodils,","That come before the swallow dares, and take","The winds of March with beauty; violets dim,","But sweeter than the lids of Juno's eyes","Or Cytherea's breath; pale primroses,","That die unmarried ere they can behold","Bright Phoebus in his strength (a malady","Most incident to maids); bold oxlips and","The crown imperial; lilies of all kinds,","The flower-de-luce being one. O, these I lack,","To make you garlands of; and my sweet friend,","To strew him o'er and o'er!"],"id":476,"m":"Oh dear! You'd be so thin that January winds would blow right through you. (To Florizel) Now, my fairest friend, I wish I had some spring flowers that might suit your time of life; (to the shepherdesses) and yours, and yours, who still carry your maidenhood on your young branches. O Proserpina, if only I had the flowers that you let fall in fright from Pluto's chariot! Daffodils, that come before the swallow dares, and take the March winds with their beauty; violets, dim, but sweeter than Juno's eyelids or the breath of Venus; pale primroses, that die unmarried before they can see the bright sun in his strength (an illness common in young girls); bold oxlips and the crown imperial; lilies of every kind, the iris being one. O, I lack these to make garlands for you, and to scatter over and over my sweet friend!"},{"t":"sp","who":"FLORIZEL","lines":["What, like a corse?"],"id":477,"m":"What, like a corpse?"},{"t":"sp","who":"PERDITA","lines":["No, like a bank for love to lie and play on;","Not like a corse; or if, not to be buried,","But quick, and in mine arms. Come, take your flowers.","Methinks I play as I have seen them do","In Whitsun pastorals. Sure this robe of mine","Does change my disposition."],"id":478,"m":"No, like a bank for love to lie and play on. Not like a corpse; or if so, not to be buried, but alive, in my arms. Come, take your flowers. I think I'm acting the way I've seen them do in Whitsun plays. This dress of mine must be changing my nature."},{"t":"sp","who":"FLORIZEL","lines":["What you do","Still betters what is done. When you speak, sweet,","I'd have you do it ever. When you sing,","I'd have you buy and sell so, so give alms,","Pray so; and, for the ord'ring your affairs,","To sing them too. When you do dance, I wish you","A wave o' th' sea, that you might ever do","Nothing but that, move still, still so,","And own no other function. Each your doing,","So singular in each particular,","Crowns what you are doing in the present deeds,","That all your acts are queens."],"id":479,"m":"Whatever you do improves on what has been done. When you speak, sweet, I'd have you speak for ever. When you sing, I'd have you buy and sell like that, give to the poor like that, pray like that, and give orders to your household singing too. When you dance, I wish you were a wave of the sea, so that you might do nothing but that, move always, always like that, and have no other task. Every single thing you do, so special in every detail, crowns what you are doing at the moment, so that all your acts are queens."},{"t":"sp","who":"PERDITA","lines":["O Doricles,","Your praises are too large. But that your youth,","And the true blood which peeps fairly through 't,","Do plainly give you out an unstained shepherd,","With wisdom I might fear, my Doricles,","You woo'd me the false way."],"id":480,"m":"O Doricles, your praises are too great. If your youth, and the honest blood that shows so fairly through it, did not plainly mark you as an innocent shepherd, I might wisely fear, my Doricles, that you were wooing me dishonestly."},{"t":"sp","who":"FLORIZEL","lines":["I think you have","As little skill to fear as I have purpose","To put you to 't. But, come; our dance, I pray.","Your hand, my Perdita. So turtles pair","That never mean to part."],"id":481,"m":"I think you have as little cause to fear as I have intention to give you any. But come, our dance, please. Your hand, my Perdita. This is how turtle-doves pair, who never mean to part."},{"t":"sp","who":"PERDITA","lines":["I'll swear for 'em."],"id":482,"m":"I'll swear for them."},{"t":"sp","who":"POLIXENES","lines":["This is the prettiest low-born lass that ever","Ran on the green-sward. Nothing she does or seems","But smacks of something greater than herself,","Too noble for this place."],"id":483,"m":"This is the prettiest low-born girl who ever ran across the green. Nothing she does or seems to be but tastes of something greater than herself, too noble for this place."},{"t":"sp","who":"CAMILLO","lines":["He tells her something","That makes her blood look out. Good sooth, she is","The queen of curds and cream."],"id":484,"m":"He's telling her something that makes her blush. Truly, she is the queen of curds and cream."},{"t":"sp","who":"CLOWN","lines":["Come on, strike up."],"id":485,"m":"Come on, strike up the music."},{"t":"sp","who":"DORCAS","lines":["Mopsa must be your mistress: marry, garlic, to mend her kissing with!"],"id":486,"m":"Mopsa must be your partner. Well, take garlic, to sweeten her kisses!"},{"t":"sp","who":"MOPSA","lines":["Now, in good time!"],"id":487,"m":"Well, really!"},{"t":"sp","who":"CLOWN","lines":["Not a word, a word; we stand upon our manners.","Come, strike up."],"id":488,"m":"Not a word, not a word. We must mind our manners. Come, strike up."},{"t":"sd","text":"Music. Here a dance of Shepherds and Shepherdesses.","id":489},{"t":"sp","who":"POLIXENES","lines":["Pray, good shepherd, what fair swain is this","Which dances with your daughter?"],"id":490,"m":"Please, good shepherd, who is the handsome young man dancing with your daughter?"},{"t":"sp","who":"SHEPHERD","lines":["They call him Doricles; and boasts himself","To have a worthy feeding. But I have it","Upon his own report, and I believe it.","He looks like sooth. He says he loves my daughter.","I think so too; for never gaz'd the moon","Upon the water as he'll stand and read,","As 'twere, my daughter's eyes. And, to be plain,","I think there is not half a kiss to choose","Who loves another best."],"id":491,"m":"They call him Doricles, and he boasts of having good land with pasture. But I only have his word for it, and I believe it. He looks honest. He says he loves my daughter. I think so too, for the moon never gazed on the water the way he'll stand and read my daughter's eyes. And to be honest, I don't think there's half a kiss between them in who loves the other best."},{"t":"sp","who":"POLIXENES","lines":["She dances featly."],"id":492,"m":"She dances gracefully."},{"t":"sp","who":"SHEPHERD","lines":["So she does anything, though I report it","That should be silent. If young Doricles","Do light upon her, she shall bring him that","Which he not dreams of."],"id":493,"m":"So she does everything, though I'm the one saying it who should keep quiet. If young Doricles gets her, she will bring him something he doesn't dream of."},{"t":"sd","text":"Enter a Servant.","id":494},{"t":"sp","who":"SERVANT","lines":["O master, if you did but hear the pedlar at the door, you would never","dance again after a tabor and pipe; no, the bagpipe could not move you.","He sings several tunes faster than you'll tell money. He utters them as","he had eaten ballads, and all men's ears grew to his tunes."],"id":495,"m":"O master, if you could only hear the pedlar at the door, you would never dance to drum and pipe again; no, even the bagpipe couldn't move you. He sings several tunes faster than you can count money. He pours them out as if he'd eaten ballads, and everyone's ears are glued to his songs."},{"t":"sp","who":"CLOWN","lines":["He could never come better: he shall come in. I love a ballad but even","too well, if it be doleful matter merrily set down, or a very pleasant","thing indeed and sung lamentably."],"id":496,"m":"He couldn't have come at a better time. He shall come in. I love a ballad almost too much, if it's sad stuff set to a merry tune, or something really cheerful sung mournfully."},{"t":"sp","who":"SERVANT","lines":["He hath songs for man or woman of all sizes. No milliner can so fit his","customers with gloves. He has the prettiest love-songs for maids, so","without bawdry, which is strange; with such delicate burdens of dildos","and fadings, \"jump her and thump her\"; and where some stretch-mouthed","rascal would, as it were, mean mischief and break a foul gap into the","matter, he makes the maid to answer \"Whoop, do me no harm, good man\";","puts him off, slights him, with \"Whoop, do me no harm, good man.\""],"id":497,"m":"He has songs for men or women of every size. No shop can fit its customers with gloves so well. He has the prettiest love songs for girls, without any rudeness, which is strange, with such dainty refrains of 'dildos' and 'fadings', 'jump her and thump her'. And where some big-mouthed rascal might mean mischief and break in with something filthy, he makes the girl answer 'Whoop, do me no harm, good man.' She puts him off and snubs him with 'Whoop, do me no harm, good man.'"},{"t":"sp","who":"POLIXENES","lines":["This is a brave fellow."],"id":498,"m":"This is a fine fellow."},{"t":"sp","who":"CLOWN","lines":["Believe me, thou talkest of an admirable conceited fellow. Has he any","unbraided wares?"],"id":499,"m":"Believe me, you're talking about a wonderfully clever fellow. Has he got any fresh goods?"},{"t":"sp","who":"SERVANT","lines":["He hath ribbons of all the colours i' th' rainbow; points, more than","all the lawyers in Bohemia can learnedly handle, though they come to","him by th' gross; inkles, caddisses, cambrics, lawns; why he sings 'em","over as they were gods or goddesses; you would think a smock were a","she-angel, he so chants to the sleeve-hand and the work about the","square on 't."],"id":500,"m":"He has ribbons of every colour in the rainbow; laces, more than all the lawyers in Bohemia can learnedly handle, though they come to him by the gross; tapes, braids, cambrics, fine linens. He sings about them as if they were gods or goddesses. You'd think a smock was a she-angel, the way he sings about the cuff and the embroidery on the front."},{"t":"sp","who":"CLOWN","lines":["Prithee bring him in; and let him approach singing."],"id":501,"m":"Please bring him in, and let him come in singing."},{"t":"sp","who":"PERDITA","lines":["Forewarn him that he use no scurrilous words in 's tunes."],"id":502,"m":"Warn him not to use any dirty words in his songs."},{"t":"sd","text":"Exit Servant.","id":503},{"t":"sp","who":"CLOWN","lines":["You have of these pedlars that have more in them than you'd think,","sister."],"id":504,"m":"There are some of these pedlars who have more in them than you'd think, sister."},{"t":"sp","who":"PERDITA","lines":["Ay, good brother, or go about to think."],"id":505,"m":"Yes, good brother, or would want to think."},{"t":"sd","text":"Enter Autolycus, singing.","id":506},{"t":"sp","who":"AUTOLYCUS","lines":["~Lawn as white as driven snow,","~Cypress black as e'er was crow,","~Gloves as sweet as damask roses,","~Masks for faces and for noses,","~Bugle-bracelet, necklace amber,","~Perfume for a lady's chamber,","~Golden quoifs and stomachers","~For my lads to give their dears,","~Pins and poking-sticks of steel,","~What maids lack from head to heel.","~Come buy of me, come; come buy, come buy;","~Buy, lads, or else your lasses cry.","~Come, buy."],"id":507,"m":"(Singing) Linen as white as driven snow, crape as black as any crow, gloves as sweet as damask roses, masks for faces and for noses, bead bracelets, amber necklaces, perfume for a lady's bedroom, golden caps and bodices for my lads to give their sweethearts, pins and steel rods for ironing ruffs, everything girls need from head to heel. Come and buy from me, come; come buy, come buy. Buy, lads, or else your girls will cry. Come and buy."},{"t":"sp","who":"CLOWN","lines":["If I were not in love with Mopsa, thou shouldst take no money of me;","but being enthralled as I am, it will also be the bondage of certain","ribbons and gloves."],"id":508,"m":"If I weren't in love with Mopsa, you wouldn't get a penny out of me. But being as enslaved as I am, it will mean buying certain ribbons and gloves as well."},{"t":"sp","who":"MOPSA","lines":["I was promised them against the feast; but they come not too late now."],"id":509,"m":"I was promised them for the feast, but they're not too late now."},{"t":"sp","who":"DORCAS","lines":["He hath promised you more than that, or there be liars."],"id":510,"m":"He has promised you more than that, or people are liars."},{"t":"sp","who":"MOPSA","lines":["He hath paid you all he promised you. Maybe he has paid you more, which","will shame you to give him again."],"id":511,"m":"He has paid you all he promised you. Maybe he's paid you more, which you'll be ashamed to give back to him."},{"t":"sp","who":"CLOWN","lines":["Is there no manners left among maids? Will they wear their plackets","where they should bear their faces? Is there not milking-time, when you","are going to bed, or kiln-hole, to whistle of these secrets, but you","must be tittle-tattling before all our guests? 'Tis well they are","whispering. Clamour your tongues, and not a word more."],"id":512,"m":"Have girls got no manners left? Will they show their petticoats where they should show their faces? Isn't there milking time, or bedtime, or the fireside, to whisper these secrets, without tittle-tattling in front of all our guests? It's a good thing they're whispering. Stop your tongues, and not another word."},{"t":"sp","who":"MOPSA","lines":["I have done. Come, you promised me a tawdry lace and a pair of sweet","gloves."],"id":513,"m":"I've finished. Come, you promised me a showy lace and a pair of scented gloves."},{"t":"sp","who":"CLOWN","lines":["Have I not told thee how I was cozened by the way and lost all my","money?"],"id":514,"m":"Haven't I told you how I was cheated on the road and lost all my money?"},{"t":"sp","who":"AUTOLYCUS","lines":["And indeed, sir, there are cozeners abroad; therefore it behoves men to","be wary."],"id":515,"m":"And indeed, sir, there are cheats about, so men should be careful."},{"t":"sp","who":"CLOWN","lines":["Fear not thou, man. Thou shalt lose nothing here."],"id":516,"m":"Don't worry, man. You'll lose nothing here."},{"t":"sp","who":"AUTOLYCUS","lines":["I hope so, sir; for I have about me many parcels of charge."],"id":517,"m":"I hope so, sir, for I'm carrying many valuable packages."},{"t":"sp","who":"CLOWN","lines":["What hast here? Ballads?"],"id":518,"m":"What have you got here? Ballads?"},{"t":"sp","who":"MOPSA","lines":["Pray now, buy some. I love a ballad in print alife, for then we are","sure they are true."],"id":519,"m":"Please, buy some. I love a printed ballad with all my heart, because then we can be sure they're true."},{"t":"sp","who":"AUTOLYCUS","lines":["Here's one to a very doleful tune. How a usurer's wife was brought to","bed of twenty money-bags at a burden, and how she longed to eat adders'","heads and toads carbonadoed."],"id":520,"m":"Here's one to a very sad tune: how a moneylender's wife gave birth to twenty moneybags at once, and how she craved adders' heads and grilled toads."},{"t":"sp","who":"MOPSA","lines":["Is it true, think you?"],"id":521,"m":"Do you think it's true?"},{"t":"sp","who":"AUTOLYCUS","lines":["Very true, and but a month old."],"id":522,"m":"Very true, and only a month old."},{"t":"sp","who":"DORCAS","lines":["Bless me from marrying a usurer!"],"id":523,"m":"Heaven save me from marrying a moneylender!"},{"t":"sp","who":"AUTOLYCUS","lines":["Here's the midwife's name to't, one Mistress Taleporter, and five or","six honest wives that were present. Why should I carry lies abroad?"],"id":524,"m":"Here's the midwife's name on it, one Mistress Taleporter, and five or six honest wives who were there. Why would I go round telling lies?"},{"t":"sp","who":"MOPSA","lines":["Pray you now, buy it."],"id":525,"m":"Please, buy it."},{"t":"sp","who":"CLOWN","lines":["Come on, lay it by; and let's first see more ballads. We'll buy the","other things anon."],"id":526,"m":"Come on, put it aside, and let's see more ballads first. We'll buy the other things later."},{"t":"sp","who":"AUTOLYCUS","lines":["Here's another ballad, of a fish that appeared upon the coast on","Wednesday the fourscore of April, forty thousand fathom above water,","and sung this ballad against the hard hearts of maids. It was thought","she was a woman, and was turned into a cold fish for she would not","exchange flesh with one that loved her. The ballad is very pitiful, and","as true."],"id":527,"m":"Here's another ballad, about a fish that appeared off the coast on Wednesday the eightieth of April, forty thousand fathoms above the water, and sang this ballad against the hard hearts of girls. It was thought she had been a woman, and was turned into a cold fish because she would not have sex with a man who loved her. The ballad is very sad, and just as true."},{"t":"sp","who":"DORCAS","lines":["Is it true too, think you?"],"id":528,"m":"Do you think that's true too?"},{"t":"sp","who":"AUTOLYCUS","lines":["Five justices' hands at it, and witnesses more than my pack will hold."],"id":529,"m":"Five magistrates have signed it, and there are more witnesses than my pack will hold."},{"t":"sp","who":"CLOWN","lines":["Lay it by too: another."],"id":530,"m":"Put that aside too. Another."},{"t":"sp","who":"AUTOLYCUS","lines":["This is a merry ballad; but a very pretty one."],"id":531,"m":"This is a merry ballad, but a very pretty one."},{"t":"sp","who":"MOPSA","lines":["Let's have some merry ones."],"id":532,"m":"Let's have some merry ones."},{"t":"sp","who":"AUTOLYCUS","lines":["Why, this is a passing merry one and goes to the tune of \"Two maids","wooing a man.\" There's scarce a maid westward but she sings it. 'Tis in","request, I can tell you."],"id":533,"m":"Why, this is an extremely merry one, and goes to the tune of 'Two girls wooing a man'. There's hardly a girl in the west who doesn't sing it. It's very popular, I can tell you."},{"t":"sp","who":"MOPSA","lines":["We can both sing it: if thou'lt bear a part, thou shalt hear; 'tis in","three parts."],"id":534,"m":"We can both sing it. If you'll take a part, you'll hear it. It's in three parts."},{"t":"sp","who":"DORCAS","lines":["We had the tune on 't a month ago."],"id":535,"m":"We learnt the tune a month ago."},{"t":"sp","who":"AUTOLYCUS","lines":["I can bear my part; you must know 'tis my occupation: have at it with","you."],"id":536,"m":"I can take my part. You must know singing is my trade. Here goes."},{"t":"sp","who":"AUTOLYCUS","lines":["~Get you hence, for I must go","~Where it fits not you to know."],"id":537,"m":"(Singing) Go away, for I must go where it's not right for you to know."},{"t":"sp","who":"DORCAS","lines":["~Whither?"],"id":538,"m":"(Singing) Where?"},{"t":"sp","who":"MOPSA","lines":["~O, whither?"],"id":539,"m":"(Singing) O, where?"},{"t":"sp","who":"DORCAS","lines":["~Whither?"],"id":540,"m":"(Singing) Where?"},{"t":"sp","who":"MOPSA","lines":["~It becomes thy oath full well","~Thou to me thy secrets tell."],"id":541,"m":"(Singing) It suits your oath very well for you to tell your secrets to me."},{"t":"sp","who":"DORCAS","lines":["~Me too! Let me go thither."],"id":542,"m":"(Singing) Me too! Let me go there."},{"t":"sp","who":"MOPSA","lines":["~Or thou goest to th' grange or mill."],"id":543,"m":"(Singing) Are you going to the farm or the mill?"},{"t":"sp","who":"DORCAS","lines":["~If to either, thou dost ill."],"id":544,"m":"(Singing) If to either, you are doing wrong."},{"t":"sp","who":"AUTOLYCUS","lines":["~Neither."],"id":545,"m":"(Singing) Neither."},{"t":"sp","who":"DORCAS","lines":["~What, neither?"],"id":546,"m":"(Singing) What, neither?"},{"t":"sp","who":"AUTOLYCUS","lines":["~Neither."],"id":547,"m":"(Singing) Neither."},{"t":"sp","who":"DORCAS","lines":["~Thou hast sworn my love to be."],"id":548,"m":"(Singing) You have sworn to be my love."},{"t":"sp","who":"MOPSA","lines":["~Thou hast sworn it more to me.","~Then whither goest? Say, whither?"],"id":549,"m":"(Singing) You've sworn it more to me. Then where are you going? Say, where?"},{"t":"sp","who":"CLOWN","lines":["We'll have this song out anon by ourselves. My father and the gentlemen","are in sad talk, and we'll not trouble them. Come, bring away thy pack","after me. Wenches, I'll buy for you both. Pedlar, let's have the first","choice. Follow me, girls."],"id":550,"m":"We'll finish this song later by ourselves. My father and the gentlemen are having a serious talk, and we won't disturb them. Come, bring your pack after me. Girls, I'll buy for you both. Pedlar, let's have first pick. Follow me, girls."},{"t":"sd","text":"Exit with Dorcas and Mopsa.","id":551},{"t":"sp","who":"AUTOLYCUS","lines":["[Aside] And you shall pay well for 'em."],"id":552,"m":"(Aside) And you'll pay well for them."},{"t":"sp","who":"AUTOLYCUS","lines":["~Will you buy any tape,","~Or lace for your cape,","~My dainty duck, my dear-a?","~Any silk, any thread,","~Any toys for your head,","~Of the new'st and fin'st, fin'st wear-a?","~Come to the pedlar;","~Money's a meddler","~That doth utter all men's ware-a."],"id":553,"m":"(Singing) Will you buy any tape, or lace for your cape, my dainty duck, my dear? Any silk, any thread, any trinkets for your head, of the newest and finest, finest wear? Come to the pedlar. Money is a go-between that sells all men's goods."},{"t":"sd","text":"Exit.","id":554},{"t":"sd","text":"Enter Servant.","id":555},{"t":"sp","who":"SERVANT","lines":["Master, there is three carters, three shepherds, three neat-herds,","three swine-herds, that have made themselves all men of hair. They call","themselves saltiers, and they have dance which the wenches say is a","gallimaufry of gambols, because they are not in 't; but they themselves","are o' the mind (if it be not too rough for some that know little but","bowling) it will please plentifully."],"id":556,"m":"Master, there are three carters, three shepherds, three cowherds and three swineherds who have dressed themselves up all in hair. They call themselves 'saltiers' (satyrs), and they have a dance which the girls say is a hotchpotch of capers, because they aren't in it. But they themselves think (if it isn't too rough for some who know nothing but bowls) it will please very much."},{"t":"sp","who":"SHEPHERD","lines":["Away! we'll none on 't. Here has been too much homely foolery already.","I know, sir, we weary you."],"id":557,"m":"Away! We'll have none of it. There's been too much homely foolery already. I know, sir, we are tiring you."},{"t":"sp","who":"POLIXENES","lines":["You weary those that refresh us: pray, let's see these four threes of","herdsmen."],"id":558,"m":"You tire those who refresh us. Please, let's see these four threes of herdsmen."},{"t":"sp","who":"SERVANT","lines":["One three of them, by their own report, sir, hath danced before the","king; and not the worst of the three but jumps twelve foot and a half","by th' square."],"id":559,"m":"One three of them, by their own account, sir, have danced before the king, and the worst of the three jumps twelve and a half feet, measured exactly."},{"t":"sp","who":"SHEPHERD","lines":["Leave your prating: since these good men are pleased, let them come in;","but quickly now."],"id":560,"m":"Stop your chattering. Since these good men are pleased, let them come in. But quickly now."},{"t":"sp","who":"SERVANT","lines":["Why, they stay at door, sir."],"id":561,"m":"Why, they're waiting at the door, sir."},{"t":"sd","text":"Exit.","id":562},{"t":"sd","text":"Enter Twelve Rustics, habited like Satyrs. They dance, and then exeunt.","id":563},{"t":"sp","who":"POLIXENES","lines":["O, father, you'll know more of that hereafter.","[To Camillo] Is it not too far gone? 'Tis time to part them.","He's simple and tells much. [To Florizel] How now, fair shepherd!","Your heart is full of something that does take","Your mind from feasting. Sooth, when I was young","And handed love, as you do, I was wont","To load my she with knacks: I would have ransack'd","The pedlar's silken treasury and have pour'd it","To her acceptance. You have let him go,","And nothing marted with him. If your lass","Interpretation should abuse, and call this","Your lack of love or bounty, you were straited","For a reply, at least if you make a care","Of happy holding her."],"id":564,"m":"(To the Shepherd) O, old man, you'll hear more of that later. (To Camillo) Hasn't it gone too far? It's time to part them. He's simple and tells a lot. (To Florizel) Well, handsome shepherd! Your heart is full of something that takes your mind off the feast. Truly, when I was young and courted as you do, I used to load my girl with trinkets. I would have ransacked the pedlar's silk treasury and poured it out for her to accept. You have let him go and bought nothing from him. If your girl took it the wrong way and called it a lack of love or generosity, you would be hard put to answer, at least if you care about keeping her happy."},{"t":"sp","who":"FLORIZEL","lines":["Old sir, I know","She prizes not such trifles as these are:","The gifts she looks from me are pack'd and lock'd","Up in my heart, which I have given already,","But not deliver'd. O, hear me breathe my life","Before this ancient sir, who, it should seem,","Hath sometime lov'd. I take thy hand! this hand,","As soft as dove's down and as white as it,","Or Ethiopian's tooth, or the fann'd snow that's bolted","By th' northern blasts twice o'er."],"id":565,"m":"Old sir, I know she does not value trifles like these. The gifts she expects from me are packed and locked up in my heart, which I have given her already, but not formally handed over. (To Perdita) O, hear me breathe out my life before this old gentleman, who, it seems, was once in love. I take your hand, this hand, as soft as a dove's down and as white, or as an African's tooth, or the snow that the northern winds have sifted twice over."},{"t":"sp","who":"POLIXENES","lines":["What follows this?","How prettily the young swain seems to wash","The hand was fair before! I have put you out.","But to your protestation. Let me hear","What you profess."],"id":566,"m":"What comes next? How prettily the young shepherd seems to wash a hand that was fair already! I have interrupted you. But go back to your declaration. Let me hear what you vow."},{"t":"sp","who":"FLORIZEL","lines":["Do, and be witness to 't."],"id":567,"m":"Do, and be a witness to it."},{"t":"sp","who":"POLIXENES","lines":["And this my neighbour, too?"],"id":568,"m":"And this neighbour of mine, too?"},{"t":"sp","who":"FLORIZEL","lines":["And he, and more","Than he, and men, the earth, the heavens, and all:","That were I crown'd the most imperial monarch,","Thereof most worthy, were I the fairest youth","That ever made eye swerve, had force and knowledge","More than was ever man's, I would not prize them","Without her love; for her employ them all;","Commend them and condemn them to her service,","Or to their own perdition."],"id":569,"m":"And he, and more than he, and men, the earth, the heavens and everything. If I were crowned the most powerful monarch, and most worthy of it, if I were the handsomest young man who ever turned heads, had more strength and knowledge than any man ever had, I would not value any of it without her love. I would use it all for her, giving it up to her service or to its own destruction."},{"t":"sp","who":"POLIXENES","lines":["Fairly offer'd."],"id":570,"m":"Generously offered."},{"t":"sp","who":"CAMILLO","lines":["This shows a sound affection."],"id":571,"m":"This shows true love."},{"t":"sp","who":"SHEPHERD","lines":["But my daughter,","Say you the like to him?"],"id":572,"m":"But, my daughter, do you say the same to him?"},{"t":"sp","who":"PERDITA","lines":["I cannot speak","So well, nothing so well; no, nor mean better:","By th' pattern of mine own thoughts I cut out","The purity of his."],"id":573,"m":"I cannot speak as well, nothing like as well, nor mean anything better. I measure the purity of his thoughts by the pattern of my own."},{"t":"sp","who":"SHEPHERD","lines":["Take hands, a bargain!","And, friends unknown, you shall bear witness to't.","I give my daughter to him, and will make","Her portion equal his."],"id":574,"m":"Join hands. It's a bargain! And, friends we don't know, you shall be witnesses to it. I give my daughter to him, and will make her dowry equal to his wealth."},{"t":"sp","who":"FLORIZEL","lines":["O, that must be","I' th' virtue of your daughter: one being dead,","I shall have more than you can dream of yet;","Enough then for your wonder. But come on,","Contract us 'fore these witnesses."],"id":575,"m":"O, that must be your daughter's own worth. When one man is dead, I shall have more than you can dream of yet, enough to make you wonder then. But come on, betroth us in front of these witnesses."},{"t":"sp","who":"SHEPHERD","lines":["Come, your hand;","And, daughter, yours."],"id":576,"m":"Come, your hand; and, daughter, yours."},{"t":"sp","who":"POLIXENES","lines":["Soft, swain, awhile, beseech you;","Have you a father?"],"id":577,"m":"Wait, young shepherd, a moment, please. Do you have a father?"},{"t":"sp","who":"FLORIZEL","lines":["I have; but what of him?"],"id":578,"m":"I do. But what about him?"},{"t":"sp","who":"POLIXENES","lines":["Knows he of this?"],"id":579,"m":"Does he know about this?"},{"t":"sp","who":"FLORIZEL","lines":["He neither does nor shall."],"id":580,"m":"He doesn't, and he won't."},{"t":"sp","who":"POLIXENES","lines":["Methinks a father","Is at the nuptial of his son a guest","That best becomes the table. Pray you once more,","Is not your father grown incapable","Of reasonable affairs? is he not stupid","With age and alt'ring rheums? can he speak? hear?","Know man from man? dispute his own estate?","Lies he not bed-rid? and again does nothing","But what he did being childish?"],"id":581,"m":"I think a father is the guest who best suits the table at his son's wedding. Tell me again, please: hasn't your father become unfit to deal with reasonable business? Isn't he stupid with age and the illnesses that change a man? Can he speak? Hear? Tell one man from another? Manage his own affairs? Isn't he bedridden, and doing nothing but what he did as a child?"},{"t":"sp","who":"FLORIZEL","lines":["No, good sir;","He has his health, and ampler strength indeed","Than most have of his age."],"id":582,"m":"No, good sir. He is healthy, and in fact stronger than most men his age."},{"t":"sp","who":"POLIXENES","lines":["By my white beard,","You offer him, if this be so, a wrong","Something unfilial: reason my son","Should choose himself a wife, but as good reason","The father, all whose joy is nothing else","But fair posterity, should hold some counsel","In such a business."],"id":583,"m":"By my white beard, if that's so, you are doing him a wrong that is hardly fitting for a son. It is reasonable that my son should choose his own wife, but just as reasonable that the father, whose whole joy lies in fine descendants, should have some say in such a business."},{"t":"sp","who":"FLORIZEL","lines":["I yield all this;","But for some other reasons, my grave sir,","Which 'tis not fit you know, I not acquaint","My father of this business."],"id":584,"m":"I grant all that. But for some other reasons, my serious sir, which it is not right for you to know, I am not telling my father about this business."},{"t":"sp","who":"POLIXENES","lines":["Let him know 't."],"id":585,"m":"Let him know it."},{"t":"sp","who":"FLORIZEL","lines":["He shall not."],"id":586,"m":"He shall not."},{"t":"sp","who":"POLIXENES","lines":["Prithee let him."],"id":587,"m":"Please, let him."},{"t":"sp","who":"FLORIZEL","lines":["No, he must not."],"id":588,"m":"No, he must not."},{"t":"sp","who":"SHEPHERD","lines":["Let him, my son: he shall not need to grieve","At knowing of thy choice."],"id":589,"m":"Let him, my son. He won't have any cause to grieve when he knows your choice."},{"t":"sp","who":"FLORIZEL","lines":["Come, come, he must not.","Mark our contract."],"id":590,"m":"Come, come, he must not. Witness our contract."},{"t":"sp","who":"POLIXENES","lines":["[Discovering himself] Mark your divorce, young sir,","Whom son I dare not call; thou art too base","To be acknowledged: thou a sceptre's heir,","That thus affects a sheep-hook! Thou, old traitor,","I am sorry that, by hanging thee, I can","But shorten thy life one week. And thou, fresh piece","Of excellent witchcraft, whom of force must know","The royal fool thou cop'st with,—"],"id":591,"m":"(Revealing himself) Witness your divorce, young sir, whom I dare not call my son. You are too low to be acknowledged: you, heir to a sceptre, who wants a shepherd's crook like this! You, old traitor, I am sorry that by hanging you I can only shorten your life by a week. And you, young piece of excellent witchcraft, who must have known the royal fool you were dealing with—"},{"t":"sp","who":"SHEPHERD","lines":["O, my heart!"],"id":592,"m":"O, my heart!"},{"t":"sp","who":"POLIXENES","lines":["I'll have thy beauty scratch'd with briers and made","More homely than thy state. For thee, fond boy,","If I may ever know thou dost but sigh","That thou no more shalt see this knack (as never","I mean thou shalt), we'll bar thee from succession;","Not hold thee of our blood, no, not our kin,","Far than Deucalion off. Mark thou my words.","Follow us to the court. Thou churl, for this time,","Though full of our displeasure, yet we free thee","From the dead blow of it. And you, enchantment,","Worthy enough a herdsman; yea, him too","That makes himself, but for our honour therein,","Unworthy thee. If ever henceforth thou","These rural latches to his entrance open,","Or hoop his body more with thy embraces,","I will devise a death as cruel for thee","As thou art tender to 't."],"id":593,"m":"I'll have your beauty scratched with briars and made plainer than your rank. As for you, foolish boy, if I ever find out that you so much as sigh that you will never see this trinket again (as I mean you never shall), we'll bar you from the throne and not count you of our blood, no, not even our kin, further off than Deucalion, the first man. Mark my words. Follow us to court. You peasant, this time, though you're full of our displeasure, we free you from its death blow. And you, enchantress, good enough for a herdsman, yes, even for him, who makes himself unworthy of you, if it weren't for our honour: if from now on you ever open these country latches to let him in, or wrap his body in your embraces, I will invent a death for you as cruel as you are tender."},{"t":"sd","text":"Exit.","id":594},{"t":"sp","who":"PERDITA","lines":["Even here undone.","I was not much afeard, for once or twice","I was about to speak, and tell him plainly","The selfsame sun that shines upon his court","Hides not his visage from our cottage, but","Looks on alike. [To Florizel] Will't please you, sir, be gone?","I told you what would come of this. Beseech you,","Of your own state take care. This dream of mine—","Being now awake, I'll queen it no inch farther,","But milk my ewes, and weep."],"id":595,"m":"Ruined, right here. I wasn't very frightened. Once or twice I was going to speak and tell him plainly that the same sun that shines on his court doesn't hide its face from our cottage, but looks on both alike. (To Florizel) Will you please go, sir? I told you what would come of this. I beg you, look after your own position. This dream of mine, now I'm awake, I'll play the queen not an inch further, but milk my sheep and weep."},{"t":"sp","who":"CAMILLO","lines":["Why, how now, father!","Speak ere thou diest."],"id":596,"m":"Why, what's the matter, old man? Speak before you die."},{"t":"sp","who":"SHEPHERD","lines":["I cannot speak, nor think,","Nor dare to know that which I know. O sir,","You have undone a man of fourscore three,","That thought to fill his grave in quiet; yea,","To die upon the bed my father died,","To lie close by his honest bones; but now","Some hangman must put on my shroud and lay me","Where no priest shovels in dust. O cursed wretch,","That knew'st this was the prince, and wouldst adventure","To mingle faith with him! Undone, undone!","If I might die within this hour, I have liv'd","To die when I desire."],"id":597,"m":"I can't speak, or think, or dare to know what I know. (To Florizel) O sir, you have ruined a man of eighty-three, who hoped to go to his grave in peace, yes, to die in the bed my father died in, to lie beside his honest bones. But now some hangman will dress me in my shroud and lay me where no priest shovels in earth. (To Perdita) O cursed wretch, who knew this was the prince, and dared to pledge yourself to him! Ruined, ruined! If I could die within this hour, I would have lived to die when I wanted."},{"t":"sd","text":"Exit.","id":598},{"t":"sp","who":"FLORIZEL","lines":["Why look you so upon me?","I am but sorry, not afeard; delay'd,","But nothing alt'red: what I was, I am:","More straining on for plucking back; not following","My leash unwillingly."],"id":599,"m":"Why do you look at me like that? I am only sorry, not afraid; delayed, but not changed at all. What I was, I am, straining forward all the more for being pulled back, not following my leash unwillingly."},{"t":"sp","who":"CAMILLO","lines":["Gracious my lord,","You know your father's temper: at this time","He will allow no speech (which I do guess","You do not purpose to him) and as hardly","Will he endure your sight as yet, I fear:","Then, till the fury of his highness settle,","Come not before him."],"id":600,"m":"My gracious lord, you know your father's temper. At this moment he will let you say nothing (which I guess you don't intend to anyway), and I fear he can hardly bear the sight of you yet. So until his Highness's fury settles, don't go before him."},{"t":"sp","who":"FLORIZEL","lines":["I not purpose it.","I think Camillo?"],"id":601,"m":"I don't intend to. Camillo, I think?"},{"t":"sp","who":"CAMILLO","lines":["Even he, my lord."],"id":602,"m":"The same, my lord."},{"t":"sp","who":"PERDITA","lines":["How often have I told you 'twould be thus!","How often said my dignity would last","But till 'twere known!"],"id":603,"m":"How often I told you it would be like this! How often I said my high rank would last only until it was known!"},{"t":"sp","who":"FLORIZEL","lines":["It cannot fail but by","The violation of my faith; and then","Let nature crush the sides o' th' earth together","And mar the seeds within! Lift up thy looks.","From my succession wipe me, father; I","Am heir to my affection."],"id":604,"m":"It cannot fail except by my breaking faith, and if I do, let nature crush the sides of the earth together and destroy every seed inside it! Lift up your face. Strike me out of the succession, father. I am heir to my love."},{"t":"sp","who":"CAMILLO","lines":["Be advis'd."],"id":605,"m":"Think carefully."},{"t":"sp","who":"FLORIZEL","lines":["I am, and by my fancy. If my reason","Will thereto be obedient, I have reason;","If not, my senses, better pleas'd with madness,","Do bid it welcome."],"id":606,"m":"I do, and with my love. If my reason will obey it, I have reason. If not, my senses, happier with madness, welcome it."},{"t":"sp","who":"CAMILLO","lines":["This is desperate, sir."],"id":607,"m":"This is desperate, sir."},{"t":"sp","who":"FLORIZEL","lines":["So call it: but it does fulfil my vow.","I needs must think it honesty. Camillo,","Not for Bohemia, nor the pomp that may","Be thereat glean'd; for all the sun sees or","The close earth wombs, or the profound seas hides","In unknown fathoms, will I break my oath","To this my fair belov'd. Therefore, I pray you,","As you have ever been my father's honour'd friend,","When he shall miss me,—as, in faith, I mean not","To see him any more,—cast your good counsels","Upon his passion: let myself and fortune","Tug for the time to come. This you may know,","And so deliver, I am put to sea","With her whom here I cannot hold on shore;","And, most opportune to her need, I have","A vessel rides fast by, but not prepar'd","For this design. What course I mean to hold","Shall nothing benefit your knowledge, nor","Concern me the reporting."],"id":608,"m":"Call it that. But it keeps my vow, so I must think it honesty. Camillo, not for Bohemia, nor for the splendour that could be gathered from it, nor for everything the sun sees, or the earth holds in its womb, or the deep seas hide in unknown depths, will I break my oath to my fair beloved here. So I beg you, since you have always been my father's honoured friend, when he misses me (and truly I don't mean to see him any more), calm his anger with your good advice. Let me and fortune fight it out for the future. This much you may know, and tell him: I have gone to sea with her, since I cannot keep her here on shore. And, most luckily for her need, I have a ship riding at anchor close by, though not fitted out for this purpose. Where I mean to go will do your knowledge no good, nor do I want it reported."},{"t":"sp","who":"CAMILLO","lines":["O my lord,","I would your spirit were easier for advice,","Or stronger for your need."],"id":609,"m":"O my lord, I wish your spirit were more open to advice, or stronger for what you need."},{"t":"sp","who":"FLORIZEL","lines":["Hark, Perdita. [Takes her aside]","[To Camillo] I'll hear you by and by."],"id":610,"m":"Listen, Perdita. (He takes her aside. To Camillo) I'll hear you in a moment."},{"t":"sp","who":"CAMILLO","lines":["He's irremovable,","Resolv'd for flight. Now were I happy if","His going I could frame to serve my turn,","Save him from danger, do him love and honour,","Purchase the sight again of dear Sicilia","And that unhappy king, my master, whom","I so much thirst to see."],"id":611,"m":"He can't be moved; he's determined to run away. Now I would be happy if I could shape his going to serve my purpose: save him from danger, do him love and honour, and win back the sight of dear Sicilia and that unhappy king, my master, whom I so long to see."},{"t":"sp","who":"FLORIZEL","lines":["Now, good Camillo,","I am so fraught with curious business that","I leave out ceremony."],"id":612,"m":"Now, good Camillo, I am so loaded with urgent business that I leave out the formalities."},{"t":"sp","who":"CAMILLO","lines":["Sir, I think","You have heard of my poor services, i' th' love","That I have borne your father?"],"id":613,"m":"Sir, I think you have heard of my small services, done out of the love I have for your father?"},{"t":"sp","who":"FLORIZEL","lines":["Very nobly","Have you deserv'd: it is my father's music","To speak your deeds, not little of his care","To have them recompens'd as thought on."],"id":614,"m":"You have deserved very nobly. It is music to my father to speak of your deeds, and no small concern of his to have them rewarded as well as he thinks of them."},{"t":"sp","who":"CAMILLO","lines":["Well, my lord,","If you may please to think I love the king,","And, through him, what's nearest to him, which is","Your gracious self, embrace but my direction,","If your more ponderous and settled project","May suffer alteration. On mine honour,","I'll point you where you shall have such receiving","As shall become your highness; where you may","Enjoy your mistress; from the whom, I see,","There's no disjunction to be made, but by,","As heavens forfend, your ruin. Marry her,","And with my best endeavours in your absence","Your discontenting father strive to qualify","And bring him up to liking."],"id":615,"m":"Well, my lord, if you are willing to believe that I love the king, and through him what's closest to him, which is your gracious self, just accept my direction, if your weighty, settled plan can be changed. On my honour, I'll show you where you will get a welcome fit for your Highness, where you may enjoy your love, from whom I see there's no separating you, except, heaven forbid, by your ruin. Marry her, and while you are away I'll do my best to calm your displeased father and bring him round to approving."},{"t":"sp","who":"FLORIZEL","lines":["How, Camillo,","May this, almost a miracle, be done?","That I may call thee something more than man,","And after that trust to thee."],"id":616,"m":"How, Camillo, can this near-miracle be done? Tell me, so that I can call you something more than a man, and trust you from then on."},{"t":"sp","who":"CAMILLO","lines":["Have you thought on","A place whereto you'll go?"],"id":617,"m":"Have you thought of a place to go?"},{"t":"sp","who":"FLORIZEL","lines":["Not any yet.","But as th' unthought-on accident is guilty","To what we wildly do, so we profess","Ourselves to be the slaves of chance, and flies","Of every wind that blows."],"id":618,"m":"Not yet. But since an unexpected accident is to blame for what we wildly do, we admit we are the slaves of chance, and flies blown by every wind."},{"t":"sp","who":"CAMILLO","lines":["Then list to me:","This follows, if you will not change your purpose,","But undergo this flight, make for Sicilia,","And there present yourself and your fair princess,","For so, I see, she must be, 'fore Leontes:","She shall be habited as it becomes","The partner of your bed. Methinks I see","Leontes opening his free arms and weeping","His welcomes forth; asks thee, the son, forgiveness,","As 'twere i' th' father's person; kisses the hands","Of your fresh princess; o'er and o'er divides him","'Twixt his unkindness and his kindness. Th' one","He chides to hell, and bids the other grow","Faster than thought or time."],"id":619,"m":"Then listen to me. If you won't change your plan but will go ahead with this escape, this is what follows. Sail for Sicilia, and present yourself and your fair princess (for I see that's what she must be) to Leontes. She shall be dressed as suits the partner of your bed. I think I see Leontes opening his generous arms and weeping out his welcomes, asking you, the son, for forgiveness, as if you were your father, kissing the hands of your young princess, torn again and again between his unkindness and his kindness: one he curses to hell, and the other he tells to grow faster than thought or time."},{"t":"sp","who":"FLORIZEL","lines":["Worthy Camillo,","What colour for my visitation shall I","Hold up before him?"],"id":620,"m":"Worthy Camillo, what excuse for my visit shall I give him?"},{"t":"sp","who":"CAMILLO","lines":["Sent by the king your father","To greet him and to give him comforts. Sir,","The manner of your bearing towards him, with","What you (as from your father) shall deliver,","Things known betwixt us three, I'll write you down,","The which shall point you forth at every sitting","What you must say; that he shall not perceive","But that you have your father's bosom there","And speak his very heart."],"id":621,"m":"Say you were sent by the king your father to greet him and comfort him. Sir, I'll write down for you how to behave towards him, and what you are to tell him as if from your father, things known only to the three of us. It will show you at every meeting what you must say, so that he will think you have your father's confidence there and speak his very heart."},{"t":"sp","who":"FLORIZEL","lines":["I am bound to you:","There is some sap in this."],"id":622,"m":"I am in your debt. There is some life in this."},{"t":"sp","who":"CAMILLO","lines":["A course more promising","Than a wild dedication of yourselves","To unpath'd waters, undream'd shores, most certain","To miseries enough: no hope to help you,","But as you shake off one to take another:","Nothing so certain as your anchors, who","Do their best office if they can but stay you","Where you'll be loath to be. Besides, you know","Prosperity's the very bond of love,","Whose fresh complexion and whose heart together","Affliction alters."],"id":623,"m":"A course more promising than a wild surrender of yourselves to pathless waters and undreamt-of shores, certain to bring miseries enough, with no hope of help except shaking off one trouble only to take on another, nothing so certain as your anchors, which do their best work if they can just hold you where you'll hate to be. Besides, you know that prosperity is the very bond of love, and hardship changes both its fresh face and its heart."},{"t":"sp","who":"PERDITA","lines":["One of these is true:","I think affliction may subdue the cheek,","But not take in the mind."],"id":624,"m":"One of these is true. I think hardship may conquer the cheek, but not capture the mind."},{"t":"sp","who":"CAMILLO","lines":["Yea, say you so?","There shall not at your father's house, these seven years","Be born another such."],"id":625,"m":"Yes, do you say so? There won't be another like her born in your father's house for seven years."},{"t":"sp","who":"FLORIZEL","lines":["My good Camillo,","She is as forward of her breeding as","She is i' th' rear our birth."],"id":626,"m":"My good Camillo, she is as far ahead of her upbringing as she is behind us in birth."},{"t":"sp","who":"CAMILLO","lines":["I cannot say 'tis pity","She lacks instructions, for she seems a mistress","To most that teach."],"id":627,"m":"I can't say it's a pity she lacks education, for she seems a teacher to most who teach."},{"t":"sp","who":"PERDITA","lines":["Your pardon, sir; for this","I'll blush you thanks."],"id":628,"m":"Pardon me, sir. For this I'll thank you with a blush."},{"t":"sp","who":"FLORIZEL","lines":["My prettiest Perdita!","But, O, the thorns we stand upon! Camillo,","Preserver of my father, now of me,","The medicine of our house, how shall we do?","We are not furnish'd like Bohemia's son,","Nor shall appear in Sicilia."],"id":629,"m":"My prettiest Perdita! But, O, the thorns we're standing on! Camillo, saviour of my father, now of me, the medicine of our house, what shall we do? We aren't equipped like the King of Bohemia's son, and won't look like one in Sicilia."},{"t":"sp","who":"CAMILLO","lines":["My lord,","Fear none of this. I think you know my fortunes","Do all lie there: it shall be so my care","To have you royally appointed as if","The scene you play were mine. For instance, sir,","That you may know you shall not want,—one word."],"id":630,"m":"My lord, don't worry about any of this. I think you know my fortunes all lie there. I'll take care to have you royally fitted out, as if the play you act were mine. For example, sir, so that you know you'll want for nothing, a word with you."},{"t":"sd","text":"They talk aside.","id":631},{"t":"sd","text":"Enter Autolycus.","id":632},{"t":"sp","who":"AUTOLYCUS","lines":["Ha, ha! what a fool Honesty is! and Trust, his sworn brother, a very","simple gentleman! I have sold all my trumpery. Not a counterfeit stone,","not a ribbon, glass, pomander, brooch, table-book, ballad, knife, tape,","glove, shoe-tie, bracelet, horn-ring, to keep my pack from fasting.","They throng who should buy first, as if my trinkets had been hallowed","and brought a benediction to the buyer: by which means I saw whose","purse was best in picture; and what I saw, to my good use I remembered.","My clown (who wants but something to be a reasonable man) grew so in","love with the wenches' song that he would not stir his pettitoes till","he had both tune and words; which so drew the rest of the herd to me","that all their other senses stuck in ears: you might have pinched a","placket, it was senseless; 'twas nothing to geld a codpiece of a purse;","I would have filed keys off that hung in chains: no hearing, no","feeling, but my sir's song, and admiring the nothing of it. So that in","this time of lethargy I picked and cut most of their festival purses;","and had not the old man come in with a whoobub against his daughter and","the king's son, and scared my choughs from the chaff, I had not left a","purse alive in the whole army."],"id":633,"m":"Ha, ha! What a fool Honesty is! And Trust, his sworn brother, a very simple gentleman! I have sold all my junk. Not a fake jewel, ribbon, mirror, scent ball, brooch, notebook, ballad, knife, tape, glove, shoelace, bracelet or horn ring left to keep my pack from starving. They crowded to see who could buy first, as if my trinkets were holy and brought the buyer a blessing. That way I saw whose purse looked fattest, and I remembered what I saw for my own good use. My clown (who only lacks something to be a reasonable man) fell so in love with the girls' song that he wouldn't move his little trotters till he had the tune and the words. That drew the rest of the herd to me so that all their other senses were stuck in their ears. You could have pinched a petticoat and they'd have felt nothing. It was nothing to cut a purse off a codpiece. I could have filed off keys hanging on chains. No hearing, no feeling, but my master's song, and admiring the nothing of it. So in this sleepy time I picked and cut most of their festival purses. And if the old man hadn't come in with a hubbub against his daughter and the king's son and scared my crows off the grain, I wouldn't have left a purse alive in the whole army."},{"t":"sd","text":"Camillo, Florizel and Perdita come forward.","id":634},{"t":"sp","who":"CAMILLO","lines":["Nay, but my letters, by this means being there","So soon as you arrive, shall clear that doubt."],"id":635,"m":"No, but my letters, getting there by this means as soon as you arrive, will clear up that doubt."},{"t":"sp","who":"FLORIZEL","lines":["And those that you'll procure from king Leontes?"],"id":636,"m":"And those you'll get from King Leontes?"},{"t":"sp","who":"CAMILLO","lines":["Shall satisfy your father."],"id":637,"m":"Will satisfy your father."},{"t":"sp","who":"PERDITA","lines":["Happy be you!","All that you speak shows fair."],"id":638,"m":"Bless you! Everything you say looks hopeful."},{"t":"sp","who":"CAMILLO","lines":["[Seeing Autolycus] Who have we here?","We'll make an instrument of this; omit","Nothing may give us aid."],"id":639,"m":"(Seeing Autolycus) Who have we here? We'll make use of him. We mustn't leave out anything that may help us."},{"t":"sp","who":"AUTOLYCUS","lines":["[Aside] If they have overheard me now,—why, hanging."],"id":640,"m":"(Aside) If they've overheard me now, why, it's hanging."},{"t":"sp","who":"CAMILLO","lines":["How now, good fellow! why shakest thou so? Fear not, man; here's no","harm intended to thee."],"id":641,"m":"Well, good fellow! Why are you shaking? Don't be afraid, man. Nobody means you any harm."},{"t":"sp","who":"AUTOLYCUS","lines":["I am a poor fellow, sir."],"id":642,"m":"I am a poor fellow, sir."},{"t":"sp","who":"CAMILLO","lines":["Why, be so still; here's nobody will steal that from thee: yet, for the","outside of thy poverty we must make an exchange; therefore discase thee","instantly,—thou must think there's a necessity in't—and change garments","with this gentleman: though the pennyworth on his side be the worst,","yet hold thee, there's some boot."],"id":643,"m":"Well, stay that way. Nobody here will steal that from you. Still, we must swap the outside of your poverty. So undress at once (you must believe it's necessary) and change clothes with this gentleman. Though he gets the worse bargain, still, here, take this; there's something extra."},{"t":"sd","text":"Giving money.","id":644},{"t":"sp","who":"AUTOLYCUS","lines":["I am a poor fellow, sir: [Aside] I know ye well enough."],"id":645,"m":"I am a poor fellow, sir. (Aside) I know you well enough."},{"t":"sp","who":"CAMILLO","lines":["Nay, prithee dispatch: the gentleman is half flayed already."],"id":646,"m":"No, please, hurry. The gentleman is half skinned already."},{"t":"sp","who":"AUTOLYCUS","lines":["Are you in earnest, sir? [Aside] I smell the trick on't."],"id":647,"m":"Are you in earnest, sir? (Aside) I smell a trick in this."},{"t":"sp","who":"FLORIZEL","lines":["Dispatch, I prithee."],"id":648,"m":"Hurry, please."},{"t":"sp","who":"AUTOLYCUS","lines":["Indeed, I have had earnest; but I cannot with conscience take it."],"id":649,"m":"Indeed, I've had earnest money, but I can't in conscience take it."},{"t":"sp","who":"CAMILLO","lines":["Unbuckle, unbuckle."],"id":650,"m":"Unbuckle, unbuckle."},{"t":"sd","text":"Florizel and Autolycus exchange garments.","id":651},{"t":"sp","who":"CAMILLO","cont":1,"lines":["Fortunate mistress,—let my prophecy","Come home to you!—you must retire yourself","Into some covert. Take your sweetheart's hat","And pluck it o'er your brows, muffle your face,","Dismantle you; and, as you can, disliken","The truth of your own seeming; that you may","(For I do fear eyes over) to shipboard","Get undescried."],"id":652,"m":"Lucky lady (may my prophecy come true for you!), you must go off into some hiding place. Take your sweetheart's hat and pull it down over your brows, muffle your face, change your clothes, and as best you can disguise your true appearance, so that (for I fear there are watchers) you can get on board unseen."},{"t":"sp","who":"PERDITA","lines":["I see the play so lies","That I must bear a part."],"id":653,"m":"I see the play is set up so that I must take a part."},{"t":"sp","who":"CAMILLO","lines":["No remedy.","Have you done there?"],"id":654,"m":"It can't be helped. Have you finished there?"},{"t":"sp","who":"FLORIZEL","lines":["Should I now meet my father,","He would not call me son."],"id":655,"m":"If I met my father now, he wouldn't call me his son."},{"t":"sp","who":"CAMILLO","lines":["Nay, you shall have no hat. [Giving it to Perdita]","Come, lady, come. Farewell, my friend."],"id":656,"m":"No, you shall have no hat. (He gives it to Perdita) Come, lady, come. Farewell, my friend."},{"t":"sp","who":"AUTOLYCUS","lines":["Adieu, sir."],"id":657,"m":"Goodbye, sir."},{"t":"sp","who":"FLORIZEL","lines":["O Perdita, what have we twain forgot?","Pray you a word."],"id":658,"m":"O Perdita, what have we two forgotten? A word, please."},{"t":"sd","text":"They converse apart.","id":659},{"t":"sp","who":"CAMILLO","lines":["[Aside] What I do next, shall be to tell the king","Of this escape, and whither they are bound;","Wherein my hope is I shall so prevail","To force him after: in whose company","I shall re-view Sicilia; for whose sight","I have a woman's longing."],"id":660,"m":"(Aside) What I'll do next is tell the king about this escape and where they're going. I hope I'll persuade him to follow them, and in his company I'll see Sicilia again, which I long to see like a pregnant woman longs for food."},{"t":"sp","who":"FLORIZEL","lines":["Fortune speed us!","Thus we set on, Camillo, to the sea-side."],"id":661,"m":"Fortune speed us! So we set off for the seaside, Camillo."},{"t":"sp","who":"CAMILLO","lines":["The swifter speed the better."],"id":662,"m":"The faster the better."},{"t":"sd","text":"Exeunt Florizel, Perdita and Camillo.","id":663},{"t":"sp","who":"AUTOLYCUS","lines":["I understand the business, I hear it. To have an open ear, a quick eye,","and a nimble hand, is necessary for a cut-purse; a good nose is","requisite also, to smell out work for the other senses. I see this is","the time that the unjust man doth thrive. What an exchange had this","been without boot! What a boot is here with this exchange! Sure the","gods do this year connive at us, and we may do anything extempore. The","prince himself is about a piece of iniquity, stealing away from his","father with his clog at his heels: if I thought it were a piece of","honesty to acquaint the king withal, I would not do't: I hold it the","more knavery to conceal it; and therein am I constant to my profession."],"id":664,"m":"I understand the business. I heard it. An open ear, a quick eye and a nimble hand are necessary for a pickpocket. A good nose is needed too, to sniff out work for the other senses. I see this is the time when the dishonest man prospers. What a swap this would have been even without anything extra! And what an extra there is with this swap! The gods must be winking at us this year, and we can do anything on the spur of the moment. The prince himself is up to some wickedness, stealing away from his father with his ball and chain at his heels. If I thought it would be honest to tell the king, I wouldn't do it. I consider it more knavish to keep it quiet, and in that I'm true to my profession."},{"t":"sd","text":"Enter Clown and Shepherd.","id":665},{"t":"sp","who":"AUTOLYCUS","cont":1,"lines":["Aside, aside; here is more matter for a hot brain: every lane's end,","every shop, church, session, hanging, yields a careful man work."],"id":666,"m":"Aside, aside. Here's more work for a busy brain. Every lane's end, every shop, church, court session and hanging gives a careful man work."},{"t":"sp","who":"CLOWN","lines":["See, see; what a man you are now! There is no other way but to tell the","king she's a changeling, and none of your flesh and blood."],"id":667,"m":"Look, look, what a man you are now! There's nothing for it but to tell the king she's a changeling and not your flesh and blood."},{"t":"sp","who":"SHEPHERD","lines":["Nay, but hear me."],"id":668,"m":"No, but listen to me."},{"t":"sp","who":"CLOWN","lines":["Nay, but hear me."],"id":669,"m":"No, but listen to me."},{"t":"sp","who":"SHEPHERD","lines":["Go to, then."],"id":670,"m":"Go on, then."},{"t":"sp","who":"CLOWN","lines":["She being none of your flesh and blood, your flesh and blood has not","offended the king; and so your flesh and blood is not to be punished by","him. Show those things you found about her, those secret things, all","but what she has with her: this being done, let the law go whistle, I","warrant you."],"id":671,"m":"Since she's not your flesh and blood, your flesh and blood hasn't offended the king, so your flesh and blood can't be punished by him. Show him those things you found with her, those secret things, all except what she has with her. Do that, and the law can whistle for you, I promise."},{"t":"sp","who":"SHEPHERD","lines":["I will tell the king all, every word, yea, and his son's pranks too;","who, I may say, is no honest man neither to his father nor to me, to go","about to make me the king's brother-in-law."],"id":672,"m":"I'll tell the king everything, every word, yes, and his son's pranks too. I can say he's been no honest man to his father or to me, trying to make me the king's brother-in-law."},{"t":"sp","who":"CLOWN","lines":["Indeed, brother-in-law was the farthest off you could have been to him,","and then your blood had been the dearer by I know how much an ounce."],"id":673,"m":"Indeed, brother-in-law was the furthest off you could have been from him, and then your blood would have been worth I don't know how much more an ounce."},{"t":"sp","who":"AUTOLYCUS","lines":["[Aside] Very wisely, puppies!"],"id":674,"m":"(Aside) Very wise, you puppies!"},{"t":"sp","who":"SHEPHERD","lines":["Well, let us to the king: there is that in this fardel will make him","scratch his beard."],"id":675,"m":"Well, let's go to the king. There's something in this bundle that will make him scratch his beard."},{"t":"sp","who":"AUTOLYCUS","lines":["[Aside] I know not what impediment this complaint may be to the","flight of my master."],"id":676,"m":"(Aside) I don't know how much this complaint may hinder my master's escape."},{"t":"sp","who":"CLOWN","lines":["Pray heartily he be at' palace."],"id":677,"m":"Pray hard that he's at the palace."},{"t":"sp","who":"AUTOLYCUS","lines":["[Aside] Though I am not naturally honest, I am so sometimes by","chance. Let me pocket up my pedlar's excrement. [Takes off his false","beard.] How now, rustics! whither are you bound?"],"id":678,"m":"(Aside) Though I'm not honest by nature, I am sometimes by chance. Let me pocket my pedlar's beard. (He takes off his false beard) Well now, peasants! Where are you going?"},{"t":"sp","who":"SHEPHERD","lines":["To the palace, an it like your worship."],"id":679,"m":"To the palace, if it please your worship."},{"t":"sp","who":"AUTOLYCUS","lines":["Your affairs there, what, with whom, the condition of that fardel, the","place of your dwelling, your names, your ages, of what having,","breeding, and anything that is fitting to be known? discover!"],"id":680,"m":"Your business there: what, with whom, what's in that bundle, where you live, your names, your ages, your wealth, your upbringing, and anything else fit to be known? Reveal it!"},{"t":"sp","who":"CLOWN","lines":["We are but plain fellows, sir."],"id":681,"m":"We're only plain fellows, sir."},{"t":"sp","who":"AUTOLYCUS","lines":["A lie; you are rough and hairy. Let me have no lying. It becomes none","but tradesmen, and they often give us soldiers the lie; but we pay them","for it with stamped coin, not stabbing steel; therefore they do not","give us the lie."],"id":682,"m":"A lie: you're rough and hairy. Let me have no lying. It only suits tradesmen, and they often give us soldiers the lie. But we pay them for it in stamped coin, not stabbing steel, so they don't give it us, they sell it."},{"t":"sp","who":"CLOWN","lines":["Your worship had like to have given us one, if you had not taken","yourself with the manner."],"id":683,"m":"Your worship nearly gave us one yourself, if you hadn't caught yourself in the act."},{"t":"sp","who":"SHEPHERD","lines":["Are you a courtier, an 't like you, sir?"],"id":684,"m":"Are you a courtier, if you please, sir?"},{"t":"sp","who":"AUTOLYCUS","lines":["Whether it like me or no, I am a courtier. Seest thou not the air of","the court in these enfoldings? hath not my gait in it the measure of","the court? receives not thy nose court-odour from me? reflect I not on","thy baseness court-contempt? Think'st thou, for that I insinuate, or","toaze from thee thy business, I am therefore no courtier? I am courtier","cap-a-pe, and one that will either push on or pluck back thy business","there. Whereupon I command thee to open thy affair."],"id":685,"m":"Whether it pleases me or not, I am a courtier. Don't you see the air of the court in these clothes? Doesn't my walk have the rhythm of the court? Doesn't your nose catch the court smell from me? Don't I reflect court contempt on your lowliness? Do you think that because I worm my way in and tease your business out of you, I'm no courtier? I'm a courtier from head to foot, and one who can push your business on there or pull it back. So I command you to tell me your business."},{"t":"sp","who":"SHEPHERD","lines":["My business, sir, is to the king."],"id":686,"m":"My business, sir, is with the king."},{"t":"sp","who":"AUTOLYCUS","lines":["What advocate hast thou to him?"],"id":687,"m":"Who is your advocate with him?"},{"t":"sp","who":"SHEPHERD","lines":["I know not, an 't like you."],"id":688,"m":"I don't know, if you please."},{"t":"sp","who":"CLOWN","lines":["Advocate's the court-word for a pheasant. Say you have none."],"id":689,"m":"'Advocate' is the court word for a pheasant. Say you haven't one."},{"t":"sp","who":"SHEPHERD","lines":["None, sir; I have no pheasant, cock nor hen."],"id":690,"m":"None, sir. I have no pheasant, cock or hen."},{"t":"sp","who":"AUTOLYCUS","lines":["How bless'd are we that are not simple men!","Yet nature might have made me as these are,","Therefore I will not disdain."],"id":691,"m":"How blessed we are who are not simple men! Yet nature might have made me like them, so I won't scorn them."},{"t":"sp","who":"CLOWN","lines":["This cannot be but a great courtier."],"id":692,"m":"He must be a great courtier."},{"t":"sp","who":"SHEPHERD","lines":["His garments are rich, but he wears them not handsomely."],"id":693,"m":"His clothes are rich, but he doesn't wear them well."},{"t":"sp","who":"CLOWN","lines":["He seems to be the more noble in being fantastical: a great man, I'll","warrant; I know by the picking on's teeth."],"id":694,"m":"He seems all the more noble for being odd. A great man, I'll bet. I can tell by the way he picks his teeth."},{"t":"sp","who":"AUTOLYCUS","lines":["The fardel there? What's i' th' fardel? Wherefore that box?"],"id":695,"m":"That bundle there? What's in the bundle? Why that box?"},{"t":"sp","who":"SHEPHERD","lines":["Sir, there lies such secrets in this fardel and box which none must","know but the king; and which he shall know within this hour, if I may","come to th' speech of him."],"id":696,"m":"Sir, there are secrets in this bundle and box that no one must know but the king, and he'll know them within the hour, if I can get to speak to him."},{"t":"sp","who":"AUTOLYCUS","lines":["Age, thou hast lost thy labour."],"id":697,"m":"Old man, you've wasted your effort."},{"t":"sp","who":"SHEPHERD","lines":["Why, sir?"],"id":698,"m":"Why, sir?"},{"t":"sp","who":"AUTOLYCUS","lines":["The king is not at the palace; he is gone aboard a new ship to purge","melancholy and air himself: for, if thou beest capable of things","serious, thou must know the king is full of grief."],"id":699,"m":"The king isn't at the palace. He has gone aboard a new ship to clear his melancholy and get some air. For, if you can understand serious things, you must know the king is full of grief."},{"t":"sp","who":"SHEPHERD","lines":["So 'tis said, sir; about his son, that should have married a shepherd's","daughter."],"id":700,"m":"So they say, sir, about his son, who was going to marry a shepherd's daughter."},{"t":"sp","who":"AUTOLYCUS","lines":["If that shepherd be not in hand-fast, let him fly. The curses he shall","have, the tortures he shall feel, will break the back of man, the heart","of monster."],"id":701,"m":"If that shepherd isn't already under arrest, let him run. The curses he'll get and the tortures he'll feel would break a man's back and a monster's heart."},{"t":"sp","who":"CLOWN","lines":["Think you so, sir?"],"id":702,"m":"Do you think so, sir?"},{"t":"sp","who":"AUTOLYCUS","lines":["Not he alone shall suffer what wit can make heavy and vengeance bitter;","but those that are germane to him, though removed fifty times, shall","all come under the hangman: which, though it be great pity, yet it is","necessary. An old sheep-whistling rogue, a ram-tender, to offer to have","his daughter come into grace! Some say he shall be stoned; but that","death is too soft for him, say I. Draw our throne into a sheepcote! All","deaths are too few, the sharpest too easy."],"id":703,"m":"Not only will he suffer everything that wit can make heavy and vengeance bitter, but all his relatives, even fifty times removed, will come to the hangman. It's a great pity, but necessary. An old sheep-whistling rogue, a ram-minder, trying to get his daughter raised to royalty! Some say he'll be stoned, but I say that death is too soft for him. Dragging our throne into a sheep pen! All deaths are too few, the sharpest too easy."},{"t":"sp","who":"CLOWN","lines":["Has the old man e'er a son, sir, do you hear, an 't like you, sir?"],"id":704,"m":"Has the old man got a son, sir, do you hear, if you please, sir?"},{"t":"sp","who":"AUTOLYCUS","lines":["He has a son, who shall be flayed alive; then 'nointed over with honey,","set on the head of a wasp's nest; then stand till he be three quarters","and a dram dead; then recovered again with aqua-vitæ or some other hot","infusion; then, raw as he is, and in the hottest day prognostication","proclaims, shall he be set against a brick wall, the sun looking with a","southward eye upon him, where he is to behold him with flies blown to","death. But what talk we of these traitorly rascals, whose miseries are","to be smiled at, their offences being so capital? Tell me (for you seem","to be honest plain men) what you have to the king. Being something","gently considered, I'll bring you where he is aboard, tender your","persons to his presence, whisper him in your behalfs; and if it be in","man besides the king to effect your suits, here is man shall do it."],"id":705,"m":"He has a son, who'll be skinned alive, then smeared with honey and set on top of a wasps' nest, then left till he's three-quarters and a bit dead, then revived with brandy or some other hot drink. Then, raw as he is, on the hottest day the almanac forecasts, he'll be set against a brick wall with the sun looking down on him from the south, and stay there till the flies have stung him to death. But why are we talking about these traitorous rascals, whose miseries we should smile at, since their crimes are so great? Tell me (for you seem to be honest plain men) what your business with the king is. If I'm rewarded handsomely, I'll take you to where he is on board, present you to him, put in a word for you in his ear, and if any man besides the king can get your requests granted, here is the man to do it."},{"t":"sp","who":"CLOWN","lines":["He seems to be of great authority: close with him, give him gold; and","though authority be a stubborn bear, yet he is oft led by the nose with","gold: show the inside of your purse to the outside of his hand, and no","more ado. Remember: \"ston'd\" and \"flayed alive\"."],"id":706,"m":"He seems to have great authority. Make a deal with him, give him gold. Though authority is a stubborn bear, it's often led by the nose with gold. Show the inside of your purse to the outside of his hand, and no more fuss. Remember: 'stoned' and 'skinned alive'."},{"t":"sp","who":"SHEPHERD","lines":["An 't please you, sir, to undertake the business for us, here is that","gold I have. I'll make it as much more, and leave this young man in","pawn till I bring it you."],"id":707,"m":"If you'll take on the business for us, sir, here's the gold I have. I'll make it as much again, and leave this young man as security until I bring it to you."},{"t":"sp","who":"AUTOLYCUS","lines":["After I have done what I promised?"],"id":708,"m":"After I've done what I promised?"},{"t":"sp","who":"SHEPHERD","lines":["Ay, sir."],"id":709,"m":"Yes, sir."},{"t":"sp","who":"AUTOLYCUS","lines":["Well, give me the moiety. Are you a party in this business?"],"id":710,"m":"Well, give me half. (To the Clown) Are you involved in this business?"},{"t":"sp","who":"CLOWN","lines":["In some sort, sir: but though my case be a pitiful one, I hope I shall","not be flayed out of it."],"id":711,"m":"In a way, sir. But though my case is a pitiful one, I hope I won't be skinned out of it."},{"t":"sp","who":"AUTOLYCUS","lines":["O, that's the case of the shepherd's son. Hang him, he'll be made an","example."],"id":712,"m":"O, that's the case of the shepherd's son. Hang him, he'll be made an example of."},{"t":"sp","who":"CLOWN","lines":["Comfort, good comfort! We must to the king and show our strange sights.","He must know 'tis none of your daughter nor my sister; we are gone","else. Sir, I will give you as much as this old man does when the","business is performed, and remain, as he says, your pawn till it be","brought you."],"id":713,"m":"Comfort, some comfort! We must go to the king and show our strange things. He must know she's not your daughter or my sister, or we're done for. Sir, I'll give you as much as this old man when the business is done, and stay, as he says, as your security till it's brought to you."},{"t":"sp","who":"AUTOLYCUS","lines":["I will trust you. Walk before toward the sea-side; go on the","right-hand. I will but look upon the hedge, and follow you."],"id":714,"m":"I'll trust you. Walk on ahead towards the seaside. Keep to the right. I'll just take a look at the hedge and follow you."},{"t":"sp","who":"CLOWN","lines":["We are blessed in this man, as I may say, even blessed."],"id":715,"m":"We're blessed in this man, I must say, really blessed."},{"t":"sp","who":"SHEPHERD","lines":["Let's before, as he bids us. He was provided to do us good."],"id":716,"m":"Let's go ahead, as he tells us. He was sent to do us good."},{"t":"sd","text":"Exeunt Shepherd and Clown.","id":717},{"t":"sp","who":"AUTOLYCUS","lines":["If I had a mind to be honest, I see Fortune would not suffer me: she","drops booties in my mouth. I am courted now with a double occasion:","gold, and a means to do the prince my master good; which who knows how","that may turn back to my advancement? I will bring these two moles,","these blind ones, aboard him. If he think it fit to shore them again","and that the complaint they have to the king concerns him nothing, let","him call me rogue for being so far officious; for I am proof against","that title and what shame else belongs to 't. To him will I present","them. There may be matter in it."],"id":718,"m":"If I wanted to be honest, I see Fortune wouldn't let me. She drops loot into my mouth. I'm being courted now with a double chance: gold, and a way to do my master the prince a good turn, which, who knows, may come back to me as promotion. I'll bring these two moles, these blind ones, aboard to him. If he thinks fit to put them ashore again, and the complaint they have for the king doesn't concern him, let him call me a rogue for being so meddlesome. I'm hardened against that title and any other shame that goes with it. I'll present them to him. There may be something in it."},{"t":"sd","text":"Exit.","id":719},{"t":"scene","act":5,"scene":"I","loc":"Sicilia. A Room in the palace of Leontes.","id":720},{"t":"sd","text":"Enter Leontes, Cleomenes, Dion, Paulina and others.","id":721},{"t":"sp","who":"CLEOMENES","lines":["Sir, you have done enough, and have perform'd","A saint-like sorrow: no fault could you make","Which you have not redeem'd; indeed, paid down","More penitence than done trespass: at the last,","Do as the heavens have done, forget your evil;","With them, forgive yourself."],"id":722,"m":"Sir, you have done enough, and shown a saint's sorrow. You could have committed no fault that you have not made up for. In fact, you have paid more penance than the wrong you did. At last, do as the heavens have done: forget your sin, and like them, forgive yourself."},{"t":"sp","who":"LEONTES","lines":["Whilst I remember","Her and her virtues, I cannot forget","My blemishes in them; and so still think of","The wrong I did myself: which was so much","That heirless it hath made my kingdom, and","Destroy'd the sweet'st companion that e'er man","Bred his hopes out of."],"id":723,"m":"While I remember her and her virtues, I cannot forget my faults towards them, and so I still think of the wrong I did myself, which was so great that it has left my kingdom without an heir and destroyed the sweetest companion a man ever placed his hopes in."},{"t":"sp","who":"PAULINA","lines":["True, too true, my lord.","If, one by one, you wedded all the world,","Or from the all that are took something good,","To make a perfect woman, she you kill'd","Would be unparallel'd."],"id":724,"m":"True, too true, my lord. If you married every woman in the world one by one, or took something good from every one there is to make a perfect woman, the one you killed would still have no equal."},{"t":"sp","who":"LEONTES","lines":["I think so. Kill'd!","She I kill'd! I did so: but thou strik'st me","Sorely, to say I did: it is as bitter","Upon thy tongue as in my thought. Now, good now,","Say so but seldom."],"id":725,"m":"I think so. Killed! She whom I killed! I did. But you strike me hard to say I did. It is as bitter on your tongue as in my thoughts. Now, please, say it only rarely."},{"t":"sp","who":"CLEOMENES","lines":["Not at all, good lady.","You might have spoken a thousand things that would","Have done the time more benefit and grac'd","Your kindness better."],"id":726,"m":"Not at all, good lady. You could have said a thousand things that would have done more good at this time and better suited your kindness."},{"t":"sp","who":"PAULINA","lines":["You are one of those","Would have him wed again."],"id":727,"m":"You're one of those who would have him marry again."},{"t":"sp","who":"DION","lines":["If you would not so,","You pity not the state, nor the remembrance","Of his most sovereign name; consider little","What dangers, by his highness' fail of issue,","May drop upon his kingdom, and devour","Incertain lookers-on. What were more holy","Than to rejoice the former queen is well?","What holier than, for royalty's repair,","For present comfort, and for future good,","To bless the bed of majesty again","With a sweet fellow to 't?"],"id":728,"m":"If you don't want that, you have no pity for the state, nor for the memory of his royal name. You don't consider what dangers may fall on his kingdom because his Highness has no heir, and devour the people standing by uncertain. What could be more holy than to rejoice that the former queen is at peace? What holier, for the restoring of royalty, for present comfort and future good, than to bless the king's bed again with a sweet partner?"},{"t":"sp","who":"PAULINA","lines":["There is none worthy,","Respecting her that's gone. Besides, the gods","Will have fulfill'd their secret purposes;","For has not the divine Apollo said,","Is 't not the tenor of his oracle,","That king Leontes shall not have an heir","Till his lost child be found? Which that it shall,","Is all as monstrous to our human reason","As my Antigonus to break his grave","And come again to me; who, on my life,","Did perish with the infant. 'Tis your counsel","My lord should to the heavens be contrary,","Oppose against their wills. [To Leontes] Care not for issue;","The crown will find an heir. Great Alexander","Left his to th' worthiest; so his successor","Was like to be the best."],"id":729,"m":"There is no one worthy, compared with her who's gone. Besides, the gods will have their secret purposes fulfilled. For hasn't the divine Apollo said, isn't it the meaning of his oracle, that King Leontes shall not have an heir till his lost child is found? And that that will happen is as monstrous to our human reason as my Antigonus breaking out of his grave and coming back to me, who, on my life, died with the baby. It is your advice that my lord should go against the heavens and oppose their will. (To Leontes) Don't worry about an heir. The crown will find one. Great Alexander left his to the worthiest, so his successor was likely to be the best."},{"t":"sp","who":"LEONTES","lines":["Good Paulina,","Who hast the memory of Hermione,","I know, in honour, O that ever I","Had squar'd me to thy counsel! Then, even now,","I might have look'd upon my queen's full eyes,","Have taken treasure from her lips,—"],"id":730,"m":"Good Paulina, who I know hold the memory of Hermione in honour, O, if only I had ruled myself by your advice! Then, even now, I might be looking into my queen's full eyes, taking treasure from her lips—"},{"t":"sp","who":"PAULINA","lines":["And left them","More rich for what they yielded."],"id":731,"m":"And leaving them richer for what they gave."},{"t":"sp","who":"LEONTES","lines":["Thou speak'st truth.","No more such wives; therefore, no wife: one worse,","And better us'd, would make her sainted spirit","Again possess her corpse, and on this stage,","(Where we offenders now appear) soul-vexed,","And begin \"Why to me?\""],"id":732,"m":"You speak the truth. No more such wives, so no wife at all. One worse, and treated better, would make her saintly spirit take over her body again, and on this stage where we sinners now stand, come troubled in soul, and begin 'Why to me?'"},{"t":"sp","who":"PAULINA","lines":["Had she such power,","She had just cause."],"id":733,"m":"If she had such power, she would have good reason."},{"t":"sp","who":"LEONTES","lines":["She had; and would incense me","To murder her I married."],"id":734,"m":"She would, and would urge me to murder the woman I married."},{"t":"sp","who":"PAULINA","lines":["I should so.","Were I the ghost that walk'd, I'd bid you mark","Her eye, and tell me for what dull part in 't","You chose her: then I'd shriek, that even your ears","Should rift to hear me; and the words that follow'd","Should be \"Remember mine.\""],"id":735,"m":"I would. If I were the ghost that walked, I'd tell you to look at her eye and tell me what dull feature in it made you choose her. Then I'd shriek so that even your ears would split to hear me, and the words that followed would be 'Remember mine.'"},{"t":"sp","who":"LEONTES","lines":["Stars, stars,","And all eyes else dead coals! Fear thou no wife;","I'll have no wife, Paulina."],"id":736,"m":"Stars, stars, and all other eyes dead coals! Don't fear a wife. I'll have no wife, Paulina."},{"t":"sp","who":"PAULINA","lines":["Will you swear","Never to marry but by my free leave?"],"id":737,"m":"Will you swear never to marry except with my free permission?"},{"t":"sp","who":"LEONTES","lines":["Never, Paulina; so be bless'd my spirit!"],"id":738,"m":"Never, Paulina, as I hope my soul is blessed!"},{"t":"sp","who":"PAULINA","lines":["Then, good my lords, bear witness to his oath."],"id":739,"m":"Then, my good lords, witness his oath."},{"t":"sp","who":"CLEOMENES","lines":["You tempt him over-much."],"id":740,"m":"You push him too hard."},{"t":"sp","who":"PAULINA","lines":["Unless another,","As like Hermione as is her picture,","Affront his eye."],"id":741,"m":"Unless another woman, as like Hermione as her portrait, comes before his eyes."},{"t":"sp","who":"CLEOMENES","lines":["Good madam,—"],"id":742,"m":"Good madam—"},{"t":"sp","who":"PAULINA","lines":["I have done.","Yet, if my lord will marry,—if you will, sir,","No remedy but you will,—give me the office","To choose you a queen: she shall not be so young","As was your former, but she shall be such","As, walk'd your first queen's ghost, it should take joy","To see her in your arms."],"id":743,"m":"I've finished. Yet if my lord will marry (if you will, sir, and it can't be helped), give me the job of choosing you a queen. She won't be as young as your first, but she will be such that, if your first queen's ghost walked, it would be glad to see her in your arms."},{"t":"sp","who":"LEONTES","lines":["My true Paulina,","We shall not marry till thou bid'st us."],"id":744,"m":"My true Paulina, we will not marry until you tell us to."},{"t":"sp","who":"PAULINA","lines":["That","Shall be when your first queen's again in breath;","Never till then."],"id":745,"m":"That shall be when your first queen breathes again. Never till then."},{"t":"sd","text":"Enter a Servant.","id":746},{"t":"sp","who":"SERVANT","lines":["One that gives out himself Prince Florizel,","Son of Polixenes, with his princess (she","The fairest I have yet beheld) desires access","To your high presence."],"id":747,"m":"Someone who says he is Prince Florizel, son of Polixenes, with his princess (the most beautiful woman I have ever seen), asks to come before you."},{"t":"sp","who":"LEONTES","lines":["What with him? he comes not","Like to his father's greatness: his approach,","So out of circumstance and sudden, tells us","'Tis not a visitation fram'd, but forc'd","By need and accident. What train?"],"id":748,"m":"Who's with him? He doesn't come in a way that matches his father's greatness. His arrival, so sudden and without ceremony, tells us it is not a planned visit, but forced on him by need and accident. What followers?"},{"t":"sp","who":"SERVANT","lines":["But few,","And those but mean."],"id":749,"m":"Only a few, and those of low rank."},{"t":"sp","who":"LEONTES","lines":["His princess, say you, with him?"],"id":750,"m":"His princess is with him, you say?"},{"t":"sp","who":"SERVANT","lines":["Ay, the most peerless piece of earth, I think,","That e'er the sun shone bright on."],"id":751,"m":"Yes, the most matchless piece of earth, I think, that the sun ever shone on."},{"t":"sp","who":"PAULINA","lines":["O Hermione,","As every present time doth boast itself","Above a better gone, so must thy grave","Give way to what's seen now! Sir, you yourself","Have said and writ so,—but your writing now","Is colder than that theme,—'She had not been,","Nor was not to be equall'd'; thus your verse","Flow'd with her beauty once; 'tis shrewdly ebb'd,","To say you have seen a better."],"id":752,"m":"O Hermione, just as every present time boasts that it is better than a better time gone by, so your grave must give way to what is seen now! (To the Servant) Sir, you yourself have said and written (but your writing is now colder than its subject) 'She has not been and will not be equalled.' That's how your verse once flowed with her beauty. It has ebbed badly, if you say you've seen a better."},{"t":"sp","who":"SERVANT","lines":["Pardon, madam:","The one I have almost forgot,—your pardon;—","The other, when she has obtain'd your eye,","Will have your tongue too. This is a creature,","Would she begin a sect, might quench the zeal","Of all professors else; make proselytes","Of who she but bid follow."],"id":753,"m":"Pardon, madam. The one I have almost forgotten (pardon me). The other, once she has caught your eye, will have your tongue too. She is a creature who, if she started a religion, could put out the zeal of every other believer and convert anyone she merely asked to follow."},{"t":"sp","who":"PAULINA","lines":["How! not women?"],"id":754,"m":"What! Not women?"},{"t":"sp","who":"SERVANT","lines":["Women will love her that she is a woman","More worth than any man; men, that she is","The rarest of all women."],"id":755,"m":"Women will love her because she is a woman worth more than any man; men, because she is the rarest of all women."},{"t":"sp","who":"LEONTES","lines":["Go, Cleomenes;","Yourself, assisted with your honour'd friends,","Bring them to our embracement."],"id":756,"m":"Go, Cleomenes. You yourself, helped by your honoured friends, bring them to our embrace."},{"t":"sd","text":"Exeunt Cleomenes and others.","id":757},{"t":"sp","who":"LEONTES","cont":1,"lines":["Still, 'tis strange","He thus should steal upon us."],"id":758,"m":"Still, it's strange he should steal in on us like this."},{"t":"sp","who":"PAULINA","lines":["Had our prince,","Jewel of children, seen this hour, he had pair'd","Well with this lord. There was not full a month","Between their births."],"id":759,"m":"If our prince, the jewel of children, had lived to see this hour, he would have made a good pair with this lord. There wasn't a full month between their births."},{"t":"sp","who":"LEONTES","lines":["Prithee no more; cease; Thou know'st","He dies to me again when talk'd of: sure,","When I shall see this gentleman, thy speeches","Will bring me to consider that which may","Unfurnish me of reason. They are come."],"id":760,"m":"Please, no more. Stop. You know he dies again for me when he is talked about. Surely, when I see this gentleman, your words will make me think of things that may rob me of my reason. They have come."},{"t":"sd","text":"Enter Florizel, Perdita, Cleomenes and others.","id":761},{"t":"sp","who":"LEONTES","cont":1,"lines":["Your mother was most true to wedlock, prince;","For she did print your royal father off,","Conceiving you. Were I but twenty-one,","Your father's image is so hit in you,","His very air, that I should call you brother,","As I did him, and speak of something wildly","By us perform'd before. Most dearly welcome!","And your fair princess,—goddess! O, alas!","I lost a couple that 'twixt heaven and earth","Might thus have stood, begetting wonder, as","You, gracious couple, do! And then I lost,—","All mine own folly,—the society,","Amity too, of your brave father, whom,","Though bearing misery, I desire my life","Once more to look on him."],"id":762,"m":"Your mother was very faithful to her marriage, prince, for she printed off a copy of your royal father when she conceived you. If I were only twenty-one, your father's image is so exactly caught in you, his very manner, that I would call you brother, as I did him, and talk of some wild things we did together before. You are most dearly welcome! And your fair princess, a goddess! O, alas! I lost a couple who might have stood like this between heaven and earth, causing wonder, as you do, gracious pair! And then I lost, through my own folly, the company and friendship of your fine father, whom, though I live in misery, I long to see once more."},{"t":"sp","who":"FLORIZEL","lines":["By his command","Have I here touch'd Sicilia, and from him","Give you all greetings that a king, at friend,","Can send his brother: and, but infirmity,","Which waits upon worn times, hath something seiz'd","His wish'd ability, he had himself","The lands and waters 'twixt your throne and his","Measur'd, to look upon you; whom he loves,","He bade me say so,—more than all the sceptres","And those that bear them living."],"id":763,"m":"It is by his command that I have landed in Sicilia, and I bring you from him all the greetings that a king, as a friend, can send his brother. If weakness, which comes with old age, had not taken away some of the strength he wished for, he would himself have crossed the lands and seas between your throne and his to see you, whom he loves (he told me to say so) more than all the sceptres and those who hold them now alive."},{"t":"sp","who":"LEONTES","lines":["O my brother,—","Good gentleman!—the wrongs I have done thee stir","Afresh within me; and these thy offices,","So rarely kind, are as interpreters","Of my behind-hand slackness! Welcome hither,","As is the spring to the earth. And hath he too","Expos'd this paragon to the fearful usage,","At least ungentle, of the dreadful Neptune,","To greet a man not worth her pains, much less","Th' adventure of her person?"],"id":764,"m":"O my brother (good gentleman!), the wrongs I have done you stir again inside me, and these duties of yours, so rarely kind, show up my own slowness! Welcome here, as spring is to the earth. And has he really exposed this paragon to the fearful, or at least rough, treatment of dreadful Neptune, to greet a man not worth her trouble, much less the risk to her person?"},{"t":"sp","who":"FLORIZEL","lines":["Good, my lord,","She came from Libya."],"id":765,"m":"My good lord, she came from Libya."},{"t":"sp","who":"LEONTES","lines":["Where the warlike Smalus,","That noble honour'd lord, is fear'd and lov'd?"],"id":766,"m":"Where the warlike Smalus, that noble, honoured lord, is feared and loved?"},{"t":"sp","who":"FLORIZEL","lines":["Most royal sir, from thence; from him, whose daughter","His tears proclaim'd his, parting with her: thence,","A prosperous south-wind friendly, we have cross'd,","To execute the charge my father gave me","For visiting your highness: my best train","I have from your Sicilian shores dismiss'd;","Who for Bohemia bend, to signify","Not only my success in Libya, sir,","But my arrival, and my wife's, in safety","Here, where we are."],"id":767,"m":"Most royal sir, from there; from him, whose tears when he parted from her showed she was his daughter. From there, with a friendly south wind behind us, we have crossed to carry out my father's order to visit your Highness. I have sent most of my followers away from your Sicilian shores to Bohemia, to report not only my success in Libya, sir, but my safe arrival, and my wife's, here where we are."},{"t":"sp","who":"LEONTES","lines":["The blessed gods","Purge all infection from our air whilst you","Do climate here! You have a holy father,","A graceful gentleman; against whose person,","So sacred as it is, I have done sin,","For which the heavens, taking angry note,","Have left me issueless. And your father's bless'd,","As he from heaven merits it, with you,","Worthy his goodness. What might I have been,","Might I a son and daughter now have look'd on,","Such goodly things as you!"],"id":768,"m":"May the blessed gods clear all infection from our air while you stay here! You have a holy father, a gracious gentleman, against whose person, sacred as it is, I have sinned. The heavens took angry note of it and have left me childless. And your father is blessed, as he deserves from heaven, with you, worthy of his goodness. What might I have been, if I could look now on a son and daughter as fine as you!"},{"t":"sd","text":"Enter a Lord.","id":769},{"t":"sp","who":"LORD","lines":["Most noble sir,","That which I shall report will bear no credit,","Were not the proof so nigh. Please you, great sir,","Bohemia greets you from himself by me;","Desires you to attach his son, who has—","His dignity and duty both cast off—","Fled from his father, from his hopes, and with","A shepherd's daughter."],"id":770,"m":"Most noble sir, what I have to report would not be believed if the proof were not so near. If you please, great sir, the King of Bohemia sends you greetings through me, and asks you to arrest his son, who has thrown off both his rank and his duty and run away from his father and his prospects with a shepherd's daughter."},{"t":"sp","who":"LEONTES","lines":["Where's Bohemia? speak."],"id":771,"m":"Where is the King of Bohemia? Speak."},{"t":"sp","who":"LORD","lines":["Here in your city; I now came from him.","I speak amazedly, and it becomes","My marvel and my message. To your court","Whiles he was hast'ning—in the chase, it seems,","Of this fair couple—meets he on the way","The father of this seeming lady and","Her brother, having both their country quitted","With this young prince."],"id":772,"m":"Here in your city. I have just come from him. I speak confusedly, as suits my amazement and my message. As he was hurrying to your court, in pursuit, it seems, of this fair couple, he met on the way this so-called lady's father and her brother, who had both left their country with this young prince."},{"t":"sp","who":"FLORIZEL","lines":["Camillo has betray'd me;","Whose honour and whose honesty till now,","Endur'd all weathers."],"id":773,"m":"Camillo has betrayed me, whose honour and honesty have stood up to every kind of weather until now."},{"t":"sp","who":"LORD","lines":["Lay 't so to his charge.","He's with the king your father."],"id":774,"m":"Accuse him of it yourself. He's with the king your father."},{"t":"sp","who":"LEONTES","lines":["Who? Camillo?"],"id":775,"m":"Who? Camillo?"},{"t":"sp","who":"LORD","lines":["Camillo, sir; I spake with him; who now","Has these poor men in question. Never saw I","Wretches so quake: they kneel, they kiss the earth;","Forswear themselves as often as they speak.","Bohemia stops his ears, and threatens them","With divers deaths in death."],"id":776,"m":"Camillo, sir. I spoke with him. He is now questioning these poor men. I never saw wretches shake so. They kneel, they kiss the ground, they contradict themselves every time they speak. The King of Bohemia stops his ears and threatens them with all kinds of deaths in one."},{"t":"sp","who":"PERDITA","lines":["O my poor father!","The heaven sets spies upon us, will not have","Our contract celebrated."],"id":777,"m":"O my poor father! Heaven has set spies on us and will not let our betrothal be celebrated."},{"t":"sp","who":"LEONTES","lines":["You are married?"],"id":778,"m":"Are you married?"},{"t":"sp","who":"FLORIZEL","lines":["We are not, sir, nor are we like to be.","The stars, I see, will kiss the valleys first.","The odds for high and low's alike."],"id":779,"m":"We are not, sir, nor are we likely to be. The stars, I see, will kiss the valleys first. High and low fare alike in this game."},{"t":"sp","who":"LEONTES","lines":["My lord,","Is this the daughter of a king?"],"id":780,"m":"My lord, is this the daughter of a king?"},{"t":"sp","who":"FLORIZEL","lines":["She is,","When once she is my wife."],"id":781,"m":"She will be, once she is my wife."},{"t":"sp","who":"LEONTES","lines":["That \"once\", I see by your good father's speed,","Will come on very slowly. I am sorry,","Most sorry, you have broken from his liking,","Where you were tied in duty; and as sorry","Your choice is not so rich in worth as beauty,","That you might well enjoy her."],"id":782,"m":"That 'once', I see by your good father's haste, will come very slowly. I am sorry, most sorry, that you have lost his favour, when you were bound to it by duty, and just as sorry that your choice is not as rich in rank as in beauty, so that you might properly enjoy her."},{"t":"sp","who":"FLORIZEL","lines":["Dear, look up:","Though Fortune, visible an enemy,","Should chase us with my father, power no jot","Hath she to change our loves. Beseech you, sir,","Remember since you ow'd no more to time","Than I do now: with thought of such affections,","Step forth mine advocate. At your request","My father will grant precious things as trifles."],"id":783,"m":"Dear, look up. Even if Fortune, showing herself our enemy, chases us along with my father, she has no power at all to change our love. I beg you, sir, remember when you were no older than I am now. Remembering such feelings, step forward as my advocate. At your request my father would grant precious things as if they were trifles."},{"t":"sp","who":"LEONTES","lines":["Would he do so, I'd beg your precious mistress,","Which he counts but a trifle."],"id":784,"m":"If he would do that, I'd ask for your precious mistress, whom he thinks only a trifle."},{"t":"sp","who":"PAULINA","lines":["Sir, my liege,","Your eye hath too much youth in 't: not a month","'Fore your queen died, she was more worth such gazes","Than what you look on now."],"id":785,"m":"Sir, my lord, there is too much youth in your eye. Not a month before your queen died, she was more worth such looks than the girl you are looking at now."},{"t":"sp","who":"LEONTES","lines":["I thought of her","Even in these looks I made. [To Florizel] But your petition","Is yet unanswer'd. I will to your father.","Your honour not o'erthrown by your desires,","I am friend to them and you: upon which errand","I now go toward him; therefore follow me,","And mark what way I make. Come, good my lord."],"id":786,"m":"I was thinking of her even as I looked. (To Florizel) But your request has not been answered yet. I will go to your father. As long as your honour has not been overthrown by your desires, I am a friend to them and to you. On that errand I am going to him now, so follow me and see what progress I make. Come, my good lord."},{"t":"sd","text":"Exeunt.","id":787},{"t":"scene","act":5,"scene":"II","loc":"The same. Before the Palace.","id":788},{"t":"sd","text":"Enter Autolycus and a Gentleman.","id":789},{"t":"sp","who":"AUTOLYCUS","lines":["Beseech you, sir, were you present at this relation?"],"id":790,"m":"Please, sir, were you there when this was told?"},{"t":"sp","who":"FIRST GENTLEMAN","lines":["I was by at the opening of the fardel, heard the old shepherd deliver","the manner how he found it: whereupon, after a little amazedness, we","were all commanded out of the chamber; only this, methought I heard the","shepherd say he found the child."],"id":791,"m":"I was there when the bundle was opened, and heard the old shepherd tell how he found it. Then, after a short amazement, we were all ordered out of the room. Only this: I thought I heard the shepherd say he found the child."},{"t":"sp","who":"AUTOLYCUS","lines":["I would most gladly know the issue of it."],"id":792,"m":"I'd very much like to know how it ended."},{"t":"sp","who":"FIRST GENTLEMAN","lines":["I make a broken delivery of the business; but the changes I perceived","in the king and Camillo were very notes of admiration. They seemed","almost, with staring on one another, to tear the cases of their eyes.","There was speech in their dumbness, language in their very gesture;","they looked as they had heard of a world ransomed, or one destroyed. A","notable passion of wonder appeared in them; but the wisest beholder,","that knew no more but seeing could not say if th' importance were joy","or sorrow; but in the extremity of the one, it must needs be. Here","comes a gentleman that happily knows more."],"id":793,"m":"I can only give a broken account of the business. But the changes I saw in the king and Camillo were real marks of wonder. They seemed almost to tear their eyelids, staring at each other. There was speech in their silence, language in their very gestures. They looked as if they had heard of a world saved or a world destroyed. A remarkable passion of wonder showed in them, but the wisest onlooker, who knew no more than what he saw, could not say whether it meant joy or sorrow. But it must have been the extreme of one or the other. Here comes a gentleman who perhaps knows more."},{"t":"sd","text":"Enter a Gentleman.","id":794},{"t":"sp","who":"FIRST GENTLEMAN","cont":1,"lines":["The news, Rogero?"],"id":795,"m":"What's the news, Rogero?"},{"t":"sp","who":"SECOND GENTLEMAN","lines":["Nothing but bonfires: the oracle is fulfilled: the king's daughter is","found: such a deal of wonder is broken out within this hour that","ballad-makers cannot be able to express it. Here comes the Lady","Paulina's steward: he can deliver you more."],"id":796,"m":"Nothing but bonfires. The oracle is fulfilled. The king's daughter is found. So much wonder has broken out within this hour that ballad-writers won't be able to express it. Here comes the Lady Paulina's steward. He can tell you more."},{"t":"sd","text":"Enter a third Gentleman.","id":797},{"t":"sp","who":"SECOND GENTLEMAN","cont":1,"lines":["How goes it now, sir? This news, which is called true, is so like an","old tale that the verity of it is in strong suspicion. Has the king","found his heir?"],"id":798,"m":"How are things now, sir? This news, which they say is true, is so like an old tale that its truth is very much in doubt. Has the king found his heir?"},{"t":"sp","who":"THIRD GENTLEMAN","lines":["Most true, if ever truth were pregnant by circumstance. That which you","hear you'll swear you see, there is such unity in the proofs. The","mantle of Queen Hermione's, her jewel about the neck of it, the letters","of Antigonus found with it, which they know to be his character; the","majesty of the creature in resemblance of the mother, the affection of","nobleness which nature shows above her breeding, and many other","evidences proclaim her with all certainty to be the king's daughter.","Did you see the meeting of the two kings?"],"id":799,"m":"It's absolutely true, if ever truth was made plain by evidence. You'll swear you see what you hear, the proofs agree so well. Queen Hermione's cloak, her jewel round its neck, the letters of Antigonus found with it, which they know are in his handwriting; the majesty of the girl, so like her mother; the nobility that nature shows in her beyond her upbringing; and many other proofs declare with complete certainty that she is the king's daughter. Did you see the meeting of the two kings?"},{"t":"sp","who":"SECOND GENTLEMAN","lines":["No."],"id":800,"m":"No."},{"t":"sp","who":"THIRD GENTLEMAN","lines":["Then you have lost a sight which was to be seen, cannot be spoken of.","There might you have beheld one joy crown another, so and in such","manner that it seemed sorrow wept to take leave of them, for their joy","waded in tears. There was casting up of eyes, holding up of hands, with","countenance of such distraction that they were to be known by garment,","not by favour. Our king, being ready to leap out of himself for joy of","his found daughter, as if that joy were now become a loss, cries \"O,","thy mother, thy mother!\" then asks Bohemia forgiveness; then embraces","his son-in-law; then again worries he his daughter with clipping her;","now he thanks the old shepherd, which stands by like a weather-bitten","conduit of many kings' reigns. I never heard of such another encounter,","which lames report to follow it, and undoes description to do it."],"id":801,"m":"Then you have missed a sight that had to be seen; it can't be described. There you could have seen one joy crown another, in such a way that it seemed sorrow wept to leave them, for their joy waded in tears. There was casting up of eyes and holding up of hands, with faces so wild that they could be recognised only by their clothes, not their features. Our king, ready to leap out of himself with joy at finding his daughter, cries, as if that joy had suddenly become a loss, 'O, your mother, your mother!' Then he asks the King of Bohemia's forgiveness, then embraces his son-in-law, then smothers his daughter with hugs again. Now he thanks the old shepherd, who stands by like a weather-beaten fountain statue that has seen many kings' reigns. I never heard of such another meeting. It lames any report that tries to follow it, and defeats any description that tries to capture it."},{"t":"sp","who":"SECOND GENTLEMAN","lines":["What, pray you, became of Antigonus, that carried hence the child?"],"id":802,"m":"Tell me, what became of Antigonus, who carried the child away from here?"},{"t":"sp","who":"THIRD GENTLEMAN","lines":["Like an old tale still, which will have matter to rehearse, though","credit be asleep and not an ear open. He was torn to pieces with a","bear: this avouches the shepherd's son, who has not only his innocence,","which seems much, to justify him, but a handkerchief and rings of his","that Paulina knows."],"id":803,"m":"Still like an old tale, which has more to tell even when belief is asleep and no ear is open. He was torn to pieces by a bear. The shepherd's son swears to it, and he has not only his innocence, which seems great, to back him, but a handkerchief and rings of Antigonus's that Paulina recognises."},{"t":"sp","who":"FIRST GENTLEMAN","lines":["What became of his bark and his followers?"],"id":804,"m":"What became of his ship and his men?"},{"t":"sp","who":"THIRD GENTLEMAN","lines":["Wrecked the same instant of their master's death, and in the view of","the shepherd: so that all the instruments which aided to expose the","child were even then lost when it was found. But O, the noble combat","that 'twixt joy and sorrow was fought in Paulina! She had one eye","declined for the loss of her husband, another elevated that the oracle","was fulfilled. She lifted the princess from the earth, and so locks her","in embracing, as if she would pin her to her heart, that she might no","more be in danger of losing."],"id":805,"m":"Wrecked at the same moment their master died, in sight of the shepherd, so that everyone who helped to abandon the child was lost at the very moment it was found. But O, the noble battle between joy and sorrow that was fought in Paulina! She had one eye lowered for the loss of her husband, the other raised because the oracle was fulfilled. She lifted the princess from the ground and locked her in such an embrace, as if she would pin her to her heart, so that she could never be in danger of losing her again."},{"t":"sp","who":"FIRST GENTLEMAN","lines":["The dignity of this act was worth the audience of kings and princes;","for by such was it acted."],"id":806,"m":"The grandeur of this scene deserved an audience of kings and princes, for it was acted by them."},{"t":"sp","who":"THIRD GENTLEMAN","lines":["One of the prettiest touches of all, and that which angled for mine","eyes (caught the water, though not the fish) was, when at the relation","of the queen's death (with the manner how she came to it bravely","confessed and lamented by the king) how attentiveness wounded his","daughter; till, from one sign of dolour to another, she did, with an","\"Alas,\" I would fain say, bleed tears, for I am sure my heart wept","blood. Who was most marble there changed colour; some swooned, all","sorrowed: if all the world could have seen it, the woe had been","universal."],"id":807,"m":"One of the most touching moments of all, which fished for my eyes (and caught the water, though not the fish), was when the queen's death was told, with the way she came to it bravely confessed and mourned by the king. How listening wounded his daughter! Until, from one sign of grief to another, she did, with an 'Alas', I'd almost say, bleed tears, for I'm sure my heart wept blood. The most marble-hearted there changed colour. Some fainted, all grieved. If the whole world could have seen it, the sorrow would have been universal."},{"t":"sp","who":"FIRST GENTLEMAN","lines":["Are they returned to the court?"],"id":808,"m":"Have they gone back to the court?"},{"t":"sp","who":"THIRD GENTLEMAN","lines":["No: the princess hearing of her mother's statue, which is in the","keeping of Paulina,—a piece many years in doing and now newly performed","by that rare Italian master, Julio Romano, who, had he himself","eternity, and could put breath into his work, would beguile Nature of","her custom, so perfectly he is her ape: he so near to Hermione hath","done Hermione that they say one would speak to her and stand in hope of","answer. Thither with all greediness of affection are they gone, and","there they intend to sup."],"id":809,"m":"No. The princess heard of her mother's statue, which Paulina keeps, a work that took many years and has just been finished by that rare Italian master Julio Romano. If he were immortal and could breathe life into his work, he would steal Nature's customers, he imitates her so perfectly. He has made Hermione so like Hermione that they say you would speak to her and wait for an answer. They have all gone there with eager love, and mean to have supper there."},{"t":"sp","who":"SECOND GENTLEMAN","lines":["I thought she had some great matter there in hand; for she hath","privately twice or thrice a day, ever since the death of Hermione,","visited that removed house. Shall we thither, and with our company","piece the rejoicing?"],"id":810,"m":"I thought she had something important there, for ever since Hermione died she has privately visited that remote house two or three times a day. Shall we go there and add our company to the rejoicing?"},{"t":"sp","who":"FIRST GENTLEMAN","lines":["Who would be thence that has the benefit of access? Every wink of an","eye some new grace will be born. Our absence makes us unthrifty to our","knowledge. Let's along."],"id":811,"m":"Who would stay away who is allowed in? Every blink of an eye some new wonder will be born. Our absence wastes our chance to know. Let's go."},{"t":"sd","text":"Exeunt Gentlemen.","id":812},{"t":"sp","who":"AUTOLYCUS","lines":["Now, had I not the dash of my former life in me, would preferment drop","on my head. I brought the old man and his son aboard the prince; told","him I heard them talk of a fardel and I know not what. But he at that","time over-fond of the shepherd's daughter (so he then took her to be),","who began to be much sea-sick, and himself little better, extremity of","weather continuing, this mystery remained undiscover'd. But 'tis all","one to me; for had I been the finder-out of this secret, it would not","have relish'd among my other discredits."],"id":813,"m":"Now, if I didn't have the stain of my old life on me, promotion would drop on my head. I brought the old man and his son aboard to the prince and told him I'd heard them talk about a bundle and I don't know what. But he was too taken up with the shepherd's daughter (as he then thought she was), who began to be very seasick, and he was little better himself, as the storm went on, so the mystery stayed hidden. But it's all the same to me. If I'd been the one to find out this secret, it wouldn't have gone down well with all my other bad deeds."},{"t":"sd","text":"Enter Shepherd and Clown.","id":814},{"t":"sp","who":"AUTOLYCUS","cont":1,"lines":["Here come those I have done good to against my will, and already","appearing in the blossoms of their fortune."],"id":815,"m":"Here come the people I've done good to against my will, already blossoming with their good fortune."},{"t":"sp","who":"SHEPHERD","lines":["Come, boy; I am past more children, but thy sons and daughters will be","all gentlemen born."],"id":816,"m":"Come, boy. I'm past having more children, but your sons and daughters will all be born gentlemen."},{"t":"sp","who":"CLOWN","lines":["You are well met, sir. You denied to fight with me this other day,","because I was no gentleman born. See you these clothes? Say you see","them not and think me still no gentleman born: you were best say these","robes are not gentlemen born. Give me the lie, do; and try whether I am","not now a gentleman born."],"id":817,"m":"Well met, sir. You refused to fight me the other day because I wasn't a born gentleman. Do you see these clothes? Say you don't see them, and still think I'm no born gentleman. You'd better say these robes aren't born gentlemen. Give me the lie, go on, and see whether I'm not a born gentleman now."},{"t":"sp","who":"AUTOLYCUS","lines":["I know you are now, sir, a gentleman born."],"id":818,"m":"I know you are a born gentleman now, sir."},{"t":"sp","who":"CLOWN","lines":["Ay, and have been so any time these four hours."],"id":819,"m":"Yes, and have been any time these four hours."},{"t":"sp","who":"SHEPHERD","lines":["And so have I, boy!"],"id":820,"m":"And so have I, boy!"},{"t":"sp","who":"CLOWN","lines":["So you have: but I was a gentleman born before my father; for the","king's son took me by the hand and called me brother; and then the two","kings called my father brother; and then the prince, my brother, and","the princess, my sister, called my father father; and so we wept; and","there was the first gentleman-like tears that ever we shed."],"id":821,"m":"So you have. But I was a born gentleman before my father, for the king's son took me by the hand and called me brother, and then the two kings called my father brother, and then the prince, my brother, and the princess, my sister, called my father father. And so we wept, and those were the first gentlemanly tears we ever shed."},{"t":"sp","who":"SHEPHERD","lines":["We may live, son, to shed many more."],"id":822,"m":"We may live, son, to shed many more."},{"t":"sp","who":"CLOWN","lines":["Ay; or else 'twere hard luck, being in so preposterous estate as we","are."],"id":823,"m":"Yes, or else it would be bad luck, being in such a preposterous position as we are."},{"t":"sp","who":"AUTOLYCUS","lines":["I humbly beseech you, sir, to pardon me all the faults I have committed","to your worship, and to give me your good report to the prince my","master."],"id":824,"m":"I humbly beg you, sir, to forgive me all the wrongs I have done your worship, and to give a good report of me to the prince my master."},{"t":"sp","who":"SHEPHERD","lines":["Prithee, son, do; for we must be gentle, now we are gentlemen."],"id":825,"m":"Please do, son, for we must be gentle now we are gentlemen."},{"t":"sp","who":"CLOWN","lines":["Thou wilt amend thy life?"],"id":826,"m":"Will you mend your ways?"},{"t":"sp","who":"AUTOLYCUS","lines":["Ay, an it like your good worship."],"id":827,"m":"Yes, if it please your good worship."},{"t":"sp","who":"CLOWN","lines":["Give me thy hand: I will swear to the prince thou art as honest a true","fellow as any is in Bohemia."],"id":828,"m":"Give me your hand. I'll swear to the prince you're as honest and true a fellow as anyone in Bohemia."},{"t":"sp","who":"SHEPHERD","lines":["You may say it, but not swear it."],"id":829,"m":"You may say it, but not swear it."},{"t":"sp","who":"CLOWN","lines":["Not swear it, now I am a gentleman? Let boors and franklins say it,","I'll swear it."],"id":830,"m":"Not swear it, now I'm a gentleman? Let peasants and farmers say it. I'll swear it."},{"t":"sp","who":"SHEPHERD","lines":["How if it be false, son?"],"id":831,"m":"What if it's false, son?"},{"t":"sp","who":"CLOWN","lines":["If it be ne'er so false, a true gentleman may swear it in the behalf of","his friend. And I'll swear to the prince thou art a tall fellow of thy","hands and that thou wilt not be drunk; but I know thou art no tall","fellow of thy hands and that thou wilt be drunk: but I'll swear it; and","I would thou wouldst be a tall fellow of thy hands."],"id":832,"m":"However false it is, a true gentleman may swear it on behalf of his friend. And I'll swear to the prince you're a brave fellow with your fists and that you won't get drunk. But I know you're no brave fellow with your fists and that you will get drunk. But I'll swear it, and I wish you would be a brave fellow with your fists."},{"t":"sp","who":"AUTOLYCUS","lines":["I will prove so, sir, to my power."],"id":833,"m":"I'll prove one, sir, as far as I can."},{"t":"sp","who":"CLOWN","lines":["Ay, by any means, prove a tall fellow: if I do not wonder how thou","dar'st venture to be drunk, not being a tall fellow, trust me not.","Hark! the kings and the princes, our kindred, are going to see the","queen's picture. Come, follow us: we'll be thy good masters."],"id":834,"m":"Yes, by all means prove a brave fellow. If I don't wonder how you dare get drunk when you're not a brave fellow, don't trust me. Listen! The kings and the princes, our relatives, are going to see the queen's image. Come, follow us. We'll be good masters to you."},{"t":"sd","text":"Exeunt.","id":835},{"t":"scene","act":5,"scene":"III","loc":"The same. A Room in Paulina's house.","id":836},{"t":"sd","text":"Enter Leontes, Polixenes, Florizel, Perdita, Camillo, Paulina, Lords and Attendants.","id":837},{"t":"sp","who":"LEONTES","lines":["O grave and good Paulina, the great comfort","That I have had of thee!"],"id":838,"m":"O wise and good Paulina, what great comfort I have had from you!"},{"t":"sp","who":"PAULINA","lines":["What, sovereign sir,","I did not well, I meant well. All my services","You have paid home: but that you have vouchsaf'd,","With your crown'd brother and these your contracted","Heirs of your kingdoms, my poor house to visit,","It is a surplus of your grace which never","My life may last to answer."],"id":839,"m":"What I did badly, sovereign sir, I meant well. You have paid in full for all my services. But that you have agreed, with your crowned brother and these betrothed heirs to your kingdoms, to visit my poor house is an extra kindness that my life may never last long enough to repay."},{"t":"sp","who":"LEONTES","lines":["O Paulina,","We honour you with trouble. But we came","To see the statue of our queen: your gallery","Have we pass'd through, not without much content","In many singularities; but we saw not","That which my daughter came to look upon,","The statue of her mother."],"id":840,"m":"O Paulina, we honour you only by troubling you. But we came to see the statue of our queen. We have walked through your gallery, and taken much pleasure in many rare things, but we did not see what my daughter came to look at: the statue of her mother."},{"t":"sp","who":"PAULINA","lines":["As she liv'd peerless,","So her dead likeness, I do well believe,","Excels whatever yet you look'd upon","Or hand of man hath done; therefore I keep it","Lonely, apart. But here it is: prepare","To see the life as lively mock'd as ever","Still sleep mock'd death. Behold, and say 'tis well."],"id":841,"m":"As she lived without equal, so her dead likeness, I truly believe, is better than anything you have ever looked at or any hand of man has made. So I keep it alone and apart. But here it is. Get ready to see life imitated as vividly as still sleep ever imitated death. Look, and say it is good."},{"t":"sd","text":"Paulina undraws a curtain, and discovers Hermione standing as a statue.","id":842},{"t":"sp","who":"PAULINA","cont":1,"lines":["I like your silence, it the more shows off","Your wonder: but yet speak. First you, my liege.","Comes it not something near?"],"id":843,"m":"I like your silence. It shows your wonder all the more. But still, speak. You first, my lord. Isn't it rather like her?"},{"t":"sp","who":"LEONTES","lines":["Her natural posture!","Chide me, dear stone, that I may say indeed","Thou art Hermione; or rather, thou art she","In thy not chiding; for she was as tender","As infancy and grace. But yet, Paulina,","Hermione was not so much wrinkled, nothing","So aged as this seems."],"id":844,"m":"Her very pose! Scold me, dear stone, so that I can say you really are Hermione. Or rather, you are her in not scolding me, for she was as gentle as infancy and grace. But still, Paulina, Hermione was not so wrinkled, not nearly as old as this seems."},{"t":"sp","who":"POLIXENES","lines":["O, not by much!"],"id":845,"m":"O, not by much!"},{"t":"sp","who":"PAULINA","lines":["So much the more our carver's excellence,","Which lets go by some sixteen years and makes her","As she liv'd now."],"id":846,"m":"So much the greater our sculptor's skill, which lets some sixteen years go by and makes her as she would be if she were alive now."},{"t":"sp","who":"LEONTES","lines":["As now she might have done,","So much to my good comfort as it is","Now piercing to my soul. O, thus she stood,","Even with such life of majesty, warm life,","As now it coldly stands, when first I woo'd her!","I am asham'd: does not the stone rebuke me","For being more stone than it? O royal piece,","There's magic in thy majesty, which has","My evils conjur'd to remembrance and","From thy admiring daughter took the spirits,","Standing like stone with thee."],"id":847,"m":"As she might have been now, it would have comforted me as much as it pierces my soul now. O, this is how she stood, with just such majestic life, warm life, as it stands coldly now, when I first courted her! I am ashamed. Doesn't the stone rebuke me for being more stone than it is? O royal work of art, there's magic in your majesty that has called my sins back to memory, and taken the spirit from your wondering daughter, who stands like stone with you."},{"t":"sp","who":"PERDITA","lines":["And give me leave,","And do not say 'tis superstition, that","I kneel, and then implore her blessing. Lady,","Dear queen, that ended when I but began,","Give me that hand of yours to kiss."],"id":848,"m":"And let me, and don't say it's superstition, kneel and beg her blessing. Lady, dear queen, whose life ended just as mine began, give me that hand of yours to kiss."},{"t":"sp","who":"PAULINA","lines":["O, patience!","The statue is but newly fix'd, the colour's","Not dry."],"id":849,"m":"O, wait! The statue has only just been set up. The paint is not dry."},{"t":"sp","who":"CAMILLO","lines":["My lord, your sorrow was too sore laid on,","Which sixteen winters cannot blow away,","So many summers dry. Scarce any joy","Did ever so long live; no sorrow","But kill'd itself much sooner."],"id":850,"m":"My lord, your sorrow was laid on too thick if sixteen winters cannot blow it away nor as many summers dry it up. Hardly any joy ever lived so long, and no sorrow that did not kill itself much sooner."},{"t":"sp","who":"POLIXENES","lines":["Dear my brother,","Let him that was the cause of this have power","To take off so much grief from you as he","Will piece up in himself."],"id":851,"m":"My dear brother, let the man who caused this have the power to take away as much grief from you as he can carry himself."},{"t":"sp","who":"PAULINA","lines":["Indeed, my lord,","If I had thought the sight of my poor image","Would thus have wrought you—for the stone is mine—","I'd not have show'd it."],"id":852,"m":"Indeed, my lord, if I had thought the sight of my poor statue would affect you like this (for the stone is mine), I wouldn't have shown it."},{"t":"sp","who":"LEONTES","lines":["Do not draw the curtain."],"id":853,"m":"Don't draw the curtain."},{"t":"sp","who":"PAULINA","lines":["No longer shall you gaze on't, lest your fancy","May think anon it moves."],"id":854,"m":"You mustn't gaze at it any longer, or your imagination may soon think it moves."},{"t":"sp","who":"LEONTES","lines":["Let be, let be.","Would I were dead, but that methinks already—","What was he that did make it? See, my lord,","Would you not deem it breath'd? And that those veins","Did verily bear blood?"],"id":855,"m":"Leave it, leave it. I wish I were dead, if I didn't think already... Who was it that made it? Look, my lord, wouldn't you think it breathed? And that those veins really carried blood?"},{"t":"sp","who":"POLIXENES","lines":["Masterly done:","The very life seems warm upon her lip."],"id":856,"m":"Masterly done. Life itself seems warm on her lip."},{"t":"sp","who":"LEONTES","lines":["The fixture of her eye has motion in 't,","As we are mock'd with art."],"id":857,"m":"Her fixed eye seems to move, so art fools us."},{"t":"sp","who":"PAULINA","lines":["I'll draw the curtain:","My lord's almost so far transported that","He'll think anon it lives."],"id":858,"m":"I'll draw the curtain. My lord is so carried away that he'll soon think it lives."},{"t":"sp","who":"LEONTES","lines":["O sweet Paulina,","Make me to think so twenty years together!","No settled senses of the world can match","The pleasure of that madness. Let 't alone."],"id":859,"m":"O sweet Paulina, make me think so for twenty years on end! No sound mind in the world can match the pleasure of that madness. Leave it."},{"t":"sp","who":"PAULINA","lines":["I am sorry, sir, I have thus far stirr'd you: but","I could afflict you further."],"id":860,"m":"I'm sorry, sir, I have stirred you so much. But I could afflict you more."},{"t":"sp","who":"LEONTES","lines":["Do, Paulina;","For this affliction has a taste as sweet","As any cordial comfort. Still methinks","There is an air comes from her. What fine chisel","Could ever yet cut breath? Let no man mock me,","For I will kiss her!"],"id":861,"m":"Do, Paulina, for this affliction tastes as sweet as any comforting medicine. Still I think there's breath coming from her. What fine chisel could ever carve breath? Let no man laugh at me, for I will kiss her!"},{"t":"sp","who":"PAULINA","lines":["Good my lord, forbear:","The ruddiness upon her lip is wet;","You'll mar it if you kiss it, stain your own","With oily painting. Shall I draw the curtain?"],"id":862,"m":"My good lord, don't. The red on her lip is wet. You'll spoil it if you kiss it, and stain your own with oil paint. Shall I draw the curtain?"},{"t":"sp","who":"LEONTES","lines":["No, not these twenty years."],"id":863,"m":"No, not for twenty years."},{"t":"sp","who":"PERDITA","lines":["So long could I","Stand by, a looker on."],"id":864,"m":"I could stand by and look on for that long."},{"t":"sp","who":"PAULINA","lines":["Either forbear,","Quit presently the chapel, or resolve you","For more amazement. If you can behold it,","I'll make the statue move indeed, descend,","And take you by the hand. But then you'll think","(Which I protest against) I am assisted","By wicked powers."],"id":865,"m":"Either stop, leave the chapel at once, or prepare yourselves for more amazement. If you can bear to watch, I'll make the statue really move, come down, and take you by the hand. But then you'll think (which I deny) that I am helped by evil powers."},{"t":"sp","who":"LEONTES","lines":["What you can make her do","I am content to look on: what to speak,","I am content to hear; for 'tis as easy","To make her speak as move."],"id":866,"m":"Whatever you can make her do, I'm happy to watch. Whatever you can make her say, I'm happy to hear. For it is as easy to make her speak as move."},{"t":"sp","who":"PAULINA","lines":["It is requir'd","You do awake your faith. Then all stand still;","Or those that think it is unlawful business","I am about, let them depart."],"id":867,"m":"You must awaken your faith. Then everyone stand still. Or anyone who thinks what I am doing is unlawful, let them leave."},{"t":"sp","who":"LEONTES","lines":["Proceed:","No foot shall stir."],"id":868,"m":"Go on. No one shall move a foot."},{"t":"sp","who":"PAULINA","lines":["Music, awake her: strike! [Music]","'Tis time; descend; be stone no more; approach;","Strike all that look upon with marvel. Come;","I'll fill your grave up: stir; nay, come away.","Bequeath to death your numbness, for from him","Dear life redeems you. You perceive she stirs."],"id":869,"m":"Music, wake her: play! (Music) It's time. Come down. Be stone no more. Come closer. Strike everyone who looks at you with wonder. Come, I'll fill up your grave. Move. No, come away. Leave your numbness to death, for dear life rescues you from him. You see, she moves."},{"t":"sd","text":"Hermione comes down from the pedestal.","id":870},{"t":"sp","who":"PAULINA","cont":1,"lines":["Start not; her actions shall be holy as","You hear my spell is lawful. Do not shun her","Until you see her die again; for then","You kill her double. Nay, present your hand:","When she was young you woo'd her; now in age","Is she become the suitor?"],"id":871,"m":"Don't start back. Her actions will be as holy as you hear my spell is lawful. Don't shun her, unless you see her die again, for then you would kill her twice. No, give her your hand. When she was young you courted her. Now, in age, has she become the suitor?"},{"t":"sp","who":"LEONTES","lines":["[Embracing her] O, she's warm!","If this be magic, let it be an art","Lawful as eating."],"id":872,"m":"(Embracing her) O, she's warm! If this is magic, let it be an art as lawful as eating."},{"t":"sp","who":"POLIXENES","lines":["She embraces him."],"id":873,"m":"She embraces him."},{"t":"sp","who":"CAMILLO","lines":["She hangs about his neck.","If she pertain to life, let her speak too."],"id":874,"m":"She hangs about his neck. If she belongs to life, let her speak too."},{"t":"sp","who":"POLIXENES","lines":["Ay, and make it manifest where she has liv'd,","Or how stol'n from the dead."],"id":875,"m":"Yes, and let her make it clear where she has lived, or how she was stolen from the dead."},{"t":"sp","who":"PAULINA","lines":["That she is living,","Were it but told you, should be hooted at","Like an old tale; but it appears she lives,","Though yet she speak not. Mark a little while.","Please you to interpose, fair madam. Kneel","And pray your mother's blessing. Turn, good lady,","Our Perdita is found."],"id":876,"m":"That she is alive, if you were only told it, you would jeer at like an old tale. But it seems she lives, though she hasn't spoken yet. Watch a little while. (To Perdita) Please come between them, fair madam. Kneel and ask your mother's blessing. (To Hermione) Turn, good lady. Our Perdita is found."},{"t":"sd","text":"Presenting Perdita who kneels to Hermione.","id":877},{"t":"sp","who":"HERMIONE","lines":["You gods, look down,","And from your sacred vials pour your graces","Upon my daughter's head! Tell me, mine own,","Where hast thou been preserv'd? where liv'd? how found","Thy father's court? for thou shalt hear that I,","Knowing by Paulina that the oracle","Gave hope thou wast in being, have preserv'd","Myself to see the issue."],"id":878,"m":"You gods, look down, and from your sacred jars pour your graces on my daughter's head! Tell me, my own one, where have you been kept safe? Where have you lived? How did you find your father's court? For you shall hear that I, knowing from Paulina that the oracle gave hope that you were alive, have kept myself alive to see the outcome."},{"t":"sp","who":"PAULINA","lines":["There's time enough for that;","Lest they desire upon this push to trouble","Your joys with like relation. Go together,","You precious winners all; your exultation","Partake to everyone. I, an old turtle,","Will wing me to some wither'd bough, and there","My mate, that's never to be found again,","Lament till I am lost."],"id":879,"m":"There's time enough for that, in case they want to spoil your joy at this moment by telling their own stories too. Go together, all you precious winners. Share your happiness with everyone. I, an old turtle-dove, will fly off to some withered branch, and there mourn my mate, who will never be found again, until I am lost too."},{"t":"sp","who":"LEONTES","lines":["O peace, Paulina!","Thou shouldst a husband take by my consent,","As I by thine a wife: this is a match,","And made between 's by vows. Thou hast found mine;","But how, is to be question'd; for I saw her,","As I thought, dead; and have in vain said many","A prayer upon her grave. I'll not seek far—","For him, I partly know his mind—to find thee","An honourable husband. Come, Camillo,","And take her by the hand, whose worth and honesty","Is richly noted, and here justified","By us, a pair of kings. Let's from this place.","What! look upon my brother: both your pardons,","That e'er I put between your holy looks","My ill suspicion. This your son-in-law,","And son unto the king, whom heavens directing,","Is troth-plight to your daughter. Good Paulina,","Lead us from hence; where we may leisurely","Each one demand, and answer to his part","Perform'd in this wide gap of time, since first","We were dissever'd. Hastily lead away!"],"id":880,"m":"O, peace, Paulina! You should take a husband with my consent, as I take a wife with yours. This is a bargain, and made between us by vows. You have found mine, but how is still to be asked, for I saw her, as I thought, dead, and have said many prayers in vain at her grave. I won't look far (for I partly know his mind) to find you an honourable husband. Come, Camillo, and take her by the hand, whose worth and honesty are well known and here confirmed by us, a pair of kings. Let's leave this place. (To Hermione) What! Look at my brother. Forgive me, both of you, that I ever put my evil suspicion between your innocent looks. This is your son-in-law, the king's son, who, by heaven's guidance, is betrothed to your daughter. Good Paulina, lead us from here, where we can take our time to ask and answer each one's part in this wide gap of time since we were first separated. Lead us away quickly!"},{"t":"sd","text":"Exeunt.","id":881}];
const STUDY={"chars":[{"name":"Leontes","group":"Sicilia","desc":"King of Sicilia, husband of Hermione and father of Mamillius. After nine months of hosting his childhood friend Polixenes, he suddenly becomes convinced that Hermione is sleeping with him and that her unborn child is not his. He orders Camillo to poison Polixenes, imprisons Hermione, orders her baby to be abandoned, and puts her on trial. He defies the oracle of Apollo until news of his son's death breaks him. For sixteen years he mourns under Paulina's guidance, until his lost daughter is found and Hermione's 'statue' comes to life."},{"name":"Hermione","group":"Sicilia","desc":"Queen of Sicilia and daughter of the Emperor of Russia. Warm, witty and dignified, she persuades Polixenes to stay when her husband cannot, and is accused of adultery for it. In prison she gives birth to a daughter. At her trial she defends her honour calmly and appeals to Apollo, who clears her. She collapses at the news of her son's death and is reported dead. Sixteen years later she is revealed, apparently a statue, in Paulina's house. She has kept herself alive, she says, in hope of seeing her daughter."},{"name":"Mamillius","group":"Sicilia","desc":"The young prince of Sicilia, son of Leontes and Hermione. He tells his mother that 'A sad tale's best for winter' and begins a ghost story just before his father bursts in. Separated from Hermione, he pines and falls ill, and his death is announced at the very moment Leontes rejects the oracle."},{"name":"Camillo","group":"Sicilia","desc":"A Sicilian lord and Leontes's trusted adviser and cupbearer. Ordered to poison Polixenes, he refuses, warns him and flees with him to Bohemia, where he serves him for sixteen years. Longing to go home, he helps Florizel and Perdita escape to Sicilia, then tells Polixenes so that the king will follow, and takes him there too. At the end Leontes pairs him with Paulina."},{"name":"Antigonus","group":"Sicilia","desc":"A Sicilian lord and Paulina's husband. He defends Hermione but cannot control his wife, and Leontes forces him to swear to take the baby girl to some remote place outside the kingdom. Hermione appears to him in a dream and tells him to name the child Perdita and leave her in Bohemia. He does so, and is chased off and killed by a bear. His ship sinks with all hands."},{"name":"Paulina","group":"Sicilia","desc":"Antigonus's wife and Hermione's fiercest defender. She carries the newborn princess to Leontes and calls him a tyrant to his face. After the trial she announces the queen's death and tells him that no penance can move the gods. For sixteen years she keeps his grief alive and makes him swear not to remarry without her consent. In the last scene she reveals the 'statue' of Hermione and brings it to life. Leontes then gives her Camillo as a husband."},{"name":"Cleomenes","group":"Sicilia","desc":"A Sicilian lord sent with Dion to the oracle at Delphi. He is overwhelmed by the oracle's thundering voice. Sixteen years later he urges Leontes to forgive himself and remarry."},{"name":"Dion","group":"Sicilia","desc":"A Sicilian lord who goes with Cleomenes to Delphi and brings back the sealed oracle. In Act 5 he argues that Leontes must remarry for the good of the kingdom."},{"name":"Emilia","group":"Sicilia","desc":"One of Hermione's ladies. She tells Paulina that the queen has given birth in prison to a daughter."},{"name":"Polixenes","group":"Bohemia","desc":"King of Bohemia and Leontes's childhood friend. Warned by Camillo that Leontes wants him dead, he flees home. Sixteen years later, worried by his son's absences from court, he visits the old shepherd's sheep-shearing feast in disguise, then reveals himself and furiously threatens his son, the shepherd and Perdita when Florizel tries to marry her. He follows the lovers to Sicilia and is reconciled with Leontes."},{"name":"Florizel","group":"Bohemia","desc":"Prince of Bohemia and son of Polixenes. He falls in love with Perdita when his falcon flies over her father's land, and visits her dressed as a shepherd called Doricles. When his father forbids the match he refuses to give her up, and, on Camillo's advice, sails with her to Sicilia, telling Leontes she is a princess from Libya."},{"name":"Archidamus","group":"Bohemia","desc":"A Bohemian lord who talks with Camillo in the opening scene about the two kings' friendship and the promise of young Mamillius."},{"name":"Perdita","group":"Bohemia","desc":"The daughter of Leontes and Hermione, abandoned on the coast of Bohemia as a newborn and brought up by the old shepherd. Her name, chosen by Hermione in Antigonus's dream, means 'the lost one'. At sixteen she is mistress of the sheep-shearing feast, hands out flowers to her guests, and argues with Polixenes about nature and art. She loves Florizel but fears his rank. In Sicilia she is recognised as the king's daughter, and kneels to the statue of her mother."},{"name":"The Shepherd","group":"Bohemia","desc":"An old shepherd, eighty-three by his own account, who finds the baby Perdita and the gold left with her, brings her up as his daughter and grows rich. Terrified by Polixenes's threats, he plans to show the king the things he found with her, is tricked by Autolycus onto Florizel's ship, and ends the play made a gentleman."},{"name":"Clown","group":"Bohemia","desc":"The shepherd's simple son. He sees the shipwreck and the bear killing Antigonus, buries what is left of him, and is robbed by Autolycus on his way to buy food for the feast. At the end, dressed in new clothes, he boasts of being 'a gentleman born' and promises to swear to Autolycus's honesty."},{"name":"Autolycus","group":"Bohemia","desc":"A rogue, pickpocket and ballad-seller, once a servant of Prince Florizel. He sings his way into Act 4, robs the Clown while pretending to have been robbed, sells trinkets and ballads at the feast while picking purses, swaps clothes with Florizel, and poses as a courtier to frighten the shepherd and his son. By taking them aboard the prince's ship he helps bring about the discovery of Perdita, though, as he says, against his will."},{"name":"Mopsa","group":"Bohemia","desc":"A shepherdess at the feast who is courted by the Clown and wants ribbons and gloves from Autolycus's pack. She sings a three-part song with Dorcas and Autolycus."},{"name":"Dorcas","group":"Bohemia","desc":"A shepherdess at the feast, Mopsa's rival for the Clown's affections."},{"name":"Time","group":"Chorus","desc":"The Chorus who opens Act 4. Turning his hourglass, he asks the audience to let him leap over sixteen years, and introduces Florizel and Perdita, now grown up."},{"name":"Mariner","group":"Others","desc":"The captain of Antigonus's ship, who warns him about the storm and the wild beasts of the Bohemian coast. He is drowned with his crew."},{"name":"Gaoler","group":"Others","desc":"The keeper of Hermione's prison. He lets Paulina speak to Emilia and agrees, nervously, to let the baby be carried out to the king."}],"scenes":{"a1s1":{"sum":"Archidamus, a lord of Bohemia, and Camillo, a lord of Sicilia, talk about the visit of Polixenes, King of Bohemia, to Leontes, King of Sicilia. Archidamus is embarrassed that Bohemia will not be able to return such lavish hospitality. Camillo describes how the two kings were brought up together and have stayed close friends through letters and gifts ever since. They praise the young prince Mamillius, who makes old people want to live to see him grown.","key":["The play opens with praise of a friendship that seems unbreakable: 'there is not in the world either malice or matter to alter it'.","Mamillius is introduced as the hope of the kingdom, which makes his death in Act 3 all the more shocking.","The relaxed prose of two courtiers gives no hint of the storm about to break."],"quiz":[{"q":"Who is visiting the court of Sicilia?","o":["Florizel","Polixenes, King of Bohemia","The Emperor of Russia","The King of Libya"],"a":1},{"q":"How have the two kings kept their friendship alive while apart?","o":["By fighting wars together against their enemies in the north","By exchanging gifts, letters and embassies","By marrying two sisters","By visiting every summer"],"a":1},{"q":"What do Camillo and Archidamus say about Mamillius?","o":["He is sickly and must be kept indoors all winter","He gives the old a reason to live","He is too proud for a boy of his age","He should be married soon to a princess"],"a":1}]},"a1s2":{"sum":"Polixenes, who has stayed nine months, insists on going home. Leontes cannot persuade him to stay, but Hermione, at Leontes's request, does, with playful wit. As Hermione gives Polixenes her hand, Leontes is suddenly seized by jealousy. He questions whether Mamillius is his son, watches his wife and friend go into the garden, and convinces himself that he is a cuckold. He tells Camillo, who refuses to believe it, and orders him to poison Polixenes. Camillo pretends to agree, then, when Polixenes notices the king's coldness, tells him the truth. They agree to flee to Bohemia that night.","key":["Leontes's jealousy erupts with no evidence and no villain to plant it: 'Too hot, too hot!'. Critics have long debated whether it has been growing secretly or comes from nowhere.","Hermione's 'He'll stay, my lord' and 'Th' other for some while a friend' are innocent, yet Leontes reads guilt into every gesture.","Camillo chooses honesty and exile over obedience: no one, he says, ever prospered by killing an anointed king."],"quiz":[{"q":"How long has Polixenes been in Sicilia?","o":["One week","Nine months","Sixteen years","Three days"],"a":1},{"q":"Who persuades Polixenes to stay?","o":["Leontes","Camillo","Hermione","Mamillius"],"a":2},{"q":"What does Leontes order Camillo to do?","o":["Spy on Hermione","Poison Polixenes","Banish Mamillius","Go to Delphi"],"a":1}]},"a2s1":{"sum":"Hermione, heavily pregnant, asks Mamillius to tell her a story; he begins 'There was a man dwelt by a churchyard'. Leontes bursts in, having learned that Polixenes and Camillo have fled, which he takes as proof of their guilt. He takes Mamillius away and publicly accuses Hermione of adultery and treason. She replies with dignity and is led to prison with her women. Antigonus and the lords plead for her, but Leontes says he has sent Cleomenes and Dion to the oracle of Apollo at Delphi, to satisfy others, not himself.","key":["Mamillius's 'A sad tale's best for winter' names the play and its mood.","Leontes compares himself to a man who has seen the spider in his cup: knowledge, he says, is what poisons him.","Hermione refuses to weep: 'I have That honourable grief lodg'd here which burns Worse than tears drown'."],"quiz":[{"q":"What kind of tale does Mamillius say is best for winter?","o":["A merry one","A sad one","A love story","A war story"],"a":1},{"q":"What makes Leontes certain he is right?","o":["A letter from Polixenes","The flight of Polixenes and Camillo","A confession from Hermione","The oracle"],"a":1},{"q":"Why has Leontes sent to the oracle?","o":["Because he doubts himself and his own eyes","To give rest to the minds of others","Because Hermione asked for it at her trial","Because Paulina demanded it"],"a":1}]},"a2s2":{"sum":"Paulina comes to the prison to see the queen but is refused. The gaoler lets her speak to Emilia, who reports that Hermione has given birth early to a healthy daughter. Paulina decides to take the baby to the king, believing that the sight of the child may soften him. The gaoler worries about letting the baby out without a warrant, but Paulina argues that the child was a prisoner only in the womb and is now free.","key":["Paulina takes on the job of telling the king the truth: 'The office Becomes a woman best'.","Hermione's words about her baby, 'My poor prisoner, I am as innocent as you', echo the theme of innocence wronged.","Paulina believes in the persuasive power of innocence: 'The silence often of pure innocence Persuades, when speaking fails'."],"quiz":[{"q":"What has Hermione given birth to?","o":["A son","A daughter","Twins","A stillborn child"],"a":1},{"q":"What does Paulina plan to do?","o":["Free the queen from prison","Show the baby to the king","Take the baby to Bohemia at once","Write to Polixenes for help"],"a":1},{"q":"Who tells Paulina the news?","o":["The gaoler","Emilia","Antigonus","Hermione herself"],"a":1}]},"a3s1":{"sum":"On their way back from Delphi, Cleomenes and Dion describe the island's sweet air, the solemn priests and the thunderous voice of the oracle. They hope that the sealed message they carry will turn out well for the queen, and hurry on to court with fresh horses.","key":["A short, calm scene of wonder and reverence between two storms.","The sacred atmosphere of Delphi prepares us to trust the oracle completely.","Cleomenes says he little likes the proclamations 'So forcing faults upon Hermione': even the king's servants doubt him."],"quiz":[{"q":"Where have Cleomenes and Dion been?","o":["Bohemia","Delphi","Libya","Russia"],"a":1},{"q":"What overwhelmed Cleomenes most?","o":["The temple's gold","The oracle's thundering voice","The priests' songs","The storm at sea"],"a":1},{"q":"How do they feel about the charges against Hermione?","o":["They are sure she is guilty","They dislike them","They have not heard of them","They think Camillo is to blame"],"a":1}]},"a2s3":{"sum":"Leontes cannot sleep. He hears that Mamillius is ill with grief for his mother, and wishes he could take revenge on Polixenes. Paulina forces her way in with the newborn baby, insists that Hermione is a 'good queen', lays the child at his feet and points out how like its father it is. Leontes calls her a witch and threatens to burn her and the baby. After she leaves, he orders Antigonus to burn the child; when the lords kneel, he instead makes Antigonus swear to carry it to some remote place outside the kingdom and leave it there. News arrives that Cleomenes and Dion are back from Delphi, and Leontes calls a public trial.","key":["Paulina is the only character who tells the king to his face that he is acting like a tyrant.","Leontes's change from 'burn it' to 'abandon it' shows him swaying 'like a feather for each wind that blows'.","Antigonus hopes that wolves and bears may show the child pity: an ironic hint of his own death."],"quiz":[{"q":"What does Paulina bring to Leontes?","o":["The oracle","The newborn baby","A letter from Hermione","Mamillius"],"a":1},{"q":"What does Leontes first order Antigonus to do with the baby?","o":["Raise it","Burn it","Send it to Polixenes","Give it to the shepherd"],"a":1},{"q":"What does Antigonus finally swear to do?","o":["Leave the baby in a remote place outside the kingdom","Take the baby to Delphi and leave it at Apollo's temple with the priests","Hide the baby with Paulina at her house","Give the baby to Camillo"],"a":0}]},"a3s2":{"sum":"Hermione is brought to trial and charged with adultery and conspiracy. She defends herself with calm dignity: she loved Polixenes only as honour required, knows nothing of conspiracy, and no longer fears death, having lost her husband's love, her son and her baby. She appeals to Apollo. The sealed oracle declares her chaste, Polixenes blameless, Camillo true, Leontes a jealous tyrant and the baby his own, and says the king will have no heir until what is lost is found. Leontes declares the oracle false. At once a servant reports that Mamillius has died. Hermione faints and is carried out. Leontes repents, but Paulina returns to say the queen is dead and that no penance can move the gods. He vows to visit their grave every day.","key":["The oracle is the turning point of the whole play: 'the king shall live without an heir, if that which is lost be not found'.","Leontes's punishment comes instantly: his son dies the moment he rejects Apollo.","Hermione's dignity under trial makes her one of Shakespeare's great wronged women: 'if powers divine Behold our human actions... innocence shall make False accusation blush'."],"quiz":[{"q":"Whose daughter is Hermione?","o":["The old King of Libya","The Emperor of Russia","King Polixenes","Lord Archidamus"],"a":1},{"q":"How does Leontes first react to the oracle?","o":["He kneels and repents","He says it is false","He laughs","He faints"],"a":1},{"q":"What news follows immediately?","o":["Polixenes is coming","Mamillius is dead","The baby is found","Camillo has returned"],"a":1}]},"a3s3":{"sum":"Antigonus lands on the coast of Bohemia with the baby as a storm rises. He tells how Hermione appeared to him in a dream, told him to leave the child in Bohemia, to name her Perdita, and warned he would never see his wife again. Believing Hermione dead and guilty, he leaves the baby with a written account and a bundle, and is chased off by a bear. An old shepherd finds the child and the gold left with her. His son, the Clown, describes seeing the ship sink and the bear tearing Antigonus apart. They take the baby home, and the Clown goes to bury what is left of Antigonus.","key":["'Exit, pursued by a bear' is Shakespeare's most famous stage direction, and marks the hinge between tragedy and comedy.","The Shepherd's line 'thou met'st with things dying, I with things new-born' sums up the play's turn.","Antigonus's dream wrongly convinces him the child is Polixenes's: even a good man is misled."],"quiz":[{"q":"What name does Hermione tell Antigonus to give the baby?","o":["Paulina","Perdita","Florizel","Mopsa"],"a":1},{"q":"What happens to Antigonus?","o":["He is drowned","He is killed by a bear","He returns to Sicilia","He becomes a shepherd"],"a":1},{"q":"What does the Shepherd find with the baby?","o":["A crown","Gold","A letter from Leontes","A sword"],"a":1}]},"a4s1":{"sum":"Time enters as a Chorus. He asks the audience not to blame him for sliding over sixteen years. Leontes, he says, has shut himself away in grief. He moves the scene to Bohemia and introduces Florizel, the son of Polixenes, and Perdita, now grown in grace, who is thought to be a shepherd's daughter.","key":["Time's speech breaks the classical rule of a play taking place within a day, and admits it.","The turning of the hourglass divides the play into two halves: winter and spring, Sicilia and Bohemia, parents and children.","Time promises a story about a shepherd's daughter, the 'argument of Time'."],"quiz":[{"q":"How many years does Time pass over?","o":["Six","Ten","Sixteen","Twenty-three"],"a":2},{"q":"What does Time say Leontes has done?","o":["Remarried a lady of the court","Shut himself away in grief","Gone to war against Bohemia","Died"],"a":1},{"q":"Which two young people does Time introduce?","o":["Mamillius and Perdita","Florizel and Perdita","Florizel and Mopsa","Camillo and Paulina"],"a":1}]},"a4s2":{"sum":"Camillo asks Polixenes for leave to return to Sicilia: he has been away fifteen years and the repentant Leontes has sent for him. Polixenes begs him to stay. He is worried about Florizel, who has been neglecting the court and spends his time at the house of a shepherd who has grown mysteriously rich and has a beautiful daughter. The king and Camillo decide to visit the shepherd in disguise.","key":["Camillo's homesickness drives the end of the play: his wish to see Sicilia again shapes everything he does.","Polixenes now fears for his son as Leontes once feared for his wife: the older generation still tries to control the younger.","Disguise moves from Florizel to his father, setting up the unmasking at the feast."],"quiz":[{"q":"Why does Camillo want to go home?","o":["He is banished","He longs for his country and Leontes has sent for him","Polixenes has insulted him","He wants to marry Paulina"],"a":1},{"q":"What worries Polixenes about Florizel?","o":["He is ill and will not see a doctor or his father","He spends his time at a shepherd's house","He has gone to war","He wants to go to Sicilia"],"a":1},{"q":"What do Polixenes and Camillo decide to do?","o":["Arrest the shepherd","Visit the shepherd in disguise","Send Florizel abroad","Write to Leontes"],"a":1}]},"a4s3":{"sum":"Autolycus enters singing about spring and confesses that he is a thief and was once Florizel's servant. The Clown appears, working out the shopping list for the sheep-shearing feast. Autolycus pretends to have been robbed and beaten, and, as the Clown helps him up, picks his pocket. He even blames the robbery on 'Autolycus', and turns down the Clown's offer of money. Once the Clown has gone, he decides to go to the feast too.","key":["Autolycus brings spring, song and crime into the play: 'When daffodils begin to peer'.","He calls himself 'a snapper-up of unconsidered trifles', a joke on his name, taken from a famous thief in Greek myth.","The Clown's kindness is exactly what lets him be robbed: a comic echo of trust abused."],"quiz":[{"q":"Who did Autolycus once serve?","o":["King Leontes of Sicilia","Prince Florizel","The old Shepherd","Lord Camillo"],"a":1},{"q":"How does Autolycus rob the Clown?","o":["At sword point","By picking his pocket while being helped up","By selling him fake ballads","By cheating at dice"],"a":1},{"q":"What is the Clown shopping for?","o":["A wedding feast in the village","The sheep-shearing feast","A funeral","His sister's dowry"],"a":1}]},"a4s4":{"sum":"At the sheep-shearing feast Perdita, dressed as queen of the feast, worries about Florizel's rank. Polixenes and Camillo arrive in disguise. Perdita gives them flowers and debates with Polixenes about whether grafted flowers improve nature. Florizel praises her, and they dance. Autolycus sells ballads and trinkets and sings with Mopsa and Dorcas. Florizel asks the Shepherd to betroth them in front of the strangers, refusing to tell his father. Polixenes throws off his disguise, threatens the Shepherd with death and Perdita with having her beauty scratched, and disowns his son if he sees her again. Camillo persuades Florizel to sail to Sicilia and present himself to Leontes, planning secretly to bring Polixenes after them. Florizel swaps clothes with Autolycus to escape. The terrified Shepherd and Clown plan to show the king what was found with Perdita, but Autolycus, posing as a courtier, takes them to Florizel's ship instead.","key":["Perdita and Polixenes argue about nature and art: he says grafting a 'gentler scion' onto 'wildest stock' improves nature, yet he refuses to let his son marry a shepherdess.","Polixenes's fury mirrors Leontes's in Act 1: a father's rage once again threatens the young.","Camillo turns the lovers' flight to his own purpose: to see Sicilia again."],"quiz":[{"q":"What name does Florizel use as a shepherd?","o":["Doricles","Smalus","Romano","Rogero"],"a":0},{"q":"What does Perdita refuse to grow?","o":["Roses, red and white, for the feast","Gillyflowers (carnations)","Rosemary and rue","Daffodils"],"a":1},{"q":"Where does Camillo advise the lovers to go?","o":["Libya","Sicilia","Russia","Delphi"],"a":1}]},"a5s1":{"sum":"Sixteen years on, Cleomenes and Dion urge Leontes to forgive himself and remarry for the sake of an heir. Paulina reminds them of the oracle, and makes Leontes swear not to marry without her consent, adding that it will not be until his first queen breathes again. Florizel and Perdita arrive, claiming she is a princess from Libya. Leontes welcomes them warmly and grieves for his own lost children. A lord announces that Polixenes is in the city and has asked Leontes to arrest his son, who has run off with a shepherd's daughter. Leontes promises to plead for the young couple with his father.","key":["Leontes's penance has lasted sixteen years, and Paulina makes sure it continues: 'She you kill'd Would be unparallel'd'.","His promise never to marry until Paulina says so prepares for the statue scene.","Leontes is drawn to Perdita without knowing why, and Paulina reminds him of Hermione: 'Your eye hath too much youth in 't'."],"quiz":[{"q":"What do Cleomenes and Dion want Leontes to do?","o":["Go to war","Remarry","Visit Bohemia","Consult the oracle again"],"a":1},{"q":"Where does Florizel say Perdita comes from?","o":["Russia","Libya","Delphi","Bohemia"],"a":1},{"q":"What does Paulina make Leontes swear?","o":["Never to marry without her consent","To forgive Polixenes and send for him at once","To name an heir from among the lords","To visit Hermione's grave"],"a":0}]},"a5s2":{"sum":"Gentlemen of the court tell Autolycus how the Shepherd's bundle has proved that Perdita is Leontes's daughter: Hermione's cloak and jewel, Antigonus's letters and the girl's likeness to her mother. They describe the meeting of the two kings, Leontes's cry of 'O, thy mother, thy mother!', Paulina's mixed joy and grief, and Perdita's tears at hearing how her mother died. Everyone has gone to see a statue of Hermione kept by Paulina, made by the Italian artist Julio Romano. The Shepherd and Clown, now made gentlemen, meet Autolycus, who asks their pardon, and the Clown promises to swear he is honest.","key":["The great recognition happens offstage, told in prose 'like an old tale', so that the play saves its wonder for the statue.","The oracle is fulfilled: 'the king's daughter is found'.","The comic Clown, 'a gentleman born' for four hours, mocks the very idea that rank depends on birth."],"quiz":[{"q":"Who made the statue of Hermione, according to the gentlemen?","o":["Paulina","Julio Romano","Camillo","An unknown Greek"],"a":1},{"q":"What proves Perdita's identity?","o":["A birthmark","Hermione's cloak and jewel and Antigonus's letters","The oracle's second message","Her resemblance to Polixenes"],"a":1},{"q":"What has happened to the Shepherd and Clown?","o":["They have been hanged","They have been made gentlemen","They have been banished","They have gone back to Bohemia"],"a":1}]},"a5s3":{"sum":"In Paulina's chapel the court gathers to see the statue of Hermione. Leontes is struck by how lifelike it is, though more wrinkled than she was. Perdita kneels to it. Paulina warns that the paint is wet and offers to draw the curtain, but Leontes begs to keep looking. She tells them that if they awaken their faith she will make the statue move. To music, Hermione steps down and embraces Leontes. She blesses Perdita and says she has kept herself alive in hope of seeing her. Paulina says she will mourn her lost husband alone, but Leontes matches her with Camillo, asks pardon of Hermione and Polixenes, and leads everyone off to tell their stories of the sixteen years.","key":["'It is requir'd You do awake your faith': the play asks the audience to believe in wonder.","Hermione speaks only to her daughter, not to Leontes: the reunion is joyful but not complete.","Mamillius and Antigonus stay dead. The ending is happy, but the losses remain."],"quiz":[{"q":"What does Paulina say about the statue's lips?","o":["They are cracked with age","The paint is still wet","They are made of gold","They move"],"a":1},{"q":"What happens when the music plays?","o":["The statue crumbles to dust before their eyes","Hermione steps down from the pedestal","Mamillius appears beside his mother, alive","Antigonus returns from Bohemia with the baby"],"a":1},{"q":"Whom does Leontes match Paulina with?","o":["Polixenes","Camillo","Cleomenes","The Shepherd"],"a":1}]}},"notes":{"9":"'Shook hands, as over a vast': the kings have kept their friendship across a great distance. The image of two friends reaching across a gap returns at the end, when they are reunited.","18":"'Nine changes of the watery star': nine months, counted by the moon. Polixenes has stayed exactly as long as a pregnancy, which Leontes will soon twist into suspicion.","30":"A 'distaff' was the stick used in spinning, a symbol of women's work. Hermione jokes that the women will beat Polixenes home with their spindles.","39":"Polixenes describes the two boys as innocent as lambs, free of everything but original sin ('the imposition clear'd Hereditary ours'). Growing up, in his telling, meant meeting temptation, in the form of the women they married.","42":"Hermione teases Polixenes for implying that she and his wife were the 'temptations'. Her joke ('lest you say Your queen and I are devils') is the kind of playful talk that Leontes misreads.","52":"'Tremor cordis': a trembling of the heart. 'Paddling palms and pinching fingers' and 'the mort o' th' deer' (the horn call at a deer's death, here a lover's sigh) show Leontes reading innocent gestures as sexual.","54":"'Neat' meant both tidy and cattle. The thought of cattle leads Leontes to horns, the mark of the cuckold, whose wife is unfaithful.","56":"This is one of the hardest passages in Shakespeare. 'Affection' (passion, or perhaps his own imagination) can make the impossible seem possible, and so Leontes argues himself into believing his fear is real.","69":"Leontes's speech is full of sexual images: his wife 'sluic'd' and his 'pond fish'd' by 'Sir Smile, his neighbour'. He comforts himself that a tenth of all husbands are cuckolds.","91":"'Hobby-horse' was slang for a loose woman. 'Flax-wench' means a girl who spins flax, and 'puts to Before her troth-plight' means she sleeps with a man before she is betrothed.","93":"Leontes's list of 'nothings' is a famous piece of obsessive rhetoric. The word 'nothing' appears nine times in a few lines, and each repetition pushes him further from the truth.","109":"Camillo refuses to kill an 'anointed king', a monarch made holy by being anointed at his coronation. Killing one was thought to be a sin against God as well as a crime.","119":"The basilisk was a mythical serpent that could kill with a look. Polixenes refuses to believe his look could harm anyone.","129":"'His that did betray the Best' is Judas, who betrayed Christ. Polixenes wishes for the worst possible fame if he is guilty.","154":"Mamillius gives the play its name. His tale of a man who 'dwelt by a churchyard' is interrupted by his father's entrance and never finished.","163":"An old belief held that a spider in a drink was poisonous only if the drinker knew it was there. Leontes says knowing the truth is what makes him sick.","193":"Delphos is Delphi, the site of the most famous oracle of Apollo in ancient Greece. In the play it is on an island, as in Shakespeare's source.","221":"'Lunes' are fits of lunacy, believed to follow the phases of the moon.","246":"'Gossips' were godparents at a christening. Paulina pretends she has come to discuss the baby's baptism.","258":"A 'mankind witch' is a masculine, violent witch. An 'intelligencing bawd' is a go-between who carries messages for lovers.","260":"'Dame Partlet' is the hen in old animal stories, bossing her husband the cock. Leontes mocks Antigonus as a henpecked husband.","267":"A 'callat' is a scold or a whore.","268":"Paulina asks Nature to give the baby no 'yellow', the colour of jealousy, so that she will never suspect her own husband as Leontes does.","274":"Paulina means that the person who burns a heretic is the real heretic. In Shakespeare's time heretics could still be burnt at the stake.","283":"'Lady Margery' is a mocking name for Paulina, and 'midwife' a sneer at her care of the baby.","294":"The journey to Delphi and back has taken twenty-three days. Leontes says the speed means Apollo wants the truth known quickly.","311":"The indictment accuses Hermione of high treason, which in Shakespeare's England included adultery by a queen. Anne Boleyn, mother of Elizabeth I, had been executed on such charges.","320":"'The child-bed privilege' was the right of a mother to rest in private for weeks after giving birth. Hermione has been dragged into court 'before I have got strength of limit'.","323":"Hermione is the daughter of the Emperor of Russia. She wishes he could see her trial with 'eyes Of pity, not revenge'.","328":"The oracle's last clause, 'if that which is lost be not found', is the riddle the rest of the play solves. Perdita's name means 'the lost one'.","350":"Paulina lists tortures used in Shakespeare's time: the wheel, the rack, burning and boiling in lead or oil.","367":"In the dream Hermione names the baby Perdita. Antigonus wrongly concludes that the queen is dead and guilty, and that the baby must be Polixenes's.","369":"Antigonus leaves with the most famous stage direction in Shakespeare. Whether the bear was a real animal, an actor in a bear skin, or something in between is still debated. The moment is both terrible and absurd.","374":"'Scape' means an escapade, a sexual slip. The Shepherd guesses the baby is the child of a lady's maid, born of 'stair-work' and 'trunk-work'.","380":"'Flap-dragon'd': a flap-dragon was a raisin snatched from burning brandy and swallowed in one gulp. The sea swallows the ship like that.","385":"A 'bearing-cloth' is a christening robe. A 'changeling' was a baby left by the fairies in exchange for a stolen human child.","395":"Time turns an hourglass, the usual symbol of time. His speech is in rhyming couplets, unlike the rest of the play's verse.","400":"Camillo says 'fifteen years' here, though Time said sixteen: one of several small inconsistencies in the text.","411":"Autolycus's songs are full of thieves' slang: a 'doxy' is a beggar's girlfriend, 'aunts' are loose women, and his 'pugging tooth' is a thieving one. The 'red blood' of spring replaces winter's pale.","413":"The Clown is working out the value of the wool: every eleven sheep give a 'tod' (28 pounds) of wool, worth just over a pound in money.","415":"'Three-man song-men' sing songs in three parts. The joke about the Puritan who 'sings psalms to hornpipes' mocks strict Protestants.","437":"Autolycus lists low trades: showing a performing ape, serving court summonses, and running a puppet show ('motion') of the Prodigal Son. Then he names himself as the robber.","455":"Flora was the Roman goddess of flowers. Perdita, dressed as queen of the feast, is crowned with flowers.","459":"Florizel lists gods who took animal or human form for love: Jupiter as a bull (to win Europa), Neptune as a ram, Apollo as a shepherd.","466":"Rosemary was for remembrance and rue for grace (it was also called 'herb of grace'). Both stay green through winter, so they suit older men.","471":"Grafting joins a shoot ('scion') from one plant onto the root ('stock') of another. Polixenes argues that this human art is itself part of nature, but he will soon forbid his own son to 'marry' a humble girl.","476":"Proserpina was carried off to the underworld by Dis (Pluto) while gathering flowers, and her return each year brought the spring. Perdita, like her, is a lost daughter whose return brings renewal.","497":"The servant innocently repeats bawdy refrains: 'dildos and fadings' were nonsense words in ballads, but also had obscene meanings.","527":"Autolycus's ballads are absurd tall tales, yet the country girls believe anything printed. The play itself will soon be called 'like an old tale'.","591":"Polixenes's sudden fury at his son echoes Leontes's in Act 1. Both are fathers and kings who believe they have been betrayed.","593":"Deucalion was the Noah of Greek myth, who survived the great flood. Polixenes means he will disown his son as far back as the first man.","608":"Florizel would not break his vow 'for all the sun sees': he chooses love over his inheritance.","633":"Autolycus lists the cheap wares he has sold: a 'pomander' is a ball of perfume, a 'table-book' a notebook. He boasts that the crowd's love of singing left their purses easy to steal.","689":"The Clown thinks an 'advocate' is a present of a pheasant, as if bribing a judge with game birds.","729":"Paulina reminds the court that the oracle has not yet been fulfilled. Alexander the Great, asked who should succeed him, was said to have answered 'the worthiest'.","766":"Smalus of Libya is invented for Florizel's cover story. Libya was a byword for a distant, exotic land.","793":"The gentlemen tell the story of the recognition in elaborate, mannered prose. Shakespeare keeps the great reunion offstage so as to save his biggest surprise for the statue scene.","809":"Julio Romano (died 1546) was a real Italian painter and architect, a pupil of Raphael. He is the only artist Shakespeare ever names, though he was not chiefly known as a sculptor.","817":"'Gentleman born' meant a man of inherited rank. The Clown's boast that he has been one 'these four hours' is a joke about the idea that birth decides worth.","844":"The statue is wrinkled: sixteen years have passed for Hermione too. The detail is the first hint that she may be alive.","869":"Paulina's spell is in solemn verse, with music. Whether Hermione has been hiding for sixteen years or is truly brought back to life is never fully explained.","878":"Hermione's first and only speech since Act 3 is to her daughter. She says nothing directly to Leontes.","880":"Leontes, who once broke a marriage with suspicion, ends the play by making one, pairing Paulina and Camillo, and asks to hear everyone's part 'Perform'd in this wide gap of time'."},"gloss":{"physics":"heals, does good to","watery":"watery (the moon, which rules the tides)","cipher":"nought, zero","sneaping":"biting, nipping","gainsaying":"refusal, denial","gest":"fixed stage of a journey; set time","distaffs":"sticks used in spinning","verily":"truly","limber":"weak, limp","unsphere":"knock out of their spheres","lordings":"little lords","verier":"greater","wag":"mischievous boy","unfledg'd":"not yet feathered, very young","tongueless":"unpraised","crabbed":"sour, bitter","bawcock":"fine fellow","smutch'd":"smudged","neat":"tidy; cattle","virginalling":"tapping, as on a keyboard","pash":"head","welkin":"sky-blue","collop":"slice of flesh, my own flesh","credent":"believable","unbreech'd":"not yet in breeches, very young","squash":"unripe pea pod","dole":"share, lot","neb":"beak, mouth","sluic'd":"flooded","barricado":"barricade","rounding":"whispering","gust":"taste, find out","pate":"head","purblind":"half-blind","hoxes":"hamstrings","cogitation":"thinking","hobby-horse":"loose woman","troth-plight":"betrothal","temporizer":"time-server, fence-sitter","bespice":"spice, poison","cordial":"medicine for the heart","blench":"swerve, stray","basilisk":"mythical serpent that killed with a look","incidency":"event, happening","posterns":"back gates","importunate":"pressing, insistent","rheums":"colds, catarrhs","pander":"go-between, pimp","federary":"accomplice","bed-swerver":"adulteress","lunes":"fits of madness","gossips":"godparents","callat":"scold, whore","losel":"good-for-nothing","dotard":"old fool","crone":"old woman","midwife":"woman who helps at a birth","posts":"messengers","arraign":"put on trial","indictment":"formal charge","continent":"chaste, self-restrained","bug":"bogeyman","commodity":"benefit","strumpet":"whore","oracle":"answer of a god, given at a shrine","profaneness":"sacrilege, contempt for the holy","glisters":"glitters, shines","bark":"ship","bairn":"child","scape":"escapade, sexual slip","changeling":"fairy child left in place of a stolen one","bearing-cloth":"christening robe","bodkin":"needle; small dagger","hogshead":"large barrel","yest":"froth, foam","curst":"savage, bad-tempered","wether":"castrated ram","tod":"28 pounds of wool","counters":"discs used for counting","nosegays":"posies of flowers","warden":"kind of pear","springe":"snare","doxy":"beggar's girlfriend","pugging":"thieving","caparison":"outfit, clothes","three-pile":"the thickest velvet","prig":"thief","wakes":"church festivals","bear-baitings":"shows where dogs attacked a chained bear","weeds":"clothes","swain":"country lad, shepherd","prank'd":"dressed up","gillyvors":"gillyflowers, striped carnations","scion":"shoot used in grafting","dibble":"tool for planting","savory":"herb","marjoram":"herb","oxlips":"wild flowers, like large cowslips","corse":"corpse","featly":"gracefully, nimbly","tabor":"small drum","inkles":"linen tapes","cambrics":"fine linens","lawns":"fine linens","cypress":"black crape","quoifs":"close caps","stomachers":"embroidered bodice fronts","poking-sticks":"rods for setting ruffs","plackets":"slits in petticoats","cozened":"cheated","cozeners":"cheats","usurer":"moneylender","carbonadoed":"grilled","gallimaufry":"jumble, hotchpotch","knack":"trinket","knacks":"trinkets","churl":"peasant","fardel":"bundle","pomander":"ball of perfume","choughs":"crows; fools","discase":"undress","boot":"something extra, profit","excrement":"growth of hair, beard","rustics":"country people","cap-a-pe":"from head to foot","pheasant":"game bird","moiety":"half","aqua-vitæ":"brandy, strong spirit","hand-fast":"custody; or betrothed","germane":"related","unparallel'd":"without equal","proselytes":"converts","issueless":"childless","attach":"arrest","amazedly":"in confusion","conduit":"fountain","character":"handwriting","franklins":"farmers who own land","boors":"peasants","tall":"brave, strong","preposterous":"(the Clown means) prosperous","singularities":"rare objects","fixture":"setting, position","vials":"jars, flasks","turtle":"turtle-dove, faithful mate","dissever'd":"separated","perdition":"ruin","Perdita":"'the lost one'","Delphos":"Delphi, home of Apollo's oracle","Jove":"Jupiter, king of the gods","Proserpina":"goddess carried off by Dis to the underworld","Dis":"Pluto, god of the underworld","Cytherea":"Venus","Phoebus":"Apollo as the sun god","Deucalion":"the Noah of Greek myth","Flora":"Roman goddess of flowers","cuckold":"man whose wife is unfaithful","cuckolds":"men whose wives are unfaithful","adultress":"woman who commits adultery","distraction":"madness, agitation"}};
const PARTS={"30":[[0,3,"If he said he longed to see his son, that would be a strong reason. But let him say so, then, and let him go. Let him swear to it and he shall not stay: we'll chase him home with our spindles."],[4,10,"(To Polixenes) Still, I'll risk asking to borrow your royal presence for one more week. When you have my husband in Bohemia, I'll give him leave to stay there a month past the date set for his departure. And yet, truly, Leontes, I love you not a tick of the clock less than any wife loves her husband. You'll stay?"]],"34":[[0,5,"'Truly'! You fob me off with feeble vows. But even if you tried to shake the stars from their spheres with oaths, I would still say 'Sir, you are not going.' Truly, you shall not go. A lady's 'truly' is as powerful as a lord's. Will you still go?"],[6,10,"Then you force me to keep you as a prisoner, not a guest. Then you will pay a jailer's fees when you leave, and save your thanks. What do you say? My prisoner or my guest? By your solemn 'truly', you shall be one or the other."]],"48":[[0,6,"What! Have I spoken well twice? When was the other time? Please tell me. Stuff us with praise and make us as fat as pets: one good deed that goes unpraised kills a thousand more that would have followed it. Praise is our wages. You can ride us a thousand furlongs with one soft kiss before you can spur us across one acre. But to the point."],[7,11,"My last good deed was to beg him to stay. What was my first? It has an older sister, unless I misunderstand you. O, I wish her name were Grace! But once before I spoke to the purpose. When? Come, tell me; I'm longing to know."]],"52":[[0,6,"(Aside) Too hot, too hot! To mix friendship so far is to mix bloods. My heart is trembling. It dances, but not for joy, not joy. This kind of welcome may look innocent, and come from warmth, generosity and a loving heart, and suit the person who gives it: it may, I grant."],[7,12,"But to be fondling palms and pinching fingers, as they are now, and giving each other practised smiles as if in a mirror, and then sighing like a horn at the death of a deer: O, that is a welcome my heart does not like, nor my brow. Mamillius, are you my boy?"]],"56":[[0,6,"You need a rough head and horns like mine to be fully like me. Yet they say we are almost as alike as eggs. Women say so, and they will say anything. But even if they were as false as black cloth dyed too often, as wind, as water, as false as loaded dice to a man who doesn't know his from mine, it would still be true to say this boy is like me."],[7,9,"Come, young sir, look at me with your sky-blue eyes. Sweet rascal! Dearest! My own flesh! Can your mother... could it be?"],[10,18,"Passion! Your force pierces to the centre. You make things possible that were thought impossible, and deal with dreams. How can this be? You work together with what is unreal and keep company with nothing. Then it is very believable that you can join with something real. And you do, beyond what is allowed, and I find it, to the poisoning of my mind and the hardening of my brow."]],"61":[[0,8,"No, truly. How nature sometimes gives away its own foolishness and tenderness, and becomes a joke to harder hearts! Looking at my boy's face, I thought I went back twenty-three years and saw myself not yet in breeches, in my green velvet coat, with my dagger blunted in case it bit its master and proved, as ornaments often do, too dangerous."],[9,11,"How like this little kernel I seemed to be then, this young pea-pod, this gentleman. (To Mamillius) My honest friend, will you let yourself be fooled?"]],"69":[[0,5,"Gone already! Inch-thick, knee-deep, over head and ears a horned cuckold! Go and play, boy, play. Your mother plays, and I play too, but such a shameful part that its ending will hiss me to my grave. Scorn and jeering will be my funeral bell. Go and play, boy, play."],[6,15,"There have been cuckolds before now, unless I'm much mistaken, and many a man, even at this moment, as I speak, holds his wife by the arm with no idea that she has been flooded while he was away, and his pond fished by his next-door neighbour, Sir Smile. Well, there's comfort in that: other men have gates, and those gates opened against their will, like mine. If every man with an unfaithful wife despaired, a tenth of mankind would hang themselves. There's no medicine for it."],[16,22,"It is a lecherous planet that strikes wherever it is in the ascendant, and believe me, it is powerful east, west, north and south. In short, there is no barricade for a womb. Make no mistake: it will let the enemy in and out with all his baggage. Thousands of us have the disease and don't feel it. Well now, boy!"]],"90":[[0,15,"My gracious lord, I may be careless, foolish and fearful. No man is free of any of these: in the endless business of the world his carelessness, his folly or his fear sometimes shows. In your affairs, my lord, if I was ever wilfully careless, it was my folly. If I ever played the fool on purpose, it was carelessness, not thinking about the outcome. If I was ever afraid to do something when I doubted how it would end, and doing it cried out against not doing it, that was a fear that often affects the wisest. These, my lord, are accepted weaknesses that even honesty is never free of. But, I beg your Grace,"],[16,18,"be plainer with me. Let me see my offence by its own face. If I then deny it, it isn't mine."]],"91":[[0,11,"Haven't you seen, Camillo (but that's beyond doubt, you have, or your eyeglass is thicker than a cuckold's horn), or heard (for when something is so plain to see, rumour cannot be silent), or thought (for a man who does not think has no mind), that my wife is unfaithful? If you will admit it, or else shamelessly deny that you have eyes, ears or thoughts, then say my wife is a whore, deserving a name as foul as any flax-girl who sleeps with a man before her betrothal. Say it and stand by it."]],"93":[[0,12,"Is whispering nothing? Is leaning cheek to cheek? Is touching noses? Kissing with open lips? Stopping a laugh with a sigh, a sure sign of failing virtue? Riding foot on foot? Hiding in corners? Wishing the clocks would go faster, hours into minutes, noon into midnight? And wishing everyone else's eyes were blind with cataracts except theirs, so they could be wicked unseen? Is this nothing? Why, then the world and everything in it is nothing, the sky above is nothing, Bohemia is nothing, my wife is nothing, and these nothings have nothing in them, if this is nothing."]],"99":[[0,11,"Why, the man who wears her like a medal hanging round his neck: Bohemia. If I had loyal servants around me, with eyes to look after my honour as well as their own profits and private gains, they would do something to stop any more of this. Yes, and you, his cupbearer, whom I raised from humble rank to high office, you who can see as plainly as heaven sees earth and earth sees heaven how I am wounded, you could spice a cup to give my enemy a sleep that lasts for ever. That drink would be medicine to me."]],"101":[[0,9,"Ask that question and go and rot! Do you think I am so muddled, so unbalanced, that I would bring this torment on myself? Stain the purity and whiteness of my sheets (keeping them clean means sleep; stained, they are goads, thorns, nettles and wasps' stings)? Cast doubt on the birth of the prince, my son, whom I think is mine and love as mine, without good reason? Would I do this? Could any man stray so far?"]],"109":[[0,13,"O miserable lady! But as for me, what position am I in? I must poison good Polixenes, and my reason for doing it is obedience to a master who is at war with himself and wants everyone who serves him to be the same. Promotion would follow the deed. If I could find thousands of examples of men who struck down anointed kings and prospered afterwards, I still would not do it. But since not a single one is recorded in brass, stone or parchment, let villainy itself refuse it. I must leave the court: whether I do it or not, it means breaking my neck. May my lucky star rule now! Here comes the King of Bohemia."]],"119":[[0,10,"Caught from me? Don't make me out to be a basilisk that kills with a look. I have looked at thousands of people who have done better for my favour, but killed none of them. Camillo, as you are certainly a gentleman, and learned and experienced too, which grace our rank as much as our parents' noble names that we inherit, I beg you, if you know anything I ought to be told, don't lock it up in silence."]],"132":[[0,4,"I don't know. But I am sure it is safer to avoid what has grown than to ask how it was born. So, if you dare trust my honesty, which is locked in this body of mine that you shall take with you as a pledge, leave tonight."],[5,14,"I will whisper the business to your followers, and get them out of the city in twos and threes by different back gates. As for me, I'll put my fortunes at your service, since they are lost here now that I have told you. Do not hesitate, for by my parents' honour I have told the truth. If you try to test it, I dare not stay, and you will be no safer than a man condemned by the king's own mouth, with his execution sworn."]],"133":[[0,4,"I believe you. I saw his heart in his face. Give me your hand. Be my pilot, and your place shall always be next to mine. My ships are ready, and my people expected me to leave two days ago."],[5,11,"This jealousy is over a precious creature: as she is rare, it must be great; as he is mighty, it must be violent; and as he believes he is dishonoured by a man who always claimed to be his friend, his revenge must be all the more bitter. Fear overshadows me."],[12,16,"May a quick escape be my friend, and comfort the gracious queen, who is the subject of his suspicion but has done nothing to earn it! Come, Camillo, I will honour you like a father if you carry my life away from here. Let us go."]],"163":[[0,9,"How blessed I am in my right judgement, my true opinion! O, I wish I knew less! How cursed I am in being so blessed! There may be a spider soaked in a cup, and a man may drink and go away and take no poison, because his mind is not infected. But if someone shows him the disgusting ingredient and tells him what he has drunk, he retches violently till his throat and sides crack. I have drunk, and seen the spider."],[10,17,"Camillo helped him in this, his go-between. There is a plot against my life and my crown. Everything I suspected is true. That false villain I hired had already been hired by him. He has revealed my plan, and I am left a pinched fool, yes, a mere toy for them to play with as they like. How did the back gates come to be so easily opened?"]],"170":[[0,14,"You, my lords, look at her, look at her well. Just start to say 'She is a fine lady', and the honesty of your hearts will add 'It's a pity she's not chaste and honourable.' Praise her just for her outward looks, which I swear deserve high praise, and at once the shrug, the 'hm' or 'ha', these little marks of slander—O, I am wrong, of mercy, for slander would brand virtue itself—these shrugs, these hums and has, will come in between 'she's fine' and 'she's honest'. But let it be known, from the man who has most cause to grieve that it is so: she's an adulteress!"]],"172":[[0,6,"You are the one who has mistaken, my lady, Polixenes for Leontes. O you thing, I won't call you by a name that fits your rank, in case rudeness, following my example, uses the same language to all ranks and leaves out the polite difference between prince and beggar."],[7,14,"I have said she's an adulteress. I have said with whom. More, she's a traitor, and Camillo is her accomplice, and someone who knows what she should be ashamed to know, except with her vile partner: that she's a bed-hopper, as bad as the women the common people call the boldest names. Yes, and she knew about their recent escape."]],"175":[[0,10,"Some evil planet is ruling. I must be patient till the heavens look on me more favourably. Good my lords, I am not inclined to weep, as women commonly are, and the lack of that useless dew may perhaps dry up your pity. But I have an honourable grief lodged here that burns worse than tears can drown. I beg you all, my lords, judge me with thoughts tempered by your charity. And so let the king's will be done."]],"186":[[0,10,"We speak for your sake, not our own. You are deceived, and by some troublemaker who will be damned for it. If I knew the villain, I would damn him here on earth. If her honour is flawed: I have three daughters, the eldest eleven, the second and third nine and about five. If this proves true, they'll pay for it. On my honour, I'll sterilise them all. They shall not reach fourteen to bear false children. They are my heirs, and I would rather geld myself than have them produce children who are not legitimate."]],"191":[[0,9,"Why, what need is there for us to discuss this with you at all, instead of following our own strong impulse? Our royal right does not need your advice. Only our natural goodness tells you this. And if you, either stupefied or skilfully pretending to be, cannot or will not taste a truth as we do, understand that we need no more of your advice. The matter, the loss, the gain and how to handle it all belong to us."]],"193":[[0,7,"How could that be? Either you are very ignorant from old age, or you were born a fool. Camillo's flight, added to their familiarity, which was as plain as anything ever guessed at, lacking only being seen, with nothing missing for proof but the sight, and every other circumstance pointing to the deed, drives me to act."],[8,15,"Still, for greater confirmation (for in an act this important it would be pitiful to be rash), I have sent Cleomenes and Dion in haste to holy Delphi, to Apollo's temple. You know they are thoroughly capable. They will bring everything from the oracle, and its holy advice will stop or spur me. Have I done well?"]],"195":[[0,9,"Though I am satisfied and need nothing more than what I know, the oracle will set other minds at rest, like his (pointing to Antigonus), whose foolish trustfulness will not rise to the truth. So we have thought it good to keep her shut away from our person, in case she is left to carry out the treachery of the two who have fled. Come, follow us. We are going to speak in public, for this business will stir us all."]],"221":[[0,6,"I'd swear it. Curse these dangerous, mad fits of the king's! He must be told about it, and he shall. The job suits a woman best. I'll take it on. If I turn out sweet-tongued, let my tongue blister and never again trumpet my red-faced anger."],[7,13,"Please, Emilia, give the queen my most obedient respects. If she dares trust me with her little baby, I'll show it to the king and promise to be her advocate as loudly as anyone. We don't know how he may soften at the sight of the child. The silence of pure innocence often persuades when speaking fails."]],"222":[[0,9,"Most worthy madam, your honour and goodness are so obvious that your generous venture cannot fail to succeed. There is no lady alive so fit for this great errand. If your ladyship will step into the next room, I'll tell the queen straight away of your most noble offer. Only today she was turning over this very plan, but dared not ask anyone of rank, in case she was refused."]],"268":[[0,7,"It is yours, and, as the old proverb says, it's so like you it's the worse for it. Look, my lords: although the print is small, it is a complete copy of the father. Eye, nose, lip, the way he frowns, his forehead; even the hollow, the pretty dimples of his chin and cheek; his smiles; the very shape of hand, nail and finger."],[8,12,"And you, good goddess Nature, who have made her so like the man who fathered her, if you have charge of the mind too, put no yellow of jealousy in it among all its colours, in case she suspects, as he does, that her children are not her husband's!"]],"278":[[0,11,"You traitor, you put your wife up to this. My child? Away with it! You, who have such a tender heart for it, take it away and see it burnt at once. You and nobody else. Pick it up now. Within the hour bring me word it's done, with good witnesses, or I'll take your life and everything else you call yours. If you refuse and want to face my anger, say so, and I'll dash out the bastard's brains with my own hands. Go, take it to the fire, for you set your wife on."]],"283":[[0,9,"I am a feather blown by every wind. Shall I live to see this bastard kneel and call me father? Better burn it now than curse it then. But so be it: let it live. No, it shall not either. (To Antigonus) You, sir, come here, you who have been so tenderly busy with Lady Margery, your midwife there, to save this bastard's life (for it is a bastard, as sure as this beard is grey). What will you risk to save this brat's life?"]],"287":[[0,8,"Listen, and do it, do you see? If you fail in any detail, it will mean death not only for you but for your foul-tongued wife, whom we pardon for now. We command you, as our sworn subject, to carry this female bastard away from here, take it to some remote and deserted place quite outside our kingdom, and leave it there, without any more mercy, to its own protection and the kindness of the weather."],[9,13,"As it came to us by strange fortune, I charge you in justice, at the risk of your soul and body's torture, to leave it as a stranger in some place where chance may nurse it or end it. Pick it up."]],"294":[[0,9,"They have been away twenty-three days. That's good speed. It means great Apollo wants the truth of this to be known quickly. Get ready, lords. Call a court session so that we can put our most disloyal lady on trial. For as she has been publicly accused, so she shall have a just and open trial. While she lives, my heart will be a burden to me. Leave me, and think about what I have ordered."]],"312":[[0,10,"Since all I can say must simply contradict the charge, and the only evidence on my side comes from me, it will hardly help me to say 'Not guilty'. My honesty, being counted as falsehood, will be taken as falsehood when I speak it. But if the gods watch our human actions, as they do, I have no doubt that innocence will make false accusation blush and tyranny tremble at patience. You, my lord, know best,"],[11,23,"though you will least seem to, that my past life has been as self-controlled, as chaste and as true as I am now unhappy, which is more than any story can match, even one invented and acted to move an audience. For look at me: partner in the royal bed, owner of half the throne, a great king's daughter, mother of a promising prince, standing here to chatter and plead for my life and honour in front of anyone who cares to come and listen. As for life, I value it as I value grief, which I would gladly be spared. As for honour, it passes from me to my children, and that is all I stand up for. I appeal"],[24,32,"to your own conscience, sir: before Polixenes came to your court, how I stood in your favour and how I deserved to; and since he came, what improper behaviour I have stooped to that I should appear like this. If I went one jot beyond the bounds of honour, in act or in will, let the hearts of everyone who hears me be hardened, and my nearest relatives cry 'Shame!' on my grave."]],"316":[[0,11,"I must not admit to any more faults than are truly mine. As for Polixenes, whom I am accused with, I confess I loved him as his honour required, with the kind of love that suits a lady like me, exactly the love, no more and no other, that you yourself commanded. Not to have given it would, I think, have been disobedience and ingratitude to you and to your friend, whose love has openly declared itself yours ever since he could speak, from a baby."],[12,17,"Now, as for conspiracy, I don't know what it tastes like, though it is served up for me to try. All I know of it is that Camillo was an honest man. And why he left your court, the gods themselves don't know, if they know no more than I do."]],"319":[[0,9,"Your actions are my dreams. You had a bastard by Polixenes, and I only dreamt it. As you were past all shame (those who do what you did always are), so you are past all truth. Denying it costs you more than it gains. For just as your brat has been thrown out, as it deserves, with no father to own it (which is more your crime than its), so you shall feel our justice, and in its mildest form, expect nothing less than death."]],"320":[[0,17,"Sir, spare your threats. The bogeyman you would frighten me with is what I seek. Life can be no benefit to me. The crown and comfort of my life, your love, I count as lost, for I feel it gone, but don't know how it went. My second joy, the first child of my body, I am barred from, as if I were infectious. My third comfort, born under the unluckiest star, is dragged from my breast, with the innocent milk in its innocent mouth, to be murdered. I myself am proclaimed a whore on every post. With shameless hatred I am denied the rest after childbirth that belongs to women of every rank. And last, I am rushed here to this place, into the open air, before I have got my strength back. Now, my lord, tell me what blessings I have left alive that I should fear to die. So go on."],[18,25,"But hear this, don't misunderstand me. It is not my life I care about (I don't value it a straw) but my honour, which I want to clear. If I am condemned on suspicion, with every other proof asleep except what your jealousy wakes up, I tell you it is cruelty, not law. Your honours all, I appeal to the oracle. Let Apollo be my judge!"]],"346":[[0,4,"Apollo, pardon my great sacrilege against your oracle! I'll make my peace with Polixenes, woo my queen again, and call back good Camillo, whom I declare a man of truth and mercy."],[5,16,"For, carried away by my jealousy to bloody thoughts and revenge, I chose Camillo to poison my friend Polixenes. And it would have been done, if Camillo's good mind had not delayed my hasty order, though I threatened him with death if he did not do it and promised him reward if he did. He, most humane and full of honour, revealed my plot to my royal guest, left his fortunes here, which you knew were great, and gave himself up to the certain risks of every uncertainty,"],[17,19,"with nothing but his honour to his name. How he shines through my rust! And how his goodness makes my deeds look blacker!"]],"350":[[0,9,"What carefully devised tortures have you for me, tyrant? What wheels? Racks? Fires? What flaying, boiling in lead or oil? What old or new torture must I suffer, when every word I say deserves the very worst you can do? Your tyranny, working together with your jealousy, fancies too weak for boys, too green and silly for girls of nine, O, think what they have done, and then go mad indeed, stark mad! For all your past follies were only a taste of it."],[10,18,"That you betrayed Polixenes was nothing: that only showed you up as a fickle and damnably ungrateful fool. Nor was it much that you would have poisoned good Camillo's honour by making him kill a king: small offences, next to something more monstrous. And among those I count throwing out your baby daughter to the crows as nothing or little, though a devil would have wept tears out of fire before doing it."],[19,27,"Nor is the death of the young prince directly blamed on you, whose honourable thoughts, high thoughts for one so young, broke his heart when he understood that a coarse and foolish father had stained his gracious mother. No, that is not charged to you. But the last thing (O lords, when I have said it, cry 'Woe!'): the queen, the queen, the sweetest, dearest creature, is dead, and vengeance for it has not yet fallen."]],"352":[[0,11,"I say she's dead. I'll swear to it. If neither word nor oath convinces you, go and see. If you can bring colour or shine to her lip or her eye, warmth to her body or breath inside it, I'll serve you as I would the gods. But, O you tyrant, don't repent these things, for they are too heavy for all your sorrows to shift. So give yourself up to nothing but despair. A thousand people kneeling for ten thousand years together, naked and fasting, on a barren mountain in constant winter and endless storm, could not move the gods to look in your direction."]],"355":[[0,8,"I am sorry for it. Whenever I find out my faults, I repent of them. Alas, I have shown too much of a woman's rashness. He is hurt to his noble heart. What's gone and can't be helped should be past grieving. Don't take my words as a torment. I beg you, rather let me be punished, for reminding you of what you should forget. Now, my good lord,"],[9,14,"sir, royal sir, forgive a foolish woman. The love I bore your queen... There, a fool again! I'll speak no more of her, nor of your children. I won't remind you of my own husband, who is lost too. Arm yourself with patience, and I'll say nothing."]],"356":[[0,11,"You only spoke well when you spoke the truth, and I take that much better than your pity. Please take me to the bodies of my queen and son. One grave shall hold them both. On it shall be written the causes of their death, to our everlasting shame. Once a day I'll visit the chapel where they lie, and weeping there shall be my only pastime. As long as nature can bear this exercise, I vow to do it every day. Come, and lead me to these sorrows."]],"367":[[0,11,"Come, poor baby. I have heard, but not believed, that the spirits of the dead can walk again. If it's true, your mother appeared to me last night, for no dream was ever so like being awake. A figure came to me, with her head leaning first one way, then the other. I never saw a vessel of sorrow so full and so beautiful. In pure white robes, like holiness itself, she came to the cabin where I lay, bowed three times before me, and, gasping to begin a speech, her eyes became two fountains. When the fit was spent,"],[12,21,"this broke from her: 'Good Antigonus, since fate, against your better nature, has made you the one to throw out my poor baby, as you swore to, there are remote enough places in Bohemia. Weep there, and leave it crying. And because the baby is counted lost for ever, please call her Perdita. For this unkind business, put on you by my husband, you will never see your wife Paulina again.' And so, with shrieks, she melted into air."],[22,32,"Badly frightened, I gradually pulled myself together and thought this was real and no dream. Dreams are trifles, but this once, yes, superstitiously, I will be ruled by this one. I believe Hermione has died, and that Apollo wants this child, being truly King Polixenes's, to be laid here, to live or die, on its true father's soil. Little blossom, good luck to you! Lie there, and there's your written account; there, these things,"]],"369":[[0,10,"which may, if fortune wills, pay for your upbringing, pretty one, and still leave something over. The storm is beginning. Poor wretch, exposed like this to loss and whatever follows, for your mother's fault! I cannot weep, but my heart bleeds, and I am most cursed to be bound by oath to do this. Farewell! The day frowns more and more. You're likely to have too rough a lullaby. I never saw the sky so dark by day. A savage roar! I'd better get on board! This is the hunt: I'm gone for ever."]],"380":[[0,10,"I wish you could see how it rages and storms, how it swallows the shore! But that's not the point. O, the pitiful cries of the poor souls! Sometimes you could see them, sometimes not. One moment the ship was poking the moon with her mainmast, the next swallowed in foam and froth, like a cork thrown into a barrel. And then on land, to see how the bear tore out his shoulder-bone, how he cried to me for help and said his name was Antigonus, a nobleman. But to finish with the ship, to see how the sea gulped it down: but first, how the poor souls roared, and the sea mocked them, and how the poor gentleman roared, and the bear mocked him, both roaring louder than the sea or the storm."]],"395":[[0,8,"I, who please some and test everyone, who am joy and terror to good and bad alike, who make mistakes and reveal them, now take it on myself, as Time, to use my wings. Don't count it a crime of mine, or of my swift passing, that I slide over sixteen years and leave what happened in that wide gap untold, since it is in my power to overturn law, and in a single hour of my own making to plant and overthrow custom. Let me pass"],[9,15,"as the same I was before the oldest order existed, or what is accepted now. I saw the times that brought them in, and I shall do the same to the newest things in fashion now, and make the glitter of the present as stale as my story seems now. If you will allow it, I turn my hourglass and move my play on as if you had slept in between."],[16,25,"Leaving Leontes, who is grieving so much over the results of his foolish jealousy that he shuts himself away, imagine me, gentle audience, now in lovely Bohemia. And remember that I mentioned a son of the king's, whom I now name to you as Florizel. And now let me hurry on to speak of Perdita, grown in grace to be the wonder of everyone. What happens to her I don't care to prophesy, but let Time's news be known when it is born."],[26,31,"A shepherd's daughter, and what comes with her, is the subject of Time's story. Allow this, if you have ever spent time worse before now. If never, then Time himself says he earnestly wishes you never may."]],"401":[[0,7,"If you love me, Camillo, don't wipe out all your other services by leaving me now. Your own goodness has made me need you. It would be better never to have had you than to lose you like this. You have started business for me that nobody but you can manage properly, so you must either stay and carry it out yourself, or take away with you the very services you have done. If I have not thanked you enough (and I cannot thank you too much), I'll make it my study to be more thankful, and my profit will be more of your friendship. Please say no more about that fatal country Sicilia."],[8,14,"The very name punishes me by reminding me of that repentant king, as you call him, my brother, now reconciled, whose loss of his most precious queen and children is still to be mourned afresh. Tell me, when did you last see Prince Florizel, my son? Kings are as unhappy when their children turn out badly as when they lose them after they have proved their worth."]],"411":[[0,11,"(Singing) When daffodils begin to peep, with hey, the girl over the valley, why then comes in the sweet of the year, for the red blood rules where winter's pale used to. The white sheet bleaching on the hedge, with hey, the sweet birds, O, how they sing! sets my thieving tooth on edge, for a quart of ale is a dish for a king. The lark that sings tirra-lirra, with hey, with hey, the thrush and the jay, are summer songs for me and my girlfriends while we lie tumbling in the hay."],[12,13,"I have served Prince Florizel, and in my time wore the finest velvet, but now I'm out of work."],[14,21,"(Singing) But shall I go and mourn for that, my dear? The pale moon shines by night, and when I wander here and there I'm most on the right road. If tinkers are allowed to live and carry their pigskin bags, then I can well give an account of myself, and swear to it in the stocks."],[22,28,"My trade is sheets: when the kite is building its nest, watch your smaller linen. My father named me Autolycus. Being born, as I was, under Mercury, the god of thieves, I was likewise a snapper-up of trifles nobody watches. Dice and whores bought me these rags, and my income is cheating fools. The gallows and a beating are too great a risk on the highway. Beating and hanging terrify me. As for the life to come, I sleep away the thought of it. A prize! A prize!"]],"415":[[0,9,"I can't do it without counters. Let me see, what am I to buy for our sheep-shearing feast? 'Three pounds of sugar, five pounds of currants, rice.' What will this sister of mine do with rice? But my father has made her mistress of the feast, and she's going all out. She has made twenty-four posies for the shearers, all singers of three-part songs, and very good ones, but they're mostly tenors and basses, except one Puritan among them, and he sings psalms to dance tunes. I must have saffron to colour the pear pies. 'Mace; dates': no, that's not on my list. 'Nutmegs, seven; a root or two of ginger,' but I can beg that; 'four pounds of prunes, and as many raisins dried in the sun.'"]],"456":[[0,9,"Sir, my gracious lord, it isn't my place to scold you for your excesses. O, forgive me for mentioning them! You have hidden your high self, the gracious model of the land, in a peasant's clothes, and dressed me, a poor humble girl, like a goddess. If it weren't that our feasts have foolery in every course, and the guests are used to swallowing it, I would blush to see you dressed like that, and faint, I think, to see myself in a mirror."]],"459":[[0,11,"Expect nothing but fun. The gods themselves, lowering their godhead for love, have taken the shapes of beasts. Jupiter became a bull and bellowed; green Neptune a ram, and bleated; and the god in robes of fire, golden Apollo, a poor humble shepherd, as I seem now. Their transformations were never for a rarer beauty, nor in such a chaste way, since my desires don't run ahead of my honour, nor my lust burn hotter than my faith."]],"461":[[0,11,"Dearest Perdita, please don't darken the fun of the feast with these far-fetched thoughts. Either I'll be yours, my fair one, or not my father's. For I cannot be my own, or anything to anyone, if I am not yours. I am firmly fixed on this, even if destiny says no. Be merry, gentle one. Strangle thoughts like these with anything you see around you. Your guests are coming. Lift up your face, as if it were the day of the wedding that we two have sworn shall come."]],"465":[[0,7,"For shame, daughter! When my old wife was alive, on this day she was pantry-maid, butler and cook, mistress and servant. She welcomed everyone, served everyone, sang her song and danced her turn, now at the top of the table, now in the middle, leaning on his shoulder and his, her face on fire with work, and she would sip to each one with the drink she took to cool it. You keep in the background,"],[8,15,"as if you were a guest and not the hostess of the party. Please welcome these strangers to us, for that's the way to make us better friends and better acquainted. Come, put out your blushes and show yourself for what you are, mistress of the feast. Come on, and welcome us to your sheep-shearing, as you want your good flock to thrive."]],"471":[[0,9,"Suppose there is. Yet nature is improved by no means but nature makes that means. So, above the art that you say adds to nature, there is an art that nature makes. You see, sweet girl, we marry a gentler shoot to the wildest stock, and make a tree of humbler kind conceive by a bud of nobler breed. This is an art that improves nature, or rather changes it, but the art itself is nature."]],"474":[[0,9,"I won't push a spade into the earth to plant a single cutting of them, any more than, if I wore make-up, I would want this young man to say it looked well and desire to have children with me only for that. Here are flowers for you: hot lavender, mint, savory, marjoram, the marigold that goes to bed with the sun and rises with him, weeping dew. These are midsummer flowers, and I think they are given to men of middle age. You're very welcome."]],"476":[[0,2,"Oh dear! You'd be so thin that January winds would blow right through you. (To Florizel) Now, my fairest friend,"],[3,19,"I wish I had some spring flowers that might suit your time of life; (to the shepherdesses) and yours, and yours, who still carry your maidenhood on your young branches. O Proserpina, if only I had the flowers that you let fall in fright from Pluto's chariot! Daffodils, that come before the swallow dares, and take the March winds with their beauty; violets, dim, but sweeter than Juno's eyelids or the breath of Venus; pale primroses, that die unmarried before they can see the bright sun in his strength (an illness common in young girls); bold oxlips and the crown imperial; lilies of every kind, the iris being one. O, I lack these to make garlands for you, and to scatter over and over my sweet friend!"]],"479":[[0,11,"Whatever you do improves on what has been done. When you speak, sweet, I'd have you speak for ever. When you sing, I'd have you buy and sell like that, give to the poor like that, pray like that, and give orders to your household singing too. When you dance, I wish you were a wave of the sea, so that you might do nothing but that, move always, always like that, and have no other task. Every single thing you do, so special in every detail, crowns what you are doing at the moment, so that all your acts are queens."]],"507":[[0,12,"(Singing) Linen as white as driven snow, crape as black as any crow, gloves as sweet as damask roses, masks for faces and for noses, bead bracelets, amber necklaces, perfume for a lady's bedroom, golden caps and bodices for my lads to give their sweethearts, pins and steel rods for ironing ruffs, everything girls need from head to heel. Come and buy from me, come; come buy, come buy. Buy, lads, or else your girls will cry. Come and buy."]],"564":[[0,2,"(To the Shepherd) O, old man, you'll hear more of that later. (To Camillo) Hasn't it gone too far? It's time to part them. He's simple and tells a lot. (To Florizel) Well, handsome shepherd!"],[3,13,"Your heart is full of something that takes your mind off the feast. Truly, when I was young and courted as you do, I used to load my girl with trinkets. I would have ransacked the pedlar's silk treasury and poured it out for her to accept. You have let him go and bought nothing from him. If your girl took it the wrong way and called it a lack of love or generosity, you would be hard put to answer, at least if you care about keeping her happy."]],"565":[[0,9,"Old sir, I know she does not value trifles like these. The gifts she expects from me are packed and locked up in my heart, which I have given her already, but not formally handed over. (To Perdita) O, hear me breathe out my life before this old gentleman, who, it seems, was once in love. I take your hand, this hand, as soft as a dove's down and as white, or as an African's tooth, or the snow that the northern winds have sifted twice over."]],"593":[[0,6,"I'll have your beauty scratched with briars and made plainer than your rank. As for you, foolish boy, if I ever find out that you so much as sigh that you will never see this trinket again (as I mean you never shall), we'll bar you from the throne and not count you of our blood, no, not even our kin, further off than Deucalion, the first man. Mark my words."],[7,16,"Follow us to court. You peasant, this time, though you're full of our displeasure, we free you from its death blow. And you, enchantress, good enough for a herdsman, yes, even for him, who makes himself unworthy of you, if it weren't for our honour: if from now on you ever open these country latches to let him in, or wrap his body in your embraces, I will invent a death for you as cruel as you are tender."]],"595":[[0,9,"Ruined, right here. I wasn't very frightened. Once or twice I was going to speak and tell him plainly that the same sun that shines on his court doesn't hide its face from our cottage, but looks on both alike. (To Florizel) Will you please go, sir? I told you what would come of this. I beg you, look after your own position. This dream of mine, now I'm awake, I'll play the queen not an inch further, but milk my sheep and weep."]],"597":[[0,11,"I can't speak, or think, or dare to know what I know. (To Florizel) O sir, you have ruined a man of eighty-three, who hoped to go to his grave in peace, yes, to die in the bed my father died in, to lie beside his honest bones. But now some hangman will dress me in my shroud and lay me where no priest shovels in earth. (To Perdita) O cursed wretch, who knew this was the prince, and dared to pledge yourself to him! Ruined, ruined! If I could die within this hour, I would have lived to die when I wanted."]],"608":[[0,11,"Call it that. But it keeps my vow, so I must think it honesty. Camillo, not for Bohemia, nor for the splendour that could be gathered from it, nor for everything the sun sees, or the earth holds in its womb, or the deep seas hide in unknown depths, will I break my oath to my fair beloved here. So I beg you, since you have always been my father's honoured friend, when he misses me (and truly I don't mean to see him any more), calm his anger with your good advice. Let me and fortune fight it out for the future. This much you may know,"],[12,18,"and tell him: I have gone to sea with her, since I cannot keep her here on shore. And, most luckily for her need, I have a ship riding at anchor close by, though not fitted out for this purpose. Where I mean to go will do your knowledge no good, nor do I want it reported."]],"615":[[0,13,"Well, my lord, if you are willing to believe that I love the king, and through him what's closest to him, which is your gracious self, just accept my direction, if your weighty, settled plan can be changed. On my honour, I'll show you where you will get a welcome fit for your Highness, where you may enjoy your love, from whom I see there's no separating you, except, heaven forbid, by your ruin. Marry her, and while you are away I'll do my best to calm your displeased father and bring him round to approving."]],"619":[[0,13,"Then listen to me. If you won't change your plan but will go ahead with this escape, this is what follows. Sail for Sicilia, and present yourself and your fair princess (for I see that's what she must be) to Leontes. She shall be dressed as suits the partner of your bed. I think I see Leontes opening his generous arms and weeping out his welcomes, asking you, the son, for forgiveness, as if you were your father, kissing the hands of your young princess, torn again and again between his unkindness and his kindness: one he curses to hell, and the other he tells to grow faster than thought or time."]],"623":[[0,10,"A course more promising than a wild surrender of yourselves to pathless waters and undreamt-of shores, certain to bring miseries enough, with no hope of help except shaking off one trouble only to take on another, nothing so certain as your anchors, which do their best work if they can just hold you where you'll hate to be. Besides, you know that prosperity is the very bond of love, and hardship changes both its fresh face and its heart."]],"633":[[0,6,"Ha, ha! What a fool Honesty is! And Trust, his sworn brother, a very simple gentleman! I have sold all my junk. Not a fake jewel, ribbon, mirror, scent ball, brooch, notebook, ballad, knife, tape, glove, shoelace, bracelet or horn ring left to keep my pack from starving. They crowded to see who could buy first, as if my trinkets were holy and brought the buyer a blessing. That way I saw whose purse looked fattest, and I remembered what I saw for my own good use."],[7,13,"My clown (who only lacks something to be a reasonable man) fell so in love with the girls' song that he wouldn't move his little trotters till he had the tune and the words. That drew the rest of the herd to me so that all their other senses were stuck in their ears. You could have pinched a petticoat and they'd have felt nothing. It was nothing to cut a purse off a codpiece. I could have filed off keys hanging on chains. No hearing, no feeling, but my master's song, and admiring the nothing of it. So"],[14,17,"in this sleepy time I picked and cut most of their festival purses. And if the old man hadn't come in with a hubbub against his daughter and the king's son and scared my crows off the grain, I wouldn't have left a purse alive in the whole army."]],"664":[[0,9,"I understand the business. I heard it. An open ear, a quick eye and a nimble hand are necessary for a pickpocket. A good nose is needed too, to sniff out work for the other senses. I see this is the time when the dishonest man prospers. What a swap this would have been even without anything extra! And what an extra there is with this swap! The gods must be winking at us this year, and we can do anything on the spur of the moment. The prince himself is up to some wickedness, stealing away from his father with his ball and chain at his heels. If I thought it would be honest to tell the king, I wouldn't do it. I consider it more knavish to keep it quiet, and in that I'm true to my profession."]],"705":[[0,11,"He has a son, who'll be skinned alive, then smeared with honey and set on top of a wasps' nest, then left till he's three-quarters and a bit dead, then revived with brandy or some other hot drink. Then, raw as he is, on the hottest day the almanac forecasts, he'll be set against a brick wall with the sun looking down on him from the south, and stay there till the flies have stung him to death. But why are we talking about these traitorous rascals, whose miseries we should smile at, since their crimes are so great? Tell me (for you seem to be honest plain men) what your business with the king is. If I'm rewarded handsomely, I'll take you to where he is on board, present you to him, put in a word for you in his ear, and if any man besides the king can get your requests granted, here is the man to do it."]],"728":[[0,10,"If you don't want that, you have no pity for the state, nor for the memory of his royal name. You don't consider what dangers may fall on his kingdom because his Highness has no heir, and devour the people standing by uncertain. What could be more holy than to rejoice that the former queen is at peace? What holier, for the restoring of royalty, for present comfort and future good, than to bless the king's bed again with a sweet partner?"]],"729":[[0,12,"There is no one worthy, compared with her who's gone. Besides, the gods will have their secret purposes fulfilled. For hasn't the divine Apollo said, isn't it the meaning of his oracle, that King Leontes shall not have an heir till his lost child is found? And that that will happen is as monstrous to our human reason as my Antigonus breaking out of his grave and coming back to me, who, on my life, died with the baby. It is your advice that my lord should go against the heavens and oppose their will. (To Leontes) Don't worry about an heir."],[13,15,"The crown will find one. Great Alexander left his to the worthiest, so his successor was likely to be the best."]],"762":[[0,6,"Your mother was very faithful to her marriage, prince, for she printed off a copy of your royal father when she conceived you. If I were only twenty-one, your father's image is so exactly caught in you, his very manner, that I would call you brother, as I did him, and talk of some wild things we did together before. You are most dearly welcome!"],[7,14,"And your fair princess, a goddess! O, alas! I lost a couple who might have stood like this between heaven and earth, causing wonder, as you do, gracious pair! And then I lost, through my own folly, the company and friendship of your fine father, whom, though I live in misery, I long to see once more."]],"763":[[0,9,"It is by his command that I have landed in Sicilia, and I bring you from him all the greetings that a king, as a friend, can send his brother. If weakness, which comes with old age, had not taken away some of the strength he wished for, he would himself have crossed the lands and seas between your throne and his to see you, whom he loves (he told me to say so) more than all the sceptres and those who hold them now alive."]],"764":[[0,9,"O my brother (good gentleman!), the wrongs I have done you stir again inside me, and these duties of yours, so rarely kind, show up my own slowness! Welcome here, as spring is to the earth. And has he really exposed this paragon to the fearful, or at least rough, treatment of dreadful Neptune, to greet a man not worth her trouble, much less the risk to her person?"]],"767":[[0,9,"Most royal sir, from there; from him, whose tears when he parted from her showed she was his daughter. From there, with a friendly south wind behind us, we have crossed to carry out my father's order to visit your Highness. I have sent most of my followers away from your Sicilian shores to Bohemia, to report not only my success in Libya, sir, but my safe arrival, and my wife's, here where we are."]],"768":[[0,10,"May the blessed gods clear all infection from our air while you stay here! You have a holy father, a gracious gentleman, against whose person, sacred as it is, I have sinned. The heavens took angry note of it and have left me childless. And your father is blessed, as he deserves from heaven, with you, worthy of his goodness. What might I have been, if I could look now on a son and daughter as fine as you!"]],"801":[[0,11,"Then you have missed a sight that had to be seen; it can't be described. There you could have seen one joy crown another, in such a way that it seemed sorrow wept to leave them, for their joy waded in tears. There was casting up of eyes and holding up of hands, with faces so wild that they could be recognised only by their clothes, not their features. Our king, ready to leap out of himself with joy at finding his daughter, cries, as if that joy had suddenly become a loss, 'O, your mother, your mother!' Then he asks the King of Bohemia's forgiveness, then embraces his son-in-law, then smothers his daughter with hugs again. Now he thanks the old shepherd, who stands by like a weather-beaten fountain statue that has seen many kings' reigns. I never heard of such another meeting. It lames any report that tries to follow it, and defeats any description that tries to capture it."]],"847":[[0,10,"As she might have been now, it would have comforted me as much as it pierces my soul now. O, this is how she stood, with just such majestic life, warm life, as it stands coldly now, when I first courted her! I am ashamed. Doesn't the stone rebuke me for being more stone than it is? O royal work of art, there's magic in your majesty that has called my sins back to memory, and taken the spirit from your wondering daughter, who stands like stone with you."]],"880":[[0,11,"O, peace, Paulina! You should take a husband with my consent, as I take a wife with yours. This is a bargain, and made between us by vows. You have found mine, but how is still to be asked, for I saw her, as I thought, dead, and have said many prayers in vain at her grave. I won't look far (for I partly know his mind) to find you an honourable husband. Come, Camillo, and take her by the hand, whose worth and honesty are well known and here confirmed by us, a pair of kings. Let's leave this place."],[12,20,"(To Hermione) What! Look at my brother. Forgive me, both of you, that I ever put my evil suspicion between your innocent looks. This is your son-in-law, the king's son, who, by heaven's guidance, is betrothed to your daughter. Good Paulina, lead us from here, where we can take our time to ask and answer each one's part in this wide gap of time since we were first separated. Lead us away quickly!"]]};
const QUOTES={"themes":{"jealousy":["Jealousy and suspicion","Leontes's sudden, baseless jealousy, how it spreads, and what it destroys."],"justice":["Tyranny and justice","The king's power, the trial, the oracle and who has the right to judge."],"loss":["Loss and recovery","Children lost and found, death, grief, and 'that which is lost'."],"time":["Time and the seasons","Winter and spring, sixteen years, youth and age, and Time himself."],"nature":["Nature and art","Flowers, grafting, breeding, the statue, and whether art can improve nature."],"family":["Parents and children","Fathers and sons, mothers and daughters, likeness, inheritance and rank."],"grace":["Grace, faith and forgiveness","Repentance, penance, faith and the forgiveness of the last scene."],"wonder":["Wonder and the old tale","Stories, ballads, tricks and miracles: what is unbelievable but true."]},"quotes":[{"id":"9","who":"CAMILLO","text":"shook hands, as over a vast; and embraced as it were from the ends of opposed winds.","themes":["time","family"],"note":"The two kings' friendship spans a great gap, like the gap of sixteen years to come."},{"id":"10","who":"ARCHIDAMUS","text":"I think there is not in the world either malice or matter to alter it.","themes":["jealousy"],"note":"Dramatic irony: within minutes the friendship collapses."},{"id":"11","who":"CAMILLO","text":"It is a gallant child; one that indeed physics the subject, makes old hearts fresh.","themes":["family","loss"],"note":"Mamillius is the kingdom's hope, which makes his loss more painful."},{"id":"18","who":"POLIXENES","text":"Nine changes of the watery star hath been The shepherd's note since we have left our throne Without a burden.","themes":["time","jealousy"],"note":"Nine months: the exact length of Hermione's pregnancy."},{"id":"34","who":"HERMIONE","text":"A lady's verily is As potent as a lord's.","themes":["justice"],"note":"Hermione's wit and confidence are what win Polixenes over."},{"id":"37","who":"POLIXENES","text":"Two lads that thought there was no more behind But such a day tomorrow as today, And to be boy eternal.","themes":["time","family"],"note":"A picture of innocent youth, before time and desire."},{"id":"39","who":"POLIXENES","text":"We were as twinn'd lambs that did frisk i' th' sun And bleat the one at th' other.","themes":["nature","time"],"note":"Pastoral imagery of innocence, which Act 4 will return to."},{"id":"49","who":"LEONTES","text":"\"I am yours for ever.\"","themes":["grace","jealousy"],"note":"Leontes remembers Hermione's first vow just before he stops believing her."},{"id":"50","who":"HERMIONE","text":"The one for ever earn'd a royal husband; Th' other for some while a friend.","themes":["jealousy"],"note":"An innocent line that Leontes hears as a confession."},{"id":"52","who":"LEONTES","text":"Too hot, too hot! To mingle friendship far is mingling bloods.","themes":["jealousy"],"note":"The first words of Leontes's jealousy: sudden, physical and feverish."},{"id":"52","who":"LEONTES","text":"I have tremor cordis on me. My heart dances, But not for joy,—not joy.","themes":["jealousy"],"note":"Jealousy as a sickness of the heart."},{"id":"56","who":"LEONTES","text":"Affection! thy intention stabs the centre:","themes":["jealousy"],"note":"Leontes knows his passion can make the unreal seem real, and still believes it."},{"id":"56","who":"LEONTES","text":"With what's unreal thou coactive art, And fellow'st nothing:","themes":["jealousy","wonder"],"note":"Jealousy works with 'nothing': the key to his self-deception."},{"id":"61","who":"LEONTES","text":"methoughts I did recoil Twenty-three years, and saw myself unbreech'd,","themes":["time","family"],"note":"Looking at his son, Leontes travels back in time."},{"id":"64","who":"POLIXENES","text":"He makes a July's day short as December;","themes":["time","family"],"note":"A child changes time itself; summer and winter, as in the title."},{"id":"69","who":"LEONTES","text":"Inch-thick, knee-deep, o'er head and ears a fork'd one!","themes":["jealousy"],"note":"Leontes imagines himself drowning in cuckoldry: 'fork'd' means horned."},{"id":"69","who":"LEONTES","text":"No barricado for a belly.","themes":["jealousy"],"note":"Coarse and bitter: Leontes now sees all women as unfaithful."},{"id":"69","who":"LEONTES","text":"Many thousand of us Have the disease, and feel't not.","themes":["jealousy"],"note":"Jealousy as a disease."},{"id":"93","who":"LEONTES","text":"Is whispering nothing?","themes":["jealousy"],"note":"Leontes builds a case out of trifles."},{"id":"93","who":"LEONTES","text":"My wife is nothing, nor nothing have these nothings, If this be nothing.","themes":["jealousy"],"note":"The repetition of 'nothing' shows a mind spinning out of control."},{"id":"94","who":"CAMILLO","text":"Good my lord, be cur'd Of this diseas'd opinion, and betimes, For 'tis most dangerous.","themes":["jealousy"],"note":"Camillo names the jealousy as a sickness."},{"id":"101","who":"LEONTES","text":"Dost think I am so muddy, so unsettled, To appoint myself in this vexation;","themes":["jealousy"],"note":"Leontes cannot believe he could be wrong, which proves he is."},{"id":"109","who":"CAMILLO","text":"If I could find example Of thousands that had struck anointed kings And flourish'd after, I'd not do't.","themes":["justice"],"note":"Camillo chooses conscience over obedience."},{"id":"130","who":"CAMILLO","text":"you may as well Forbid the sea for to obey the moon As or by oath remove or counsel shake The fabric of his folly,","themes":["jealousy"],"note":"Nothing can shake Leontes's belief."},{"id":"133","who":"POLIXENES","text":"This jealousy Is for a precious creature: as she's rare, Must it be great;","themes":["jealousy"],"note":"Polixenes sees that Hermione's worth makes the jealousy more violent."},{"id":"154","who":"MAMILLIUS","text":"A sad tale's best for winter.","themes":["time","wonder","loss"],"note":"The line that gives the play its title and mood."},{"id":"158","who":"MAMILLIUS","text":"Dwelt by a churchyard.","themes":["loss","wonder"],"note":"Mamillius's unfinished ghost story foreshadows his own death."},{"id":"163","who":"LEONTES","text":"I have drunk, and seen the spider.","themes":["jealousy"],"note":"Leontes believes knowledge has poisoned him."},{"id":"165","who":"LEONTES","text":"Though he does bear some signs of me, yet you Have too much blood in him.","themes":["family","jealousy"],"note":"Leontes begins to doubt even his son."},{"id":"170","who":"LEONTES","text":"She's an adultress!","themes":["jealousy","justice"],"note":"The public accusation."},{"id":"174","who":"LEONTES","text":"He who shall speak for her is afar off guilty But that he speaks.","themes":["justice"],"note":"Tyranny: defending the accused becomes a crime."},{"id":"175","who":"HERMIONE","text":"There's some ill planet reigns: I must be patient till the heavens look With an aspect more favourable.","themes":["grace","time"],"note":"Hermione trusts to time and the heavens."},{"id":"175","who":"HERMIONE","text":"I have That honourable grief lodg'd here which burns Worse than tears drown:","themes":["loss","justice"],"note":"Dignified suffering, without tears."},{"id":"177","who":"HERMIONE","text":"this action I now go on Is for my better grace.","themes":["grace"],"note":"Hermione sees her suffering as a test that will honour her."},{"id":"187","who":"LEONTES","text":"You smell this business with a sense as cold As is a dead man's nose:","themes":["jealousy"],"note":"Leontes insults the lords' doubt."},{"id":"191","who":"LEONTES","text":"Our prerogative Calls not your counsels,","themes":["justice"],"note":"Leontes claims absolute power."},{"id":"220","who":"EMILIA","text":"\"My poor prisoner, I am as innocent as you.\"","themes":["justice","family"],"note":"Mother and baby are both innocent prisoners."},{"id":"221","who":"PAULINA","text":"The silence often of pure innocence Persuades, when speaking fails.","themes":["justice","family"],"note":"Paulina hopes the baby will speak for itself."},{"id":"244","who":"PAULINA","text":"I Do come with words as med'cinal as true,","themes":["jealousy","grace"],"note":"Paulina as the king's physician."},{"id":"254","who":"PAULINA","text":"Good queen, my lord, good queen: I say, good queen,","themes":["justice"],"note":"Paulina repeats the words Leontes cannot bear."},{"id":"266","who":"PAULINA","text":"Whose sting is sharper than the sword's;","themes":["justice","jealousy"],"note":"Slander wounds deeper than any weapon."},{"id":"268","who":"PAULINA","text":"Although the print be little, the whole matter And copy of the father:","themes":["family","nature"],"note":"The baby is a printed copy of Leontes: likeness proves parentage."},{"id":"274","who":"PAULINA","text":"It is an heretic that makes the fire, Not she which burns in 't.","themes":["justice"],"note":"Paulina turns the charge back on the accuser."},{"id":"274","who":"PAULINA","text":"I'll not call you tyrant;","themes":["justice"],"note":"She calls him a tyrant by saying she won't."},{"id":"283","who":"LEONTES","text":"I am a feather for each wind that blows.","themes":["jealousy"],"note":"A rare moment of self-knowledge."},{"id":"288","who":"ANTIGONUS","text":"Wolves and bears, they say, Casting their savageness aside, have done Like offices of pity.","themes":["nature","loss"],"note":"Dramatic irony: a bear will kill Antigonus but spare the baby."},{"id":"288","who":"ANTIGONUS","text":"Poor thing, condemn'd to loss!","themes":["loss"],"note":"'Loss' links to Perdita's name and the oracle."},{"id":"307","who":"LEONTES","text":"Let us be clear'd Of being tyrannous, since we so openly Proceed in justice,","themes":["justice"],"note":"Leontes insists the trial is fair, which makes it clear it is not."},{"id":"312","who":"HERMIONE","text":"if powers divine Behold our human actions, as they do, I doubt not, then, but innocence shall make False accusation blush, and tyranny Tremble at patience.","themes":["justice","grace"],"note":"Hermione trusts in divine justice."},{"id":"312","who":"HERMIONE","text":"For life, I prize it As I weigh grief, which I would spare. For honour, 'Tis a derivative from me to mine,","themes":["justice","family"],"note":"She fights for honour, not life, for her children's sake."},{"id":"319","who":"LEONTES","text":"Your actions are my dreams.","themes":["jealousy","wonder"],"note":"Leontes turns her words around: his fantasies are her guilt."},{"id":"320","who":"HERMIONE","text":"Sir, spare your threats: The bug which you would fright me with, I seek.","themes":["loss","justice"],"note":"Hermione no longer fears death."},{"id":"320","who":"HERMIONE","text":"I tell you 'Tis rigour, and not law.","themes":["justice"],"note":"The clearest statement of what Leontes has done."},{"id":"320","who":"HERMIONE","text":"Apollo be my judge!","themes":["justice","grace"],"note":"Her appeal to the oracle."},{"id":"323","who":"HERMIONE","text":"The Emperor of Russia was my father.","themes":["family","justice"],"note":"Hermione is royal in her own right."},{"id":"328","who":"OFFICER","text":"Hermione is chaste; Polixenes blameless; Camillo a true subject; Leontes a jealous tyrant;","themes":["justice","jealousy"],"note":"The oracle clears everyone but the king."},{"id":"328","who":"OFFICER","text":"the king shall live without an heir, if that which is lost be not found.","themes":["loss","family"],"note":"The riddle the rest of the play solves."},{"id":"333","who":"LEONTES","text":"There is no truth at all i' th' oracle:","themes":["justice","jealousy"],"note":"Leontes's blasphemy, punished at once."},{"id":"340","who":"LEONTES","text":"Apollo's angry, and the heavens themselves Do strike at my injustice.","themes":["justice","grace"],"note":"Mamillius's death breaks Leontes's delusion."},{"id":"346","who":"LEONTES","text":"How he glisters Thorough my rust!","themes":["grace"],"note":"Camillo's goodness shows up Leontes's sin."},{"id":"350","who":"PAULINA","text":"What studied torments, tyrant, hast for me?","themes":["justice"],"note":"Paulina's furious attack begins."},{"id":"350","who":"PAULINA","text":"the queen, the queen, The sweet'st, dear'st creature's dead,","themes":["loss"],"note":"The announcement of Hermione's death."},{"id":"352","who":"PAULINA","text":"A thousand knees Ten thousand years together, naked, fasting, Upon a barren mountain, and still winter In storm perpetual, could not move the gods To look that way thou wert.","themes":["grace","time"],"note":"Paulina imagines endless winter penance."},{"id":"356","who":"LEONTES","text":"Once a day I'll visit The chapel where they lie, and tears shed there Shall be my recreation.","themes":["grace","loss"],"note":"Leontes begins his sixteen years of penance."},{"id":"367","who":"ANTIGONUS","text":"Perdita I prithee call't.","themes":["loss","family"],"note":"The name means 'the lost one'."},{"id":"367","who":"ANTIGONUS","text":"Dreams are toys,","themes":["wonder"],"note":"Antigonus doubts his dream, then trusts it, and draws the wrong conclusion."},{"id":"369","who":"ANTIGONUS","text":"This is the chase: I am gone for ever.","themes":["loss"],"note":"His last words, before the bear."},{"id":"385","who":"SHEPHERD","text":"thou met'st with things dying, I with things new-born.","themes":["time","loss","nature"],"note":"The hinge of the play: from death to new life."},{"id":"387","who":"SHEPHERD","text":"This is fairy gold, boy, and 'twill prove so.","themes":["wonder"],"note":"The Shepherd's luck is like something out of a folk tale."},{"id":"395","who":"TIME","text":"I that please some, try all: both joy and terror Of good and bad, that makes and unfolds error,","themes":["time"],"note":"Time makes errors and reveals them: the play's whole pattern."},{"id":"395","who":"TIME","text":"I turn my glass, and give my scene such growing As you had slept between.","themes":["time","wonder"],"note":"Sixteen years pass in a line."},{"id":"395","who":"TIME","text":"Perdita, now grown in grace Equal with wondering.","themes":["time","grace","wonder"],"note":"Perdita has grown in grace, a key word of the play."},{"id":"401","who":"POLIXENES","text":"Kings are no less unhappy, their issue not being gracious, than they are in losing them when they have approved their virtues.","themes":["family","loss"],"note":"Polixenes fears for his son as Leontes lost his."},{"id":"411","who":"AUTOLYCUS","text":"When daffodils begin to peer,","themes":["time","nature"],"note":"Spring arrives in the play with Autolycus's song."},{"id":"411","who":"AUTOLYCUS","text":"For the red blood reigns in the winter's pale.","themes":["time","nature"],"note":"Red blood replaces winter's pallor."},{"id":"411","who":"AUTOLYCUS","text":"a snapper-up of unconsidered trifles.","themes":["wonder"],"note":"Autolycus's description of himself as a thief."},{"id":"451","who":"AUTOLYCUS","text":"A merry heart goes all the day,","themes":["time"],"note":"A cheerful song that lightens the play."},{"id":"455","who":"FLORIZEL","text":"no shepherdess, but Flora Peering in April's front.","themes":["nature","time"],"note":"Perdita as the goddess of spring."},{"id":"458","who":"PERDITA","text":"To me the difference forges dread.","themes":["family"],"note":"Perdita is afraid of the difference in rank."},{"id":"459","who":"FLORIZEL","text":"since my desires Run not before mine honour, nor my lusts Burn hotter than my faith.","themes":["grace"],"note":"Young love that is chaste, unlike Leontes's suspicions."},{"id":"461","who":"FLORIZEL","text":"Or I'll be thine, my fair, Or not my father's.","themes":["family"],"note":"Florizel chooses love over inheritance."},{"id":"466","who":"PERDITA","text":"For you there's rosemary and rue; these keep Seeming and savour all the winter long.","themes":["nature","time"],"note":"Winter flowers for older men."},{"id":"468","who":"PERDITA","text":"our carnations and streak'd gillyvors, Which some call nature's bastards:","themes":["nature","family"],"note":"Flowers bred by human art, called 'bastards'."},{"id":"471","who":"POLIXENES","text":"we marry A gentler scion to the wildest stock,","themes":["nature","family"],"note":"Irony: Polixenes defends a 'marriage' of high and low in plants, but not for his son."},{"id":"471","who":"POLIXENES","text":"This is an art Which does mend nature, change it rather, but The art itself is nature.","themes":["nature"],"note":"The play's central argument about nature and art."},{"id":"476","who":"PERDITA","text":"O Proserpina, From the flowers now that, frighted, thou let'st fall From Dis's waggon!","themes":["nature","loss","time"],"note":"Perdita, like Proserpina, is a lost daughter whose return brings spring."},{"id":"476","who":"PERDITA","text":"daffodils, That come before the swallow dares, and take The winds of March with beauty;","themes":["nature","time"],"note":"Spring flowers and youth."},{"id":"478","who":"PERDITA","text":"No, like a bank for love to lie and play on;","themes":["nature"],"note":"Flowers for love, not death."},{"id":"479","who":"FLORIZEL","text":"When you do dance, I wish you A wave o' th' sea, that you might ever do Nothing but that,","themes":["time","nature"],"note":"Florizel wishes to stop time."},{"id":"479","who":"FLORIZEL","text":"That all your acts are queens.","themes":["family","grace"],"note":"Perdita's royalty shows through, though no one knows it."},{"id":"483","who":"POLIXENES","text":"Nothing she does or seems But smacks of something greater than herself, Too noble for this place.","themes":["family","nature"],"note":"Nature reveals Perdita's birth."},{"id":"484","who":"CAMILLO","text":"she is The queen of curds and cream.","themes":["nature"],"note":"A royal title for a country girl."},{"id":"519","who":"MOPSA","text":"I love a ballad in print alife, for then we are sure they are true.","themes":["wonder"],"note":"A joke about believing stories, as the play asks us to do."},{"id":"569","who":"FLORIZEL","text":"I would not prize them Without her love;","themes":["family","grace"],"note":"Florizel values love over power."},{"id":"591","who":"POLIXENES","text":"Mark your divorce, young sir,","themes":["family","justice"],"note":"Polixenes unmasks: a father's fury, like Leontes's."},{"id":"593","who":"POLIXENES","text":"I'll have thy beauty scratch'd with briers and made More homely than thy state.","themes":["justice","family"],"note":"A tyrant's threat."},{"id":"595","who":"PERDITA","text":"The selfsame sun that shines upon his court Hides not his visage from our cottage, but Looks on alike.","themes":["nature","justice","family"],"note":"Perdita's natural sense of equality."},{"id":"595","who":"PERDITA","text":"This dream of mine— Being now awake, I'll queen it no inch farther, But milk my ewes, and weep.","themes":["loss","wonder"],"note":"Perdita's dream seems over."},{"id":"599","who":"FLORIZEL","text":"I am but sorry, not afeard; delay'd, But nothing alt'red:","themes":["grace","time"],"note":"Florizel's constancy."},{"id":"604","who":"FLORIZEL","text":"From my succession wipe me, father; I Am heir to my affection.","themes":["family"],"note":"Florizel gives up his inheritance for love."},{"id":"618","who":"FLORIZEL","text":"we profess Ourselves to be the slaves of chance,","themes":["time","wonder"],"note":"Chance, not plans, steers the lovers."},{"id":"623","who":"CAMILLO","text":"Prosperity's the very bond of love,","themes":["family"],"note":"Camillo's realism about love."},{"id":"624","who":"PERDITA","text":"I think affliction may subdue the cheek, But not take in the mind.","themes":["grace"],"note":"Perdita's quiet strength, like her mother's."},{"id":"633","who":"AUTOLYCUS","text":"Ha, ha! what a fool Honesty is!","themes":["wonder"],"note":"Autolycus's cheerful cynicism."},{"id":"718","who":"AUTOLYCUS","text":"If I had a mind to be honest, I see Fortune would not suffer me:","themes":["wonder","grace"],"note":"Fortune keeps helping the rogue."},{"id":"723","who":"LEONTES","text":"Whilst I remember Her and her virtues, I cannot forget My blemishes in them;","themes":["grace","loss"],"note":"Sixteen years of remembering."},{"id":"724","who":"PAULINA","text":"she you kill'd Would be unparallel'd.","themes":["loss","grace"],"note":"Paulina keeps Leontes's guilt alive."},{"id":"729","who":"PAULINA","text":"That king Leontes shall not have an heir Till his lost child be found?","themes":["loss","family"],"note":"Paulina reminds the court of the oracle."},{"id":"745","who":"PAULINA","text":"That Shall be when your first queen's again in breath;","themes":["grace","wonder"],"note":"A hint of what is to come."},{"id":"752","who":"PAULINA","text":"As every present time doth boast itself Above a better gone,","themes":["time","loss"],"note":"Paulina warns against forgetting the past."},{"id":"762","who":"LEONTES","text":"I lost a couple that 'twixt heaven and earth Might thus have stood, begetting wonder,","themes":["loss","family"],"note":"Leontes thinks of his lost children."},{"id":"785","who":"PAULINA","text":"Your eye hath too much youth in 't:","themes":["time","family"],"note":"Leontes is drawn to his unknown daughter."},{"id":"796","who":"SECOND GENTLEMAN","text":"the oracle is fulfilled: the king's daughter is found:","themes":["loss","wonder"],"note":"The oracle comes true."},{"id":"798","who":"SECOND GENTLEMAN","text":"This news, which is called true, is so like an old tale that the verity of it is in strong suspicion.","themes":["wonder"],"note":"The play admits it is like an old tale."},{"id":"801","who":"THIRD GENTLEMAN","text":"cries \"O, thy mother, thy mother!\"","themes":["loss","family"],"note":"Joy and grief at once."},{"id":"805","who":"THIRD GENTLEMAN","text":"She had one eye declined for the loss of her husband, another elevated that the oracle was fulfilled.","themes":["loss","grace"],"note":"Paulina's mixed feelings."},{"id":"809","who":"THIRD GENTLEMAN","text":"would beguile Nature of her custom, so perfectly he is her ape:","themes":["nature","wonder"],"note":"Art that rivals nature."},{"id":"817","who":"CLOWN","text":"See you these clothes?","themes":["family","wonder"],"note":"Rank as a matter of clothes."},{"id":"841","who":"PAULINA","text":"prepare To see the life as lively mock'd as ever Still sleep mock'd death.","themes":["nature","wonder"],"note":"Art imitating life."},{"id":"844","who":"LEONTES","text":"Hermione was not so much wrinkled, nothing So aged as this seems.","themes":["time"],"note":"Time has marked Hermione too."},{"id":"847","who":"LEONTES","text":"does not the stone rebuke me For being more stone than it?","themes":["grace","jealousy"],"note":"Leontes's guilt before the statue."},{"id":"850","who":"CAMILLO","text":"My lord, your sorrow was too sore laid on, Which sixteen winters cannot blow away,","themes":["time","grace"],"note":"Sixteen winters of grief."},{"id":"857","who":"LEONTES","text":"The fixture of her eye has motion in 't, As we are mock'd with art.","themes":["nature","wonder"],"note":"Art or life?"},{"id":"859","who":"LEONTES","text":"No settled senses of the world can match The pleasure of that madness.","themes":["wonder"],"note":"Leontes welcomes a happy madness after a destructive one."},{"id":"861","who":"LEONTES","text":"What fine chisel Could ever yet cut breath?","themes":["nature","wonder"],"note":"The statue seems to breathe."},{"id":"867","who":"PAULINA","text":"It is requir'd You do awake your faith.","themes":["grace","wonder"],"note":"Faith is needed for the miracle."},{"id":"869","who":"PAULINA","text":"'Tis time; descend; be stone no more;","themes":["time","wonder","grace"],"note":"The statue comes to life."},{"id":"869","who":"PAULINA","text":"Bequeath to death your numbness, for from him Dear life redeems you.","themes":["grace","loss"],"note":"Death gives back what it took."},{"id":"872","who":"LEONTES","text":"O, she's warm! If this be magic, let it be an art Lawful as eating.","themes":["wonder","nature"],"note":"Leontes touches her and finds her alive."},{"id":"876","who":"PAULINA","text":"That she is living, Were it but told you, should be hooted at Like an old tale;","themes":["wonder"],"note":"The play's last joke about its own improbability."},{"id":"876","who":"PAULINA","text":"Our Perdita is found.","themes":["loss","family"],"note":"The oracle's last words come true."},{"id":"878","who":"HERMIONE","text":"You gods, look down, And from your sacred vials pour your graces Upon my daughter's head!","themes":["grace","family"],"note":"Hermione's only words are for her daughter."},{"id":"879","who":"PAULINA","text":"I, an old turtle, Will wing me to some wither'd bough,","themes":["loss"],"note":"Paulina's own loss is not forgotten."},{"id":"880","who":"LEONTES","text":"both your pardons, That e'er I put between your holy looks My ill suspicion.","themes":["grace","jealousy"],"note":"Leontes asks forgiveness."},{"id":"880","who":"LEONTES","text":"Perform'd in this wide gap of time, since first We were dissever'd.","themes":["time"],"note":"The play's last image: the wide gap of time."}]};
const EX={"intro":["Leontes, King of Sicilia, has been entertaining his childhood friend Polixenes, King of Bohemia, for nine months. When his wife Hermione persuades Polixenes to stay longer, Leontes is suddenly seized by jealousy. He orders his adviser Camillo to poison Polixenes, but Camillo warns him and they flee together. Leontes imprisons the pregnant Hermione, where she gives birth to a daughter.","Leontes orders the baby to be abandoned and puts Hermione on trial. The oracle of Apollo declares her innocent and says the king will have no heir 'if that which is lost be not found'. Leontes rejects it; at once his son Mamillius dies and Hermione is reported dead. Antigonus leaves the baby on the coast of Bohemia, names her Perdita, and is killed by a bear. A shepherd finds and raises her.","Sixteen years later Perdita and Polixenes's son Florizel are in love. When Polixenes forbids the match, Camillo sends the couple to Sicilia, where Perdita is recognised as the king's daughter. Paulina, Hermione's loyal friend, then shows Leontes a statue of his queen, which comes to life. The lost is found, but Mamillius and Antigonus stay dead."],"timeline":[{"s":"a1s1","t":"Camillo praises the kings' lifelong friendship","id":9},{"s":"a1s2","t":"Hermione persuades Polixenes to stay","id":44},{"s":"a1s2","t":"Leontes is seized by jealousy: 'Too hot, too hot!'","id":52},{"s":"a1s2","t":"Leontes orders Camillo to poison Polixenes","id":99},{"s":"a1s2","t":"Camillo warns Polixenes and they flee","id":124},{"s":"a2s1","t":"Mamillius begins a sad tale for winter","id":154},{"s":"a2s1","t":"Leontes accuses Hermione of adultery","id":170},{"s":"a2s1","t":"He has sent to the oracle at Delphi","id":193},{"s":"a2s2","t":"Hermione gives birth to a daughter in prison","id":220},{"s":"a2s3","t":"Paulina lays the baby before the king","id":256},{"s":"a2s3","t":"Antigonus must abandon the baby","id":287},{"s":"a3s1","t":"The messengers return from Delphi","id":300},{"s":"a3s2","t":"Hermione defends herself at her trial","id":312},{"s":"a3s2","t":"The oracle declares her innocent","id":328},{"s":"a3s2","t":"Mamillius is dead","id":339},{"s":"a3s2","t":"Paulina says Hermione is dead","id":350},{"s":"a3s3","t":"Antigonus names the baby Perdita","id":367},{"s":"a3s3","t":"Antigonus is pursued by a bear","id":369},{"s":"a3s3","t":"The Shepherd finds the baby","id":385},{"s":"a4s1","t":"Time passes over sixteen years","id":395},{"s":"a4s2","t":"Polixenes worries about Florizel","id":403},{"s":"a4s3","t":"Autolycus robs the Clown","id":431},{"s":"a4s4","t":"Perdita gives flowers to her guests","id":466},{"s":"a4s4","t":"Polixenes and Perdita argue about nature and art","id":471},{"s":"a4s4","t":"Polixenes unmasks and forbids the match","id":591},{"s":"a4s4","t":"Camillo sends the lovers to Sicilia","id":619},{"s":"a5s1","t":"Leontes swears not to marry without Paulina's leave","id":738},{"s":"a5s1","t":"Leontes welcomes Florizel and Perdita","id":762},{"s":"a5s2","t":"The oracle is fulfilled: Perdita is found","id":796},{"s":"a5s3","t":"The statue of Hermione comes to life","id":869}],"irony":{"10":"Archidamus says nothing could alter the friendship. In the next scene it is destroyed.","11":"Camillo says the old long to see Mamillius grow up. He will die as a child.","44":"Hermione's success in keeping Polixenes is exactly what condemns her.","106":"Camillo promises to poison Polixenes, but has no intention of doing it.","154":"Mamillius wants a sad tale for winter. His own life becomes one.","193":"Leontes sends to the oracle to confirm his suspicion. It will condemn him.","288":"Antigonus hopes bears will show the child pity. A bear will kill him, and the child will live.","307":"Leontes wants to be cleared of tyranny. The oracle will call him a tyrant.","362":"Antigonus says he will not be long. He never returns to the ship.","385":"The Shepherd jokes that the baby is a changeling. She really is a royal child left in place of a lost one.","471":"Polixenes praises grafting a gentle shoot onto a wild stock, then forbids his son to marry a shepherdess.","483":"Polixenes says Perdita is too noble for this place. She is a king's daughter.","574":"The Shepherd promises Perdita a dowry equal to her husband's wealth. Her true birth will more than match it.","724":"Paulina says the woman Leontes killed would be unequalled. She knows Hermione is alive.","745":"Paulina says Leontes may marry when his first queen breathes again. She is planning exactly that.","785":"Paulina tells Leontes his eye has too much youth in it. He is admiring his own daughter.","849":"Paulina warns that the statue's paint is not dry. It is a living woman."},"devices":{"disease":"Disease, poison and infection","seasons":"Winter, spring and the seasons","flowers":"Flowers, gardens and grafting","beasts":"Animals and beasts","sea":"Sea, storm and voyage","art":"Printing, copies, art and statues"},"dev":{"39":[["beasts","The boys as innocent lambs.","We were as twinn'd lambs"]],"54":[["beasts","Cattle, and so horns: Leontes cannot stop thinking of the cuckold.","And yet the steer, the heifer, and the calf Are all call'd neat."]],"61":[["flowers","Mamillius as a little kernel and pea pod.","this kernel, This squash"]],"64":[["seasons","A child shortens a summer's day to winter's length.","He makes a July's day short as December;"]],"67":[["beasts","Hermione as a bird offering its beak.","How she holds up the neb, the bill to him!"]],"69":[["disease","Jealousy as a sickness that thousands carry unknowingly.","Have the disease, and feel't not."]],"76":[["sea","Polixenes's stay as an anchor that would not hold.","You had much ado to make his anchor hold:"]],"94":[["disease","Camillo calls the jealousy a diseased opinion that must be cured.","this diseas'd opinion"]],"97":[["disease","Leontes imagines Hermione's sin as an infection of the liver.","Were my wife's liver Infected as her life"]],"118":[["disease","Camillo hints at a sickness he dare not name.","There is a sickness"]],"130":[["sea","The sea obeying the moon: nothing can change Leontes's mind.","Forbid the sea for to obey the moon"]],"154":[["seasons","Winter is the season for sad stories.","A sad tale's best for winter."]],"163":[["disease","Knowledge as poison: a spider in the cup.","A spider steep'd"]],"260":[["beasts","Paulina as a bossy hen, Antigonus as a henpecked cock.","unroosted By thy Dame Partlet here."]],"268":[["art","The baby as a printed copy of her father.","Although the print be little, the whole matter And copy of the father:"]],"288":[["beasts","Wild beasts who might nurse the child.","Wolves and bears, they say,"]],"352":[["seasons","Endless winter as the image of hopeless penance.","and still winter In storm perpetual"]],"380":[["sea","The sea swallows the ship in one gulp.","how the sea flap-dragon'd it"]],"411":[["seasons","Spring arrives with Autolycus's song.","When daffodils begin to peer,"]],"466":[["flowers","Winter herbs for remembrance and grace.","rosemary and rue"]],"471":[["flowers","Grafting as a marriage of high and low.","A gentler scion to the wildest stock,"]],"474":[["flowers","Midsummer flowers for middle-aged men.","The marigold, that goes to bed with th' sun"]],"476":[["flowers","Spring flowers for youth and love.","daffodils, That come before the swallow dares"]],"623":[["sea","A voyage without a map as a picture of reckless love.","To unpath'd waters, undream'd shores"]],"762":[["art","Florizel's mother printed off a copy of his father.","For she did print your royal father off,"]],"764":[["seasons","Florizel is welcome as spring to the earth.","As is the spring to the earth."]],"809":[["art","Julio Romano as nature's ape, copying her perfectly.","so perfectly he is her ape"]],"841":[["art","Art imitating life as sleep imitates death.","To see the life as lively mock'd"]],"850":[["seasons","Sixteen winters of grief.","sixteen winters cannot blow away"]],"861":[["art","No chisel can carve breath.","What fine chisel Could ever yet cut breath?"]]},"stage":{"palace":"A room in the royal palace","prison":"The outer room of a prison","street":"A street in a Sicilian town","court":"A court of justice","coast":"A desert shore of Bohemia, near the sea","time":"Time, the Chorus, with his hourglass","road":"A road near the shepherd's cottage","cottage":"The shepherd's cottage, at the sheep-shearing","gate":"Before the palace of Leontes","chapel":"The chapel in Paulina's house, where the statue stands"},"stagerules":[["prison","prison"],["court","court"],["sea","coast"],["chorus","time"],["road","road"],["cottage","cottage"],["before the palace","gate"],["paulina","chapel"],["palace","palace"],["street","street"]],"place":{"a1s1":"Leontes's palace","a1s2":"Leontes's palace","a2s1":"Leontes's palace","a2s2":"The prison","a2s3":"Leontes's palace","a3s1":"On the road from Delphi","a3s2":"The court of justice","a3s3":"The coast of Bohemia","a4s1":"Time, the Chorus","a4s2":"Polixenes's palace","a4s3":"A road in Bohemia","a4s4":"The shepherd's cottage","a5s1":"Leontes's palace","a5s2":"Before the palace","a5s3":"Paulina's chapel"},"arc":{"Winter":[2,7,9,8,10,5,10,7,4,4,1,4,6,2,1],"Spring":[5,2,3,3,2,4,1,5,6,3,9,9,4,8,10]},"arcnote":"Winter rises fast as Leontes's jealousy takes hold, peaks at the trial, when his son dies and his wife is reported dead, and stays high as the baby is abandoned and Antigonus is killed. The turn comes in 3.3, when the Shepherd finds 'things new-born'. After Time's sixteen-year leap, spring takes over: Autolycus's songs and the sheep-shearing feast are full of flowers, youth and love, though Polixenes's fury brings back a touch of winter. In Act 5 the grief of Sicilia lingers, but spring returns with the lost daughter and ends in full bloom when the statue lives.","context":[["Date","Probably written in 1610 or 1611. The astrologer Simon Forman saw it at the Globe on 15 May 1611 and wrote a summary. It was first printed in the First Folio of 1623, as the last of the comedies."],["Source","Shakespeare's main source was Robert Greene's prose romance Pandosto (1588). He swapped the kingdoms (Greene's jealous king rules Bohemia) and changed the ending: in Greene the queen really dies and the king later kills himself. Paulina, Antigonus's bear, Autolycus and the statue are Shakespeare's additions."],["Romance","The Winter's Tale is one of Shakespeare's late plays, often called romances, with Pericles, Cymbeline and The Tempest. They share lost children, long journeys, the passing of years, and endings of reunion and forgiveness that feel almost miraculous. 'Romance' is a later critics' label: the First Folio of 1623 prints this play and The Tempest among the comedies, Cymbeline among the tragedies, and leaves Pericles out."],["Time and the unities","Classical rules said a play should take place in one day and one place. Sir Philip Sidney had mocked plays in which a child is born and grows up in two hours. Shakespeare does exactly that, and makes Time come on stage to ask our patience."],["The coast of Bohemia","Bohemia, now part of the Czech Republic, has no coast. Ben Jonson joked about the mistake. Shakespeare took the geography from Greene, and it adds to the fairy-tale feel of the play."],["The bear","Bears were familiar to Londoners from the bear-baiting pits near the Globe. Some scholars think a real tame bear may have been used; others imagine an actor in a bear skin. Either way the moment mixes horror and comedy."],["Apollo and the oracle","The play is set in a pagan world. The oracle of Apollo at Delphi was the most famous in ancient Greece. Leontes's defiance of the god, punished instantly, makes his jealousy a sin as well as a crime."],["Queens on trial","Shakespeare's audience would remember that Anne Boleyn, mother of Elizabeth I, had been tried and executed on charges of adultery and treason. Hermione's trial echoes that history, but she is cleared."],["Statues and religion","Protestant England distrusted statues of saints as idolatry. Paulina insists her 'spell' is lawful and asks the audience to 'awake your faith'. The scene plays with the line between art, magic and miracle."],["Julio Romano","Julio Romano (about 1499 to 1546) was an Italian painter and architect, a pupil of Raphael. He is the only Renaissance artist Shakespeare names. He was famous for lifelike work, though better known as a painter than a sculptor."],["Rogues and ballads","Autolycus belongs to the world of the 'coney-catching' pamphlets (some by Robert Greene himself) that described London tricksters. Printed ballads of strange news, sold by pedlars, were hugely popular."],["Sheep-shearing feasts","Sheep-shearing was a country festival with food, dancing and a 'mistress of the feast'. The scene draws on pastoral poetry, Whitsun plays and real rural customs, and its dance of satyrs may echo a masque at court."]],"critics":{"jealousy":[["Samuel Taylor Coleridge, lectures and notes (1810s)","Paraphrased: Coleridge contrasted Leontes with Othello. Othello is worked upon by a villain; Leontes's jealousy comes from within, from a mind already inclined to suspicion."],["Stanley Cavell, 'Recounting Gains, Showing Losses' (1987)","Paraphrased: Cavell read Leontes's jealousy as a form of philosophical scepticism: his real terror is that he cannot know whether his children are his own."]],"wonder":[["Samuel Johnson, edition of Shakespeare (1765)","Paraphrased: Johnson agreed with an earlier editor that the play, with all its absurdities, is very entertaining."],["Harold Bloom, Shakespeare: The Invention of the Human (1998)","Paraphrased: Bloom praised Autolycus as one of Shakespeare's most delightful rogues, a burst of comic life that the play needs between its tragic first half and its solemn ending."]],"nature":[["G. Wilson Knight, The Crown of Life (1947)","Paraphrased: Knight saw the play as a myth of great creating nature, in which the statue scene is a kind of resurrection."],["Lytton Strachey, 'Shakespeare's Final Period' (1904)","Paraphrased: Strachey thought the late plays showed a Shakespeare grown bored, more interested in poetry than in the people he created. Later critics have mostly rejected this view."]],"time":[["E. M. W. Tillyard, Shakespeare's Last Plays (1938)","Paraphrased: Tillyard found in the late plays a pattern of prosperity, destruction and regeneration, which The Winter's Tale shows most completely, stretched across two generations."],["Northrop Frye, A Natural Perspective (1965)","Paraphrased: Frye linked the play's movement from winter to spring with seasonal myth and ritual, a cycle of death and rebirth."]],"family":[["Carol Thomas Neely, Broken Nuptials in Shakespeare's Plays (1985)","Paraphrased: Neely argued that the play finally accepts women's sexuality and childbirth, which Leontes had feared, and that women's friendship, between Hermione, Paulina and Perdita, makes the ending possible."],["Ben Jonson, conversations with William Drummond (1619)","Paraphrased: Jonson mocked Shakespeare for bringing in shipwrecked men in Bohemia, a country with no sea within a hundred miles."]],"grace":[["Northrop Frye, 'Recognition in The Winter's Tale' (1963)","Paraphrased: Frye saw the recognition scenes as a return to a lost world of innocence, in which the play's final miracle restores more than it can explain."],["Nevill Coghill, 'Six Points of Stage-Craft in The Winter's Tale' (1958)","Paraphrased: Coghill argued that the features critics called clumsy, such as Time, the bear and the hidden Hermione, are deliberate pieces of stagecraft that work powerfully in the theatre."]]},"why":["Sets up a friendship that seems to bridge any distance.","Dramatic irony: the friendship breaks within minutes.","Builds up Mamillius as the kingdom's hope before his death.","Nine months: the timing feeds Leontes's suspicion.","Shows Hermione's wit and power with words.","Polixenes's picture of eternal boyhood, before time and desire.","Innocence imagined as pastoral, preparing for Bohemia.","Hermione's first vow, remembered just before trust collapses.","An innocent line that Leontes twists into guilt.","The first outbreak of jealousy, sudden and physical.","Jealousy as a sickness of the heart.","Leontes knows passion can deceive him, yet believes it.","Jealousy feeding on 'nothing': the core of his delusion.","Shows Leontes looking back through time at his son.","A child changes the seasons: links to the play's title.","Coarse, drowning imagery of cuckoldry.","Leontes's bitterness turns against all women.","Jealousy as a disease carried unknowingly.","Leontes builds a case out of trifles.","The obsessive repetition of 'nothing'.","Camillo names jealousy as a dangerous sickness.","Leontes's certainty is itself the sign of his madness.","Camillo's conscience refuses regicide.","Nothing can shake Leontes's belief, any more than the tides.","Polixenes sees why the jealousy is so violent.","The title line, and the mood of the first half.","An unfinished ghost story that foreshadows death.","Leontes believes knowledge is what poisons him.","Leontes's doubt spreads to his son.","The public accusation that shames the queen.","Tyranny: speaking for the accused becomes a crime.","Hermione trusts to time and the heavens.","Dignified grief without tears.","Hermione sees her suffering as an honour.","Leontes mocks the lords as cold and dull.","Leontes claims absolute royal power.","Mother and child as innocent prisoners.","Paulina hopes the baby's silence will persuade.","Paulina presents herself as the king's physician.","Paulina's defiant repetition of 'good queen'.","Slander as worse than a sword.","Likeness proves parentage: the baby as a printed copy.","Paulina turns the charge of heresy against the king.","She calls him a tyrant by refusing to.","A rare moment of self-knowledge for Leontes.","Ironic hope that beasts will show pity.","'Loss' anticipates Perdita's name and the oracle.","Leontes insists on fairness while acting unfairly.","Hermione's faith in divine justice.","She defends honour for her children's sake, not her life.","Leontes reverses reality: her actions are his dreams.","Hermione no longer fears death.","The plainest verdict on Leontes's trial.","Her appeal to the god.","Hermione's royal birth adds weight to her wrong.","The oracle clears everyone but the king.","The riddle that the rest of the play solves.","Leontes's blasphemy, punished at once.","Leontes recognises divine punishment.","Camillo's goodness exposes Leontes's sin.","Paulina's fury at the tyrant.","The announcement of Hermione's death.","Endless winter penance that cannot move the gods.","The start of sixteen years of mourning.","The naming of the lost one.","Antigonus doubts dreams, then trusts this one wrongly.","Antigonus's last words before the bear.","The hinge from death to new life.","The Shepherd's luck feels like a folk tale.","Time makes and reveals errors: the play's pattern.","Sixteen years vanish in the turn of an hourglass.","Perdita has grown in grace.","Polixenes's worry about his son mirrors Leontes's.","Spring arrives in a song.","Warm blood replaces winter's pallor.","Autolycus defines himself as a petty thief.","A cheerful song that lightens the play.","Perdita as the goddess of spring.","Perdita fears the gap in rank.","Florizel's love is chaste and faithful.","Florizel chooses love over inheritance.","Winter herbs suited to older men.","Flowers bred by art, called 'bastards'.","Polixenes praises mixing high and low in plants, not people.","The central statement about nature and art.","Perdita as Proserpina, a lost daughter who brings spring.","Spring flowers for young love.","Flowers for love, not for death.","Florizel wishes to stop time.","Perdita's royalty shows in everything she does.","Nature shows Perdita's true birth.","A royal title for a country girl.","A joke about believing in printed stories.","Florizel values love above power.","Polixenes's fury mirrors Leontes's.","A tyrant's threat against Perdita.","Perdita's natural sense of equality.","Perdita thinks her dream is over.","Florizel's constancy under pressure.","Florizel gives up the throne for love.","Chance, not planning, now steers the lovers.","Camillo's realism about love and money.","Perdita's quiet strength, like her mother's.","Autolycus's cheerful contempt for honesty.","Fortune rewards the rogue.","Leontes's continuing remorse.","Paulina keeps Leontes's guilt alive.","Paulina recalls the oracle's condition.","A secret hint at Hermione's return.","Paulina warns against forgetting the past.","Leontes grieves for lost children in front of his daughter.","Leontes is drawn to his unknown daughter.","The oracle comes true.","The play admits it is like an old tale.","Joy and grief at once.","Paulina's divided feelings.","Art rivalling nature.","Rank as a matter of new clothes.","Art imitating life, as sleep imitates death.","Time has aged Hermione too: a clue she lives.","Leontes's guilt before the statue.","Sixteen winters of grief.","Art or life?","A happy madness replaces a destructive one.","The statue seems to breathe.","Faith is needed for the miracle.","The statue comes to life.","Death gives back what it took.","Touch proves she is alive.","The play's last joke about its improbability.","The oracle's condition is fulfilled.","Hermione's only words are for her daughter.","Paulina's own loss is remembered.","Leontes asks forgiveness.","The play ends on the wide gap of time."],"insults":[{"id":"97","f":"a gross lout, a mindless slave","who":"Leontes","n":"Leontes turns on his most loyal adviser for doubting him."},{"id":"89","f":"thou art not honest","who":"Leontes","n":"Honesty is the one thing Camillo has, and Leontes denies it."},{"id":"170","f":"She's an adultress!","who":"Leontes","n":"The public insult that destroys the queen's name."},{"id":"172","f":"O thou thing,","who":"Leontes","n":"Leontes refuses even to call Hermione by her rank."},{"id":"172","f":"A bed-swerver","who":"Leontes","n":"Coarse slang for an unfaithful wife, spoken before the court."},{"id":"258","f":"A mankind witch!","who":"Leontes","n":"A woman who speaks boldly is called a masculine witch."},{"id":"258","f":"A most intelligencing bawd!","who":"Leontes","n":"Paulina is called a go-between for lovers."},{"id":"260","f":"Thou dotard!","who":"Leontes","n":"Antigonus mocked as an old fool ruled by his wife."},{"id":"264","f":"A nest of traitors!","who":"Leontes","n":"Every loyal subject becomes a traitor in Leontes's eyes."},{"id":"267","f":"A callat Of boundless tongue","who":"Leontes","n":"Paulina's free speech is treated as a crime."},{"id":"269","f":"A gross hag!","who":"Leontes","n":"Abuse runs out of argument."},{"id":"281","f":"You're liars all.","who":"Leontes","n":"The king rejects his whole court."},{"id":"350","f":"What studied torments, tyrant, hast for me?","who":"Paulina","n":"Paulina calls the king a tyrant to his face."},{"id":"352","f":"But, O thou tyrant!","who":"Paulina","n":"The insult Leontes most fears, and now deserves."},{"id":"591","f":"Thou, old traitor,","who":"Polixenes","n":"Polixenes turns on the Shepherd who has done nothing wrong."},{"id":"591","f":"fresh piece Of excellent witchcraft","who":"Polixenes","n":"Perdita's beauty is called witchcraft, as Paulina was called a witch."},{"id":"593","f":"For thee, fond boy,","who":"Polixenes","n":"Polixenes scorns his own son, as Leontes doubted his."},{"id":"438","f":"prig, for my life, prig:","who":"Clown","n":"The Clown curses the thief, not knowing he is talking to him."},{"id":"440","f":"Not a more cowardly rogue in all Bohemia.","who":"Clown","n":"The joke: Autolycus is listening to an insult about himself."},{"id":"674","f":"Very wisely, puppies!","who":"Autolycus","n":"Autolycus mocks the Shepherd and Clown in an aside."},{"id":"703","f":"An old sheep-whistling rogue, a ram-tender,","who":"Autolycus","n":"Autolycus, posing as a courtier, frightens them with courtly scorn."},{"id":"718","f":"these two moles, these blind ones","who":"Autolycus","n":"The rogue's view of honest country people."}],"pronouns":[{"id":"45","f":"Hermione, my dearest, thou never spok'st To better purpose.","n":"Leontes uses the loving 'thou' to his wife, just before jealousy strikes."},{"id":"167","f":"Has made thee swell thus.","n":"The same 'thou', now full of contempt."},{"id":"171","f":"you, my lord, Do but mistake.","n":"Hermione keeps to the respectful 'you', even under attack."},{"id":"97","f":"I say thou liest, Camillo, and I hate thee,","n":"Leontes's 'thou' to Camillo turns from trust to rage."},{"id":"274","f":"I'll not call you tyrant;","n":"Paulina is careful to use 'you' to the king while she lectures him."},{"id":"350","f":"What studied torments, tyrant, hast for me?","n":"After the queen's 'death', Paulina drops the courtesy and uses 'thou'."},{"id":"480","f":"O Doricles, Your praises are too large.","n":"Perdita uses 'you' to the prince she loves, aware of his rank."}],"predict":{"a1s1":["Leontes and Polixenes quarrel","The friendship is tested at once"],"a1s2":["Camillo poisons Polixenes","Camillo warns Polixenes and they flee"],"a2s1":["Hermione confesses","Hermione is imprisoned"],"a2s2":["The gaoler refuses the baby","Paulina takes the baby to the king"],"a2s3":["Leontes keeps the baby","The baby is sent away"],"a3s1":["The oracle condemns Hermione","The oracle clears Hermione"],"a3s2":["Leontes accepts the oracle","Leontes defies the oracle"],"a3s3":["Antigonus returns home","The baby is found by strangers"],"a4s1":["The story stays in Sicilia","The story moves to Bohemia"],"a4s2":["Polixenes forgets his son","Polixenes spies on his son"],"a4s3":["Autolycus is caught","Autolycus goes to the feast"],"a4s4":["Polixenes blesses the match","The lovers run away"],"a5s1":["Leontes arrests the lovers","Leontes helps the lovers"],"a5s2":["Perdita is sent back to Bohemia","Everyone goes to see the statue"]},"contrast":[{"t":"Leontes and Polixenes","pts":["Childhood friends, both kings and fathers.","Leontes's jealousy explodes in Act 1; Polixenes's rage explodes at the feast.","Both turn on their own children, and both are reconciled at the end."],"ids":[69,593]},{"t":"Hermione and Perdita","pts":["Mother and daughter, both accused of being unworthy.","Hermione defends herself with dignity; Perdita answers Polixenes with plain good sense.","Both are thought lost, and both are restored together."],"ids":[312,595]},{"t":"Paulina and Camillo","pts":["Both refuse to obey a tyrant's unjust orders.","Paulina speaks out loudly; Camillo acts quietly and flees.","Both serve their kings for sixteen years and are married at the end."],"ids":[274,109]},{"t":"Leontes and Florizel","pts":["Leontes doubts a faithful wife; Florizel trusts a girl far below him.","Leontes speaks in tangled, broken verse; Florizel in clear, lyrical praise.","Leontes destroys a family; Florizel risks his inheritance to make one."],"ids":[93,461]},{"t":"Antigonus and the Shepherd","pts":["Both old men take charge of the baby.","Antigonus obeys an order to abandon her; the Shepherd chooses to save her.","Antigonus dies; the Shepherd's kindness is rewarded with riches and rank."],"ids":[288,385]}],"keyfacts":[["Full title","The Winter's Tale"],["Written","About 1610 to 1611; seen at the Globe in May 1611; first printed in the First Folio of 1623"],["Genre","Comedy, as the First Folio classes it (the last of its comedies); modern critics call it a late play, 'romance' or tragicomedy"],["Setting","Sicilia (Leontes's palace, the prison, the court of justice, Paulina's house) and Bohemia (the coast, Polixenes's palace, the shepherd's cottage)"],["Protagonists","Leontes, Hermione and Perdita"],["Antagonist","Leontes's own jealousy; later Polixenes's anger"],["Inciting incident","Hermione persuades Polixenes to stay, and Leontes becomes jealous (1.2)"],["Turning point","Leontes defies the oracle and Mamillius dies (3.2); the Shepherd finds the baby (3.3)"],["Climax","The statue of Hermione comes to life (5.3)"],["Time span","Two parts separated by a gap of sixteen years"],["Major conflicts","Jealousy against trust; a king's power against truth; parents against children; nature against art"],["Themes","Jealousy, tyranny and justice, loss and recovery, time and the seasons, nature and art, parents and children, grace and forgiveness, wonder"]],"analysis":{"LEONTES":["Leontes has the most sudden fall of any Shakespeare hero. Unlike Othello he has no Iago: his jealousy appears out of nowhere, in the middle of a happy scene, and feeds on 'nothing'.","His language shows his mind breaking down: broken syntax, coarse sexual images, frantic questions and repetitions ('Is whispering nothing?'). He knows passion can make the unreal seem real, and believes it anyway.","As a king he becomes a tyrant, overriding his lords, threatening Paulina with fire and defying even the oracle. Only the death of his son breaks the spell: 'Apollo's angry'.","In Act 5 he is a penitent who has mourned for sixteen years. His humility before the statue ('does not the stone rebuke me For being more stone than it?') earns the restoration that follows, though not everything lost comes back."],"HERMIONE":["Hermione is warm, witty and confident in Act 1, teasing Polixenes into staying. Her ease with him is innocent, which is exactly what makes the accusation so cruel.","Under attack she shows extraordinary dignity. She refuses to weep, protects her women and trusts that 'innocence shall make False accusation blush'.","At her trial she speaks as a queen and the daughter of an emperor, fighting for her honour rather than her life, and appealing to Apollo.","She then disappears for sixteen years. When she returns as the 'statue' she speaks only to Perdita. Her silence towards Leontes leaves audiences to decide whether she forgives him."],"PAULINA":["Paulina is the conscience of the play. She is the only one who tells the king to his face that he is a tyrant, and she does it knowing he may burn her.","She is practical as well as brave: she brings the baby to the king, believing that 'The silence often of pure innocence Persuades'.","For sixteen years she keeps Leontes's guilt alive and controls his future, making him swear not to remarry without her consent. Some see her as cruel; others as the healer he needs.","In the last scene she becomes almost a magician or priest, staging the statue's 'resurrection'. Her own loss, Antigonus, is not restored, though Leontes gives her Camillo."],"POLIXENES":["Polixenes is Leontes's mirror. In Act 1 he is the innocent victim of suspicion, describing a childhood of lambs and innocence.","Sixteen years later he spies on his own son in disguise. His argument with Perdita about grafting reveals a contradiction: he approves of mixing high and low in plants, but not in marriage.","When he unmasks, his fury echoes Leontes's: he threatens the Shepherd with hanging and Perdita with disfigurement, and disowns Florizel.","In the end he is reconciled with Leontes and accepts the marriage once Perdita is proved royal, which some find too convenient."],"CAMILLO":["Camillo is the loyal servant who refuses an evil order. Asked to poison Polixenes, he chooses exile over murder: no one prospered by killing an anointed king.","He serves Polixenes for sixteen years, but longs for Sicilia. His homesickness drives the end of the play.","He is a skilled manager of other people, sending the lovers to Sicilia while secretly planning to bring Polixenes after them.","Leontes's praise ('How he glisters Thorough my rust!') shows how goodness shows up sin. At the end he is paired with Paulina."],"PERDITA":["Perdita's name means 'the lost one', and her story fulfils the oracle. Raised as a shepherd's daughter, she has a natural nobility that everyone notices.","At the feast she is modest, witty and strong-minded. She refuses to grow streaked gillyflowers, which she sees as unnatural, and argues with a king.","Her flower speech, with its memory of Proserpina, links her to the return of spring and of lost daughters.","Faced with Polixenes's anger, she says plainly that the same sun shines on court and cottage. In Sicilia she kneels to her mother's statue, and Hermione's only words are for her."],"AUTOLYCUS":["Autolycus is a rogue, pickpocket, pedlar and singer, named after a thief in Greek myth. He bursts into the play with a spring song and brings comedy after tragedy.","He robs the Clown by pretending to be robbed, then sells ballads and trinkets at the feast while picking purses.","He is a master of disguise: a beggar, a pedlar, and, in Florizel's clothes, a courtier who terrifies the Shepherd and his son.","He helps bring about the happy ending by accident. He never reforms properly, and the Clown's promise to swear he is honest is one of the play's last jokes."]},"motifs":[["Winter and spring","The title's sad tale for winter, a child who turns July into December, endless winter penance, then daffodils, spring flowers and youth.",[25,14,62,73,81,121]],["Disease and poison","Jealousy as a fever, a diseased opinion, a spider in the cup, and Paulina as the king's physician.",[10,17,20,27,38]],["Flowers and grafting","Perdita's herbs and flowers, the argument about gillyflowers and grafting, and Flora and Proserpina.",[81,82,83,85,86,87]],["Old tales and ballads","Mamillius's tale, Autolycus's ballads, and news so strange it is 'like an old tale'.",[25,26,92,113,129,68]],["Likeness and copies","Children as printed copies of their parents, a boy as like his father as eggs, and a girl too noble for her station.",[41,28,13,110,90,122]],["Statues and stone","The statue that seems to breathe, Leontes 'more stone' than it, and life returning.",[116,118,120,124,126,128]]],"symbols2":[["The oracle","Apollo's sealed message: the voice of truth that Leontes rejects and the riddle the whole play solves."],["The bear","The violence of nature and the hinge of the play: tragedy ends and comedy begins with a death that is half terrible, half absurd."],["The bundle","The fardel of cloak, jewel, letters and gold left with the baby: proof of birth that waits sixteen years to be opened."],["Flowers","Perdita's flowers stand for the seasons and the ages of life: rosemary for the old, daffodils for the young."],["The hourglass","Time turns his glass and sixteen years pass: the play's two halves divided by a single gesture."],["The statue","Art that becomes life: Hermione's return, and the play's question about faith and wonder."]],"bigq":[["Why does Leontes become jealous?",["There is no Iago: the jealousy seems to come from nowhere","Polixenes's nine-month stay and Hermione's pregnancy feed his fear","Polixenes's talk of lost childhood innocence may unsettle him","Shakespeare may be showing how sudden and irrational jealousy is"],[9,10,11,3]],["Is Hermione really dead for sixteen years?",["Paulina announces her death and swears to it","Antigonus dreams of her ghost","Paulina visits the remote house two or three times a day","Hermione says she 'preserved' herself to see the outcome"],[61,64,108,131]],["Is the ending truly happy?",["The lost daughter is found and Hermione restored","Mamillius and Antigonus stay dead","Sixteen years are lost for ever","Hermione speaks to Perdita, not to Leontes"],[130,131,132,134]],["Is Paulina a heroine or a tormentor?",["She is the only one who tells the king the truth","She risks her life for the baby","She keeps Leontes's guilt alive for sixteen years","She controls whether he may remarry"],[39,42,60,106]],["Is the play a tragedy, a comedy or something else?",["The first half is tragic: deaths, a trial, a lost child","The second half is pastoral comedy, with songs and young love","The bear and Time mark the change between them","The ending is a wonder more than a comic wedding"],[67,25,113,129]],["Does nature or upbringing make Perdita who she is?",["Raised as a shepherd's daughter, she seems a queen","Polixenes says nature includes the art that improves it","Perdita rejects 'nature's bastards' in her garden","Everyone notices she is 'Too noble for this place'"],[83,84,89,90]]],"symbols":{"Time":"\\btimes?\\b","Winter":"\\bwinters?\\b|\\bdecember\\b|\\bjanuary\\b","Spring":"\\bspring\\b|\\bapril\\b|\\bsummer\\b|\\bdaffodils?\\b","Lost":"\\blost\\b|\\bloss\\b|\\bperdita\\b","Grace":"\\bgrace\\b|\\bgracious\\b","Oracle":"\\boracle\\b|\\bapollo\\b"},"solo":{"109":1,"232":1,"367":1,"395":1,"411":1,"633":1,"664":1,"718":1,"813":1},"iago":[["a2s1","Leontes sends Cleomenes and Dion to Apollo's oracle at Delphi."],["a3s1","The messengers return in awe with the sealed oracle."],["a3s2","The oracle declares Hermione chaste and Leontes a jealous tyrant: the king will have no heir 'if that which is lost be not found'."],["a3s2","Leontes calls the oracle false, and at once Mamillius dies."],["a3s2","Hermione collapses and is reported dead. Leontes repents."],["a3s3","Antigonus names the baby Perdita, 'the lost one', and leaves her in Bohemia."],["a3s3","The Shepherd finds her with the cloak, jewel, letters and gold that will one day prove who she is."],["a4s1","Time passes over sixteen years. Perdita grows up."],["a4s4","Florizel loves Perdita. When Polixenes forbids the match, Camillo sends them to Sicilia."],["a4s4","Autolycus takes the Shepherd and his bundle aboard Florizel's ship."],["a5s1","Paulina reminds the court of the oracle and binds Leontes not to remarry."],["a5s1","Florizel brings Perdita, unrecognised, to her father's court."],["a5s2","The bundle is opened: 'the oracle is fulfilled: the king's daughter is found'."],["a5s3","The statue lives, and Paulina tells Hermione: 'Our Perdita is found'."]],"rel":{"1":[["LEONTES","POLIXENES","trust"],["LEONTES","HERMIONE","love"],["HERMIONE","POLIXENES","trust"],["LEONTES","POLIXENES","hate"],["LEONTES","CAMILLO","trust"],["CAMILLO","POLIXENES","trust"],["CAMILLO","LEONTES","deceive"],["LEONTES","MAMILLIUS","love"]],"2":[["LEONTES","HERMIONE","hate"],["HERMIONE","MAMILLIUS","love"],["PAULINA","HERMIONE","love"],["PAULINA","LEONTES","hate"],["ANTIGONUS","HERMIONE","trust"],["LEONTES","ANTIGONUS","hate"],["LEONTES","PAULINA","hate"]],"3":[["LEONTES","HERMIONE","hate"],["PAULINA","LEONTES","hate"],["ANTIGONUS","PERDITA","love"],["SHEPHERD","PERDITA","love"],["LEONTES","CAMILLO","trust"],["PAULINA","LEONTES","deceive"]],"4":[["FLORIZEL","PERDITA","love"],["PERDITA","FLORIZEL","love"],["POLIXENES","FLORIZEL","hate"],["POLIXENES","CAMILLO","trust"],["CAMILLO","POLIXENES","deceive"],["AUTOLYCUS","CLOWN","deceive"],["AUTOLYCUS","SHEPHERD","deceive"],["SHEPHERD","PERDITA","love"]],"5":[["LEONTES","PERDITA","love"],["LEONTES","HERMIONE","love"],["LEONTES","POLIXENES","trust"],["PAULINA","LEONTES","deceive"],["PAULINA","CAMILLO","love"],["HERMIONE","PERDITA","love"],["FLORIZEL","PERDITA","love"],["CLOWN","AUTOLYCUS","trust"]]},"examq":[["How does Shakespeare present Leontes's jealousy?",[9,11,12,18,19,27]],["How does Shakespeare present Hermione?",[4,31,32,48,51,131]],["How does Shakespeare present Paulina?",[37,39,42,60,106,125]],["How does Shakespeare present time?",[3,69,70,121,126,134]],["How does Shakespeare present nature and art?",[82,83,84,85,116,124]],["How does Shakespeare present forgiveness at the end of the play?",[63,105,120,127,128,133]]],"check":["Answered the exact question in the first sentence","Made a clear argument (a thesis) and kept to it","Used short quotations, built into my own sentences","Explained what the quotation suggests, not just what it says","Zoomed in on a single word or image","Named a method (soliloquy, imagery, chorus, irony, verse and prose, structure) and its effect","Linked to context where it helps the argument","Considered more than one interpretation, especially of the ending","Referred to both halves of the play, Sicilia and Bohemia","Ended with a conclusion that answers the question"],"models":[[9,"Shakespeare presents Leontes's jealousy as a sudden illness that attacks the body before it takes over the mind.","The doubled 'Too hot, too hot!' sounds like a man feeling a fever rise: the jealousy is physical before it is reasoned.","'Mingling bloods' moves from friendship to sex to family, the three things Leontes now doubts.","Jacobean audiences believed passions came from imbalances of the body's humours, which makes the medical language fitting.","Some productions show signs of suspicion earlier in the scene, suggesting the jealousy has been growing in secret."],[48,"Shakespeare presents Hermione at her trial as calm, dignified and confident in divine justice.","The conditional 'if powers divine Behold' quickly becomes certainty: 'as they do'.","Personification makes 'False accusation blush' and 'tyranny Tremble': Hermione imagines the guilty shamed by the innocent.","The trial of a queen for adultery would recall Anne Boleyn for Shakespeare's audience.","Her composure can also be read as despair: she says she no longer values her life."],[56,"Shakespeare makes the oracle's last clause the riddle that shapes the rest of the play.","'Lost' points at once to the abandoned baby, whose name, Perdita, means 'lost'.","The conditional 'if' leaves hope open: what is lost might be found.","Pagan oracles in Greek tragedy are always fulfilled, so the audience expects this one to be too.","The oracle says nothing of Hermione's death, which leaves room for the final surprise."],[67,"Shakespeare uses the Shepherd's words to mark the turn of the play from tragedy to comedy.","The balanced contrast of 'dying' and 'new-born' divides the play into its two halves.","Plain prose from a countryman replaces the anguished verse of the court.","Seasonal festivals celebrated the death of winter and the birth of spring.","The comedy is dark: the Clown has just described men drowning and a man eaten by a bear."],[84,"Shakespeare uses Polixenes's argument about grafting to question the line between nature and art.","The paradox 'The art itself is nature' suggests human skill is part of the natural order.","The image of marrying 'A gentler scion to the wildest stock' is ironic, since he refuses that marriage for his son.","Gardening manuals of the time described grafting in detail; audiences knew the practice.","Perdita's refusal to grow 'nature's bastards' shows she values purity, perhaps hinting at her own birth."],[125,"Shakespeare makes the restoration of Hermione depend on faith.","The command 'awake your faith' turns the audience into believers as well as watchers.","Paulina's language is like a priest's or a magician's, which she insists is lawful.","In Protestant England, statues and miracles were suspect, which makes the scene daring.","Rationally, Hermione has been alive and hidden; theatrically, it feels like resurrection. The play allows both."]],"lang":{"un":[["175","That honourable grief lodg'd here which burns Worse than tears drown:","Here is lodged an honourable grief, which burns worse than tears can drown."],["283","better burn it now Than curse it then.","It would be better to burn it now than to curse it then."],["604","From my succession wipe me, father;","Father, wipe me from the line of succession."],["320","The bug which you would fright me with, I seek.","I seek the bogeyman you would frighten me with."]],"pun":[["54","We must be neat; not neat, but cleanly, captain:","neat","pun","Tidy, and also cattle: and cattle have horns, the sign of a cuckold."],["69","Thy mother plays, and I Play too;","Play","pun","A child's play, a wife's sexual 'play', and acting a part on stage."],["93","Is whispering nothing?","nothing","pun","'Nothing' sounded like 'noting' (watching closely), which is exactly what Leontes is doing."],["367","Perdita I prithee call't.","Perdita","pun","In Latin 'perdita' means 'lost', so the name carries the oracle's riddle."],["689","Advocate's the court-word for a pheasant.","pheasant","pun","The Clown thinks an 'advocate' means a gift, like a pheasant, to win a judge's favour."],["649","Indeed, I have had earnest; but I cannot with conscience take it.","earnest","pun","Camillo asks if he is 'in earnest' (serious); 'earnest' also means a down payment."]],"st":[["52","Too hot, too hot! To mingle friendship far is mingling bloods.",[["hot","Fever and lust together: the heat of jealousy."],["mingle","Friendship imagined as physical mixing."],["bloods","Passion, and also family line: the start of his doubt about his children."]]],["385","thou met'st with things dying, I with things new-born.",[["thou","Father and son divide the two halves of the play between them."],["dying","The first half: death and loss."],["new-born","The second half: Perdita and renewal."]]],["869","'Tis time; descend; be stone no more;",[["time","Time again, the play's great power."],["descend","A movement down from the pedestal, like a goddess coming to earth."],["stone","Hermione's stillness, and Leontes's stony heart, soften together."]]]]},"dir":[["52","Too hot, too hot!","How should Leontes say this aside, as Hermione gives Polixenes her hand?",[["In a sudden, choked whisper","The jealousy strikes like a fever with no warning."],["Coldly and deliberately","He has suspected for some time and now sees 'proof'."],["With a bitter laugh","He half knows he is being absurd and cannot stop."]]],["170","She's an adultress!","How should Leontes deliver the accusation to the court?",[["Shouting, out of control","His madness is plain to everyone but himself."],["Quietly, with total conviction","His calm makes him more frightening."],["Breaking down as he says it","He is destroying himself as well as her."]]],["320","Apollo be my judge!","How should Hermione make her appeal at the trial?",[["With ringing confidence","She is sure the god will clear her."],["Exhausted, barely standing","She has just given birth and has lost everything."],["Turning away from Leontes to the court","She no longer expects anything from him."]]],["369","This is the chase: I am gone for ever.","How should the bear be staged?",[["A realistic, terrifying bear","The audience feels the full horror of Antigonus's death."],["An obvious actor in a costume","The moment becomes comic, preparing for the turn to comedy."],["Only a roar and shadows","The audience imagines the worst."]]],["591","Mark your divorce, young sir,","How should Polixenes unmask himself?",[["Throwing off his disguise in a rage","His fury echoes Leontes's in Act 1."],["Slowly and coldly","He has planned this moment and enjoys his power."],["With visible pain","He loves his son and is hurt by his deceit."]]],["869","'Tis time; descend; be stone no more;","How should Hermione come down from the pedestal?",[["Very slowly, as if waking","Sixteen years of stillness end gently."],["Suddenly, to gasps","The miracle is a shock."],["Hesitantly, looking at Leontes","She is unsure whether to forgive him."]]]],"ev":{"ctx":[["360","805"],["254","395","880"],["395","745","867"],["395","850"],["360","363"],["360","805"],["328","333","346"],["311","323","307"],["867","872","846"],["809","846"],["411","519","633"],["415","455","468"]],"cr":{"jealousy":[["52","163","333"],["56","165","319"]],"wonder":[["395","798","876"],["411","633","519"]],"nature":[["470","471","872"],["468","395"]],"time":[["154","395","850"],["455","468","154"]],"family":[["762","876","328"],["360","363"]],"grace":[["867","39","805"],["395","360","867"]]}}};
